# simpl_core integration guide

> **Maintained by**: the `simpl_core` repo, auto-synced to simpl_knowledge.
> **Source of truth**: `simpl-techs/simpl_core/.agent/SKILL.md`

Payment frequency is separate from cost allocation: `payment_frequency` is `one_time`, `monthly`, `annual`, or null (unknown). Vendor assignments and manual expense batches persist it; classification carries it to the ledger and preserves manual decisions. It does not generate future transactions or require an end date. Existing decisions remain null; `covers_months` is never interpreted as cadence. Apply the additive `roi_payment_frequency` migration before deploying the updated Core/API.

Google Play descriptors resolve to `google play` before bank references are stripped; Google Cloud descriptors, with or without a star, resolve to `google cloud`. Play and unidentified Google charges are proposed for purchase-by-purchase review. Do not infer an app or payment frequency from the amount.

## What this library is

`simpl_core` is the shared Python business layer for the simpl platform. It contains reusable domain models, repositories, and services for autopilot, outreach, CRM, email, notifications, reports, ROI / capital allocation (`simpl_core.roi`), and related platform workflows.

Request accounting uses the coordinated tracker migration and library release.
`simpl_ia` captures each model response; `simpl_core` binds agent/node/pipeline
and business attribution. `track_cost` suppresses duplicate run aggregates when
request evidence exists. Unknown prices remain visible; never synthesize costs
from an unmatched provider-export difference.

## Installation

```bash
poetry add "simpl-core[composio-sdk] @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_core.git@main"
```

Use extras only when needed:

```bash
poetry add "simpl-core[composio-sdk] @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_core.git@main"
poetry add "simpl-core[agent-framework] @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_core.git@main"
poetry add "simpl-core[mailbox] @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_core.git@main"
poetry add "simpl-core[parquet] @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_core.git@main"
poetry add "simpl-core[decks] @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_core.git@main"
poetry add "simpl-core[foundry] @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_core.git@main"
```

The `mailbox` extra (aioimaplib, aiosmtplib, google-cloud-kms, dnspython) is what an `imap` email connection needs at runtime; see "Email on other providers (IMAP/SMTP)". Nothing imports it at `import simpl_core.services.email`.

The `foundry` extra (`simpl_foundry`) is what `agent_framework.experiment_tools` and `row_store.source_reader` import. `import simpl_core`, `simpl_core.agent_framework` and `simpl_core.row_store` need neither of them.

Base `simpl-core` installs `simpl-ia` (same pin as other platform repos) so `import simpl_ia` works in the venv. The `agent-framework` extra adds `simpl-ia[agents]` (pydantic-ai) for `simpl_core.agent_framework` and `simpl_ia.agents`; that submodule is not imported at `import simpl_core`.

## Basic usage

```python
from simpl_core.services.autopilot.engine import AutopilotEngine
from simpl_core.services.autopilot.eligibility_service import EligibilityService
from simpl_core.roi import (
    calculate_company_cost,
    build_people_costs_from_payroll,
    resolve_canonical,
)
```

`roi.people_costs` is rebuilt from PAYROLL bank lines via `build_people_costs_from_payroll`: a wage is filed in the month it pays for (`RETRIBUZIONE MESE LUGLIO 2026`) and F24 on Revolut is dropped. A wage is recognised by its beneficiary whether the statement names it (`IACOPO MODICA`) or the Revolut API sync does (`To IACOPO MODICA`); the roster says whose each name is (`PayrollPerson.bank_names`, from `roi.people.bank_names`, read through `WageNames.from_roster`), and a named benefit top-up (`Ricarica Fringe Mia`) is matched on the roster's bank names and person names too. The built-in lists in `payroll.py` only cover the people hired before the roster carried their names and a roster name wins over them, so a new hire's first wage needs a roster row, not a release. `plan_classification` sends every F24 delegation (`is_tax_delegation`) to PAYROLL whatever its vendor decision, so the withholdings and contributions it pays are never counted as spend on top of the reconstructed people cost. For an employee with RAL on the roster, a month that carries a wage costs the gross (RAL / 12) plus employer INPS/INAIL/TFR (`monthly_payroll_cost`), not the net paid: the withholdings leave through F24 on an account we do not ingest. The net only says which months were paid. `PayrollPerson.start` prorates the first month by day, and `inps_relief_monthly` / `inps_relief_months` waive employer INPS for a hiring incentive. From the month of `PayrollPerson.gestione_separata_from` (from `roi.people.gestione_separata_from`; a month counts when its first day is on or after that date, `PayrollPerson.in_gestione_separata(month)`), a director paid through INPS Gestione Separata costs the gross plus the company's two thirds of the Gestione Separata rate (`NORME_2026["aliquota_gestione_separata"]`, 33.72% for 2025-26, to be confirmed by the payroll consultant), with no INAIL, no TFR and no hiring relief: `monthly_payroll_cost(..., gestione_separata=True)`. Benefits (Evinrude, Coverflex) are the amounts actually paid, employees only; an unnamed benefit is shared only among the employees already on the payroll that month (from `PayrollPerson.start`, else the first wage), so a hire carries no cost before their first month; a welfare top-up is spread evenly over the months of its calendar year the person was on the payroll (a mixed "buoni pasto e welfare" line stays in its month). Contractors cost their invoices, one a month whenever they were paid (`contractor_invoice_months`): an invoice is for the month it was paid in unless that month already has a later invoice (by the contractor's own numbering, `34/2025` in the reference); then it is for the nearest earlier month left without one. So the latest invoice paid in a month is that month's, and older ones paid alongside it fill the months before. `calculate_company_cost` stays the business-plan RAL calculator and does not write the ledger. `roi.retro` is the quarter calendar of the one-shot backfill (`quarters_between`, `mondays_between`); who owes it, and from when, is decided by simpl_api from `roi.people_costs` and `roi.time_weeks` (paid months before the weekly survey with no hours), not from a list of names. `roi.people.retro_completed_at` hides it once saved.

ROI catalog `kind` is `line` (standing) or `project` (time-boxed). `awaiting_review` rows are visible to `created_by` and to anyone with `time_weeks` on that row, until publish/merge/reject. Merge (`recategorize`) remaps every person's time onto a live destination — published or another awaiting proposal (`merged_into`). Close applies to projects (`closed_on`). A proposal carries the proposer's words in `proposal_note`, and `publish` / `recategorize` / `close` stamp `decided_by` and `decided_at` when the caller passes the actor. A project takes its `category`, `lag_profile`, `roi_ranked` and `sort_order` from its line through `catalog.child_defaults(parent, ...)` — never derive them again in a consumer, or a project published under a different line keeps the category of the line it was proposed under. `remap_person_weeks` moves every hour onto the destination; `split_person_weeks(rows, source_id=..., shares=[(dest, pct), ...])` is the same move split across several destinations for one person, and refuses shares that do not add up to 100. `TimeWeekRepository.people_on(initiative_id)` says who filed time on a row, which is what an admin needs before deciding where it goes.

Ledger target: a `roi.attribution_ledger` row names either an `initiative_id` or a `person`, never both (database check). `append_person_split(records, subject_id, people, source)` splits an expense evenly over people. In `build_dataset`, each person's share follows that person's survey hours in the expense's month, as vendor cost on those lines, the same way their wage is spread. A share whose person filed no hours that month follows their nearest month with hours (the earlier one on a tie), still booked in its own month; only a person who never filed hours keeps it in `RoiDataset.undistributed[person]`, so it stays visible.

Month by month: `InitiativeSnapshot.monthly` (`YYYY-MM` → cost) carries people and vendors together, `monthly_people` the people part, and `monthly_vendor(month)` the rest. What no line carries is kept per month on `RoiDataset`: `people_without_hours` (wages of people who filed no hours, or less than 100%), `undistributed_by_month` (seats whose person never filed hours) and `untagged_by_month` (bank lines with no attribution). For any month, the non-transit lines' `monthly` plus those three and `deferred_by_month` add up to that month's `roi.people_costs` plus its non-transit spend; transit rows (`PAYROLL`, `NON-SPESA`) are not cost.

Spread payments: `roi.attribution_ledger.covers_months` (whole batch, default 1) says how many months a line's euro covers from its booking month; a vendor assignment's `covers_months` is copied onto the lines the classifier writes (trip lines stay 1); its `payment_frequency` is copied onto trip lines too, so a vendor paid once (a trip's lodging) leaves the burn there as well. `build_dataset(..., as_of=date)` puts `amount / covers_months` in each covered month up to `as_of`'s month (today by default), on lines or on seats that then follow that month's hours. `RoiDataset.deferred_by_month` is cash minus what the lines carry (the rest of the payment in its month, minus each later month's share) and `deferred_after` is what falls after the last month shown. Only `line`, `people` and `payer` spend spreads (`vendors.SPREAD_MODES`), 1 to `MAX_COVERS_MONTHS` (60) months; `require_valid_spread` is the shared check.

Classification (`simpl_core.roi.classify.plan_classification`) turns vendor and trip decisions into ledger targets, one bank line at a time:

- A line in a trip goes where the trip goes.
- Otherwise the vendor assignment in force on the line's day decides. `line` goes to its initiative; `people` is split evenly over people; `payer` goes to the card holder; `payroll` goes to `PAYROLL`; `excluded` goes to the transit row `NOT_SPEND_ID` (`NON-SPESA`).
- `each` (a shop bought from for many reasons, like Amazon) decides nothing for the vendor: each line waits for a person to decide it (a `manuale` batch) and is `unchanged` once they have. `targets_for(mode, initiative_id=, people=, payer=, roster=)` is the one mapping from a decision to ledger targets, for a vendor and for a single line (`LINE_MODES`: line, people, payer, excluded).
- Lines no decision reaches are returned in `pending`, keyed by reason: vendor still to classify, trip-mode vendor outside any trip, a line-by-line vendor's line nobody decided, or payer not on the roster.
- A line attributed by hand is `protected`. A line whose ledger already agrees is `unchanged`.

`append_targets` writes a batch that mixes initiative and person shares. The old pattern rules (`tagging.plan_tagging`) are no longer used by the API.

Trips (`simpl_core.roi.trips`): `plan_trips(lines, known, decided)` groups travel spend by card MCC. A trip needs an anchor: airline or hotel ranges 3000-3999, Airbnb or travel agency 4722, hotel 7011, train 4112, coach 4131 or 4511. Taxis, meals, tolls, fuel and parking only join a trip on its dates. Lines chain while consecutive ones are at most 2 days apart. Lines already placed, and lines someone took out of a trip (`roi.trip_lines.trip_id` null), are never regrouped. New lines falling on a known trip's dates join it. New trips go to `DEFAULT_TRIP_INITIATIVE` (INI-60 Team e retreat) until someone decides otherwise.

Trip spend by category: `trip_category(mcc, vendor)` says what a trip line paid for, one of `TRIP_CATEGORIES` (`flights`, `rail_coach`, `lodging`, `meals`, `groceries`, `car`, `local_transport`, `unclear`). It reads the MCC; for a travel agency (4722) it reads the canonical vendor key instead (Airbnb, Booking or hotel → lodging, Kiwi → flights). An unknown agency or a line with no code is `unclear`. A hand correction is stored in `roi.trip_lines.category` (`TripLineRepository.set_category`, None clears it; `chosen_categories()` lists them) and beats the code. `TripRepository.merge(trip_id, into=...)` folds a trip into another: its lines move, the target's dates widen to cover both, the target keeps its name and decision, and the source row is deleted. That is how a booking paid weeks ahead rejoins its journey. `TripRepository.move_line(transaction_id, from_trip=..., into=...)` moves a single line instead: it keeps its category, both trips' dates are reset to their first and last line, and the trip left with no lines is deleted. Lines that change initiative either way reach the ledger on the next apply.

Revolut statement (`simpl_core.roi.revolut.parse_statement`): reads the Revolut Business CSV into `StatementLine`s with the shape `roi.transactions` already holds. The description is `Description · Reference` (the reference is how payroll dates a wage). The counterpart is the transfer beneficiary, or the description when there is none. The amount is spend, positive with the fee included; card and fee refunds come out negative. Top-ups, incoming transfers, movements that are not completed and non-EUR rows are left out and counted by reason in `skipped`. Transfers to our own other account are imported: the vendor catalog marks them `excluded`. `TransactionRepository.upsert_many` is one bulk upsert by id and answers how many lines were new.

Italian working days (`simpl_core.roi.business_days`): `national_holidays(year)` (fixed national holidays, Easter Monday, and 4 October from 2026 per Legge 151/2025), `is_business_day`, `next_business_day` (a tax deadline on a Saturday, Sunday or holiday moves there) and `add_business_days`. Pure, no I/O; city patron saints do not count.

Customer VAT numbers and fiscal codes (`simpl_core.roi.tax_ids`, `roi.tax_id_check_repository`): `read_tax_ids(tax_ids, fiscal_code, country)` takes a Stripe customer's tax IDs (`StripeTaxId(type, value)`, only `eu_vat` is checked), its metadata `fiscal_code` (what the A-Cube Stripe app sends as CodiceFiscale) and its address country, and returns a `TaxIdReading`: the `IdFiscaleIva` and `codice_fiscale` the e-invoice should carry (None = leave CodiceFiscale empty) and `Finding`s, each with the Stripe field to change and the value to `write` (`sure` False when only the customer can say). Italian VAT numbers are valid by their check digit (`italian_vat_check_digit_ok`), fiscal codes by their control character (`fiscal_code_ok`); a fiscal code written `IT` + the VAT number (SDI 00324) is fixed to the digits, one in lower case or with spaces is written clean (00200), a person's code beside an Italian VAT number is VERIFY. `sent_id_fiscale_iva` / `sent_codice_fiscale` are what the app sends today, as written. `mask_tax_id` for logs. `status(reading, vies_registered=...)` gives OK / FIX / VERIFY, where VIES counts only for VAT numbers of other EU countries. `typed_value(field, value)` turns a value a person typed into what Stripe should hold (an `eu_vat` with prefix, shape and Italian check digit; 11 digits are Italian; a fiscal code of 11 digits or 16 characters with its control character, an `IT` before 11 digits dropped) or raises `TypedTaxIdError(issue)`; `can_empty_fiscal_code(reading)` is true only next to a valid VAT number (else SDI 00417). Pure, no I/O. `TaxIdCheckRepository` caches VIES answers in `roi.tax_id_checks`; a non-answer (`valid` None) never replaces a real one.

Stripe payouts (`simpl_core.roi.payouts`, `roi.payout_repository`): `payout_from_stripe` and `payout_items(payout_id, balance_transactions)` turn a payout and its balance transactions (read with `data.source`, `data.source.invoice`, `data.source.charge`, `data.source.charge.invoice` expanded) into `Payout` and `PayoutItem`s (charge, refund, fee charged on its own, a failed payout's money back, other), stored in `roi.stripe_payouts` / `roi.stripe_payout_items`. `payout_breakdown` groups a payout by Stripe invoice (charged, refunded, fees, net; the nets add up to the payout) and `stripe_fees_by_month` gives Stripe's fees per UTC month (processing net of VAT, fees charged on their own, VAT Stripe charged) for the reverse-charge self-invoice. `match_payouts(payouts, movements, matched)` finds each payout's bank movement: a line from Stripe of the same net amount to the cent, booked within `MATCH_WORKING_DAYS` (3) working days of the arrival date, the closest first; a negative payout is money going out. `payout_state` says matched, in transit, failed, `dati_banca_assenti` (no line and the account's data does not cover the window) or `non_abbinato`; `destination_accounts` maps a Stripe destination to its bank. Pure, no I/O.

Tax deadlines (`simpl_core.roi.tax_deadlines`, `roi.tax_deadline_repository`): SimpL S.r.l.'s deadlines (ordinary regime, calendar year) for the years in `YEARS` only; any other year raises, since its dates were not checked against the sources. `tax_deadlines(year, vat_regime=None)` / `tax_deadlines_between(start, end)` list `TaxDeadline`s (stable `key` = rule and period, e.g. `iva_mensile:2026-09`; `action` `versamento` or `invio`; `nominal_on` as the law says and `due_on` after `due_date`: next working day, and 1-20 August to 20 August; `sources` cite the law and the AdE calendar). With no regime both monthly and quarterly VAT are listed, each marked `vat_regime`; with no `isa` both the ordinary 30 June 2026 income-tax date and the ISA one (20 July 2026, DL 63/2026) are listed, marked `isa`. `conditional` marks a deadline due only if something the rules cannot see holds (the stamp duty on e-invoices). Deadlines due from 2027 cite the consolidated codes (testi unici) that replace most of the laws then; the AdE calendar is linked only where it lists the deadline (published up to `ADE_CALENDAR_UNTIL`). An F24 in the bank never makes a deadline done: `is_tax_payment` (an F23/F24 text, `payroll.is_tax_delegation`; or the Spese label `tasse` on a line to the Agenzia delle Entrate / Erario that is no `FEE` and not the account's stamp duty) picks the lines, `payments_near` puts each in every payment deadline's `found_window` it falls in (a week before to three working days after the due date, or after `late_until` for IRES/IRAP and the Chamber of Commerce fee, payable 30 days late with `late_surcharge`), `bank_evidence` says found / nothing / no data / waiting, and `deadline_status` is the one resolver of a deadline's state (`confermata`, `f24_trovato`, `da_fare`, `in_tempo_con_maggiorazione`, `da_confermare`, `dati_banca_assenti`, `scaduta`; one that may not apply, `is_certain` false, is never `scaduta`). People's word is a history (`roi.tax_deadline_confirmations`, rows only added: `fatto`, `non_dovuto`, `annullata`; `current_confirmation` reads the newest): `TaxDeadlineConfirmationRepository.confirm` / `cancel(key, by=)` / `history(keys)`. Requires API migration `20261022140000_roi_tax_deadline_confirmations.sql`.

Supplier e-invoices (`simpl_core.roi.supplier_invoices`, `roi.invoice_payments`, `roi.supplier_invoice_repository`): `parse_fatturapa(document)` reads a FatturaPA document in A-Cube's JSON shape (`fatturapa_from_xml` / `fatturapa_from_file` turn an XML or `.p7m` file into the same dictionary) into `SupplierInvoice`s, one per body, with amounts as owed: a credit note (TD04, TD08) is negative and `payable` leaves out the withholding SimpL pays with the F24. The id (`invoice_id`) is a hash of supplier tax id, type, number and date, so A-Cube and a Cassetto download never duplicate. `is_received_by_simpl` (buyer `IT` + `SIMPL_VAT_NUMBER`) and `is_payable` (not a TD16-TD28 integration or self-invoice) decide what is stored in `roi.supplier_invoices`. `match_payments(invoices, vendor_of, lines, decisions)` is computed on read and stores nothing: a person's word (`roi.supplier_invoice_payments`, `paga` with an amount or `non_paga`) first, then a line citing the invoice number, then the only line for exactly what is due (or the only one within `NEAR_DUE` of the due date), all within `DAYS_BEFORE`/`DAYS_AFTER` of the invoice; a line equal to 2-4 open invoices is only a `Proposal`. A line is the supplier's when its vendor is (`vendor_of_supplier`: `roi.vendor_tax_ids` set by a person, else `guess_vendor` from the name's words) or when its text holds every distinctive word of the supplier's name (an intermediary such as TeamSystem Payments). Matches count as paid only when a person confirmed the vendor (`Payment.counted`). `invoice_states` and `supplier_summaries` give each invoice's paid, open and status and each supplier's invoiced, paid, open, overdue and spend without an invoice; `payment_candidates` lists the lines a person may pick. `VendorRepository.fold_into` carries a vendor's VAT numbers to the vendor it is folded into.

Issued e-invoices (`simpl_core.roi.issued_invoices`, `roi.issued_invoice_repository`): `read_fatturapa(document)` (in `supplier_invoices`, which `parse_fatturapa` builds on) reads a FatturaPA document either way round into `EInvoice`s: seller and buyer `Party` (tax id, fiscal code, name), `simpl_role` (`SimplRole`: `cedente`, `cessionario`, `nessuno`), amounts as invoiced (negative on a credit note) and `related_numbers` (DatiFattureCollegate, the invoice a credit note corrects). `transmitted(invoice, sdi_status=, sdi_notes=, source=, source_ref=, received_at=)` makes the `IssuedInvoice` of one transmission, for a sale or a TD16-TD29 self-invoice only; `fiscal_key` is type|number|date|seller's VAT number. `roi.issued_invoices` keeps one row per transmission, rejected ones included, and a fiscal key once among the rows SDI did not reject (partial unique index). `IssuedInvoiceRepository.record(rows)` reads stored transmissions first, then adds new ones: a row's own `sdi_status` only moves forward (`SdiStatus`: `in_attesa` → `inviata` → `consegnata` / `non_consegnata` / `scartata`, `nel_cassetto` → `consegnata` / `non_consegnata`, `may_move`), a read behind it is ignored and only an accepted → `scartata` read comes back in `RecordOutcome.refused` (`is_refused`). A twin of a kept fiscal key (another transmission or the Cassetto) lends its status as `twin_sdi_status` when further on; `shown_status` is the one to show (delivered wins). `sdi_notes` is None while a rejection's reasons are unread; `noted_refs` lists the rejected ones already read. What counts (D11) is pure: `counts_as_sale` (SimpL seller, not self-billed, not rejected), `counts_as_revenue` (a sale SDI accepted: what roi.revenues books), `counts_for_vat`, `vat_debit`, `vat_credit` (a TD16-TD19 reverse-charge self-invoice owes and deducts its VAT, never revenue or cost; the other self-billed types raise `UnsettledVatRule`, an open question for the accountant). Requires API migration `20261022100000_roi_issued_invoices.sql`.

Cassetto fiscale (`simpl_core.roi.cassetto`, `roi.cassetto_repository`): `destination(invoice)` says where an `EInvoice` goes by SimpL's role: `Direction.ISSUED` (SimpL sells), `Direction.SELF` (a TD16-TD29 self-invoice SimpL transmitted, also when it names SimpL as the buyer), `Direction.RECEIVED` (a supplier invoice SimpL pays), None (SimpL is neither party: refuse the file). `from_cassetto(invoice, source=, source_ref=)` is `transmitted` with `SdiStatus.IN_CASSETTO` (`nel_cassetto`): accepted by SDI (in `ACCEPTED`, so it counts like a delivered one), delivery unknown; a twin with a delivery status beats it (`_RANK`). `is_metadata_file(name)` is the portal's `<file>_metaDato.xml`; `read_metadata(bytes)` gives its `SdiReceipt` (IdentificativoSdI, NomeFile) and `metadata_target(name)` the invoice file it is named after. `admission(sdi_id=, stored=)` decides an uploaded issued/self-billed invoice: `STORE` only with SDI's metadata, else `ALREADY_PRESENT` when a non-rejected transmission holds its fiscal key, `REJECTED` when only rejected ones do, `NO_METADATA` when none (the last two are refused, so a rejected invoice never turns into revenue). `IssuedInvoiceRepository.statuses_by_key(keys)` feeds it. A party named only by CodiceFiscale 03951480122 reads as `IT03951480122` (seller and buyer). `supplier_invoice(einvoice)` is one `SupplierInvoice` (what `parse_fatturapa` maps). `monthly_counts(session, start)` counts each month's non-rejected issued/self-billed invoices and received ones by `Book` (`issued`, `self` = TD16-TD19, `self_unsettled` = TD20-TD23/TD27-TD29, the ones `vat_rule_settled` leaves open, as the Self-invoices tab lists them apart; `book_of_issued`) and `InvoiceSource`; `coverage(counts, start=, end=)` fills every month with zeros. `CassettoStatusRepository.save/current` keeps one `CassettoCheck` per company (`CassettoState`: `non_attivo`, `non_pianificato`, `attivo`, `errore`), keeping the last known `last_download_at`. Requires API migration `20261022110000_roi_cassetto.sql`.

Incoming transfers ↔ issued invoices (`simpl_core.roi.issued_invoice_payments`, `roi.issued_invoice_payment_repository`): computed on read, only people's word stored (`roi.issued_invoice_payments`, `paga` with an amount or `non_paga`, FK to `roi.issued_invoices`; `IssuedInvoicePaymentRepository.decide` / `forget` / `list_all`). `amounts_due(invoices)` is what each counted sale asks: total less withholding, less the credit notes naming it; `open_amounts(invoices, collected_on_stripe)` is nothing for the numbers the caller read as collected on Stripe (the Stripe invoice paid, not paid out of band: a sale paid with the customer's Stripe balance has no charge in any payout, so payouts cannot tell). `match_transfers(invoices, collected_on_stripe, movements, decisions)` returns `invoice_payments.Payment`s: confirmed ones (`counted`, `MatchHow.CONFIRMED`, any amount: several sales per transfer, part of a sale) and proposals (not counted) for money in that is not a Stripe payout (`is_transfer_in`; pass the full amount, before the bank fee), within `transfer_window` (`DAYS_BEFORE` 5 working days before the invoice date to `DAYS_AFTER` 90 working days after its due date, `roi.business_days`): a reference citing the invoice number first (`refers_to`: a number shorter than `SHORT_NUMBER` only with the customer's name too), then the only open amount among the sales of the customer the transfer names, with no other transfer of the customer for that amount, never for a transfer whose reference cites any sale. A rejected pair is never proposed again. `transfer_candidates` lists the open sales a person may link a transfer to (the customer's first, no window, `CANDIDATES_SHOWN`); `check_link` raises `LinkRefused` when the amount is more than the transfer has left or the sale has open. `transfer_covers(invoices, decisions)` gives each sale's confirmed `TransferCover` (`paid`, `due`, `full`). Requires API migration `20261023120000_roi_issued_invoice_payments.sql`.

Self-invoices (`simpl_core.roi.self_invoices`, `roi.self_invoice_xml`, `roi.self_invoice_repository`): `classify(line, facts, italian_invoice_vendors=...)` says whether a bank line (`financials.ledger`, USD included) needs a TD17 (services from abroad), TD18 (goods from the EU) or TD19 (goods already in Italy) self-invoice, with the Guida AdE v1.9 section cited in each rule. The facts about a supplier (`SupplierFacts`: country, what it sells, foreign tax id, address) come from a person, once per vendor (`roi.self_invoice_suppliers`); lines that are no purchase (Revolut exchange/loan/tax/top-up types, spend natura giroconto, stipendi, tasse-contributi) and money in from an unclassified vendor need none; without facts a screened line (`SCREENED_MCCS`, `SCREENED_NATURES` or a foreign currency) is `to_classify` even when the vendor has an Italian SDI invoice (which clears only its unscreened euro lines), and so is a foreign supplier with an Italian VAT number or one already charging Italian VAT. A refund is a credit note (same type, negative amounts). `to_euro` divides by an ECB-style rate the caller picks (which day's rate is an open question for the commercialista), `amounts` and `missing_fields` work on a draft's `DraftFields` (VAT only 4, 5, 10 or 22: `VAT_RATES`; `tax_code` drops spaces and the country prefix from IdCodice, at most 28 characters, never cut). Drafts live in `roi.self_invoices` (`bozza` -> `approvata`, either -> `annullata`; `require_transition`), one open per bank line, one number per year (`NumberTaken`); `move(..., updated_at=)` refuses an approval of a version edited since. `self_invoice_xml.render` builds the FatturaPA 1.2.3 file (FPR12, SimpL as CessionarioCommittente) and `validate` checks it against the official XSD copied into `roi/fatturapa_xsd/` (lxml from the `search` extra, imported inside the function; no download). Nothing here transmits to SDI. `fold_into` also moves a vendor's self-invoice facts. Any Stripe bank line (a payout matched in `roi.stripe_payouts`, the `STRIPE_VENDOR` vendor, or text naming Stripe, `payouts.mentions_stripe`) is no purchase; Stripe's fees are one purchase per month instead (`classify_stripe_fees(MonthFees, facts)`: TD17, but to classify until a person picks the rate on Stripe's facts, `SupplierFacts.vat_rate` / `vat_nature`, 22% or exempt 0% with Natura `N4` (`EXEMPT`, `EXEMPT_NATURE`, `vat_choice_error`; the XML adds Natura and RiferimentoNormativo); a month with VAT on its fees is to classify too; `stripe_fees_taxable`, `stripe_fees_key` / `stripe_fees_month` (ValueError on a malformed key) give the draft id `stripe-fees:2026-09`, `stripe_fees_open_on` the 10th of the next month, `SelfInvoiceSource.STRIPE_FEES`, no ledger id). Duplicates: `transmitted_documents(issued, copies)` merges `IssuedInvoiceRepository.self_billed()` with `SupplierInvoiceRepository.self_billed_copies()` (SimpL's self-invoices delivered back to it; neither import stores them today) by fiscal key. `transmitted_twins(supplier, original_number=, month=, taxable_eur=, transmitted=, own=)` keeps the not-rejected ones of the same supplier (`same_supplier`: tax id with or without prefix, or the plain name): one whose DatiFattureCollegate carry `original_number` is `TwinMatch.DOCUMENT` whenever dated, one linking other invoices is no twin; else within `purchase_window(month)` (the purchase month and the next) `AMOUNT` when it links nothing and the taxable equals, otherwise `PERIOD`. DOCUMENT and AMOUNT block (`twin_reason`), PERIOD only warns (`twin_warning`). `own_transmission(supplier, number=, document_date=, transmitted=)` is the draft itself sent (same number, year and supplier), `number_taken(number, year, transmitted)` a number SDI already has that year. `SelfInvoiceRepository.open_between(start, end)` lists drafts and approved ones by document date, else bank day. Requires API migrations `20261022150000_roi_self_invoices.sql` and `20261023130000_roi_self_invoices_stripe_fees.sql`.

Proposed bank lines (`simpl_core.roi.bank_line_proposals`, `roi.bank_line_proposal_repository`, model `BankLineProposal` in `financials.bank_line_proposals`): movements on an account no feed reads (`financials.accounts.fed_by = 'proposals'`, Banca Sella, `SELLA_BANK`), proposed by an agent (`by="agente:<agent>"`) or the transfer rule (`TRANSFER_RULE`), approved or rejected once by a person (`proposta` -> `approvata` | `rifiutata`; `ProposalAlreadyDecided`, `ProposalNotFound`). `ProposedLine` (kind `f24` | `giroconto` | `altro`, direction, cents, `evidence`, optional `deadline_key` for an F24, `Source`s) is checked by `require_valid_line`. `BankLineProposalRepository.propose(line, by=)` returns `(row, created)` and answers the proposal already standing for the same movement (account, direction, kind, amount, within `TRANSFER_MATCH_DAYS`, not rejected); `approve(id, by=, booked_on=, amount=, note=)` writes in the caller's transaction a `financials.ledger` row (`source_transaction_id = movement_id(id)`, uuid5, never the other leg's id), an outflow's `roi.transactions` line with that uuid as id (F24 counterpart `F24_COUNTERPART`, so people cost, never a supplier), and for an F24 with `deadline_key` a `fatto` confirmation with the total approved for that key; `reject` writes nothing else. `propose_missing_transfers(account)` proposes the inflow of every Revolut line `REVOLUT_TO_SIMPL` since the account opened that no proposal (any state) stands for (`transfers_to_propose`, pure). `balance(account_id)` is the account's ledger sum. Requires API migration `20261029200000_banca_sella_proposed_lines.sql`.

Banca Sella statements (`simpl_core.roi.sella_statement`, `roi.bank_statement_line_repository`, model `BankStatementLine` in `financials.bank_statement_lines`): `read_sella_statement(raw: bytes)` reads the home banking's "Lista movimenti" CSV (UTF-8 or cp1252; columns `COLUMNS`, Italian amounts, newest first, last line "Saldo al gg/mm/aaaa") into a `SellaStatement` (lines oldest first, `balance`, `balance_on`) or raises `StatementUnreadable` with the reason in Italian. `SellaLine.key` is the operation code + sign + amount (a debit and its fee share the code), stable across exports. `movements_of(lines)` makes `StatementMovement`s: an F24 delegation's rows (same day, same text) become one movement of their sum, a "Giroconti" row a transfer, a fee counterpart `SELLA_FEES`, any other outflow its own description. `standing_for(movements, standing)` (pure, `bank_line_proposals`) finds the proposal already standing for each movement (kind, direction, amount, operation or value day within `TRANSFER_MATCH_DAYS`, nearest first, one each); `BankLineProposalRepository.standing_for_statement(account_id)` lists the open or approved proposals no statement row stands for yet, `record(line, by=)` adds a proposal with no lookalike check (three equal fees are three movements) under `lock_account(account_id)`. `BankStatementLineRepository.known_keys(account_id, keys)` / `add(account_id, lines, proposal_id=, import_id=)` keep each statement row loaded once. Requires API migration `20261103120000_banca_sella_statements.sql`.

VAT settlement estimate (`simpl_core.roi.vat`, `roi.vat_setting_repository`): pure rules for the Accounting → VAT page, a cash estimate, never the official settlement. `VatPeriod` is a month (`2026-10`) or a quarter (`2026-Q4`), `parse_period`, `period_of(day, regime)`, `periods_until(day, regime)`. `position(period, issued=, purchases=, deductible=)` sums the VAT owed and deducted with D11 (`counts_for_vat`, `vat_debit`, `vat_credit`; a reverse-charge self-invoice owes and deducts the same VAT, a self-billed type with an open rule lands in `unsettled`, drafts never count) and returns its `VatLine`s. A received invoice (`VatPurchase`, read by `SupplierInvoiceRepository.vat_purchases(start, end)` with its Italian receipt day) is deducted in the period it was received, or the period before when received by the 15th of the next month (monthly, DPR 100/1998 art. 1) or of the second month (quarterly, AdE forum 2019, unconfirmed), never across a year end (`deduction_period`); one with no receipt day is never placed (`undated`); a received self-billed copy is not a purchase. `deductible` is the percent per supplier tax id (100 unless set). `position(..., issued_since=, received_since=)` also says how much of the period the invoices on record cover (`Coverage` `nessuna` / `parziale` / `completa`; `debit_coverage` on the period's days, `credit_coverage` up to `deduction_window_end`, the 15th rule): missing invoices are never zero. `settle(positions, opening_credit=, advance=)` (None = nobody typed it) carries a credit period to period, adds 1% to a quarterly amount due and takes the December advance off December or Q4; each `Settlement` lists what it was computed without (`missing`, carried through the year) and its `bound`: `esatto`, `tetto` (a credit, the opening credit or the advance missing: the most that could be due) or `nd` (sales missing). `historical_advance` / `forecast_advance` estimate the December advance (88%, L. 405/1990 art. 6 c. 2) with a bound, no number when the base misses sales. `tax_code` (6001-6012, 6031-6033, Q4 6099) and `vat_deadline` map a period to its `roi.tax_deadlines` deadline. `forecast(position, today=, renewals=, other_sales_debit_per_day=, credit_per_day=)` adds the Stripe renewals scheduled to the period end (`scheduled_sales`, each `Renewal` repeated at its interval) and the daily trend over `trend_window(today)` (the 3 full months before); `credit` is None without a credit trend. Settings people type are a history in `roi.vat_settings` (`SettingKind`: `regime`, `credito_iniziale`, `detraibilita`, `liquidato`, `acconto`; rows only added, a row without value takes one back): `VatSettingRepository.current()` / `set(...)` / `clear(kind, key, by=)`. Requires API migration `20261023100000_roi_vat_settings.sql`.

Accounting reports (`simpl_core.roi.pnl`, F8): pure, no I/O. Two readings of the same months. Accrual: revenue is the sales that count (`Sale`, D11 `counts_as_revenue`); the `recurring` share of a sale with a Stripe service period longer than `SPREAD_FROM_DAYS` is spread by days over it (`service_months`, the last month takes the rounding), one-off fees stay in the invoice month, and a credit note carries what was left of its invoice's period. Costs are the ROI's (`accrual_costs`: the payslips of `roi.people_costs` plus each `BankLine.spend`, the line as the ROI timeline spreads it), except that the part of a line paying a received invoice counts the invoice's net (VAT out) over the same months, and an invoice's unpaid net counts on its date. Cash: `Collected` rows (simpl_api `roi_collected`) and the Spese lines in the month they left (`cash_costs`: the `payroll` part is people, an F24 taxes via `payroll_category`; `not_cost` stands apart; the rest by natura). `NATURA_CATEGORY` is the one map from a Spese natura to a `Category`: the five `OPERATING` ones (personale, servizi, affitti_utenze, materie_consumi, altri_costi; unknown natura or none = altri_costi) above EBITDA, and `imposte_tasse` (tasse-contributi) below it. `revenue_bridge` and `cost_bridge` split accrual minus cash over a period into named parts (`RevenuePart`, `CostPart`) that add up to it exactly; `AGAINST_ROI` marks the parts that set accrual apart from the ROI (received invoices' VAT, unpaid invoices). A self-invoice never adds a cost (the line it documents is the cost, `Documented`), and an invoice paid by a PAYROLL line is in people cost already (`in_people_cost`). `tfr_by_person` is gross / 13.5 for employees only (not contractors, not Gestione Separata); `withholding_due` groups professionals' withholding by payment month with its F6 deadline.

ROI vendor catalog (`simpl_core.roi.vendors`): `vendor_key` cleans a bank description into the merchant name. It drops booking and order codes (`Airbnb * Hm22p4qmkw`, `Google Cloud Lrw2jl`, `Ryanair224tz5g3g`), billing months and legal suffixes, and keeps what follows the star for payment processors (`Paypal *deepseek`, `Sumup *trattoria`). `roi.vendor_aliases` folds the other names a vendor shows up under (`canonical_vendor`). `roi.vendor_assignments` says where a vendor's spend goes from `valid_from` on, with `mode`:

- `line`: one initiative.
- `people`: split over those people's survey hours, so a seat follows whoever uses it.
- `trip`: decided per trip.
- `payer`: each charge follows the survey hours of the card holder who paid it (`payer_person` maps "Nicolo' Fugallo" to the roster name "Nicolò").
- `payroll`: people cost.
- `excluded`: not a cost.
- `split`: shared by several lines or projects. `shares` holds `(initiative_id, percent)` pairs adding up to 100 (any number of lines, `require_valid_shares`); `split_weights` turns them into ledger weights rounded to 4 decimals that add up to exactly 1 (the largest share takes the rounding), so the line's euro is spent once. `roi.vendor_assignments.shares` (jsonb, SQL NULL for other modes) stores them. It spreads over months like `line` and can be a line-by-line answer.

A change of use is a new assignment, never an edit of the past. A quiz answer is valid from `FROM_THE_START`. `roi.vendors.proposed_*` is the answer the quiz offers already selected, never a decision. `assignment_on(assignments, vendor, day)` picks the one in force. `require_valid_assignment` is the same rule the table's checks enforce. `roi.vendors.area` and `reviewer` only decide who is asked about a vendor. `first_triage(mccs)` guesses a new vendor's area from its merchant codes: all travel or meals means `travel` with the Team e retreat proposal, all software means `tech`, anything else `admin`. `VendorRepository.fold_into(key, into)` makes a duplicate an alias; its own aliases follow it. `roi.transactions` also keeps the Revolut `kind` and the card `payer`.

Vendor decisions (`simpl_core.roi.vendor_decisions`): the dashboard, the quiz and the finance agent decide vendors through one module, so the same checks hold for all of them (vendor known, destinations live per `is_live_destination`, people on the roster, `require_valid_assignment`). `decide_vendor(session, assignment, decided_by=, note=)` is a person's decision. It also reviews any open agent act on that vendor: `confermata` when it agrees, `cambiata` when it does not. `agent_decide_vendor(session, assignment, agent=, reason=)` decides only a vendor with no assignment at all. It writes `decided_by="agente:<role>"` and the reason as the note, and records a `roi.agent_actions` row in `da_rivedere`. `merge_vendor` / `agent_merge_vendor` fold an undecided duplicate the same way; the agent's act keeps the duplicate's row and aliases, so `undo_agent_merge(session, id, by=)` brings the vendor back undecided and marks the act `cambiata`. `simpl_core.roi.vendor_lines` (`lines_by_vendor`, `vendors_all_in_trips`) is the one grouping of bank lines by vendor that simpl_api's vendor pages and the finance agent share. `confirm_agent_action(session, id, by=)` confirms an act; a reviewed act cannot be reviewed again (`agent_actions.require_review`). Nothing here writes the ledger: simpl_api's classification turns decisions into attributions.

Spend tags (`simpl_core.roi.spend_tags`, `spend_tagging`, `spend_tag_repository`) never move a euro in the ROI. People define `roi.tag_groups`. A group is `exclusive` when a line carries at most one of its tags, so its totals add up to the spend once; a non-exclusive group is for search only. `roi.tags` belong to a group and are `attivo`, `archiviato` or `unito` (`merged_into`); `is_spend` false marks movements that are not spend (transfers to our own accounts), which spend reports leave out. A group holds at most `MAX_TAGS_PER_GROUP` active tags. `create_tag` refuses a key or a name that already exists in the group. `roi.transaction_tags` keeps one row per (line, tag), `attivo` or `rimosso`, with `source` `agente` or `manuale`, the reason and who decided. A partial unique index on `(transaction_id, exclusive_group)` over active rows enforces one tag per exclusive group. `tag_line` / `untag_line` lock the line and apply `plan_tag` / `plan_untag`. A person always has the last word: the agent never removes a person's tag, never puts back a tag a person removed, and never overrides a person's choice in an exclusive group. A refusal comes back as `TagOutcome.refused`, not as an error. `merge_tags` moves a tag's lines into another tag of the same group. `untagged_line_ids(session, group)` is the agent's queue.

Vendor labels and Natura (same modules): a label of a non-exclusive group can sit on a vendor (`roi.vendor_tags`, one row per (vendor, tag), `attivo`/`rimosso`, `agente`/`manuale`, same rules), and so can one Natura (`spend_tags.VENDOR_EXCLUSIVE_GROUPS`), chosen by a person only. Every line of the vendor (`vendor_lines.vendor_of`) carries a label unless the line decided that label itself, on or off; it carries the vendor's Natura over the agent's guess on the line, unless a person chose the line's own Natura or took that very one off: `spend_tags.inherited_keys` and `carried_keys`. `tags_of_lines(session, lines)` answers each line's vendor key and `CarriedTag`s (`inherited` true for the vendor's), the one resolver the Spese API, the Accounting reports and `untagged_line_ids` read. `tag_vendor` refuses the other exclusive groups (Tema, Utilizzo, Evento, Ricorrenza are decided line by line) and a Natura from the agent; a new Natura on a vendor takes its old one off; `untag_line` on an inherited label or Natura leaves the line an exception (`rimosso`); taking the vendor's Natura off a line also takes off the agent's guess it hid, so the line has none. A person putting back the agent's tag makes it theirs (`plan_tag`). A label never takes lines out of the spend (`require_spend_label`). `merge_tags` of labels or Natura moves vendors' tags and lines' exceptions; `VendorRepository.fold_into` moves a merged vendor's labels unless the target decided them, and its Natura unless the target has one. `roi.vendors.brand` (`set_vendor_brand`, `require_brand`) is the name the Spese page groups sibling vendors under (Anthropic API and Claude.ai are "Claude"); neither labels nor brand move a euro in the ROI. Requires API migration `20261020190000_spend_labels.sql`.

Who says a vendor is paid once (`simpl_core.roi.one_off`): only `payment_frequency = one_time` on the vendor's assignment takes its lines out of the burn. The agent's Ricorrenza tag `una-tantum` (`ONE_OFF_TAG`) only proposes it: `proposes_one_time(assignment, one_off)` is true when every outflow of the vendor carries it and nobody said how the vendor is paid (no assignment, or a `SPREAD_MODES` one with no frequency and `covers_months` 1). `one_time_proposals_of(lines, carried, assignments, today=)` (pure, over `tags_of_lines`) and `one_time_proposals(session, today=)` answer the vendor keys; the quiz and the ROI's Fornitori tab show them for a person to confirm. The other recurrence tags describe the spend and move nothing.

Confirming and saved questions (`spend_tagging`, `spend_questions`, `spend_question_repository`): `confirm_line_tags(session, line_ids, keys=None|[...], decided_by=)` and `confirm_vendor_tags(session, vendor_keys, keys=, decided_by=)` make the agents' active tags a person's in one statement, under the same per-line and per-vendor advisory locks as the tagging commands, and answer the (owner, tag) pairs confirmed; Utilizzo (`perimetro`) is never confirmed there. `roi.spend_questions` holds the questions saved at the top of the Spese page: a label used once (case-insensitive) and the page's filters as a URL query, of `QUESTION_PARAMS` only (`question_query`); `save_question` / `delete_question`. Requires API migration `20261021120000_spend_questions.sql`.

Product reconciliation (`simpl_core.roi.spend_scope.product_scope`): pass active tag keys mapped to group keys, `not_spend`, MCC and movement kind. Only explicit `perimetro` product API, compute or platform subscription tags yield `eligible` with a tracker kind. Missing/mixed use or contradictory company nature/MCC yields `review`; company and non-spend yield `excluded`. This never writes tracker costs or ROI allocations. The perimeter's six keys cannot be created, renamed, archived or merged via tagging commands. `tema_spesa` is the extensible exclusive operational-theme catalog; legacy `tema` is historical and cannot accept new tags. Requires API migration `20261014160000_expense_themes_and_product_scope.sql`; migration does not infer existing lines' use.

Personal spend on the company card (`simpl_core.roi.reimbursements`): `suspicion(counterpart=, mcc=, kind=, excluded=)` says why a line looks personal (clothes, health, beauty, streaming and games, jewellery, betting, pets, toys by MCC or shop name; or a card payment already sent to `NON-SPESA`), or None. A doctor is recognised by MCC only, because an accountant is "Dott." too. `roi.reimbursements` holds the owner's review of a line, `ReimbursementStatus`: `da_rimborsare` (personal, `person` owes it), `rimborsato` (with `repaid_on`) or `aziendale` (company spend after all, no person). `ReimbursementRepository.set_status` locks the row and enforces `require_transition` (a repaid line goes back to owed before it can become company spend) and `require_fields`. The ledger stays the single source of where the euro goes: a personal line's attribution is the transit row `REIMBURSE_ID` (`RIMBORSI`), out of the ROI like `NON-SPESA`, written only by the app's reimbursement command.

Prefer importing the specific service, repository, or model you need. Keep app-layer orchestration in the app repo and reusable domain behavior in `simpl_core`.


ROI product identity: pass the transaction's `mcc` to `canonical_vendor(..., mcc=...)`
in every reader and writer. ChatGPT descriptors keep their product name before
card references are stripped; generic OpenAI is API spend. Apple MCC 5732 means
hardware, 5817/5818 or an explicit `/bill` descriptor means digital services;
Apple without evidence stays separate for review. Never infer the product from
the amount. `first_triage(..., vendor=key)` supplies the matching proposal.
Category `asset` contains Hardware (Comuni / Assegnati) and Domini. Asset rows
receive spend and stay out of the time survey and ROI ranking; the existing
`covers_months` setting controls spreading, with no new automatic lifetime.

## Rules and conventions

- Put reusable platform business logic in `simpl_core`, not in `simpl_api` or `simpl_flow` copies.
- Survey weeks: `roi.time_weeks.pct` is the normalised split (a week adds up to 100) and is what the metrics read; `hours` is what the person typed, kept beside it (null on weeks saved as percentages). `TimeWeekRepository.replace_week(person, week, allocations, hours=...)` writes both; merges and splits (`remap_person` / `split_person`) move a week's typed hours with the shares, and leave them null when the week had none. `filled_weeks_by_person()` reads every person's filled weeks in one query; `weeks_of(person)` returns one person's rows, oldest first. `roi.people.email` links a roster row to its dashboard login; `PersonRepository.by_email(email)` finds it in any letter case and never returns a `_`-prefixed row.
- `SurveyTokenRepository.ensure(person)` mints a personal survey token and never rotates an existing one; `ensure_many(persons)` does the same for a list in one insert that ignores conflicts, so two callers racing on the same person cannot fail on the primary key. Both refuse the synthetic `_`-prefixed roster rows.
- `InitiativeRepository.next_id()` holds `pg_advisory_xact_lock` on the table: id minting is a `max()+1` read-then-write and the survey proposes from a public token endpoint.
- Keep database access behind repositories or services. Do not scatter raw SQL across consumers.
- Use `simpl_orm` for shared database patterns and `simpl_tracker` for tracking/logging integrations.
- Treat exported services, repositories, models, and schemas as shared API. Update this skill when changing their public behavior.
- A seller's active hours are `outreach_prefs.active_hours` = `{"start": "HH:MM", "stop": "HH:MM", "timezone": "<IANA name>"}`: local wall clock, start inclusive, stop exclusive, start before stop, and a missing field takes 07:00 / 19:00 / UTC. `simpl_core.utils.parse_active_hours(prefs)` returns the configured `ActiveHours` or `None` for unset or invalid, and the caller decides what `None` means. `BusinessHoursScheduler.for_outreach_prefs(prefs)` schedules inside that window (Monday to Friday) and falls back to the default 09:00-18:00 Europe/Rome. `TriggerSchedulerService` builds it per trigger user, and the Sales Domain wake gate holds wakes outside a configured window. A host that still calls `BusinessHoursScheduler()` for a seller's action ignores their hours: pass the seller's prefs instead. The rules copy `sales.resolve_active_hours` in simpl_api, which gates the extension task queue and the per-user email claim (fallback 07:00-19:00 UTC).
- A person's call hours are `outreach_prefs.call_availability` = `{"timezone": "<IANA name>", "weekly": {"monday": [{"start": "HH:MM", "stop": "HH:MM"}, ...], ...}, "dates": {"YYYY-MM-DD": [ranges, or [] for no calls]}}`, set in the app's Settings (ENG-1158). `simpl_core.utils.call_availability.parse_call_availability(prefs)` returns a `CallAvailability` (`ranges_on(day)`: the date's own ranges, else its weekday's; `days_with_calls(first, last)`) or `None` for unset or invalid; `call_availability_problem(raw)` says in words why a value is refused (simpl_api returns it as the 422). SimpL's booking page and the outreach reply's free times read it; unset, each keeps its earlier hours (`CallAvailability.weekdays(start, stop, zone)` from `active_hours`, else 09:00-18:00). It is not the LinkedIn sending window: `active_hours` stays about when the extension acts.

## Agent framework

`simpl_core.agent_framework` is a database-driven runtime for gating and routing AI
producer output. Pipelines, agents, validators, routes, and prompts are versioned
rows, not code. It needs the `agent-framework` extra.

Open a run against a published config version, then walk its pipeline:

```python
from simpl_core.agent_framework import resolve_engine_entry

entry = await resolve_engine_entry(
    db,
    config_key="my_root",          # required; the framework has no default key
    config_version=None,           # None selects the active version
    llm_client_resolver=resolver,
)
pipeline = await entry.loader.get_pipeline("my_pipeline")
# stamp entry.config_version_id onto the run audit
```

`resolve_engine_entry` raises `LookupError` when the config key has no ledger
version and `ConfigValidationError` when the resolved version is broken with no
good fallback. It never falls back to reading whatever component rows happen to be
active.

Nested `pipeline_id` references are a closed-document invariant. Missing
references, cycles, excess depth, and mismatched `subgraph.version` pins across
`subgraph` and fan-out pipeline branches fail `af_config_migrate`, projection,
and loader boot/load before a walk starts.

Root and nested walks use one immutable `PipelineRunInputs` envelope. Its
`domain` payload must be deeply immutable; static children share it without
copying, and mapped children freeze DB-shaped mappings and lists into an
immutable local `item` plus
`parent_items` ancestry. Consumers extend `PipelineRunInputs` for their typed,
data-only fields; clients, sessions, and services stay on coordinator factories.
Admit the envelope at the root with `PipelineRunInputs.root(domain)`. Child
failures remain branch-local: fan-out and joins report `completed`, `partial`,
`failed`, or `empty` aggregate state without terminating sibling work. Pipeline
fan-outs tolerate `partial` and `empty` aggregates; agent fan-outs pass only
when every branch passes. Public consumer-envelope fields are exposed to
`domain_inputs` resolvers and may not shadow a domain key.

**A deterministic agent takes its settings from the policy.** A `deterministic`
row with an `implementation_class` may carry `params`; `load_producer` passes them
to the class as keyword arguments, so a policy can tune a threshold or a budget
without a release. The class owns the names and their defaults and must accept a
walk with none; a name it does not take fails the walk at load (`TypeError`), and
`params` on any other row is refused at parse. Rows without `params` hash as
before.

**A `data_producer` reads a table without code.** Its `read` names a `table`, the
`columns` to take and `where` clauses whose values are literals or resolved from the
run (`domain_inputs`, `producer_output`, `verdicts`); `cardinality` is `one` or `many`.
The table is one of the platform database's by default. With `source: row_store` it
is a table of the row store instead, named as the store names it
(`{dataset}__v{version}`), and the read returns its live rows:

```yaml
- kind: data_producer
  id: post_text
  read:
    source: row_store
    table: sales_posts__v2
    columns: [text, language]
    where:
      - column: key
        op: eq
        value: {resolver: domain_inputs, path: source_key}
  output_schema:
    text: {type: str, column: text}
    language: {type: "str | None", column: language}
```

A host that runs such a read needs the `row-store` extra and `ROW_STORE_URI`. The
live validation checks the columns and their declared types against the table in the
store, as it checks a database table.

A dataset version spread over shards (`row_store.dataset_configs.shards` above 1) has no
`{dataset}__v{version}` table: its rows are in `{dataset}__s{k:02d}__v{version}`, one
table a shard. A `data_producer` cannot read it yet. Code reads it with
`ShardedTable(config, dataset, version, shards)` and writes it with
`ShardedDataset(store, spec, shards)`.

**A `data_consumer` writes to the database, or to a Parquet dataset.** With
`source: parquet`, `table` names a Parquet dataset the host registered
(`parquet_dataset`, below) and each write is one part file of the walk's rows, or, inside
`defer_parquet_writes()`, rows staged for one part per dataset at the host's flush
(below). Every merge lists every part, so a host whose walks hold a few rows each stages
them. The operation is `insert_ignore`: the dataset's merge keeps a key's first row, so
`conflict_on`, `update_where` and `fill_null_columns` are refused. Map every column
explicitly; values must already have their column's type (a datetime with a time zone
for a timestamp column, not ISO text):

```yaml
- kind: data_consumer
  id: labels_sink
  write:
    source: parquet
    table: opus_llmgraph_v1
    operation: insert_ignore
    for_each: {resolver: producer_output, path: rows}
    values:
      label_id: {item: label_id}
      text: {item: text}
      nodes: {item: nodes}
```

The host passes its datasets to the executor, and the executor to the coordinator:
`NodeCoordinator(..., data_executor=GenericDataExecutor(db, parquet_sinks={name:
dataset}))` (without `data_executor` a coordinator writes to its database only). Such a
write never opens a session on `db`. It raises `LookupError` when the host has no sink of
that name, `ValueError` inside `defer_data_writes` (unless `defer_parquet_writes` is open
too), `ParquetRowError` for a row the schema refuses, and `OSError` (`TimeoutError`
included) when storage refuses the part.
The loader's boot validation checks writes against the database only and skips parquet
writes: run `executor.parquet_write_problems(owner, spec)` on the policy's parquet writes
before the first walk, so a value naming no column, or a non-null column with no value,
stops the host before it pays for work whose rows it cannot write. The node passes its
input through, as every sink does.

One part per claim instead of one per walk: open `defer_parquet_writes()` around the
claim's walks, then `await batch.flush()` before settling the claim's keys as stored.

```python
from simpl_core.agent_framework.data_io import defer_parquet_writes

with defer_parquet_writes() as staged:   # one claim
    ...                                  # its walks: each parquet write is staged
parts = await staged.flush()             # one part per dataset; then settle stored
```

A staged write is checked against the schema at once (`ParquetRowError` in the walk that
staged it) and copied. `flush` writes each dataset's rows as one part, a key staged twice
kept once (its lowest `order` value, as a merge keeps it), and returns the `PartRef`s; on
an `OSError` or a cancellation the rows not written stay staged. Flush after the claim's
walks finish: rows a walk stages during a flush are kept, but may wait for the next
flush. Rows never flushed are never written. The
scope holds at most `max_rows` (10,000) rows (`ValueError` past it); nested scopes are
refused; `post_write_class` is refused on a staged write.

An `llm_agent`'s `model` is a nested policy — a primary target plus ordered
fallbacks — resolved per agent through an `LLMClientResolver` built from
host-supplied credentials:

```python
from simpl_core.agent_framework.llm_client_resolver import (
    DefinitionLLMClientResolver,
    provider_bindings_from_env_keys,
)

resolver = DefinitionLLMClientResolver(
    provider_bindings_from_env_keys(openai_api_key=os.environ["OPENAI_API_KEY"])
)
```

Each target names the API its model speaks: `api: chat` (the default) for a
language model, `api: decisions` for a decision model on `openrouter` -- any model
OpenRouter serves on its decisions API, such as
`perplexity/pplx-decider-v1.1-27b-20261006`, keyed by the same `openrouter`
binding as chat. A chain may mix the two; one name may appear once per API.

```yaml
model:
  name: perplexity/pplx-decider-v1.1-27b-20261006
  provider: openrouter
  api: decisions
  provider_routing: {zdr: true}
  fallbacks:
    - name: z-ai/glm-5.3-flash
      provider: openrouter
```

An `llm_agent` call sends `temperature: 0` unless its policy says otherwise:
`model.temperature` (on the primary; it applies to every target of the chain) is a
number from 0 to 2, or `omit` to send none, for a model that refuses one. No Claude
Opus 5.5 endpoint on OpenRouter takes a temperature, so with `require_parameters`
OpenRouter finds no endpoint unless the policy says `omit`. It is a word and not
`null` because a stored spec drops null fields.

```yaml
model:
  name: anthropic/claude-opus-5.5
  provider: openrouter
  provider_routing: {zdr: true, data_collection: deny, require_parameters: true}
  temperature: omit
```

**Each target has its own attempt deadline, and may have an output cap.**
`attempt_timeout_seconds` is the wall-clock deadline of each attempt on a target (a
request, or a stream's open); unset, it is the policy's (the primary's), else the agent's
`timeout_seconds`. `max_output_tokens` caps what each request to the target may generate,
reasoning included, and is sent as `max_tokens` on that target's requests only (the
smaller of it and a call's own `max_tokens`); unset, it is the policy's, else no cap is
sent. An answer cut off at its cap (`finish_reason: length`) is a failed attempt: it
spends the target's budget and the chain moves on; when the last model's answer is cut
off, the call fails with simpl_ia's `OutputTruncated` (an `UnexpectedModelBehavior`). A
target's failed attempts may take at most two of its deadlines in a call (its time share;
one deadline when its budget is a single attempt), and the call is bounded as a whole by
the sum of its targets' shares, plus 10%.
Both are greater than 0; an `lm_model` row's targets refuse them.

```yaml
model:
  name: deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash
  provider: openrouter
  attempt_timeout_seconds: 90    # this target's, and each fallback's that sets none
  max_output_tokens: 16000       # the same
  fallbacks:
    - name: deepseek-v4-pro
      provider: deepseek
      attempt_timeout_seconds: 240
```

**Internal routing picks the OpenRouter upstream.** A target with `routing: internal`
(OpenRouter chat or decision targets only) is sent one pinned upstream at a time, chosen by simpl_ia
from the routing table: every endpoint of the model that passes the target's own
`provider_routing` filters (`zdr`, `quantizations`, `only`, `ignore`), ranked by the
estimated cost of one of the agent's calls (the endpoint's price × the agent's token mix,
3 input tokens per output token when it has none; an open `order` keeps its upstreams
first, ranked among themselves). Every failed attempt on a model, on any of its upstreams
and across the whole run, spends its budget (`max_invalid_responses`, else the policy's, else `retries + 1`, 4 by default); an
upstream that failed is not tried again in the run. An upstream that keeps timing out is
skipped on its own while the others keep serving the model. `routing` unset (or `gateway`) sends
`provider_routing` as written. `timeout_skip` sets, per target, the process's own skip
(`threshold`, `window_seconds`, `cooldown_seconds`); each unset field takes the primary's,
then the host's `AI_TIMEOUT_SKIP_*` variables, then simpl_ia's defaults (5 timeouts in
300 s, a 300 s wait; `threshold: 0` is off). `health` sets, per target, the values the
fleet's health state machine judges its offerings by (simpl_ia's `HealthConfig`:
`window_seconds`, `min_attempts`, `degrade_rate`, `open_rate`, `recover_rate`,
`first_cooldown_seconds`, `max_cooldown_seconds`, `probe_answers`, `fleet_guard_share`,
`misfit_min_offerings`, `misfit_min_other_attempts`, `capability_cooldown_seconds`,
`enabled`); each unset field takes
the primary's, then simpl_ia's defaults (below). Both are passed through as written.
`ranking` (`price`, the default, or `effective`), `objective`, `constraints`,
`exploration_share` and `adaptive_deadline` set how the target orders its upstreams by
what they measured (below); each unset value, or unset field of a nested one, takes the
primary's, then simpl_ia's default. The nested ones are simpl_ia's `ObjectiveConfig`,
`ConstraintsConfig` and `AdaptiveDeadlineConfig`, passed through as written and dumped
with only the fields written, so a stored fallback that sets one field keeps inheriting
the others. `certification_required: true` keeps a target on internal routing off every
offering not validated (certified or observed) for a capability the call needs, unless no
offering is (unset takes the primary's, else false). `output_mode` (`native`, `tool` or
`prompted`; unset is simpl_ia's `auto`) pins how a target returns its typed answer, refused
by simpl_ia when the target cannot serve it; an `lm_model` target refuses it.
`ttft_deadline_seconds` (greater than 0) bounds how long each of a target's streams may go
without a first chunk: past it the attempt ends as a timeout (`FirstTokenTimeout`) and the
chain moves on; unset takes the primary's, else twice the offering's p95 time to first chunk
for the agent once measured, else none. `max_reasoning_tokens` (greater than 0) aborts a
stream that reasons past it before its answer starts, as a rejected, truncated attempt
(`OutputTruncated` on the last model); unset takes the primary's, else none. Either one
makes the target's requests streams, so they are watched. `hedge: true` (unset takes the
primary's, else off) lets a call with no tools send a second request to the target's next
upstream when the first has no first chunk by its offering's p95 time to first chunk, only on
offerings marked as stopping billing on cancel (below); both attempts are recorded (the
loser `cancelled`, `hedge: lost`) and both spend the target's budget. An `lm_model` target
refuses all three.

```yaml
model:
  name: z-ai/glm-5.3-flash
  provider: openrouter
  routing: internal
  timeout_skip: {threshold: 5}
  health: {min_attempts: 40}
  provider_routing: {zdr: true, quantizations: [fp8, fp16, bf16, fp32, unknown]}
```

**A target can name a canonical model instead of a provider.** A primary or fallback
with no `provider` (`model: {name: deepseek-v4.1-flash}`) names a model of the model
catalog; simpl_ia sends it to every provider the active catalog offers it on, under each
provider's own name and with the credentials the host bound for that provider, as one
chain position whose attempts share one budget, ranked by price or by `ranking:
effective`. Its `provider_routing` filters its OpenRouter endpoints only
(`quantizations`, `require_parameters`, `sort: price`; simpl_ia refuses the rest), and
its `prompt_cache` reaches its OpenAI member only; `base_url`, `routing` and `api:
decisions` are refused on it. `require_zdr: true` (on such a target or a bucket only, not
inherited) keeps it on zero-data-retention offerings, except the own offering of each
provider `zdr_exempt_providers: [deepseek, openai, cheaper_inference]` names (valid with
`require_zdr` only). A target naming a provider is unchanged. A new
config version may name only a canonical model the active catalog lists:
`ConfigVersionRepository.insert_version` raises `UnknownCanonicalModelError` (a
`ConfigAuthoringError` and a `ValueError`) otherwise, and `af_config_migrate` checks the
names against a catalog document (`catalog=`, `--catalog`; by default the one simpl_core
ships, `MODEL_CATALOG_DOCUMENT`). Since `require_zdr` is not inherited, a new version whose
primary sets it must give every fallback its own: a canonical model or bucket with
`require_zdr: true`, an OpenRouter target with `provider_routing.zdr: true`, a
provider's model the same catalog marks `zdr` (`ModelCatalogDocument.zdr_offers()`), or a
model on a provider the primary's `zdr_exempt_providers` names;
`insert_version` and `af_config_migrate` raise `ZdrFallbackError` (a
`ConfigAuthoringError` and a `ValueError`) otherwise. The shipped catalog marks no
provider `zdr`, so a direct provider can follow a `require_zdr` primary only when the
primary exempts it; stored versions are not re-checked.

```yaml
model:
  name: deepseek-v4.1-flash
  require_zdr: true
  fallbacks:
    - {name: glm-5.3-flash, require_zdr: true}
    - {name: z-ai/glm-5.3, provider: openrouter, provider_routing: {zdr: true}}
```

**A target can name a bucket instead of a model,** for an agent that names no model:
`model: {bucket: fast_check}` (a primary or a fallback; no `name`, no `provider`) names a
bucket of the active model catalog. simpl_ia stands it at one chain position per tier of
the bucket: the models of one tier are interchangeable, so a call may go to any of them
for cost or speed, each model keeping its own attempt budget, deadline and cap (the
catalog model's, else the target's, else the policy's); a later tier serves only once the
models before it are spent or failing. Every `fallback_index` simpl_ia reports is a
position, so a target after a bucket is numbered past its tiers, and the audit row's
`fallback_index` of an answer is that position. `provider_routing`, `prompt_cache`,
`base_url`, `routing` and `api: decisions` are refused on it; `require_zdr: true` is
allowed (not inherited). A new config version may name only a bucket the active catalog
lists: `insert_version` raises `UnknownBucketError` (a `ConfigAuthoringError` and a
`ValueError`) otherwise, and `af_config_migrate` checks against a catalog document as it
does canonical names. `configured_model_name` is `None` for a bucket primary, which
`LLMAgent` builds without a `model` (simpl_ia refuses a per-call model in its place).

```yaml
model:
  bucket: fast_check
  fallbacks:
    - {name: z-ai/glm-5.3, provider: openrouter}
```

**The model catalog** is the config-ledger key `model_catalog` (`config_kind
'model_catalog'`; `MODEL_CATALOG_KEY`, `MODEL_CATALOG_KIND` in
`simpl_core.agent_framework.authoring`), versioned and activated like any other key but
never projected: processes read the active version's content whole. Every offer of a
model names the same snapshot: where a provider's name is an alias (DeepSeek's
`deepseek-flash` serves DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017), the other providers' offers name that
snapshot's own id (`deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017` on OpenRouter), not a name that
serves an older one. Its document (`ModelCatalogDocument`, `parse_model_catalog(content)`):

```yaml
models:
  deepseek-v4.1-flash:
    offers: {deepseek: deepseek-flash, openrouter: deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017, cheaper_inference: ""}
    budgets: {attempt_timeout_seconds: 60, max_output_tokens: 8000, max_invalid_responses: 3}
    zdr: {deepseek: true}
quantization:
  ladder: [[bf16, fp16, fp32], [fp8, mxfp8], [int8], [fp6], [nvfp4, mxfp4, fp4], [int4]]
  default_min: fp4
  unknown: allowed
  never: []
buckets:
  fast_check:
    tiers: [[deepseek-v4.1-flash, glm-5.3-flash], [gpt-6-luna]]
    objective: {minimize: cost, tie_band: 0.10, then: tps}
    constraints: {min_tps: 30, max_p90_latency_s: 45, max_p_bad: 0.03}
  reviewer_zdr:
    requires: {zdr: true}
    tiers: [[glm-5.3-flash, deepseek-v4.1-flash]]
```

`offers` maps a provider (`openai`, `deepseek`, `openrouter`, `cheaper_inference`) to
its own name for the model; a blank name is no offer yet. `budgets` are the model's
defaults for a target naming it, below the target's own and above the policy's. `zdr` is
the model's zero-data-retention status on a provider without sub-providers, from the
account's terms. `quantization` is the precision policy: `ladder` (rungs, highest
first), `default_min` (the lowest rung cleared for every agent, `null` for none named; a
precision on a lower rung serves only an agent whose clearance for it is `allowed`),
`unknown` (how a precision no rung names is treated, an offering reporting none included:
`allowed` keeps it; `requires_clearance` is refused by the write gate) and `never`
(precisions no agent is sent to, whatever its clearance). `buckets` serve agents that name no model: `tiers` lists each tier's canonical models,
best first; `objective` and `constraints` are simpl_ia's `ObjectiveConfig` and
`ConstraintsConfig` for the bucket's tiers (between a target's own and its policy's; only
the fields written are kept); `requires.zdr` keeps every tier on zero-data-retention
offerings. The write gate (`insert_version` with `config_kind='model_catalog'`, and
`af_config_migrate(config_kind='model_catalog')`) refuses a document that does not fit
this shape, `unknown: requires_clearance`, an offer whose name its provider cannot take, a
canonical name written twice,
a provider's model offered under two canonical names, a bucket with no tier or an empty
one, a tier naming a model the catalog does not list, and a model named twice in one
bucket, with `ModelCatalogError`; the key `model_catalog` holds catalog versions only.
The catalog simpl_core ships is
`src/simpl_core/agent_framework/model_routing/model_catalog.yaml` (version 3: the models
outreach names, `deepseek-v4.1-flash` on DeepSeek and on OpenRouter as
`deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017`, `glm-5.3-flash` and `glm-5.3` on OpenRouter,
`gpt-6.1-sol` and `gpt-6-luna` on OpenAI and OpenRouter, Cheaper Inference ids blank,
first-party ZDR unset, every precision from fp4 up routable and int4 only for an agent
cleared for it, and the buckets above). Its seed, step `seed_model_catalog_3` of
`migrations/20261127_offering_listed_speed_and_model_catalog_3.sql`, registers version 3
with parent 1 and activates it only while version 1 is the active one. A new version is a seed:

```bash
simpl_core af_config_migrate --yaml src/simpl_core/agent_framework/model_routing/model_catalog.yaml \
  --dest migrations/ --config-key model_catalog --config-kind model_catalog \
  --version 4 --parent 3 --prefix <migration number> --summary "..." --activate
```

The adaptive LLM routing migration, `migrations/20261111_adaptive_llm_routing.sql`, holds every
`llm_routing` object and the routing objects of `agent_framework` and `sales_view` as
steps of one file. It is applied with `psql -X -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d <database> -f <file>`
(never the SQL editor: it builds indexes `CONCURRENTLY` and uses psql's `\gset`, `\if` and
`\gexec`), after simpl_tracker's routing cost tracking migration, and run again from the
top after a failure; `migrations/20261111_adaptive_llm_routing_rollback.sql` undoes it
step by step, last step first. Its step `seed_model_catalog_1` registers and activates
version 1 (only while no `model_catalog` version was ever activated), before any version
naming a canonical model. Step `seed_model_catalog_2` registers version 2, the shipped
document with the buckets `fast_check` (`[[deepseek-v4.1-flash, glm-5.3-flash],
[gpt-6-luna]]`) and `reviewer_zdr` (`[[glm-5.3-flash, deepseek-v4.1-flash]]`, ZDR only),
**inactive**: a person reviews and activates it (`SELECT
agent_framework.activate_config_version('model_catalog', '2')`) before any version naming
a bucket can be written.

The routing table is `llm_routing.offering` (table model
`simpl_core.models.LlmRoutingOffering`), filled every hour by simpl_flow's
`sync_openrouter_listing(db)` (`simpl_core.agent_framework.model_routing`) with every
endpoint of each OpenRouter chat or decision model an active policy names (a `typesafe`
target included) or the active catalog offers, under the name the policy or catalog
gives; an endpoint is ZDR when the gateway's ZDR list names it under that name or under
the id the gateway answers it as (a dated snapshot answers as its model's id). It reads
with the account's OpenRouter key (`sync_openrouter_listing(db, api_key=...)`, else
`OPENROUTER_API_KEY`; without one it logs `listing_without_key` and reads keyless):
OpenRouter then lists only the endpoints the account's data policy allows, and fills each
one's speed over its last 30 minutes, which the row keeps as `listed_tps` (tokens per
second) and `listed_ttft_ms` (time to first token), each `{p50, p75, p90, p99}`, handed to
simpl_ia's `Offering` (step `offering_listed_speed` of migration
`20261127_offering_listed_speed_and_model_catalog_3.sql`, applied before a simpl_core that
reads them is deployed). Each model
is written in
its own transaction: a model whose listing cannot be read or written keeps its rows and
is named in `ListingSyncResult.failed_models`. `sync_first_party_listing(db, *,
dry_run=False, http=None, cheaper_inference_api_key=None,
cheaper_inference_base_url=None)` writes one row per model the active catalog offers on
DeepSeek, OpenAI or Cheaper Inference, with upstream `''` (they have no sub-providers), no
prices (the price book prices them), Cheaper Inference's precision `unknown`, and `zdr`
from the catalog's `zdr` for the provider, else, on Cheaper Inference, from its
zero-data-retention catalog (`GET /models?zdr=true`, read with the account's key: the
argument, else `CHEAPER_INFERENCE_API_KEY` / `CHEAPER_INFERENCE_BASE_URL`); a row the
catalog no longer offers is unlisted. When Cheaper Inference's catalog cannot be read
(no key included), its rows keep their listing and its models are in `failed_models`.
Without an active catalog it does nothing. Run it with `sync_openrouter_listing` in the
hourly listing job. A host opts in once per
process with `routing = await use_routing_table(db)`, which keeps the listed rows in
memory (reloaded in the background at most once a minute) and hands them to simpl_ia,
and calls `await routing.aclose()` before it exits, while `db` is still open. A host
that does not, or a model the table does not list, falls back to the target's own
closed list (`only`, or `order` with `allow_fallbacks: false`), else to the gateway.
A process with no offering source that runs an agent whose policy names a canonical model
or a bucket (`needs_routing_table(policy)`) gets the same install on the Loader's database
the first time, before the agent is built (`ensure_routing_table(db)`, called by
`execute_agent` and `execute_agent_stream`; concurrent first calls share one install), so
simpl_flow's flows and strategy runs can run such policies; a host's own install is left
alone. When the database an install made this way was made on has been shut down, the
next such agent installs it again on its own Loader database and drains the old
recorders, logging what they held as lost. Nothing drains an install made this way at
exit: the outcomes and routes counted since the recorders' last flush (30 s) are lost,
so a host that needs them installs and closes the table itself.

Each offering is priced from the shared price book, simpl_tracker's
`cost_tracking.llm_token_prices` (input, cache read and output, USD per token, at the
tier a call made now is billed at: DeepSeek's `peak` or `off_peak`, `standard`
elsewhere; the row of the endpoint's tag, or the model-level row, tag `''`, for an
offering of DeepSeek, OpenAI or Cheaper Inference), and from the listing when the book has no
row for it; the offering's `price_source` (`book` or `listing`) says which. A listed
price of 0 counts as none: an offering listed at $0 with no book row is unpriced, so
simpl_ia ranks it after the priced ones instead of as the cheapest.
`PriceBookRepository.token_prices()` reads every row with its `pricing_tier`, so the
view needs simpl_tracker's tier migration (the `pricing_tier` column) before a host runs
this simpl_core; without it the read fails as a missing view does. A failed read of the
book keeps the book a previous load read and, before any has, prices every offering from
the listing; it logs `agent_framework.model_routing.routing_prices_unavailable` as an
error at every load. The listed offerings are the one read a load needs (when it fails
the process keeps its previous copy and logs
`agent_framework.model_routing.routing_table_load_failed`); every other part (pauses,
health, attributions, statistics, capabilities, epochs, token mixes, clearances,
canaries, catalog) is
read in a savepoint of its own, and one that cannot be read keeps the rows a previous
load read, and is empty only when none has (its table missing for instance); each
failed read logs an error, `agent_framework.model_routing.routing_table_part_unavailable`
with its `part`, `error` and `kept`. Kept pauses still end at their own time. The table also hands simpl_ia each agent's token mix,
`llm_routing.agent_token_mix`: the median input tokens (cached included) and output
tokens (reasoning included) of the agent's answered calls to each model over the last 7
days, kept for groups of at least 5 calls.

**Every call's route is recorded.** `use_routing_table` also installs a `RouteRecorder`.
After each `LLMAgent` call (`check` or `stream`), answered or failed, the chain simpl_ia
built (its route log: per target the routing, budget, attempt deadline, output cap, token
mix, the candidates with rank, estimated cost per call and price source, and the upstreams
left out with a reason such as `zdr`, `quantization`, `ignored` or `paused:agent`) and the
call's attempts are
written in the background to `llm_routing.routing_decision` and `llm_routing.attempt`,
under the call's agent run id and node run id. `llm_routing.decision_explained` renders
one line per decision and target, with the agent when its `agent_framework.agent_runs`
row exists; the target's deadline follows its budget, then its cap when it has one (a
target logged without them shows neither). An exclusion reads by whether the upstream is
still sent: `not X (reason)` when left out, `X last (blamed)` when tried after every other
upstream, `X kept (reason)` for any other shadow exclusion, and `sent without the
precision filter (precision_filter_unmatched)`; X is the upstream, else the member's
provider:

```text
openrouter z-ai/glm-5.2 (internal, budget 3, deadline 90 s, cap 16000 tokens, mix 4200 in / 900 out): 1 deepinfra/fp8 $0.00412 book; 2 together $0.00519 listing; not wafer (ignored)
```

Each attempt row carries `health_class`, the class simpl_ia gave it (`answered`,
`timeout`, `server`, `capability`, `invalid`, `cancelled`; null for an entry without one),
and `attempt_key`, the `attempt_id` simpl_ia gives the request,
which simpl_tracker writes on the request's cost receipt as `routing_attempt_id`: join
`llm_routing.attempt.attempt_key` to `cost_tracking.llm_request_receipt.routing_attempt_id`
to read what an attempt cost. It is unique where set and null for an entry without a
UUID id; an attempt whose key is already stored is skipped and logged as
`agent_framework.model_routing.route_attempts_duplicate`, and the rest of its route is
written.

The decision also keeps `attempt_timeout_seconds`, the agent's per-attempt budget the call
ran with (`LLMAgent`'s `timeout_seconds` when the client bounds each attempt, else null;
a target's own deadline is in the chain), and
each attempt `streamed` and `stream_duration_ms`: a request's `latency_ms` is its whole
response time, a stream's is its time to first chunk, and `stream_duration_ms` the whole
stream, both from when the request was sent.

**Response times per day.** `llm_routing.response_time_daily` rolls the attempts up per
UTC day of the attempt's start, pipeline, node, agent, provider, model and upstream (`''`
for none; the pipeline and node of the agent run's node run, else of the decision's; the
agent is the run's `agent_id`, else the `process_name` of a receipt of the call):

| Column | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `calls`, `attempts`, `answered` | calls with an attempt in the row, attempt rows, those answered |
| `budget_hits` | attempts that ended on the per-attempt deadline (`ModelAttemptTimeout`) or an HTTP timeout raised as its own class (`llm_routing.attempt_hit_budget`); an HTTP timeout wrapped in `ModelAPIError` is not told apart |
| `latency_avg_ms`, `latency_p50_ms`, `latency_p90_ms`, `latency_p95_ms` | answered attempts: a request's `latency_ms`, a stream's `stream_duration_ms` |
| `ttft_p50_ms`, `ttft_p95_ms` | answered streams' `latency_ms` |
| `output_tokens_per_s_p50` | answered receipts' output tokens (reasoning included) per second of the attempt |
| `budget_seconds` | the most common attempt deadline of the row's calls: each attempt's own `deadline_seconds`, written with the attempt (its target's `attempt_timeout_seconds`, on a bucket tier its model's, else the call's) |
| `cost_usd` | known amounts of the cost entries of the attempts' receipts (a receipt's entry has its id); null when none is known |
| `input_tokens`, `output_tokens` | the same receipts' tokens (input with cached, output with reasoning), duplicates left out; null without a receipt |
| `failed_calls` | the row's calls with no attempt answered on its provider and model |
| `estimated_cost_usd` | the failure-cost estimates (`cost_tracking.cost_estimate`) that `cost_tracking.estimate_counts` admits of the receipts of the row's attempts that did not answer, duplicates left out; null without any. Never part of `cost_usd` |
| `answered_input_tokens` | the input tokens of the answered attempts' receipts that report cached tokens; null without any |
| `cache_hit_share` | those receipts' cached tokens over `answered_input_tokens`; null when that is null or 0 |

A call with attempts on several upstreams or models counts in each of their rows.
`llm_routing.refresh_response_time_daily(p_since date)` recomputes each UTC day from
`p_since` to today that has attempts, replacing all its rows, and returns the rows
written; a day without attempts keeps its rows, so the rollup outlives the attempts'
30-day retention. It reads each attempt's agent and deadline and its decision's node run,
never `agent_runs`. With `pg_cron` and the analytics switch on (below),
`response_time_daily_recent` runs it at minute 5 of every hour over yesterday and today,
and `estimated_costs_refresh` at 04:45 UTC daily over the last 21 days (the window
failure-cost estimates are written over, so a new estimate and its replacement by a late
bill land within a day) while `llm_routing.failure_estimates_in_use(now() - interval '21
days')`: simpl_tracker's failure-estimates switch is on, or an estimate of the last 21 days
exists (false before simpl_tracker's tables exist); without pg_cron, a host schedules the
same calls.
`scripts/backfill_response_times.py` (dry run unless `--apply`) fills earlier days from
the LLM runs' `agent_runs.attempts`, latency, counts and budget hits only, one UTC day at
a time with `--pause-seconds` (0.5) between days, and never overwrites a row.

The Activity tab reads `sales_view.llm_activity(p_from date, p_to date, p_group text[])`
(security invoker, execute for `service_role`), a wrapper of `llm_routing.llm_activity`:
the rows of the UTC days in `[p_from, p_to]`, one per combination of the dimensions
`p_group` names among `day`, `pipeline_id`, `node_id`, `agent`, `provider`, `model`,
`upstream` (an unknown name raises `invalid_parameter_value`; an empty array gives one
total row). Columns, in order: `day`, `pipeline_id`, `node_id`, `agent`, `provider`,
`model`, `upstream` (null when not grouped by), `calls`, `attempts`, `answered`,
`budget_hits`, `latency_avg_ms`, `latency_p50_ms`, `latency_p95_ms`, `ttft_p50_ms`,
`output_tokens_per_s_p50`, `budget_seconds`, `cost_usd`, `input_tokens`,
`output_tokens`, `failed_calls`, `estimated_cost_usd`, `cache_hit_share`. Counts, costs,
estimates and tokens are summed. Over several stored rows the averages and percentiles
are the rows' values weighted by their answered attempts (exact for the average,
approximate for percentiles); `cache_hit_share` is the rows' shares weighted by their
`answered_input_tokens` (exact); `budget_seconds` is the value with the most calls. The
routing migration's step `llm_response_times` is applied before a host runs this
simpl_core: without its columns every route write is dropped.

A failed write is logged and never fails the call; `routing.aclose()` drains the route
recorder and the epoch recorder beside the health recorder, within the same
`timeout_seconds`, and logs `agent_framework.model_routing.route_decisions_lost` (and
`epochs_lost`) for what it could not write.
`refresh_routing_inputs(db)` (simpl_flow's listing flow runs it after the sync) rebuilds
the token mixes from `cost_tracking`; it returns `RoutingMaintenance(token_mixes)` and
raises what the database raises.

**Scheduled jobs: a switch for the analytics, retention always on.** Every analytics
pg_cron job the routing and cost migrations schedule (offering statistics and capability
marks every 5 minutes, each a job of its own, cache statistics and model fit hourly,
detectors and certification queueing hourly, response times hourly, pipeline run costs
every 15 minutes and nightly, cancel billing daily at 01:23 UTC, prompt families and
estimated costs daily) filters its command on `WHERE llm_routing.analytics_jobs_on()`, or,
for `pipeline_run_costs_recent`, whose command is a `CALL`, tests it in a `DO` block
(the estimated costs also on `failure_estimates_in_use`, below): while the one row of
`llm_routing.job_setting` (service role only) has `analytics_jobs` false, the migration's
default, the job returns at once without reading anything. Turn them on with

```sql
UPDATE llm_routing.job_setting SET analytics_jobs = true, updated_at = now();
```

and off the same way. The functions themselves do not read the switch, so a host or a
person can still call them. The retention jobs never read it and run as procedures that
delete in bounded batches, each committed on its own with a pause between them (call them
outside a transaction block; each returns its counts):
`routing_evidence_retention` at 03:50 UTC runs `CALL llm_routing.prune_routing_evidence()`
(attempts and decisions written more than 30 days ago, health buckets older than 48 hours,
epochs that ended more than 90 days ago), `agent_run_messages_retention` at 03:40 UTC runs
`CALL agent_framework.prune_agent_run_messages()` (30 days) and
`pipeline_run_costs_retention` at 03:55 UTC runs
`CALL agent_framework.prune_pipeline_run_costs()` (runs started more than 90 days ago).

**Health is shared by the fleet.** `use_routing_table` also installs a `HealthRecorder`
as simpl_ia's health sink. Every attempt simpl_ia classifies (answered, timeout, server
error, capability error, invalid answer; a cancelled one is dropped), on any target, is
counted in the process per minute, offering (provider, model, upstream; `""` when pinned
to none) and agent (`LLMAgent` passes its definition id as simpl_ia's `agent_name`), with a
histogram of the answered attempts' latency, and written every 30 seconds through
`llm_routing.record_health` into `llm_routing.health_bucket` (kept 48 hours), with the
settings of each offering's latest outcome. A failed write is logged and retried (5 tries,
30 s apart), and never fails the call; `routing.aclose()` writes what is still counted,
one try within 5 seconds, and logs `agent_framework.model_routing.health_outcomes_lost`
for what it could not write. The process's own timeout skip stays as the fast local path.

`record_health` adds the buckets to every other process's, then runs each named
offering's transition under its lock, over the last `window_seconds` (600):

- **Rate.** Failures (timeouts, server errors) over attempts (answered, timeouts, server
  errors; capability errors and invalid answers left out), counted once `min_attempts`
  (20) remain, and only from the end of the offering's last global pause.
- **Attribution.** An agent failing at `open_rate` or more on an offering whose other
  agents have `misfit_min_other_attempts` (10) or more attempts there and fail below
  `degrade_rate` is left out of that offering's rate (and of the fleet guard's view of
  it), on a model with a single offering too, and has one open `agent_attribution`
  finding on that offering (none for a chain without an agent name), resolved once a
  window passes without the blame; blamed again within a window of that resolution, the
  same finding is reopened rather than a new one written. The routing table hands each offering the agents of
  its open `agent_attribution` findings last seen within their window
  (`Offering.blamed_agents`), and simpl_ia tries that offering after every other for
  those agents only: never skipped, never paused, logged in the route's `excluded` as
  `blamed` with `shadow: true`. An agent left out so on
  `misfit_min_offerings` (2) or more offerings is a workload misfit: one open
  `workload_misfit` finding once its attempts there reach `min_attempts`. On an offering
  where the others fail too, or are too idle to judge, its failures count.
- **States.** `healthy` → `degraded` at `degrade_rate` (10%), → `open` at `open_rate`
  (25%); `degraded` → `healthy` below `recover_rate` (5%). `open` writes a global
  `offering_pause` (reason `health`) for `first_cooldown_seconds` (300), doubled at each
  reopening up to `max_cooldown_seconds` (a day) and reset after a full window healthy;
  once that pause ends (expired, or by hand) the offering is `probing`: `probe_answers`
  (3) answers make it healthy, a failure reopens it.
- **Capability.** An agent with 2 or more capability errors (400, 404, 422) there gets an
  agent pause (reason `capability`) for `capability_cooldown_seconds` (a day) and one
  `capability_error` finding; one error does neither.
- **Fleet guard.** While two or more, and at least `fleet_guard_share` (half), of the
  offerings with traffic are failing or paused for health, no offering opens (it stays
  `degraded`), and one `fleet_guard` finding is open.
- **Overrides** (unexpired): `ban` keeps the offering open through a pause (reason
  `override`; for one agent, that agent's pause), `exempt` never opens it (nor pauses an
  agent for capability), `force_state` sets its state.

Every process adopts the pauses with its routing table (within a minute), and each
offering's `health` with it: simpl_ia ranks a `degraded` one with the gateway's degraded
endpoints. An offering stored `open` with no global pause in force loads as `probing`
(`routing_table.loaded_health`), so canonical and bucket targets try it again once its
pause ends. An offering pinned to no upstream gets a state but no pause, and loads as
`probing` once its cooldown since opening has passed. A finding is
resolved once a window passes without it being seen.

The dashboard reads `sales_view.llm_routing_offerings` (each offering with `paused`, a
global pause in force, `open_pauses`, and `health_state`, `health_reason`,
`health_counts`; an open offering whose pause ended, or without an upstream whose cooldown
passed, reads `probing`),
`sales_view.llm_routing_pauses` (every pause in force with its scope, agent, `reason`
and length), `sales_view.llm_routing_findings` (open first) and
`sales_view.llm_routing_overrides` (active first), ends a pause by id with
`sales_view.end_llm_routing_pause(pause_id, ended_by)`, and sets and ends overrides with
`sales_view.set_llm_routing_override(p_provider, p_model, p_upstream, p_agent, p_action,
p_state, p_expires_at, p_by) → bigint` (`p_agent` null for every agent; `p_state` only
with `force_state`) and `sales_view.end_llm_routing_override(p_id, p_by) → boolean`. A
ban pauses at once; ending it ends its pause. Every process stops skipping for an ended
pause at its next reload. The `llm_routing` views and functions they wrap are security
invoker; the `sales_view` objects are for service_role only. The routing migration's step
`llm_routing_health` is applied before a host runs this simpl_core. `scripts/replay_health.py` replays the state machine over the
`agent_runs` attempt trail in a scratch database and prints every transition and finding.

**Offerings are ranked by what they measured.** The routing table also hands simpl_ia
each offering's statistics (`RoutingTable.offering_stats(agent, provider, model,
upstream)`: the agent's row, else the offering's pooled row, else `None`) and each
agent's token profile (`RoutingTable.token_profile(agent, provider, model)`), from
`llm_routing.offering_stats` and `llm_routing.agent_token_profile`. simpl_ia scores every
internal target's candidates with them on every call and logs the effective ranking
beside the price one (`effective_rank`, `cost_per_answer_usd`, `latency_s`, `p_bad`,
`deadline_s` per candidate; `ranking` and `exploration` per target); only a target with
`ranking: effective` sends it.

- `llm_routing.attempt_outcome` (view; `llm_routing.attempt_outcomes(p_since, p_until
  DEFAULT now(), p_decision_ids bigint[] DEFAULT NULL)` for the attempts written in a
  window, both ends included, only those of `p_decision_ids` when given) is each
  attempt with its agent and its deadline as the route recorder wrote them on the attempt
  (`decision_row` takes the agent; `attempt_deadline_seconds`: the chain entry's
  `attempt_timeout_seconds`, else, on a bucket tier, the attempt's model's under the
  entry's `models`, else the call's), read without `agent_runs` or the chain; its whole
  latency and a
  stream's time to first chunk, its receipts' input, cached, output and reasoning tokens
  and billed or priced cost (duplicates left out), and, for an attempt that did not
  answer while `cost_tracking.settings.failure_estimates` is on, its counting estimate.
- `offering_stats`, per provider, model, upstream and agent (`''` is the pooled row):
  exponentially decayed over every attempt kept, with no window: the pooled row weighs
  every agent's attempts by 0.5 per hour of age, an agent's row its attempts by 0.5 per
  week, an attempt's age counted from its start (its write, if earlier); a row weighing
  under 1e-9 attempts is dropped. Only `answered`, `timeout` and `server` attempts count. `p_fail` is failures (timeout, server) over
  attempts, `p_late` late answers (above 0.8 × the target's deadline) over answers, each
  under a Beta prior of mean 5% worth 10 attempts, `(hits + 0.5) / (count + 10)`; an
  agent's are shrunk toward the pooled row instead, `(own + 20 × pooled) / (count + 20)`,
  when there is one. `samples` (weighted answers, rounded), `attempts`, latency p50/p90/p95,
  `ttft_p50_ms`, `ttft_p95_ms` (over at least 5 weighted streams, rounded),
  `tokens_per_s` (a stream's from its first chunk), `cache_hit_share`,
  `answered_cost_usd` and `failed_cost_usd` (cost plus estimate, unknown counting 0) are
  weighted the same. A quantile is read from a decayed histogram of buckets 2^(1/8) wide
  (about 9%): the weighted mean of the first bucket whose cumulative weight reaches the
  share, the exact value when that bucket's values are equal.
- `agent_token_profile`, per agent, provider and model: median and p90 input (cached
  included), median cached, median and p90 output (reasoning left out) and reasoning
  tokens of the answered attempts with a receipt, weighted 0.5 per week of age and read
  from histograms the same way, for at least 5 samples (weighted answers, rounded).
  `agent_token_mix` stays for `token_mix`.
- `llm_routing.refresh_offering_stats(p_now timestamptz DEFAULT NULL) → integer` adds the
  attempts written since its last run to stored decayed sums and histograms
  (`offering_stats_sum`, `offering_stats_histogram`, `agent_token_sum`,
  `agent_token_histogram`, each stored relative to `offering_stats_state.reference_at`, so
  an attempt touches only its own rows), then rewrites the three tables as of `p_now` (the
  database's clock when null) and returns the rows written, one at a time. Each attempt
  folded in the last 2 days is recorded (`offering_stats_folded`) with the tokens, cost and
  failure estimate it was folded with: one a slow transaction commits after a refresh is
  still added, once, and each is read again at 1 hour and 2 days old; when its cost or
  tokens changed (a late bill, a receipt delivered after the fold, a daily failure
  estimate), its recorded contribution is subtracted and its current one added. Every
  product of a decayed weight goes through `llm_routing.product_or_zero(value, factor)`,
  0 under 1e-290 instead of a float8 underflow, so evidence never touched again decays to
  0 and the refresh keeps running. Without stored sums, or for a `p_now` before the last
  refresh's, it rebuilds them; `llm_routing.rebuild_offering_stats(p_now)` rebuilds them
  from every attempt kept. Both give the same statistics for the same attempts once their
  costs and tokens are those last read; the prune leaves the sums of the attempts it
  deletes in place. With `pg_cron`, `offering_stats_refresh` runs it every 5 minutes
  while the analytics switch is on, else a host schedules it. `OfferingStatsRepository`
  reads the tables and calls `refresh(now=)` and `rebuild(now=)`.
- `agent_model_fit`, per agent, provider and model (the provider's name): the agent's
  `answers` (attempts of class `answered` or `invalid`) and `invalid` ones of the last 14
  days, each weighted 0.5 per week of age; attempts with no agent count nowhere.
  `llm_routing.refresh_model_fit(p_now timestamptz DEFAULT NULL) → integer` replaces it,
  one at a time; with `pg_cron`, `model_fit_refresh` runs it at minute 53 of every hour
  while the analytics switch is on, else a host schedules it. `OfferingStatsRepository.model_fits()` reads it and
  `refresh_model_fit(now=)` calls it. The routing table sums an agent's rows over each
  canonical model's catalog offers and hands simpl_ia the rate,
  `RoutingTable.model_fit(agent, model) → float | None` = `(invalid + 0.5) / (answers +
  10)` (a Beta prior of mean 5% worth 10 answers; `None` unmeasured): a bucket tier's
  candidate's cost per usable answer is divided by 1 − its model's rate. The routing
  migration's step `model_fit` is applied before a host runs this simpl_core: without the
  table every routing table load fails and keeps its previous copy.
- `offering_stats_input_size`, per `offering_stats` row and `input_size` (`lt_2k`,
  `2k_8k`, `8k_32k`, `ge_32k`, as simpl_ia's `routing_table.input_size` names the input
  tokens of the attempt's receipts; `llm_routing.input_size(tokens)` in SQL): `samples`,
  `attempts` (the weighted answers), latency p50/p90/p95, `ttft_p50_ms`, `ttft_p95_ms` and
  `tokens_per_s` of the row's answers of that size, for at least 5 samples; written by
  `refresh_offering_stats`. The routing table hands them to simpl_ia as the statistics'
  `by_input_size` (with the row's `p_fail` and `p_late`), which scores a call on its
  bucket's latencies, first chunks and speed: the agent's token profile places the call,
  else the rendered prompt's length over 4 (`LLMAgent` passes `prompt_chars`).
  `OfferingStatsRepository.input_size_stats()` reads them.

`scripts/routing_shadow_report.py --since YYYY-MM-DD [--until] [--agent]` (read only)
prints, per agent and target, the calls that logged an effective ranking, how many had a
different first offering than the price order, how many were explored, and the average
logged cost per answer and latency of each order's first offering. The routing
migration's step `offering_stats` is applied before a host runs this simpl_core: without
`attempt.health_class` every route write is dropped, and without the two tables every
routing table load fails and keeps its previous copy.

**Tail control: the cancel-billing mark and the off-policy report.**

- `llm_routing.offering_cancel_billing` (provider, model, upstream, `cancels`,
  `overbilled`, `cancel_stops_billing`, `last_cancel_at`, `computed_at`): over 30 days, the
  streams cancelled on the offering that its provider billed (a cost entry with
  `cost_source = 'provider'`) and whose input is known (the receipt's, else the same run's
  answer on the same model). A cancel is overbilled when its bill pays for more than 32
  output tokens past what it streamed, the output a bill pays for being its amount less the
  uncached input at the listed prompt price, over the listed completion price.
  `cancel_stops_billing` is true with 5 cancels or more and none overbilled, false with 5 or
  more and one overbilled, null below 5. `llm_routing.refresh_cancel_billing(p_now
  timestamptz DEFAULT NULL) → integer` replaces it; with `pg_cron`,
  `cancel_billing_refresh` runs at 01:23 UTC daily while the analytics switch is on, else
  a host schedules it.
  `OfferingStatsRepository.cancel_billing()` and `refresh_cancel_billing(now=)` read and
  call it. The routing table gives each simpl_ia `Offering` its mark as
  `cancel_stops_billing` (`None` until marked): simpl_ia hedges only on offerings marked
  true. `sales_view.llm_routing_capabilities` lists it as one more capability per listed
  offering, `cancel_stops_billing`: `observed` (true), `failed` (false) or `unknown`.
- `sales_view.llm_routing_off_policy_report(p_from date, p_to date)` (over
  `llm_routing.off_policy_report`, same arguments and rows; both days UTC and included; a
  reversed range raises) estimates per agent, by self-normalised importance sampling over
  the calls whose route logged a propensity for its first upstream, what its calls would
  have cost per answer and taken had each started on the price order's first candidate, and
  on the effective order's. A call counts by its chain's `fallback_index` 0 entry: a chosen
  canary's propensity, else the canary's (1 without) times the exploration draw's, or the
  stored `routing_epoch` propensity of the epoch and home it followed (kept, new or
  switched), or 1; no propensity under an epoch override, an unstored epoch or no draw at
  all. A call matching an order weighs 1 / propensity; its cost is all its attempts' known
  amounts plus counting estimates, its latency from its first attempt's start to the end of
  its last answered one. Intervals are 95%, from the residuals clustered by draw (an epoch's
  calls share one), null with fewer than 2 draws and never below 0. Columns: `agent`
  (`''` without one), `calls`, then `price_calls`, `price_cost_per_answer_usd`,
  `price_cost_per_answer_low_usd`, `price_cost_per_answer_high_usd`, `price_latency_s`,
  `price_latency_low_s`, `price_latency_high_s`, and the same seven for `effective_`;
  ordered by agent. It reads simpl_tracker's receipt tables through `attempt_outcomes`,
  over the attempts written from a day before `p_from` to a day after `p_to`.

The routing migration's step `tail_control` is applied before a host runs this simpl_core:
without `ttft_p95_ms` and the two tables every routing table load fails and keeps its
previous copy.

**Prompt families stay on a warm cache.** simpl_ia keys every call's prompt family from
the agent, its `prompt_id` (`LLMAgent` passes the definition's when the client takes it)
and its tool names, sends that key where a provider can use it, and, for a target with
`ranking: effective`, keeps the family on one upstream of each model for an epoch (the
provider's cache lifetime, at most 30 minutes). This package keeps what the fleet shares:

- Each attempt row also carries `family` (the family key), `prefix_fingerprint` (`text[]`,
  cumulative hashes of the request's tool definitions and system prompt per 4,000
  characters) and `cache_affinity` (Cheaper Inference's `hit`, `miss`, `new` or `stale`);
  null when the entry has none.
- `llm_routing.routing_epoch` (family, provider, model, `epoch_start`, `epoch_end`,
  `upstream`, `snapshot_version`, `candidates` jsonb, `draw` jsonb, `propensity`; kept 90
  days): every epoch a process planned. `use_routing_table` installs an `EpochRecorder` as
  simpl_ia's epoch sink; it writes plans in the background through
  `llm_routing.record_epoch(...) → boolean`, which stores a plan only when no epoch of the
  family on that model covers any part of its period, so the first process wins and the
  others follow its row after their next reload (at most a minute). The routing table
  hands simpl_ia each family's latest epoch per model of the last day
  (`RoutingTable.family_epoch(family, provider, model)`), and `snapshot_version()`, a hash
  of the prices and statistics it loaded: the same in every process that loaded the same
  rows, so they seed the same draw.
- `llm_routing.cache_stats` (family, provider, model, upstream): `warm_hit_share`, the
  cached share of the input tokens of the family's answers made within an epoch length of
  its previous answer on that offering, and `calls_per_epoch`, its answers on the model per
  epoch-length period it answered in (the same on every upstream), over 14 days weighted
  0.5 per week of age; the epoch length is the latest planned on the model in the last
  day, else 300 s. `RoutingTable.cache_warmth(family, provider, model, upstream)` and
  `family_rate(family)` (the largest over its models) hand them over.
  `llm_routing.refresh_cache_stats(p_now timestamptz DEFAULT NULL) → integer` replaces the
  table; with `pg_cron`, `cache_stats_refresh` runs it at minute 41 of every hour while the
  analytics switch is on, else a host schedules it.
- `llm_routing.prompt_family` (family, `agents text[]`, `shared_prefix_blocks`,
  `updated_at`): for each family seen in 7 days, the agents whose families' attempts on
  one of the same models agree on its first fingerprint block, its own included, and the
  leading blocks all of them agree on. `RoutingTable.family_agents(family)` hands the
  agents to simpl_ia, which asks Cheaper Inference for an org-wide cache scope when there
  are several. `llm_routing.refresh_prompt_families(p_now timestamptz DEFAULT NULL) →
  integer` replaces it; with `pg_cron`, `prompt_family_refresh` runs at 03:20 UTC daily
  while the analytics switch is on.

`AffinityRepository` writes the epochs, reads the three tables and calls both refreshes.
The routing migration's step `cache_affinity` is applied before a host runs this
simpl_core: without the attempt columns every route write is dropped, and without the
tables every routing table load fails and keeps its previous copy.

**Offerings are marked by capability, from real calls and from a certification suite.**
simpl_ia lists on every attempt entry the capabilities the request needed (`tools`,
`structured_output.native`, `.tool` or `.prompted`, `reasoning`), and routes by each
offering's marks: an offering `failed` for a needed capability is left out, `certified`
or `observed` ones rank ahead of `unknown` ones within their band. This package keeps the
marks and hands them over:

- Each attempt row also carries `capabilities` (`text[]`, sorted; null when the entry has
  no list).
- `llm_routing.offering_capability` (provider, model, upstream, capability, `status`
  `certified` | `observed` | `failed`, `source` `certification` | `observed`,
  `suite_version`, `evidence` jsonb, `updated_at` (when the evidence was seen),
  `expires_at` (a certification's `certified` mark, 30 days on, its `failed` mark 7 days
  on; a `failed` mark from real calls', 24 hours after its evidence)). A missing row is `unknown`. `RoutingTable.load` gives each
  simpl_ia `Offering` its marks as `capabilities` (`None` without any; a mark whose
  `expires_at` has passed is left out, so it reads `unknown`) and counts them in
  `snapshot_version()`.
- `llm_routing.mark_capabilities_from_attempts(p_since timestamptz) → integer`: per
  offering and needed capability of the attempts written since `p_since` on an upstream,
  the latest accepted answer (`health_class = 'answered'`) marks `observed`; the latest
  capability error (`'capability'`: HTTP 400, 404, 422) marks `failed` only when 2 or more
  distinct agents (the attempts' own `agent`) had a capability error for it in those
  attempts, and
  marks nothing otherwise; neither changes a stored mark that is `certified` or newer.
  Each offering whose latest attempt for a capability was a
  capability error gets one open `capability_error` finding with `agent` null and
  `details.capabilities` naming each such capability (its errors, the last one's time and
  the agents). With `pg_cron`, `capability_marks` runs it over the last 15 minutes every
  5 minutes while the analytics switch is on, apart from the statistics refresh, so a
  refresh that fails does not skip the marks.
- `llm_routing.certification_queue` (provider, model, upstream, `trigger`, `queued_at`)
  holds the offerings waiting for the suite. It is filled by triggers: a new offering
  (`new_offering`), a listing change of quantization, context length or supported
  parameters, or a relisting (`listing_change`; every change of those, `zdr` and `listed`
  is kept in `llm_routing.offering_listing_change` (field, `old_value`, `new_value` jsonb,
  `seen_at`)), a new `capability_error` finding (`capability_error`), a detector alarm
  (`drift`), and `llm_routing.enqueue_due_certifications(p_now timestamptz DEFAULT NULL)
  → integer` for every listed offering without a completed run in 30 days (`uncertified`,
  `expired`). Only listed offerings are queued.
- `llm_routing.run_offering_detectors(p_now timestamptz DEFAULT NULL)` returns its alarms
  (provider, model, upstream, `detector`, `statistic`, `threshold`): `error_cusum` and
  `invalid_cusum` (Bernoulli CUSUM over `health_bucket` minutes against the offering's
  7-day rate, above 5), `latency_page_hinkley` (log-latency of answered attempts centred
  per agent, above 20), `invalid_peer` and `reasoning_peer` (modified z-score against the
  model's other offerings, above 3.5; the reasoning one from receipts). Each offering is
  judged on its evidence since its latest alarm, at most a day. An alarm writes one open
  `drift` finding (`details`: `detector`, `statistic`, `threshold`, `alarms`), deletes the
  offering's `offering_stats` rows (the next refresh rebuilds them) and queues it; a drift
  finding not seen for a day is resolved. With `pg_cron`, `offering_detectors` runs it and
  the due check hourly while the analytics switch is on.
- `sales_view.llm_routing_capabilities` (over `llm_routing.capability_status`): one row per
  listed offering and capability, in order: `provider`, `model`, `upstream`,
  `upstream_name`, `capability`, `status` (`unknown` without a mark), `source`,
  `suite_version`, `updated_at`, `expires_at`, `expired`.

The suite is `simpl_core.llm_routing.certification` (the `agent-framework` extra). Its
cases are ledger agents in that package's `agent_policy.yaml` (config key
`llm_routing_certification_root`), each run by `execute_agent(..., model_policy=)` on a
policy pinned to the offering (gateway routing, `provider_routing.only: [upstream]`,
`allow_fallbacks: false`, one attempt, no fallbacks), so every call writes its
`agent_runs` row, tool calls, model exchange and receipts under one node run
(`llm_routing_certification`, correlation `certification_run:<id>`). Cases: tools (the
lookup tool called with the right key among five decoys, per sample), each
structured-output mode (a nested schema answered valid, per sample, an answer after an
invalid attempt included; a mode the client never sends to the offering is not judged),
reasoning (more reasoning tokens at `high` than at `low`), caching (cached tokens on a
repeated long prefix), context (a document of half the window read whole) and a streamed
latency baseline (time to first token, tokens per second; no mark). A capability whose
cases all passed is `certified`; it is `failed` on one case failed by a capability error
(HTTP 400, 404 or 422), or on 2 failed cases (`FAILING_SAMPLES`; every case, for a
capability judged on fewer); fewer failures leave it unmarked. A capability tested by a
single case (reasoning, caching, context) runs that case once more in the same run when
it fails without a capability error (`<case>.rerun`, recorded like any case), and is
`failed` only when the re-run fails too. A timeout, a server error
or a missing receipt decides nothing. A run's `certified` marks expire after 30 days
(`CERTIFIED_TTL`), its `failed` ones after 7 (`FAILED_TTL`).

```python
from simpl_core.llm_routing.certification import run_certification, work_certification_queue

outcome = await run_certification(db, "openrouter", model, upstream, trigger="manual",
                                  max_cost_usd=1.0)
result = await work_certification_queue(db, max_offerings=5, max_cost_usd=5.0)
```

`run_certification` writes `llm_routing.certification_run` (trigger, suite version,
status `running` | `completed` | `stopped` | `error`, `cost_usd`), one
`llm_routing.certification_result` per case (capability or `baseline`, `case_name`,
`passed`, null when undecided, `details`, `agent_run_id`) and the marks, and returns
`CertificationOutcome(run_id, provider, model, upstream, status, cost_usd, cases, marks)`.
It counts each call's cost from its receipts and stops before a call that would pass
`max_cost_usd` (`stopped`, marking only the capabilities it judged whole); it raises
`OfferingNotFound` for an offering not listed and `CertificationRunFailed` (with `run_id`
and `cost_usd`) when a started run ends on an error, stored as `error`.
`work_certification_queue` (simpl_flow's certification flow) first queues what is due,
then runs the oldest queued offerings one at a time, at most `max_offerings`, within
`max_cost_usd` for the whole pass; a failed run is logged
(`llm_routing.certification.run_failed`) and its offering queued again. It returns
`CertificationQueueResult(offerings, completed, stopped, errors, skipped, cases, cost_usd,
budget_exhausted)`. `CertificationRepository` reads and writes the tables and calls the
SQL functions.

Publishing the suite's agents is a ledger version, as for any document: `simpl_core
af_config_migrate --yaml src/simpl_core/llm_routing/certification/agent_policy.yaml --dest
migrations/ --config-key llm_routing_certification_root --version 1 --parent 0 --prefix
<migration number> --summary "..." --activate`, then a person applies the seed; until a
version is active, both functions raise `LookupError`. The placeholder policy in the
document sends nothing. The routing migration's step `certification` is applied before a
host runs this simpl_core: without `attempt.capabilities` every route write is dropped,
and without `offering_capability` every routing table load leaves the capability marks
empty.

**Offerings without an upstream.** DeepSeek, OpenAI and Cheaper Inference serve a model as
one offering, stored with upstream `''`. A member of a canonical target on one of them
reports no upstream; the health recorder counts it toward the offering `''`, the
statistics read it there, `mark_capabilities_from_attempts` marks that offering from
attempts sent to no upstream (never OpenRouter's own routing, which has no such
offering), a `capability_error` finding naming no upstream queues it, and the detectors
read its attempts. Such an offering gets health states and findings but no pause (a pause
needs an upstream). The routing table hands simpl_ia the active catalog's canonical
models (`RoutingTable.catalog_model(name)`, simpl_ia's `CatalogModel` with the offers
that name a model) and its buckets (`RoutingTable.catalog_bucket(name)`, simpl_ia's
`CatalogBucket(name, tiers, objective, constraints, require_zdr)`), reloaded with the
rest; a catalog version whose content fails the gate keeps the catalog in hand and logs
`agent_framework.model_routing.model_catalog_unreadable` once. The routing migration's
step `first_party_offerings` is applied before a host runs this simpl_core: without it
every DeepSeek, OpenAI and Cheaper Inference row the first-party sync writes is refused.

**Routing proposals.** Changes to *what* runs (the model an agent names, the lowest
precision it may run on, its time budget and output cap, its prompt) are prepared by the
nightly routing advisor and decided by a dashboard admin; the router's own choice of
provider and sub-provider is never a proposal.

```python
from simpl_core.llm_routing.advisor import run_routing_advisor

result = await run_routing_advisor(db)  # AdvisorResult(created, applied, verified,
                                        #   regressed, reverted, expired, reopened, moved)
```

`run_routing_advisor(db, *, now=None, settings=None)` (the `agent-framework` extra; simpl_flow's
`llm_routing_advisor_flow` runs it at 03:40 UTC) expires open proposals older than 30
days (a proposal sent back to open counts from `verification.stale.at`), writes each accepted ledger patch as a new **inactive** config version (version
`<active>-proposal-<id>`, parent the active one, `source = 'dashboard'`, `created_by` the
admin) or sends it back to `open` with `verification.stale.reason` when it no longer applies
(element or path gone, value changed, validation refused), moves each stranded ledger patch
(applied, its version never activated, its parent no longer the active version, so the
ledger refuses to activate it) onto the active version as `<active>-proposal-<id>`
(recorded in `verification.moves`: `at`, `from`, `to`) when the values it replaces are
unchanged there, else sends it back to `open` with `verification.stale` (`version` the one
it had written), verifies applied proposals for
14 days after they take effect (a clearance when accepted, a ledger version when
activated), reverts each one it judges `regressed` in the same transaction, and opens new
ones. A regressed ledger patch is reverted by activating its version's parent while its
version is the active one; a regressed clearance by restoring the clearance its patch's
`from` records (deleting it when there was none) while the clearance is the one the patch
wrote. The proposal stays `regressed` and keeps the revert in `verification.revert`
(`at`, `reverted`, then `config_key`, `activated`, `deactivated`, or `clearance`,
`replaced`, `restored`, or `reason` when nothing was reverted, the ledger's refusal
included when another version became active meanwhile), and each revert logs
`agent_framework.model_routing.routing_proposal_reverted` (warning) with `proposal_id`,
`kind`, `subject`, `reasons`, `revert` and `page`. New proposals each log
`agent_framework.model_routing.routing_proposal_created` (warning) with `proposal_id`,
`kind`, `subject`, `usd_per_day` and `page` (`/dashboard/monitoring/routing-proposals`).
`AdvisorSettings` holds its windows and thresholds. Kinds: `workload_misfit` (an open
misfit finding; ledger patch raising `timeout_seconds`, or `model.attempt_timeout_seconds`
when the policy sets it, to 1.5 times the larger of the budget and the p95 latency, and
`model.max_output_tokens` below 1.5 times the p90 output; a task outside the ledger),
`prompt_not_cacheable` (a family under 15% cache hits over 7 days and 200 answers whose
shared prefix is under half its input; task), `retired_model` (an active policy naming a
model no listing or catalog offers, or one answering under another name; ledger patch to
the new name when known, else a task), `certification_failed` (information),
`cheaper_model` and `lower_precision` (from `llm_routing.trial` when it exists), and
`add_model_to_tier` (a passed trial of an agent whose active policy names a bucket, its
reference a model of one of the bucket's tiers and its candidate another catalog model the
bucket does not name: a task to add the candidate to that tier in a new catalog version,
one per bucket, tier and model, subject `bucket`, `tier`, `model`, `tier_models`,
`agents`; such a trial proposes no `cheaper_model`).

`llm_routing.proposal`: `id`, `kind`, `subject` (agent, model, offering, config_key as they
apply), `finding_id`, `evidence` (figures and `query`, the SQL behind them), `impact`
(`usd_per_day`, `usd_per_day_low`, `usd_per_day_high`, `seconds_per_call`; positive is a
saving), `risk` (`low` | `medium` | `high`), `reversible`, `patch`, `status` (`open` →
`accepted` → `applied` → `verified` | `regressed`, or `dismissed` / `expired`),
`dedupe_key` (one open per key), `created_at`, `decided_by`, `decided_at`,
`dismissed_reason`, `applied_config_version`, `verify_until`, `verification` (`before`,
`after`, `guardrails`, `observed_usd_per_day`, `result`, `reasons`, `judged_at`). A patch is
`{"type": "ledger", "config_key", "changes": [{"element": "<kind>:<id>", "path": "a.b",
"value", "from"?}]}`, `{"type": "clearance", "agent", "model", "precision", "status":
"excluded" | "allowed", "trial_id"?, "from"?}` (accepting sets `from` to the clearance it
replaces: `status`, `trial_id`, `decided_by`, `decided_at`, or null),
`{"type": "task", "text"}` or `{"type": "none"}`;
`llm_routing.proposal_patch_problem(patch jsonb) → text` says what is wrong with one.
`llm_routing.clearance` (agent, model, precision, `status`, `trial_id`, `decided_by`,
`decided_at`) holds accepted clearances.

For the dashboard (security invoker, `service_role` only):

- `sales_view.llm_routing_proposals`: every proposal, open first, then by
  `usd_per_day` descending, then newest: `id`, `kind`, `status`, `open`, `subject`,
  `finding_id`, `evidence`, `impact`, `usd_per_day`, `risk`, `reversible`, `patch`,
  `patch_type`, `dedupe_key`, `created_at`, `decided_by`, `decided_at`,
  `dismissed_reason`, `applied_config_version`, `applied_version_activated_at`,
  `applied_version_active`, `verify_until`, `verification`.
- `sales_view.llm_routing_proposal_track_record`: per `kind`, `proposed`, `open`,
  `accepted` (accepted, applied, verified or regressed), `applied`, `verified`,
  `regressed`, `dismissed`, `expired`, `mean_predicted_usd_per_day` and
  `mean_observed_usd_per_day` (over the verified and regressed ones).
- `sales_view.accept_routing_proposal(p_id bigint, p_by text, p_patch jsonb DEFAULT NULL)
  → llm_routing.proposal`: accepts an open proposal; `p_patch` replaces the stored patch
  when it is well formed and of the same type (same `config_key`; a clearance keeps its
  agent, model and precision). A clearance is written at once (`applied`), a task or
  information patch is `applied`, a ledger patch `accepted` for the advisor.
- `sales_view.dismiss_routing_proposal(p_id bigint, p_reason text, p_by text) →
  llm_routing.proposal`: dismisses an open or accepted proposal, or an applied ledger patch
  whose version was never activated; the reason is required.

Both functions raise with a message prefix: `proposal_not_found`, `proposal_not_open`,
`invalid_patch`, `by_required`, `reason_required`. `ProposalRepository` wraps them and
reads the views. The routing migration's step `routing_proposals` is applied before the
advisor runs or the Proposals page is deployed.

**Clearance trials.** Every precision from the catalog's `default_min` (fp4) up is
routable, `unknown` and precisions the ladder does not name included; a precision is left
out for an agent when the model catalog's `quantization.never` lists it, an accepted
clearance excludes it there, or it is on a rung below `default_min`'s and no clearance
allows it there. The routing table hands simpl_ia all of it
(`RoutingTable.precision_policy(agent, model) → PrecisionPolicy(never, excluded, allowed,
ladder, default_min, unknown) | None`, `None` when it excludes nothing; `model` the
canonical name on a canonical target, else the provider's name, as clearances are keyed),
every running canary (`RoutingTable.canary(agent, model) → CanaryPlan(trial_id,
upstream, provider, share) | None`) and whether it has loaded the catalog
(`RoutingTable.catalog_loaded()`: true after a load that read an active catalog passing its
gate, or found none; a load that cannot read the offerings or the catalog leaves it false,
so canonical targets refuse unsent).
The trials that decide what to exclude are `simpl_core.llm_routing.trials` (the
`agent-framework` extra):

```python
from simpl_core.llm_routing.trials import queue_replay_trial, run_due_trials

result = await run_due_trials(db, max_trials=1)   # TrialPassResult(pruned_cases, reopened, queued,
                                                  #   replays, completed, stopped, cancelled,
                                                  #   errors, canaries_checked, canaries_ended,
                                                  #   canaries_started, cost_usd,
                                                  #   budget_exhausted)
trial_id = await queue_replay_trial(db, agent="worker", provider="openrouter",
                                    model="acme/lite-1", upstream="lite/fp8")
```

`run_due_trials(db, *, max_trials, settings=None, llm_client_resolver=None, clock=...)`
(simpl_flow's `llm_routing_trials_flow`, daily, shipped off) **refuses every pass**: it
raises `TrialRulesNotReady` before reading or writing anything, since the replay and canary
decision rules cannot tell a better candidate from noise; nothing is run, spent or written.
`queue_replay_trial` still queues a replay, which waits queued. The pass it refuses deletes
the cases of trials that ended 90 days ago, reads every running canary, queues the
re-trials provider-side changes call for, queues the replays due, runs up to `max_trials`
replays oldest first, and starts the canaries of replays that passed. Nothing
that costs money runs while the month's trial budget is 0 (the default). A subject is an
`llm_agent` of an active pipeline version on a typed policy without tools (its own, its
memory's or sandbox's: a replay would run them again); its primary target is the model
tried. It is eligible with 200 good runs (producer runs that answered, outside trials and
certification) in 14 days at one precision, its current one; its reference is the offering
of the target that answered most of them. A replay is due for each other precision of the
target below the reference's rung of the catalog's ladder, or off it, the cheapest offering
per precision, unless the catalog never allows it, a clearance excludes it for the agent,
or it was tried in the last 30 days. A zero-data-retention agent (its primary sets
`require_zdr: true`, or `provider_routing.zdr: true` on OpenRouter) replays only on ZDR
offerings, its judge calls too, every call pinning `zdr: true` in its OpenRouter routing
(`replay_policy(..., zdr=True)`); inputs are not redacted.

A replay re-runs the newest 300 good runs of the agent's latest prompt version (at least
200, or it is cancelled) through `execute_agent(..., model_policy=)` with the definition's
policy pinned to the reference twice (R1, R2) and to the candidate once (C) (reasoning,
output mode, attempt budget, timeouts, output cap and temperature kept; `replay_policy`),
each call writing its `agent_runs` row under node run `llm_routing_trial` (correlation
`llm_routing_trial:<id>`). Each output field is compared with R1 by its schema type: enums,
booleans, ids (`id`, `*_id`) and free mappings exactly, numbers within 5%, lists by set F1
(at least 0.8), other strings as free text, which the judge decides when they differ beyond
case and whitespace (agent `llm_routing_trial_judge` of `agent_policy.yaml` beside the
package, config key `llm_routing_trials_root`, pinned to the cheapest listed offering of its
model). A case whose R1 failed, or whose other runs got no answer (timeout, server), is
kept and not compared. The excess disagreement is disagree(C, R1) − disagree(R2, R1); its
bound is the exact Clopper–Pearson one-sided 95% upper bound of the cases only C disagreed
on, less the share only R2 disagreed on. A replay passes when the bound is at most 2
points, no error channel (`wrong_tool`, `bad_arguments`, `invalid_output`, from the retry
prompts) rises more than 1 point over the reference's and the candidate has no new schema
failure; it is `inconclusive` under 200 compared cases. Each case is stored with its cost
and spent from the month's budget in one transaction; the replay stops (`stopped`) before
a case the month's budget cannot cover and resumes in a later pass with room. A trial
whose agent's or judge's ledger version, or a judge offering, is missing keeps its status
with `result.blocked`, counted in `errors`, before any call.

A replay that passed on the same target, for an agent whose primary target routes
internally (canonical, or `routing: internal`), queues a canary: while `running` it is
published to simpl_ia at a 10% share. Each pass reads the calls drawn into it (route log
`canary` on `llm_routing.routing_decision`, its attempts, audit rows and validation runs),
spends the chosen calls' cost (receipts, else the agent's token mix at the candidate's
price) and ends it `fail` on a guardrail breach (an always-valid mSPRT on the validator
failure rate, invalid answers and fix loops, each 2 points, and the latency, 1.25 times),
`pass` or `fail` after 3 days and 300 chosen and control calls on the Newcombe upper bound
of the bad-outcome rate's rise (no answer, or a failed validation) against 1.5 points,
`inconclusive` after 14 days; it is `stopped` (unpublished) while the month's budget is
spent. `TrialSettings` holds every threshold. The routing advisor turns finished trials into
proposals: a failed one excludes that precision for the agent (`lower_precision`), a
passed one of a cheaper model names it (`cheaper_model`); a trial's offerings carry
`canonical` on a canonical target, which the advisor reads as the model. `queue_replay_trial`
queues a replay of any listed offering against an eligible agent's reference (`LookupError`
when the agent is not a subject or not eligible or the offering not listed, `ValueError`
for a non-ZDR candidate of a ZDR agent; `None` when the same replay is open).

`llm_routing.trial`: `id`, `kind` (`replay` | `canary`), `agent`, `model`, `reference` /
`candidate` (`provider`, `model`, `upstream`, `precision`, `zdr`, `canonical`?), `status`
(`queued` | `running` | `stopped` | `completed` | `cancelled` | `error`), `settings`,
`parent_id` (the replay a canary follows), `created_at`, `started_at`, `updated_at`,
`ended_at`, `cost_usd`, `result` (`verdict` `pass` | `fail` | `inconclusive` on completion,
`reasons` and the figures). `llm_routing.trial_case`: `trial_id`, `case_no`,
`agent_run_id`, `fields`, `agreement`, `cost_usd`, `compared_at`. `llm_routing.trial_budget`
(`month`, `budget_usd`, `spent_usd`) and `llm_routing.trial_setting` (`monthly_budget_usd`,
`updated_by`, `updated_at`; one row, 0 by default); `llm_routing.trial_budget_for(at)`,
`spend_trial_budget(amount, at)` and `set_trial_budget(amount, by, at)`. For the dashboard
(security invoker, `service_role` only):

- `sales_view.llm_routing_trial_budget`: one row, `monthly_budget_usd`, `updated_by`,
  `updated_at`, the current `month` (UTC) with its `budget_usd`, `spent_usd` and
  `remaining_usd`.
- `sales_view.set_llm_routing_trial_budget(p_budget_usd numeric, p_by text) →
  llm_routing.trial_setting`: sets the monthly budget and the current month's; raises
  `by_required` or `invalid_budget` (null or negative).

**Re-trials after a provider-side change.** When the candidate or reference offering of
the latest passed trial of an agent, model and candidate changes its precision
(`quantization`) or build (`context_length`, `supported_parameters`) in
`llm_routing.offering_listing_change`, or gets a `drift` finding, after that trial ended,
the pass queues one fresh replay of the same agent, model and candidate through
`queue_replay_trial`, its settings carrying `trigger: provider_change`, the `change`
(`key` `listing:<id>` or `drift:<id>`, `kind`, offering, `at`, `details`) and `retrial_of`.
A change is handled once (a trial naming its key, or one of the same subject created after
it, stands for it). Every clearance the passed trial decided gets `evidence.reconfirm`
(`retrial_id`, `change`, `marked_at`); its status is left as decided. A re-trial
`queue_replay_trial` refuses is logged (`llm_routing.trials.retrial_skipped`) and tried
again next pass. `llm_routing.clearance` gains `evidence` (JSON object, `{}` by default).

`TrialRepository` reads and writes the tables and calls the functions. Publishing the
judge is a ledger version: `simpl_core af_config_migrate --yaml
src/simpl_core/llm_routing/trials/agent_policy.yaml --dest migrations/ --config-key
llm_routing_trials_root --version 1 --parent 0 --prefix <migration number> --summary "..."
--activate`, then a person applies the seed. The routing migration's step
`clearance_trials` is applied before a host runs this simpl_core: every routing table
load reads `llm_routing.trial`, and fails without it.

Credentials are never authored in a policy document — the document names a
provider, the host binds it. `Loader.validate_boot()` checks every typed policy
against the resolver it will run on — the host's, or `default_llm_client_resolver()`
when the host passes none — and fails if a binding is missing.

**New versions declare typed policies only.** A new config version whose
`llm_agent` declares `model` as a bare string is refused where versions enter the
ledger: `af_config_migrate` and `ConfigVersionRepository.insert_version` both call
`validate_new_version(content, catalog_names=...)` and raise `BareModelNameError` (a
`ConfigAuthoringError` and a `ValueError`), or `UnknownCanonicalModelError` for a
canonical model `catalog_names` does not list. A bare name leaves the provider to the
host, which guessed it from the name. Versions already stored are never re-checked:
a bare-name row still projects, loads and runs on the shared `llm_client`, so
nothing running today changes. A host that publishes versions should map
`BareModelNameError` to a 4xx.

A typed policy always resolves through `resolve_agent_llm_client`. When the host
passes no `llm_client_resolver`, `default_llm_client_resolver()` binds
`<PROVIDER>_API_KEY` and `<PROVIDER>_BASE_URL` from the environment for `OPENAI`,
`DEEPSEEK`, `OPENROUTER` and `CHEAPER_INFERENCE`, so a typed row works on a host
that predates typed policies. Sales Domain capability workers whose row is typed are
built with `client.make_agent(...)` from that client rather than from the host's
`model_factory` (which still serves bare-name rows);
`SalesDomainComposition(llm_client_resolver=...)` and
`build_node_coordinator(llm_client_resolver=...)` take a host resolver when there is
one. Their `agent_runs` rows name the target that answered — model, provider,
reasoning, fallback index — and the chain's attempts, as `LLMAgent`'s do.
`LLMAgent.last_api` is the answering target's API, and `execute_agent` puts it
beside `provider` in the execution's `audit` payload (`"api": "decisions"` for a
decision model's answer), so a caller can tell the two apart without reading model
names. `execute_agent(..., model_policy=)` and `execute_agent_stream(..., model_policy=)`
run an `llm_agent` on another `ModelPolicySchema` than its row's (any other kind raises
`ValueError`); the call is audited and accounted as the row's agent.
`answered_target(client, run_result, agent_id=...)` names the target that answered
a run built outside `LLMAgent`.

A `self_hosted` target is a server the caller runs (llama.cpp, vLLM): it names its
`base_url` (required there, refused on any other provider), needs no binding, and
writes no cost receipt. Any non-decision target may add `extra_body`, request fields
merged into every call it sends (a call's own fields win).

An `openai` target may set `prompt_cache: explicit`: on gpt-5.6 and later the call caches
its system prompt and nothing after it, so the rest of each request is billed as uncached
input instead of being written to the cache (simpl_ia's `PromptCacheMode`); on an older
OpenAI model it changes nothing. It is refused on any other provider, and on a policy it
covers the primary only. Leave it off an agent that calls tools: explicit mode stops
OpenAI caching the growing tool-call history.

An `llm_agent` may set `request_layout`, the messages a call sends in order, so content
that repeats across calls can sit early in the request and be cached on its own:

```yaml
request_layout:
  - {prompt: full}                    # system message: the whole prompt
  - {inputs: [context.customer_prompts_source, context.icp_prompts_source], cache: true}
  - {rest: true}                      # final user message: every input not sent yet
```

A `prompt` block sends `full`, or `core` and `fragments` as two system messages. An
`inputs` block sends one user message holding the values at its dotted paths, each at
its path; `cache: true` ends it with a cache breakpoint (at most three). The layout
opens with the core, places the prompt once, ends with `rest`, and no path repeats or
contains another. A block with nothing to send sends no message. Without a layout the
whole prompt is the system message and every input one user message, which is also what
`[{prompt: full}, {rest: true}]` sends. Only `rest` is subject to the client's
`AI_MAX_USER_PROMPT_CHARS` cap and its context-window truncation.

**`lm_model`: a self-hosted model asked with its family's own request.** For a model
that takes its task as request fields rather than a prompt (NuExtract reads its
template from `chat_template_kwargs`), an `lm_model` row names the request format and
the model; a call's inputs become that format's one fixed request. Called from code
nodes only, with `execute_agent`; never a pipeline node, validator, fixer or fallback
(`Loader.validate_boot` refuses that).

```yaml
- kind: agent_definition
  id: graphia_ner
  version: 1
  agent_kind: lm_model
  request_format: nuextract          # required
  max_tokens: 1024                   # required; a call may pass its own
  model:                             # every target self_hosted; fallbacks allowed
    name: nuextract3
    provider: self_hosted
    base_url: http://127.0.0.1:8092/v1
    http_timeout_seconds: 1800       # the bound on each request
  audit_sample_rate: 0.01
```

An `lm_model` row refuses `prompt_id`, `output_schema`, `output_constraints`, tools,
`memory`, `timeout_seconds` (each request's bound is the target's
`http_timeout_seconds`), `stream_timeout_seconds` and the node-only fields
(`implementation_class`, `default_context_projection_id`, `default_fix_strategy`,
`cache_policy`, `global_validators_exclude`, `on_failure`) and `model.temperature`
(its request format sets it); its targets refuse `reasoning`, `max_invalid_responses`,
`attempt_timeout_seconds` and `max_output_tokens`.

```python
from simpl_core.agent_framework import execute_agent

execution = await execute_agent(
    loader=loader, agent_id="graphia_ner", role="producer",
    inputs={"text": document, "template": template_json_text, "instructions": text},
)
if execution.lm_failure is None:
    found = execution.output          # the checked JSON object
else:
    kind = execution.lm_failure.kind  # unreachable | no_answer | not_json | cut | off_template
```

`nuextract` sends the text as the only (user) message, `max_tokens` (the call's, else
the row's), `temperature: 0`, and `chat_template_kwargs` with `template`,
`instructions` and `enable_thinking: false`; no `response_format`. The model's text
never reaches the caller: `output` is the JSON object (a Markdown fence around it
accepted; every key a template key, missing keys allowed, so `{}` is valid; a key the
model names more than once with lists has those lists joined in answer order, any
other repeat keeps its last value), or
`lm_failure` (`LMModelFailure`) says why there is none: `unreachable` (no attempt
reached a server: it is down, stop), `no_answer` (a server was reached and no target
answered: an error status, a timeout, a body with no answer), `cut` (`finish_reason`
`length`, checked before reading),
`not_json` (an empty answer included), `off_template` (`extra_keys`). Every failure
carries the call's attempt trail (`attempts`, simpl_ia's, each with its `failure`),
also written to the audit row. Inputs the
format does not take raise `ValueError` before any request. `read_answer(text,
answer_keys=...)` is the same check, for an answer kept from an earlier call.

Each call writes its own `agent_runs` row, in `execute_agent` on the Loader's
database: a failed call always; an answered one when `audit_sample_rate` draws it in
(once per call, keyed on its id), also inside a pipeline with `audit: false`. A row
with no rate follows the run, as an `llm_agent` does: every call in an audited run,
failures only in one that opted out; `audit: false` on the row, failures only. A
cancelled call writes no row, since a cancellation is not an error; its attempt trail
(the request in flight closed as `cancelled`) is logged. No token receipt. Tests pass
`completion_client_for=lambda policy: CompletionClient(policy, retry_pause_seconds=0,
transport=httpx.MockTransport(handler))`.

**A producer's output keeps the confidence its model answered with.** A decision
model (`api: decisions`, such as TypeSafe's Jev) answers each field of the output
type with a probability, which pydantic-ai puts in the response's
`provider_details`. `LLMAgent` adds it to the producer's output as `_confidence`
(`MODEL_CONFIDENCE_KEY`): `fields`, each field's confidence from 0 (undecided) to 1,
and `probabilities`, each pick-one field's full distribution. A language model
reports none, so an answer from a fallback behind a decision model has no
`_confidence`. The key is on the `agent_runs` output and
on the run surface as `<node>._confidence`. An edge routes on it directly with a
dotted key — `{key: <node>._confidence.probabilities.seller.owner, op: gt, value: 0.1}`,
or `{key: <node>._confidence, op: is_null}` for a fallback's answer — and a
`producer_output` path read from an `llm_agent` may start with `_confidence`.

**A `when` key may be a dotted path into nested values.** A key that is itself a
resolved input (or run-surface key) is read directly, and always wins. Any other
key is read through its longest dotted prefix that is one, walking the remaining
segments into nested dicts; a missing segment, or a segment applied to a
non-dict, reads as null, so `is_null` matches and every numeric operator does
not. `gte_key` and `not_member` resolve the key they name the same way. `lt` and
`gt` are the strict numeric comparisons beside `lte` and `gte`, with the same
contract: a missing or non-numeric operand on either side never matches.
`output_constraints` accept a dotted key that walks through declared `object`
fields, or into a `dict` field.

**A failed producer call says what kind of failure it was.**
`AgentExecution.require_output()` raises `ProducerCallFailed`, a `RuntimeError`
with the message it always had (so `except RuntimeError` and rescue chains keyed
on the text are unaffected), carrying `agent_id`, `failure`, the call's live
attempt trail and `kind`, from `classify_producer_failure`: `thread` (every
answer was unusable: invalid, or cut off at its target's output cap), `rejected` (the provider refused the request: 400, 413,
422), `infrastructure` (5xx, 429, 408, 401, 403, 404, a transport error, a
deadline, a cancelled attempt, the call ceiling, a reply the client could not read
as a completion: `UnexpectedModelBehavior`) or `unclassified` (attempts
that disagree, an empty trail, anything unknown). It is code, not a model: each
attempt is read by its recorded outcome, its exact error class and, for an HTTP
error, the `status_code: NNN,` prefix of its message, and the call is a kind
only when every attempt is. A caller that retries work decides what to charge
from it, so an outage does not use up retries meant for bad input. It is raised
from `AgentExecution.error`, the exception the call already reported: for a call no
model in the chain answered, a `ModelChainFailed` whose message lists every model
with its HTTP timeout, its attempt deadline ("model budget") and what it did, a model
whose answer was cut off at its cap included. An exception a caller
raises from it therefore belongs to that alert, and `already_reported()` says so.

Tests use `FixedClientResolver` or `OfflineModelResolver` from
`simpl_core.agent_framework.testing`. `OfflineModelResolver(model_for=...)` builds
each agent on the real policy client (`OfflineModelClient`), with every chain member
answering offline under its target's profile and provider name; `model_for(definition,
target)` scripts a member, e.g. a primary that raises `ModelAPIError`. Both refuse a
definition without a typed policy.

Domain behaviour is supplied two ways: **registries** keyed by a string in a policy
row (`PrimitiveRegistry`, `ResolverRegistry`, `RouteHandlerRegistry`, `ToolRegistry`,
`MemoryProviderRegistry`, `AgentKindRegistry`), filled at bootstrap import; and
**factory callbacks** on `NodeCoordinator` keyed by the live node. Every callback
but `resolver_context_for` is optional, and a node needing an absent one fails
closed rather than guessing.

Rules that bite consumers:

- Manifest membership, **not** `is_active`, is what a ledger-resolved run reads.
  Projected component rows are inserted inactive; re-filtering loader output on
  `is_active` empties the graph.
- A route handler reads only what it declares in `requires`. Dispatch it with
  `await RouteHandlerRegistry.context_for(route, resolver_context)`, which resolves
  those inputs into `ctx.resolved` under the key a routing rule's `when` uses (the
  path's leaf). A `RouteHandlerContext` built by hand carries none of them, and the
  routing engine does not pass the matched rule's inputs on. A declared input that
  cannot be resolved raises `RouteHandlerInputError` and the handler does not run,
  so the resolver context must hold what the routing node's context held.
- A policy change needs a **new** ledger version. Editing a projected version's
  content in place and re-running a seed migration are both silent no-ops.
- Routing must be total: every routing position needs an unconditional catch-all,
  or startup fails.
- A pipeline is acyclic except through a bounded back-edge:
  `{from: b, to: a, when: ..., iterate: true, max_iterations: N}`.
  - One walk takes it at most N times, then the source node's catch-all.
  - It needs a `when` (it cannot be the catch-all) and a node to go back to (not
    `__end__`).
  - A back-edge into the entry node needs the pipeline's explicit `entry`.
  - A revisited node records one node run per visit, and its output replaces the
    previous one on the surface.
- A producer routes on its verdict's `final_output`: the answer its fix loop kept, or
  the best candidate an exhausted cap surfaces, never a first answer a validator
  rejected. The engine keeps the caller's `ResolverContext.producer_output` on that
  same output.

### Turning the audit off

`audit: false` on a `pipeline` or an `agent_definition` stops the audit rows for
work nobody reads back, such as a context stage that loads the same tables on every
walk. It is declared in the ledger like any other field, so it takes a new version.

- A pipeline that opts out writes no `node_runs`, `agent_runs`, `tool_call_runs`,
  `validation_runs`, `memory_runs` or `pipeline_run_steps` for anything it runs.
  The subgraph or fan-out node that runs it from an audited parent keeps its own
  node run.
- An agent that opts out writes nothing under the node that runs it. That node's
  own run stays, because it is the pipeline's record. Called from another node (a
  fan-out branch, a join, a validator), it loses its `agent_runs` row and nothing
  else.
- Nothing inside an unaudited run can turn audit back on.
- `audit_sample_rate` (`0` to `1`, not beside `audit: false`) audits that share of
  the runs; the rest behave as `audit: false`. Drawn once per run (once per call for
  an `lm_model` row, whose drawn rows are written whatever the run declared).
- Errors are always recorded: a node run ending on an unexpected error, or a failed
  agent run, is written with the unrecorded node runs above it. Nothing else of the
  unaudited run is. Expected interruptions are not errors.
- The node still runs, routes, alerts and uses the cache as before. Run ids are still
  minted, so alerts and cost records can name a node run that has no row.

A consumer that writes its own rows against a node run (say, a column pointing at
`node_runs`) checks `simpl_core.agent_framework.audit_enabled()` first. The
framework's own writers already do. To record an error under an unaudited run,
call `node_run.record_node_run_late(handle)` first and write only if it returns
`True`.

Full guide: `docs/agent_framework/11-operating-it.md#turning-the-audit-off`.

### Cost per pipeline run

**Every cost recorded inside a node run names the run.** `open_node_run` binds
simpl_tracker's `LLMAccountingScope(node_run_id=, pipeline_run_id=)` for the node's
duration, so a model receipt, a scraping or data-provider charge, a speech or Recall
receipt and a `track_cost` total recorded inside the node carry both ids (simpl_tracker's
recorders read them from the scope from simpl-tracker 0.8.32; an id the caller passes
wins). A nested node run's scope replaces `node_run_id`; `LLMAgent`'s own scope (process
name, agent run id, attribution) opens inside it. A host whose tracker has no
`llm_accounting` module runs the node unscoped and logs
`agent_framework.llm_agent.accounting_unavailable` once.

**`agent_framework.pipeline_run_costs`** keeps one row per pipeline run and pipeline:
a sub-pipeline shares its root's `pipeline_run_id`, so the key is
`(pipeline_run_id, pipeline_id)`. Columns: `is_root`, `parent_pipeline_id` (the pipeline
of the node run that entered it; NULL for a root), `started_at` / `ended_at` (earliest
start, latest end of its node runs), `status` (`failed` when one of its node runs is
`failed`, `failed_closed` or `error`; `running` when one still runs; else `succeeded`),
`end_node_id` (the node of its node run that ended last), `own_cost_usd`,
`total_cost_usd` (own plus every pipeline below it), `unpriced_entries` /
`total_unpriced_entries` (entries without a known amount), `cost_by_service` (own, by
cost service name), `model_calls` (own `ai_llm` entries), `computed_at`, and
`estimated_cost_usd` / `total_estimated_cost_usd` (own, and own plus every pipeline below
it: the failure-cost estimates of `cost_tracking.cost_estimate` that
`cost_tracking.estimate_counts(receipt_id)` admits, that is no late bill replaced them and
their provider's bill for that UTC day is not loaded; 0 without any). Estimates are never
part of `own_cost_usd` or `total_cost_usd`.

- An entry belongs to the run its `service_cost_entry.pipeline_run_id` names, and to the
  pipeline of its node run; an entry naming the run but no node run of it (recorded
  between nodes, or under a node run that has no row) counts in the run's root.
- A pipeline run whose node runs were never written (`audit: false` on the root, or a
  sampled-out run) has no row; an unaudited sub-pipeline's costs land in its root.
- An estimate counts like an entry: in the run its `pipeline_run_id` names, under the
  pipeline of its `node_run_id`, else under the run's root
  (`agent_framework.pipeline_run_estimates(runs)`). Without `cost_tracking.cost_estimate`
  the estimated amounts are 0.
- `agent_framework.pipeline_node_costs` (`pipeline_run_id`, `pipeline_id`, `node_id`
  (NULL: entries without a node run), `started_at` (the pipeline row's), `cost_usd`,
  `entries`): each node's known amounts and entries, written with its run's rows.
- `agent_framework.refresh_pipeline_run_costs_for(runs)` recomputes the given runs,
  replacing all their rows in both tables, and returns the `pipeline_run_costs` rows
  written; `agent_framework.refresh_pipeline_run_costs(p_since)` runs it over every run
  with a node run started at or after `p_since`, and
  `agent_framework.refresh_changed_pipeline_run_costs()` over the runs that changed since
  its last run: a node run started or a cost entry written from 5 minutes before the time
  `pipeline_run_costs_watermark` stores (the last day on a first run), and the runs of the
  last day still running; it then stores the time it read up to. Each of these is one
  statement, so a window it cannot finish within the statement timeout writes nothing.
  `CALL agent_framework.refresh_pipeline_run_costs_in_batches(p_slice, p_budget)` (15
  minutes and 90 seconds by default; outside a transaction block) does the changed runs'
  work one slice at a time, each slice committed with the watermark moved to its end, and
  stops starting slices once the time spent plus its longest slice passes `p_budget`; the
  next call goes on from the watermark. Once the watermark reaches now, it works through
  the pending recheck the same way: `agent_framework.queue_pipeline_run_costs_recheck(p_since)`
  queues the runs started from `p_since` to now (merged with a pending one) in
  `pipeline_run_costs_recheck`. It returns `batches`, `refreshed` (rows written),
  `refreshed_through` and `recheck_from` (NULL with no recheck pending). A late bill changes
  an entry without writing one, so the recheck picks it up. With `pg_cron` and the
  analytics switch on, `pipeline_run_costs_recent` calls the procedure every 15 minutes and
  `pipeline_run_costs_nightly` queues a recheck of the last 3 days at 02:30 UTC; without
  it, a host schedules the same calls. A first fill after the migration and
  simpl_tracker's run-id backfill: `SELECT agent_framework.refresh_pipeline_run_costs(now()
  - interval '7 days')`. `estimated_costs_refresh` (04:45 UTC daily) runs it over the last
  21 days, the window failure-cost estimates are written over, together with the response
  time refresh, while failure estimates are in use (the switch on, or an estimate of the
  last 21 days). `pipeline_run_costs_retention` (03:55 UTC, always) calls
  `agent_framework.prune_pipeline_run_costs()`, which deletes the rows of pipelines started
  more than 90 days ago, with their node rows, in batches.

Reads, each over the runs started in `[p_from, p_to]` (UTC days), as `sales_view`
functions of the same name and arguments (security invoker, execute for `service_role`):

| Function | Rows |
|---|---|
| `pipeline_cost_summary(p_from date, p_to date, p_include_estimates boolean DEFAULT false)` | per `pipeline_id`: `runs`, `is_root` (a root in one of them), `own_avg_usd`, `own_p50_usd`, `own_p95_usd`, `total_avg_usd`, `total_p50_usd`, `total_p95_usd`, `unpriced_entries` (own), `end_nodes` (jsonb, end node → runs), `own_estimated_usd`, `total_estimated_usd` (average estimates per run); roots first |
| `pipeline_cost_trend(p_pipeline_id text, p_from date, p_to date, p_include_estimates boolean DEFAULT false)` | per UTC `day` with runs: `runs`, the same six averages and percentiles, `own_estimated_usd`, `total_estimated_usd` |
| `pipeline_cost_runs(p_pipeline_id text, p_from date, p_to date, p_limit int DEFAULT 50, p_before timestamptz DEFAULT NULL, p_include_estimates boolean DEFAULT false)` | the pipeline's `pipeline_run_costs` rows, newest first, at most `p_limit` (1 to 1000), started before `p_before` (pass the last row's `started_at` for the next page); the stored `estimated_cost_usd` / `total_estimated_cost_usd` come last as `own_estimated_usd` / `total_estimated_usd` |
| `pipeline_run_cost_detail(p_pipeline_run_id text)` | `(kind, key, pipeline_id, cost_usd, entries)` for kinds `node`, `agent` (the agent run's `agent_id`, else the entry's `process_name`), `model` (an `ai_llm` entry's requested model, else its `model_or_sku`) and `service`; a NULL key is an entry with no node run, agent run or model. Each kind's rows add up to the run's `total_cost_usd` |
| `cost_by_pipeline_node(p_from date, p_to date)` | `(pipeline_id, node_id, cost_usd, entries, runs)` from the stored `pipeline_node_costs`; `node_id` NULL for entries with no node run |

Costs are the entries' known amounts (`cost_usd_exact`, else `cost_usd`); an entry
without one adds nothing to a cost and is counted in `entries` and the unpriced counts.
Entries of every environment count. With `p_include_estimates` true, the own and total
amounts, averages and percentiles of the first three reads add each run's estimates; the
estimated columns are there whatever the flag. The routing migration's step
`pipeline_run_costs` reads `service_cost_entry.pipeline_run_id` and `node_run_id`, which
simpl_tracker's routing cost tracking migration adds; the step before it,
`node_runs_pipeline_started_at_index`, makes sure of the `node_runs` index its refresh
reads, `idx_node_runs_pipeline_started_at` (`pipeline_id, started_at DESC`, production's
own), building it `CONCURRENTLY` only where it is missing; the rollback keeps it. The
refreshes find their runs pipeline by pipeline through it
(`pipeline_runs_started_since(p_since)`), so a node run without a `pipeline_id` brings
no run in. Its step `estimated_run_costs` adds the estimates to the pipeline costs,
the call log and the
Activity rollup (the estimate readers find nothing while `cost_tracking.cost_estimate` or
`cost_tracking.estimate_counts` is missing), and a caller passing `p_include_estimates`
needs it.

### LLM call log and model exchange

**Every LLM run's exchange is stored once, beside its `agent_runs` row.** `LLMAgent.check`
and `stream` capture the call's pydantic-ai messages (the answered run's whole history,
else what was exchanged before it failed) into a `ModelExchange` on `last_exchange`, and
`audit_snapshot()` and `execute_agent`'s `audit["exchange"]` carry it. Each writer stores
it in the session of the run's row: `DbAgentRunRecorder` (from a snapshot), the
validate/fix engine, `record_lm_model_run` and the experiment rounds. A consumer that
builds its own `ProducerAuditSnapshot` from an `LLMAgent` passes
`exchange=agent.last_exchange` (or uses `agent.audit_snapshot(inputs)`); without it the
row has no exchange. An `lm_model` call captures none: its request is built from its
inputs and its answer is its output.

- `agent_framework.agent_run_messages` (one row per run, deleted with it, kept 30 days):
  `messages`, `reasoning_chars` (thinking characters stored), `turns` (model responses),
  `tool_schema_ids`: the `agent_framework.tool_schemas` rows (`tool_name`, `schema` =
  `{name, description, parameters_json_schema[, strict]}`, unique `schema_sha256` of its
  canonical JSON) of the function tools offered, in order (the output tool is not stored:
  it is the agent definition's `output_schema`).
  A message is `{"kind": "request" | "response", "role": "user" | "assistant", "parts"}`
  (a request adds `instructions` when sent, a response `model_name`, `provider_name`,
  `finish_reason`, `state` when known). A part has `part_kind` (pydantic-ai's), `role`
  (`system`, `user`, `tool`, `assistant`), `tool_name` / `tool_call_id` where it has
  them, and its content (`content`, `args` for a tool call) or a reference:
  `{"ref": "prompt"}` (the system prompt, when the `agent_runs` row names it: `prompt_id`
  at `prompt_version`, or a `prompt_composition` pointer),
  `{"ref": "inputs"}` (the user prompt, when it is the row's `inputs` rendered; under a
  `request_layout`, `{"ref": "inputs", "paths": [...]}` for a block's user message, the
  inputs at those dotted paths, and `{"ref": "inputs", "without": [...]}` for the last
  user message, the inputs without the paths the blocks named, each with the block's
  `"cache_point"` when it ended in one),
  `{"ref": "tool_call", "sequence_number": n}` (a call the tool recorder kept, and its
  return when the row holds the same value), `{"ref": "output"}` (the final answer, when
  the row's `output` holds it: the same JSON, or with the keys the framework adds,
  `_confidence`, `_completion`, the thinking under `reasoning`, or a verdict of it).
  Everything else is inline (assistant text, thinking, tool calls no tool ran, rejected
  answers, retry prompts, a request layout's system prompt parts), masked with
  simpl_tracker's `mask_secrets` (text that is JSON is masked as its structure).
- `agent_runs` has no column of the exchange: a run is written whether or not these
  tables exist. A failure storing any of them logs `agent_framework.model_exchange.*`
  (or `agent_framework.llm_agent.exchange_capture_failed`) and leaves the row out (the
  schemas: `tool_schema_ids` empty); the run and its `agent_runs` row are never
  affected. Without a `prompt_version` or a composition, the system prompt stays inline
  in the messages. The run's prompt is the ledger row its `agent_runs` row names, hashed
  by the framework's own hashes: the definition's `body_sha256`, or the composition
  pointer's; the run path never adds a `prompt_definitions` row.
- `PromptRegistry.record_execution(executable, context=, agent_run_id=)` marks each
  executed version's change event after the model answered and never raises: a version
  with no change event, or a failed write, logs
  `agent_framework.prompt_registry.execution_not_recorded` with `prompt_name`,
  `prompt_version` and `agent_run_id`.

The Logs page reads five `sales_view` functions (wrappers of the `agent_framework` ones,
security invoker, execute for `service_role`):

| Function | Returns |
|---|---|
| `llm_call_log(p_from timestamptz, p_to timestamptz, p_filters jsonb DEFAULT '{}', p_limit int DEFAULT 50, p_before timestamptz DEFAULT NULL, p_before_id uuid DEFAULT NULL)` | LLM runs (`model` set) created in `[p_from, p_to)` (a NULL bound is open), newest first, at most `p_limit` (1 to 1000), and with `p_before` only those with `(created_at, id) < (p_before, p_before_id)` (pass the last row's `created_at` and `agent_run_id` for the next page). `p_filters` narrows on `pipeline_id`, `node_id`, `agent_id`, `model`, `provider`, `outcome`: each a string (an exact match) or `{"include": [...], "exclude": [...]}`, both lists of strings and optional, an empty one setting no condition (a value in `include` and in none of `exclude`; a NULL value is dropped by an `include` and kept by an `exclude`); another key or shape raises `invalid_parameter_value`. Columns: `agent_run_id uuid`, `created_at timestamptz`, `pipeline_id text`, `pipeline_run_id text`, `node_id text` (of the run's node run), `node_run_id uuid`, `agent_id text`, `agent_version text`, `model text`, `provider text`, `outcome text` (`answered` with an output, else `failed`), `latency_ms int`, `attempts int` (its `llm_routing.attempt` rows, else the length of its `attempts` trail), `cost_usd numeric`, `input_tokens bigint`, `output_tokens bigint` (its receipts by `agent_run_id`, a duplicate left out, cost from their cost entries; NULL when none), `has_messages boolean`, `estimated_cost_usd numeric` (the run's failure-cost estimates by `cost_estimate.agent_run_id` that `estimate_counts` admits, never part of `cost_usd`; NULL when none) |
| `llm_call_histogram(p_from timestamptz, p_to timestamptz, p_bucket_seconds int, p_filters jsonb DEFAULT '{}')` | The rows `llm_call_log` lists for the bounds and filters, without paging, counted by `bucket_start = date_bin(p_bucket_seconds, created_at, p_from)`: each non-empty bucket in order with `calls bigint`, `failed bigint` and the sums of `llm_call_log`'s `cost_usd numeric`, `input_tokens bigint`, `output_tokens bigint` (0 when no call of the bucket has one; NULL in every bucket when the bounds and filters hold more than 2,000 calls, whose receipts it does not read). Raises `invalid_parameter_value` for `p_bucket_seconds` under 60, a NULL or infinite bound, more than 500 buckets, or filters `llm_call_log` refuses |
| `llm_call_filter_values(p_key text, p_from timestamptz, p_to timestamptz, p_search text DEFAULT NULL, p_limit int DEFAULT 50, p_filters jsonb DEFAULT '{}')` | `value text, calls bigint`: the distinct non-NULL values of the filter key `p_key` (one of `llm_call_log`'s six) among the rows it lists for the bounds and `p_filters` with `p_key`'s own entry left out; most calls first, then by value, at most `p_limit` (1 to 200); `p_search` keeps the values holding it, ignoring case. Another key raises `invalid_parameter_value` |
| `llm_call_explore(p_from timestamptz, p_to timestamptz, p_metric text, p_group_by text DEFAULT NULL, p_subgroup text DEFAULT NULL, p_rollup text DEFAULT 'day', p_filters jsonb DEFAULT '{}', p_rank_by text DEFAULT NULL, p_order text DEFAULT 'top', p_limit int DEFAULT 10, p_other boolean DEFAULT true, p_time_zone text DEFAULT 'UTC')` | `bucket_start timestamptz, group_value text, subgroup_value text, value double precision, calls bigint`: `p_metric` of the rows `llm_call_log` lists for the bounds and filters, one row per non-empty cell, by bucket and then by series rank. `p_group_by` and `p_subgroup` are NULL or one of `model`, `provider`, `agent_id`, `pipeline_id`, `node_id`, `outcome` (a NULL value grouped as `(none)`; `group_value` / `subgroup_value` NULL without them). `p_rollup`: `total` (one bucket, `bucket_start = p_from`), `hour`, `day`, `week` (ISO, Monday) or `month`, calendar buckets in the IANA zone `p_time_zone` (hours 3600 s apart from the local hour boundary at or before `p_from`). A series is one (`group_value`, `subgroup_value`), ranked over the whole range by `p_rank_by` (default `p_metric`): `top` highest first, `bottom` lowest first, NULL last, ties to more calls, then to the values in order; the first `p_limit` (1 to 50) are kept, and with `p_other` the runs of the rest form one series `group_value = '__other__'`, `subgroup_value` NULL, its value computed from those runs. Metrics: `calls`, `failed`, `failure_rate`, `cost_usd`, `cost_per_call` (over calls with a cost), `tokens_input`, `tokens_output`, `tokens_total`, `blended_cost_per_1m`, `latency_avg`, `latency_p50`/`p90`/`p95`/`p99` (ms), `throughput_avg`/`p50`/`p90` (output tokens per latency second, runs with output tokens and latency above 0), `attempts_avg`, `fallback_rate` (calls with more than one attempt / calls), `estimated_cost_usd`; sums are 0 without amounts, percentiles interpolate (`percentile_cont`), a ratio is NULL without a denominator. Raises `invalid_parameter_value` for a NU