| `llm_call_detail(p_agent_run_id uuid) RETURNS jsonb` | NULL for an unknown run, else `{run, prompt, tool_schemas, messages, tool_calls, attempts, route, costs, estimates}`: `run` the `agent_runs` row plus its node run's `pipeline_id`, `pipeline_run_id`, `node_id`, `prompt_definition_id` (the `prompt_definitions` row named `prompt_id` at `prompt_version`, without a composition), `prompt_sha256` (that row's `body_sha256`, else the composition pointer's; null when the prompt is in no definition and there is no pointer) and its `agent_run_messages` row's `tool_schema_ids` (null without one); `prompt` `{resolved_by, rows, composition}` (without a composition, the row named `prompt_id` at `prompt_version`: `"prompt_version"`; else the composition's member rows in order, a missing one with `id` and `body` null: `"composition"`; NULL when none applies); `tool_schemas` the rows of `tool_schema_ids` in order; `messages` the stored list with each reference resolved in place and kept (`content`, or `args` for a tool-call part, set to the prompt body, the run's `inputs` (at an inputs reference's `paths`, or without its `without` paths, followed by its `cache_point` as a list when it has one: `agent_framework.referenced_inputs`), its `output`, or the tool call's `input_args` / `output`; `tool_call` the `tool_call_runs` row; `resolved` false when the row is gone), NULL without a stored row; `tool_calls` the `tool_call_runs` rows by `sequence_number`; `attempts` the `llm_routing.attempt` rows in sequence, each with `receipts`, `input_tokens`, `output_tokens`, `cost_usd` of its receipts (`routing_attempt_id = attempt_key`); `route` the `llm_routing.decision_explained` rows; `costs` the cost entries of the run's receipts; `estimates` the run's `cost_tracking.cost_estimate` rows that `estimate_counts` admits, by `created_at`. Lists are `[]` when empty |

`llm_call_explore` comes from `migrations/20261125_llm_call_explore.sql`, applied after the
call log filters migration (`migrations/20261124_llm_call_log_filters.sql`); it replaces
no function of that file or of the routing migration.

The routing migration's steps `agent_run_messages` (tables, retention, reads)
and `agent_runs_llm_index` (`idx_agent_runs_llm_created`, built `CONCURRENTLY` outside a
transaction) store and serve the exchange; `llm_call_log` is planned for each call's
filters, and the steps `agent_runs_llm_provider_index` and `agent_runs_llm_model_index`
(each one index built `CONCURRENTLY` outside a transaction) serve a `provider` or `model`
filter, and `idx_node_runs_pipeline_started_at` a `pipeline_id` filter; `agent_runs`
keeps its shape, so a host runs this simpl_core before them too (each exchange write then
fails in its savepoint and is logged). With `pg_cron`, `agent_run_messages_retention` runs
`CALL agent_framework.prune_agent_run_messages()` daily (a procedure deleting 5,000 rows
a batch, each committed, with a pause between); without it, a host schedules the same
call.

### Streaming producers

A `streaming_producer` node runs its agent once, emitting `StreamEvent`s onto a
`StreamChannel` as it goes, then closes the turn with a terminal `message_final`
(or `error`) event. There is no blocking critic and no fix loop. The framework owns
the event vocabulary and the channel; the bridge that forwards events onto SSE or a
WebSocket is yours — wire it through the `stream_channel_for` / `stream_produce_for`
factories on `NodeCoordinator`.

Two node fields exist only on this kind:

- `framework_produce: true` lets the framework run the agent itself through
  `execute_agent_stream` when no consumer produce is wired, with the agent's model
  policy in force. It is an opt-in: with no consumer produce and no opt-in the node
  fails closed before any model is resolved, because that produce sends the
  prompt row to a live model with live tools and a walk with no wiring — an eval,
  a replay — must not do that by default.
- `stream_field: <name>` makes `token_delta` events carry only that output field's
  text, recovered from the JSON as it streams (text, fenced, or output-tool
  arguments alike). Without it, deltas carry the model's raw output text — JSON
  for a structured turn. The field must be a `str` of the agent's `output_schema`;
  startup warns by name when it is not.

An `llm_agent` bounds a streamed turn with `stream_timeout_seconds` (default ten
minutes) — the whole answer, tool round trips included — while `timeout_seconds`
bounds only the *opening* of each model's stream. A streamed turn runs at the
provider's default temperature and never re-asks the model: a schema-invalid
payload ends the turn. Once `message_final` has been delivered the run is recorded
with that output even if a cancellation lands during close.

Full guide: `docs/agent_framework/08-streaming-output.md`.

### Composed prompts

An `llm_agent`'s `prompt_id` may name a `prompt_composition` instead of a
`prompt_definition`. The composition is assembled when the agent runs: its `core`
first, then every fragment whose `when` matches the agent's `inputs`, in the order
declared, each body stripped of trailing newlines and joined by `separator`.
Members are ordinary `prompt_definition` entries.

```yaml
  - kind: prompt_composition
    id: v1/outreach/writer-system         # what agent_definition.prompt_id names
    version: 1
    display_name: Writer System Prompt    # optional
    core: v1/outreach/writer-core         # always first
    separator: "\n\n"                     # the default
    fragments:                            # ordered; included when `when` matches
      - prompt_id: v1/outreach/writer-channel-email
        when: {all: [{key: channel, op: eq, value: email}]}
      - prompt_id: v1/outreach/writer-mode-reply
        when: {all: [{key: writing_brief.mode, op: eq, value: reply}]}
    is_active: true
```

- `when` is the routing grammar, read against the agent's `inputs` exactly as
  `LLMAgent.check` receives them: dotted keys walk nested dicts, a missing key
  reads null, an empty `all` always matches.
- A `prompt_id` listed in several entries is included once, at its first entry's
  position, when any entry matches: that is how a fragment says "A or B". Those
  entries must be next to each other, so the order of `fragments` is the order of
  the prompt; the schema rejects entries for one fragment split by another's.
- A `prompt_composition` and a `prompt_definition` never share an id: the
  composition would hide the definition. `af_config_migrate` and projection
  reject the document.
- `core` may not be listed as a fragment. `Loader.validate_boot()` fails when a
  member does not resolve.
- Two members of one composition never carry the same line: text two members
  need goes in a fragment of its own, selected wherever both apply, or in the
  core. `af_config_migrate` and projection reject a document in which two members
  share a line of three or more words (whitespace collapsed);
  `shared_member_lines(composition, definitions)` in
  `simpl_core.agent_framework.prompt_composition` lists those lines offline.
- Same composition version, same member versions, same inputs: same bytes. The
  core is always the leading bytes, so provider prefix caching shares it across
  every selection. Nothing per run belongs in a member; run data goes in the user
  message.

`PromptRegistry.resolve(prompt_id, loader=..., inputs=...)` and
`resolve_for_execution(prompt_id, context=..., inputs=...)` take the inputs.
`resolve` accepts `inputs=None` and keeps only the fragments that match with no
input: the check-only form, for boot gates and previews. `resolve_for_execution`
raises `ValueError` when the id names a composition and `inputs` is `None`, so a
call never runs on the core alone by omission; a single-definition prompt still
resolves without inputs. The resolved `ExecutablePrompt` names the composition
(`prompt_id`, `version`) and carries the rendering on `composition`;
`composition_pointer()` is what the run records.

- `LLMAgent.check(inputs, prompt_selection=[...])` and `stream(...,
  prompt_selection=[...])` compose with a fixed list of fragments instead of
  evaluating `when`; they pass it as `selection=` to `resolve_for_execution`, which
  `resolve` and `compose_prompt(..., selection=...)` also take. Use it to re-compose
  an earlier rendering, such as a rewrite of a draft whose inputs are no longer
  all at hand: pass the fragment ids of that draft's pointer (`members` after the
  core). The body is the core, then exactly the named fragments, each once, in the
  composition's declaration order whatever order the list has; `[]` is the core
  alone. With a selection, `resolve_for_execution` accepts `inputs=None`. A
  selection that names the core or an id that is not a fragment of the composition
  raises `ValueError`, and so does a selection for a prompt id that is not a
  composition. The recorded pointer keeps its shape: its members are the core and
  the fragments used.
- A loader you write for `PromptExecutionContext` needs only `get_prompt(prompt_id)`.
  `get_prompt_composition(prompt_id)` is optional: it is called only when the
  loader has it, and a loader without it (a test stub, say) serves no compositions,
  so every id resolves as a definition or a registered fallback. Implement it,
  returning `None` for an id that is not a composition, when the loader serves
  compositions.

`agent_runs.prompt_id` / `prompt_version` name the composition, and
`agent_runs.prompt_composition` (also on `ProducerAuditSnapshot.prompt_composition`
and the executor's `audit["prompt_composition"]`) holds the pointer:

```json
{"id": "v1/outreach/writer-system", "version": 1, "body_sha256": "<sha256 of the body>",
 "separator": "\n\n",
 "members": [{"prompt_id": "v1/outreach/writer-core", "version": 3, "body_sha256": "..."},
             {"prompt_id": "v1/outreach/writer-mode-reply", "version": 1, "body_sha256": "..."}]}
```

The pointer and the versioned `prompt_definitions` rows it names are the record:
no composed body is stored anywhere else.
`simpl_core.agent_framework.prompt_composition.reproduce_composed_body(pointer,
db=db)` rebuilds the exact body from those member versions and raises
`ValueError` when a digest disagrees. `compose_prompt(composition=...,
definitions=..., inputs=...)` and `select_fragments(composition, inputs)` are the
pure functions behind it, for tests that check a composition offline.

For a composed prompt, `(prompt_id, prompt_version)` identifies the composition,
not the text the model read. Two runs with the same pair can carry different
bodies: the fragments selected differ with the inputs, and the composition's
version does not change when a member's text changes, since members are
versioned on their own and pinned by the config version. An analysis that
compares prompt text groups by `prompt_composition->>'body_sha256'`, or by the
member versions in `prompt_composition->'members'`, not by `prompt_version`.

Apply `migrations/20261015b_af_prompt_compositions.sql` before this version is
merged or released. The `AgentRun` model maps `agent_runs.prompt_composition` on
every write, and a loader without a manifest filters graph elements on the enum
value `prompt_composition`, which errors on a database that lacks it. A service
that pins this package at `main` picks the version up on its next relock.

Full guide: `docs/agent_framework/11-operating-it.md#prompts`.

Full guide: `docs/agent_framework/`.

### Alerting

Every exception the framework handles is classified in one place,
`simpl_core.agent_framework.alerting`, and handed to `simpl_tracker`:

- **`#warning`** hears every exception the framework catches, converts into a
  fail-closed status, or lets escape a public entry point — once.
- **`#error`** is for work that stopped or a gate that went missing: boot gates
  failing, a malformed pipeline / validator / routing row dropped, a node whose
  author chose `escalate`, `block` or `error`, an active config version with no good
  version to fall back to — and whatever your rules promote.
- **Log only**: policy doing its job (a validator rejecting content, an exhausted fix
  loop, an unmatched route, a transient error still being retried) and any
  `ExpectedInterruption`.

Register your promotions once at bootstrap, beside handler registration:

```python
from simpl_core.agent_framework import AlertRule, add_alert_rules

add_alert_rules(
    # The ops checks exist to be seen: page on them.
    AlertRule(
        event="agent_framework.engine.validator_failed",
        pipeline_id="pipeline_ops_agent",
        severity="error",
    ),
)
```

- **Rules.** The first matching rule wins, and every field a rule sets must match:
  `event`, `exception` (by `isinstance`), `pipeline_id` (the innermost pipeline of
  the run), `node_id_prefix`. `group_by` adds run-context keys to the alert
  fingerprint. A `silent` rule needs a `reason`.
- **Rules belong to the process.** `add_alert_rules` appends only the rules not
  installed yet, compared by value, after the ones already there, and never touches
  the other settings. Every app registers its own promotions with it, the shared
  `pipeline_ops_agent` rule included, and a host that embeds another app keeps both
  apps' rules whichever bootstraps first. `alert_rules()` returns what is installed.
- **Process settings.** `configure_alerting` changes only what it is given:
  `enabled=False` keeps the logs and sends no alerts, `shared_dedup=False` keeps the
  tracker's own dedup store, and `rules=` replaces every installed rule, other apps'
  included, so pass it only for a deliberate full replace.
- **Expected interruptions.** Subclass `ExpectedInterruption` for a failure nobody
  can act on (a dropped extension channel whose work re-queues itself). It is
  recorded on its node run and logged, never alerted, and never reported as a crash.
  The node run's `error` carries `"expected": true` (so does a cancelled node's), and a
  failed fan-out whose every branch was one logs
  `agent_framework.coordinator.fan_out_interrupted` instead of `fan_out_all_failed`.
  Every subclass inherits `__simpl_notify__ = False`, which `simpl_tracker` reads: the
  exception stays silent when you log it yourself with `error=exc` too, even as another
  exception's `__cause__`. Only an explicit `notify="warning"` or `notify="error"` posts
  it.
- **Cancellations you cause on purpose.** A `CancelledError` escaping a walk is reported
  as `agent_framework.run.cancelled`, because from inside the run a job timeout and a
  deliberate stop look alike. When your code cancels a task on purpose, for example a
  worker handing its walk back at shutdown, call
  `expect_cancellation(task, reason="worker_shutdown")` before `task.cancel()`. From
  then on a cancellation inside that task is an expected interruption: logged, never
  alerted. `expected_cancellation_reason()` returns the reason inside the task. The mark
  lasts for the task's life, so only mark a task that ends with the cancellation.
- **Fan-out branches.** Each branch in a gather join's `gathered` dict carries
  `error_type` (the exception class it ended on, or `None`) and `expected`, beside
  `error`; read those instead of matching on the message. The fan-out's own
  `produced` adds `expected_error_branch_count`.
- **Run identifiers.** Pass your own ids as `labels={"user_id": ..., "session_id": ...}`
  to `resolve_engine_entry`, `walk_graph`, `execute_agent` or `execute_agent_stream`.
  The Sales Domain wrappers take `labels` too and hand them to every entry point they
  call: `run_sales_domain_for_user`, `run_sales_domain_pipeline`, `run_framework_walk`
  (which also binds them around its `finalize`), `open_sales_domain_runtime`,
  `resolve_admission_route`, and `TriggerRegistry.wake_agent` for its inline run.
  Every alert raised inside the call carries them, including one from a nested walk,
  a node run or a spawned task, and a label whose key ends in `_id` is counted as an
  alert entity. Labels are never persisted. The framework binds `correlation_id`,
  `pipeline_id`, `pipeline_run_id`, `node_id`, `node_run_id`, `agent_id` and the
  config version itself; a label may not take those names. You no longer need
  `bind_run_context` around a walk.
- **Which run wrote a row.** `run_reference(origin=None, **extra)` (exported from
  `simpl_core.agent_framework`) returns the `run_ref` of the outreach observability
  contract: `origin` (default `agent_framework`), `pipeline_id`, `pipeline_run_id`,
  `node_run_id`, `root_node_run_id`, `config_version_id`, `correlation_id`, plus what
  you pass (`decision_node`, `batch_id`, `source_action_id`), only keys with a value.
  Outside a run it is `None` unless you name an `origin` (`fast_path`, `route_handler`,
  `api`, `legacy`). `stamp_run_reference(target, key="run_ref", **kw)` sets it on a dict
  and never overwrites one already there. simpl_core stamps it for you inside a run:
  `JourneyRepository.insert_action` (`payload.run_ref`), every trigger it creates
  (`trigger_data.created_by`, with the optional `batch_id` / `source_action_id` of
  `create_trigger`, `try_insert` and `batch_id` of `schedule_followup`) and account
  coordination events (`payload.run_ref`).
- **Report once.** Before alerting on an exception you caught from the framework,
  check `already_reported(exc)`: `True` means the tracker accepted an alert for it, or
  for an exception it was raised `from`, on `#warning` or `#error`. An alert the tracker
  refused (no notifier, a refused send) or failed to deliver does not count, so your own
  alert still applies. `resolve_engine_entry`, `walk_graph`, `execute_agent`,
  `execute_agent_stream`, `Loader.validate_boot` and `PlanRunner.run` report an
  escaping exception as `agent_framework.run.crashed` (or `run.cancelled`, one per
  pipeline) and re-raise it unchanged. Within one run, alerts group by incident under
  its outermost node run. A later event there only logs when it carries the same
  exception as an earlier alert (or one raised `from` it), or when it carries no
  exception and is an outcome of that failure, such as `error_terminal`,
  `node_error_terminal` or `transient_error_exhausted`. Exception class never decides: a
  different failure posts even when it raises the same class, and so does anything more
  severe. Exact repeats reach the tracker, which counts them in one message. A failure
  raised through a node's retries alerts once, as `transient_error_exhausted`. Only an
  alert that reached an operator groups: one the tracker refused, or accepted and then
  failed to deliver, clears its run's group, so the next outcome there posts. An outcome
  that already folded while that alert was still queued or in flight is not posted
  again; delivery is not durable.
- **Step trace.** `walk_graph` writes `pipeline_run_steps` as each step finishes, and
  once more when the walk ends, even when it raised. It is on by default for a root walk
  whose node runs go to the database (`DbNodeRunRecorder`) and that has a
  `pipeline_run_id`. Pass `step_recorder=DbStepRecorder(db)` (or any `StepRecorder`)
  to trace elsewhere, or `step_recorder=None` to turn it off. `error` is SQL NULL on
  success and `{"type", "message", "expected"}` on failure; a failed write alerts as
  `agent_framework.step_recorder.write_failed` and the recorder keeps writing. Delete a
  step recorder of your own.
- **Shared dedup.** The framework installs the Postgres dedup store itself on its first
  walk with a database (a `DbNodeRunRecorder`), once per notifier, so a recurring
  failure is one thread across flow runs; `configure_alerting(shared_dedup=False)` opts
  out. Processes also agree on who posts: one of them posts a new alert, or its
  re-post or escalation, and the others only count it. Only a process that does not run
  the framework calls `install_alert_dedup_store`.
- **Config fallbacks** are reported by the framework
  (`agent_framework.config_ledger.fallback_applied` / `fallback_exhausted`) for every
  config key. A fallback handler you register runs after that report and no longer
  needs to alert.
- **Tracker version.** Routing an alert to a channel other than its log level's, and
  carrying the exception on warnings, need
  `simpl_tracker.logging.NOTIFY_API_VERSION >= 2`. Against an older tracker only
  events whose channel matches their log level notify. On version 2 and later every
  framework call passes an explicit `notify=` (`False` for log only), so a tracker
  default that notifies exceptions never overrides the classification. A tracker with
  `ignore_callsite_modules` names the framework caller, not the alerting package, as
  each line's callsite. A tracker whose log calls answer with a bool tells the framework
  whether it accepted each alert, and the framework counts an alert as reported only
  then; a tracker that answers nothing counts as accepting. Shared-dedup delivery claims
  need a tracker that knows the `pending` dedup action and calls the store's `release`.
  Posting a deferred alert once the other post failed needs a tracker that calls the
  store's `recheck`; replacing a deleted message once needs one that passes `message_id`
  to `forget`; clearing a lost alert's group needs
  `simpl_tracker.notifications.add_undelivered_listener`. Against an older tracker a
  `pending` alert counts as sent, a deleted message's row is forgotten outright, and a
  lost alert's group keeps its rank.
- **What you delete.** Config-fallback handlers, per-item Discord alerts that repeat
  what the framework already reported (or guard them with `already_reported`), and
  your own step recorder. What stays yours: `labels=`, your `ExpectedInterruption`
  subclasses, and the rules that promote an event to `#error`.
- **Renamed events.** `agent_framework_tool_failed` →
  `agent_framework.tool_audit.tool_failed`; `tool_audit.unresolved_agent_id` and
  `tool_audit.missing_agent_run` → `agent_framework.tool_audit.*`;
  `plan_review_notification_failed` →
  `agent_framework.plan_lifecycle.review_notification_failed`.

`EVENTS` lists every event with its kind, default severity and reason, and
`testing.strict_run` derives its deny list from the same registry.

#### Alert dedup across processes

The tracker's notifier collapses repeats of one alert: the first occurrence posts,
repeats edit that message, a count of 100 or 1000 posts again, and a warning still
recurring an hour after it began escalates once to `#error`. Its own store lives in
process memory, which two Cloud Run jobs never share. The framework keeps that state in
Postgres instead (`agent_framework.alert_fingerprints`, migration
`20260914_af_alert_fingerprints.sql`), so every process shares one count and one
Discord message per alert.

- **No wiring for a framework consumer.** The first `walk_graph` whose node runs go to
  the database (`DbNodeRunRecorder`) calls `ensure_alert_dedup_store(db)` with that
  database, whatever its `step_recorder`. It reads the notifier through
  `simpl_tracker.current_notifier()` and installs `PostgresDedupStore` on it when the
  tracker is 0.5.0 or later, the notifier's config leaves dedup enabled, its store is
  still the tracker's default `InMemoryDedupStore`, and you have not opted out. The store
  takes the notifier's own `dedup_window_seconds`, `repost_thresholds` and
  `escalate_after_seconds`, so your tracker YAML's `notifications.dedup` settings apply.
  It decides once per notifier: a notifier that a later `configure_from_yaml` puts in
  place gets the store too, a walk that runs before the tracker is configured leaves the
  decision to the next walk, and every later walk costs a lookup. A failure to install
  never breaks the walk. One store per database and dedup settings serves every notifier
  in the process.
- **Opting out.** `configure_alerting(shared_dedup=False)` keeps the tracker's own
  store. A store you install on the notifier yourself is also left alone.
- **A process that notifies without walking the framework**, and only such a process,
  calls `install_alert_dedup_store(database_url)` after configuring the tracker, and
  again after any later `configure_from_yaml` / `configure_from_block`, which builds a
  fresh notifier. It returns `False` when the tracker predates 0.5.0, no notifier is
  configured, or the store cannot be built.
- **Database.** `ensure_alert_dedup_store` takes a URL or your `DatabaseManager` (it
  uses the URL the manager's engine connects with). The connecting role needs
  `SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE` on the table. The store opens its own small engine
  on its own thread, and works over the direct host and either Supabase pooler.
- **Delivery claims.** Counting an occurrence and claiming its delivery happen in one
  short transaction. When the tracker's rule says post, re-post or escalate, the row
  takes a claim (`claim_token`, `claim_expires_at`, 180 seconds). Any other process
  counting that fingerprint meanwhile sends nothing new, or edits the existing message
  when it lost a re-post or escalation. A post completes the claim only while the claim
  is still its own, so a claim taken over after it expired keeps the newer message. A
  post that was not sent gives its claim back at once. A process that dies mid-post
  holds back that fingerprint's next message until the claim expires. A process that
  sent nothing because another held the claim keeps its alert deferred and asks the
  store again (`PostgresDedupStore.recheck`, which never counts the occurrence twice): it
  edits the message once one exists, and posts under a claim of its own when the other
  post failed or its claim expired. When an edit finds the Discord message deleted,
  `forget(fingerprint, message_id=...)` drops the row only while it still holds that
  message, so two processes that both hit the deleted message replace it once.
- **Failure.** The store never raises into the notifier. When the database fails or
  stalls (5 s), that call dedups in memory, the database is retried after 30 s, and the
  outage is logged once on the stdlib `simpl_core.agent_framework.alerting.dedup_store`
  logger. At exit it flushes queued alerts through itself before closing.

### Publishing a config version

A policy change reaches a running graph only as a **new** ledger version. A
version's content is read once, when projection turns it into component rows;
editing an applied version, or re-running its migration, changes nothing.
`af_config_migrate` writes the seed migration that carries a version. It applies
nothing. Reverting is the same operation pointed backwards --
`activate_config_version(<key>, <earlier version>)`.
Every activation checks the parent (`agent_framework.activate_config_version`, which
`ConfigVersionRepository.activate`, the config ledger's activate endpoint and the seeds
call): a version activates only when no version of its key is active, it is the active
one, its `parent_version` is the active one, or it is an ancestor of the active one (a
rollback). Any other raises `activation_refused` (`check_violation`;
`ActivationRefused` from the repository, a 409 from simpl_api) before any write: a seed
generated on a parent that is no longer active fails and must be regenerated on the
active version.
The checked form `activate_config_version(key, version, expected_active, content_hash)`
also refuses unless the active version is `expected_active` (NULL: none) and the version
holds `content_hash`, and changes nothing when the version is already active with that
hash. Generated activations (expecting the parent), the advisor's revert, simpl_api's
endpoint when given `expected_active_version` and the dashboard use it; the two-argument
form stays for files already written.
It is the sole supported YAML seed generator; do not restore or alias retired
`sync_yaml`.

```bash
simpl_core af_config_migrate \
    --yaml path/to/policy.yaml --dest migrations/ \
    --config-key my_root --version 3 --parent 2 --prefix 20260807 \
    --summary "one line on what changed" [--activate]
```

```python
from simpl_core import af_config_migrate, assert_newest_seed_matches_document
```

`--prefix` is your repo's own migration number; the filename is
`<prefix>_seed_<config_key>_<version>.sql`. Without `--activate` the version is
inserted inert, to be activated as a separate deploy step. The author is your
git `user.email` unless you pass `--created-by` or set
`AGENT_FRAMEWORK_LEDGER_CREATED_BY`.

It refuses, writing nothing, when the document fails the authoring gates, when
it is identical to its parent, or when the target files already exist (pass
`replace=True` only for a version applied nowhere).

`--config-kind model_catalog` (`config_kind='model_catalog'`, key `model_catalog` only)
seeds a model catalog version: the document passes the catalog's write gate instead of
the pipeline rules, and the INSERT sets `config_kind` (a pipeline seed leaves the column
to its default and is written as before). A pipeline document naming canonical models
is checked against `--catalog` (`catalog=`), by default the catalog simpl_core ships.

`--delta` (`delta=True`) writes a seed that carries only the lines changed since
`--parent`, whose seed must be in `--dest` (full or delta). Applied, it reads the
parent row's `content`, checks its sha256, applies the hunks, checks the result's
sha256 and inserts the full document -- the same row a full seed writes. It raises,
inserting nothing, when the parent is not registered or holds another document,
or when the version is registered with other content; re-running it is a no-op.

`seed_document(migrations_dir, config_key, version)` returns the document a
directory's seeds register for a version, following delta seeds back to a full
one and checking every hash. `delta_seed_statements(...)` renders just the delta
`DO` block, for a repository that writes its own seed header and rollback.

`assert_newest_seed_matches_document(migrations_dir=..., document=..., config_key=...)`
raises `AssertionError` when the authored document has drifted from the newest
seed carrying it (embedded, or rebuilt by a delta seed). Call it from a test; it
imports no test framework.

Both need the `agent-framework` extra — they validate through the framework's
schemas. `import simpl_core` itself stays free of that dependency; the cost is
paid on first access to either name.

## Email signature

`simpl_core.services.email.signature` sanitizes HTML stored on `sales.user.email_signature_html` and appends it once, under the draft and before the unsubscribe footer. The column is added by simpl_api migration `20260907_user_email_signature.sql`. Apply that migration before deploying a build whose `User` model selects the column.

## Email bounces and dead addresses

- **Recognition** (`services/email/delivery_notice.detect_delivery_failure_notice`, pure): a mail-system sender (`mailer-daemon`, `postmaster`, Exchange's `microsoftexchange…`), or a named address with a failure marker; `failed_recipients` are the addresses the notice's own text names. `address_dead` is true only when the failure says the address is gone (5.1.x, 5.2.1, 5.4.4 or `DEAD_ADDRESS_PHRASES`) and never for a policy refusal (5.7.x: the receiver refused the sender), a refusal of the sender itself (`sender_refused`) or a full mailbox. `sender_refused` is true for RFC 3463 5.1.7 / 5.1.8 (the *sender's* address) and `SENDER_REFUSED_PHRASES` (Microsoft 365's "550 5.1.8 Access denied, bad outbound sender", sent by the seller's own tenant for every email of a mailbox it restricted for suspected spam).
- **Recording** (`services/email/bounces.record_dead_addresses`): mailbox sync calls it on a thread a connection holds, before it leaves the mail-system messages out (`EmailSyncOrchestrator._record_dead_addresses`, best effort). An address a notice reports dead, and that the seller emailed in that thread, gets a `sales_outreach.email_bounce` row (one per notice and address, so a re-sync records nothing twice), `email_status = 'bounced'` on every lead's `lead_contact` rows for it (`LeadContactRepository.mark_email_bounced`), and an `email.message.bounced` event on the connection.
- **Refused sender** (`bounces.record_sender_refusals`, called by `EmailSyncOrchestrator._record_sender_refusals`, best effort): a `sender_refused` notice naming an address the seller emailed in that thread kills no address. It writes one `email.mailbox.blocked` event per notice on the connection (`event_data.send_refusal = 'blocked'`, `recipients`, `smtp_status`, `notice_message_id`; a re-sync records nothing twice), at the notice's time. Send pacing reads it as a block of the mailbox (see the claim rules: 72 h without outreach, then 7 days at 10 a day): the provider accepted the email at send time, so the failed-send refusal never fires for it.
- **Global**: `EmailBounceRepository.is_dead(email)` is true when any customer's mailbox got such a notice for it; simpl_flow's journey email flow then blocks the send (`email.message.blocked`, reason `recipient_address_dead`), as it does for an opt-out.
- **Exchange's system sender** is a machine address (`migrations/20261019d_exchange_system_sender_automated.sql`, one row in `mappings.email_automated_pattern`): sync leaves its notices out like `mailer-daemon`'s instead of storing them as the lead's reply.
- Notices are not stored, so none wakes an outreach walk. A notice on a thread no connection holds is not read.
- **Closed mailbox** (`delivery_notice.detect_retired_mailbox_notice`, `bounces.record_retired_mailboxes`, called by `EmailSyncOrchestrator._record_retired_mailboxes`): the lead's own mailbox answering "this address is no longer active, write to X" (IT/EN/FR/DE/ES, only a sentence about that very mailbox). The address the seller emailed gets the same `email_bounce` row and `bounced` status as a bounce, but an `email.address.retired` event (no `email.message.bounced`: the email was delivered, so send pacing does not count it). An X whose local part spells the lead's name (`sender_identity.is_same_person`) is recorded as the lead's, private to the seller, source `email_retired_mailbox`, primary when the seller had none; any other X is left to outreach (a colleague: `target_change`). The notice is stored and wakes the walk.
- **Dead addresses are not written to**: `SellerContactReach.visible` leaves out `bounced` rows like `another_person` ones, so `find_best_contact`, outreach's lead addresses, enrichment's contact lookup and the strategy reach never pick a dead address; `mark_email_bounced` also drops the row's primary flag.

## FullEnrich answers (API v1 and v2)

`simpl_core.schemas.fullenrich_webhook.FullEnrichWebhookPayload` parses a FullEnrich result in either shape: v1 (`datas[].contact.most_probable_email`) and v2 (`data[].contact_info.most_probable_work_email`, an object with `email` and `status`). `first_email()` and `first_phone()` answer the same for both, so the webhook in simpl_api does not care which API the request went out on (ENG-1194; v1 is being shut down by the vendor). In v2 only the work address and `most_probable_phone` are read, and the person's profile is dropped on parsing, so it never reaches `contact_enrichment_log.result_payload`. `simpl_core.enrichment.is_persistable_enriched_email_status` keeps `CATCH_ALL`, `INVALID` and `INVALID_DOMAIN` (new in v2) out of `lead_contact`.

## Voice profiles and prompt layers

- **Prompt layers** (`services/prompt_layer_scope`): the customer, ICP and seller template tiers after the ICP's `prompt_layer_scope` and the lent-identity rule (a lent SimpL identity keeps only her `voice_guide`). Outreach's writer and the voice profile both scope through it, so they read the same layers.
- **Settings** (`services/voice_profiles/settings`): `plan_and_write_settings(metadata)` reads a work customer's `sales.customer.metadata` into `PlanAndWriteSettings(enabled, situations, style_precedence, writer_agent)`; outreach, simpl_flow and simpl_api read the settings through it alone, so the defaults live there: `plan_and_write_drafting` is on unless it is `false`, `plan_and_write_situations` absent is `None`, every situation with a reader section (`drafting.SUPPORTED_SITUATIONS`: today `first_touch`, `response` and `nudge`, so a situation added there reaches every customer that named none), and `[]` builds profiles without drafting, `style_precedence` is `customer_voice` unless it is `house_style`, `plan_and_write_writer` names the agent that drafts in `message_writer`'s place (`writer_agent`, `None` for `message_writer`). A malformed flag reads as off and a malformed list as no situation, each with a warning.
- **Source hash** (`services/voice_profiles/layers`): `scope_prompt_layers(...)` keeps the five voice fields (`company_brief`, `value_props_matrix`, `voice_guide`, `outreach_strategy`, `mission`); `voice_profile_source_hash` is the sha256 of their text. `calendar_link`, `research_topics` and the scope key are not voice, so changing them never rebuilds a profile.
- **Extraction** (`services/voice_profiles/extractor`): the model call is the caller's. simpl_flow makes it as the agent framework's `voice_profile_extractor` row (config key `voice_profile_root`, simpl_flow `pipelines/voice_profiles/agent_policy.yaml`), which holds the prompt and the model and whose output schema mirrors `VoiceProfileDraft`; the drafts refuse fields they do not have. `ground_voice_profile` then keeps only items whose quote the layers contain (`services/quote_grounding`) and signatures and fixed texts found word for word. A fixed text carries its `language` (ISO 639-1, `None` for any) and an `alternative_group`: texts sharing one are versions of the same text (translations, two closings), and a message uses one of them. Offers and cases get ids `template_offer_<n>` / `template_case_<n>`. Text that steers the pipeline instead of the message goes to `ignored_instructions`. What the prompt asks for (repeated moves as options, a fixed structure as a rule, no details of one example) is the ledger's, kept aligned with simpl_api's `voice_rules_step0_prompt.md` and simpl_writer's `step0_client_profile.md`. A new ledger version rebuilds the profiles an older one built, which keep serving until then; a grounding change here needs a new ledger version too, and a new draft field needs this package first, then the ledger's output schema.
- **Storage** (`repositories/voice_profile_cache`, migration `20261027_voice_profile_cache.sql`): one row per `source_hash`. `get_profile` returns the last profile built from that text; `record_failure` counts consecutive failures and never removes a built profile; `record_built` clears the failure state. simpl_flow's `refresh_voice_profiles` writes it; outreach reads it.
- **Drafting rules** (`services/voice_profiles/drafting`, pure): which stage drafts a message (`ladder_lookup`, `plan_and_write_lookup`, `drafting_pipeline`), the writer's payload with the voice profile (`with_voice_profile`), the CRM fact list (`drafting_facts`), the reader's situations (`message_situation`: `first_touch`, a cold first message; `response`, the lead spoke last and the brief's mode is `reply`, `follow_up` or `farewell`, `is_response`; `nudge`, a `follow_up` the seller writes after their own unanswered message, on the same exclusions as the first touch, `is_nudge`, read with `unanswered_count`, how many seller messages in a row went unanswered), thresholds, output schema (`reader_output_schema`: the reader's schema without the other situations' scores) and verdict (`settle_reading`, `best_attempt`). The outreach walk and simpl_api's ICP studio both draft by them.
- **Message checks** (`services/outreach/message_checks`): the outreach ledger's code checks on a message, registered as agent-framework primitives on import of the package: the escalation ladder's names (`fact_consistency`, `no_repetition`, `commercial_claims_grounded`, `no_access_disclosure`, `call_page_link`, `booking_link_as_briefed`, ...) and plan-and-write's `draft_*` names (the writer's language check `draft_in_target_language_words` is the exception: `message_checks/draft_language` reads the draft with `services/written_language`, the same word count simpl-outreach's thread build reads a lead's replies with; it is not imported by the package, simpl_api imports it itself while simpl-outreach registers its own copy, because a key registered twice raises). Every process that walks the drafting stage imports it; nothing else may register these kinds. The booking-link check (`booking_link_as_briefed`, `draft_carries_the_booking_link_as_briefed`; inputs `content`, `calendar_link` and an optional `voice_profile`) reads a link without the punctuation after it, and never counts a link the customer's own fixed texts or signatures write.
- **Drafting stage** (`services/outreach/message_drafting`): what `outreach_message_drafting` runs in both hosts: `keep_best_reading.KeepBestReadingProducer`, the stage's gate keys (`stage_keys.DRAFTING_STAGE_KEYS`: `reading_situation`, `house_style`, `decision_channel`, `account_responsibility_enabled`), the reader's payload (`reading.reader_payload`, whose `thread` is the conversation a response answers, `[]` when the caller passes none; `reading_unavailable`) and the `writing_brief` resolver. `walk.walk_message_drafting(db, entry, ...)` walks the stage from the active ledger for a message with no thread (the ICP studio): the host passes the upstream surface, the brief, the writer's payload, the listed fact ids, `ThreadlessDraftFacts` and a `run_agent` port, and gets the kept draft, the review's verdict and the reading. The walk resolves the stage's `gate_keys`, `ai_context` and `journey_log` thread-less (`threadless_resolvers`; a thread fact the stage's routing reads off the outreach walk answers as no thread does, `NO_THREAD_GATE_KEYS`: `vendor_pitch_turn` is `False`) for its own length through `agent_framework.resolvers.base.scoped_resolvers`, which shadows registered names inside a block without registering anything, so it runs beside any process's own resolvers.
- **Language names** (`services/language_names`): `normalize_language` / `normalize_language_list` read a language value as its plain English name (`"el"`, `"Modern Greek (1453-)"`, `"ελληνικά"` are `"Greek"`), `None` when unreadable or one letter. The outreach walk, the draft checks and the ICP studio share it.
- **A user's language** (`services/user_language`, ENG-1261): what SimpL writes to one of its users is in `user_language(ui_language, otherwise=...)`: the language they chose in the app (`sales.user.ui_language`, 'en' | 'it'), else the caller's behaviour of today (`language_of(spoken_languages)` for emails, 'en' for screens). `agent_language(reply_language, ui_language=, spoken_languages=)` is the agent's: the reply language set in Settings → Agent, else that rule; `template_language` reads it as 'it' or 'en' for fixed text. Never for text to leads.
- **Layer reads** (`services/voice_profiles/layer_reads`): `VoiceLayerReads(session)` reads the layers a connection's walk reads: `connection_keys(ulc_id)` (customer through `sales.connection_customer`, ICP, seller, lent) and the active customer, ICP and seller rows by key (`SavedLayer`: the voice fields, the ICP's `prompt_layer_scope`, and `saved_at`, the row's `updated_at`), the most recently saved when there are several. simpl_flow's `refresh_voice_profiles` and `rebuild_pending` both read through it, so the profile built and the profile checked hash the same text; `LayerKeys` is the combination flow's refresh groups connections by.
- **Rebuild pending** (`services/voice_profiles/rebuild_pending`): `pending_rebuild(session, ulc_id=, now=)` returns `PendingRebuild(source_hash, saved_at, expected_by)` when the work customer drafts with plan-and-write, the connection's scoped layers have no built profile and no failed build, and a layer was saved less than `REBUILD_EXPECTED_WITHIN` (15 minutes) ago; else `None`. simpl_api refuses a regenerate (409 `voice_profile_rebuilding`) while it returns one, so a regenerated draft never misses the profile the next walk reads; past the window the regenerate goes through.
- **Quote grounding** (`services/quote_grounding`): `fold_for_quote_matching`, `locate_quote`, `ground_quoted_items`. Folds Markdown, typography and invisible characters, never letters; also used by outreach's company-research extractor.

## Email send-as aliases

Connected Gmail/Outlook accounts can send from a verified alias stored on `integration.user_connection.config`:

```python
from simpl_core.services.email import (
    list_user_send_as_addresses,
    set_user_send_from_email,
    SEND_FROM_EMAIL_CONFIG_KEY,
)

addresses = await list_user_send_as_addresses(
    user_id,
    "gmail",
    session=session,
    db_manager=db_manager,
    composio_api_key=...,
    composio_toolkit_versions=...,
)

await set_user_send_from_email(
    user_id,
    "gmail",
    "sales@company.com",
    session=session,
)
```

When `send_from_email` is set, `get_connected_email_service()` applies it automatically on outbound sends. Per-request overrides remain available via `SendEmailRequest(from=...)`. Aliases must already be verified in Gmail or enabled in Microsoft 365 before Composio can send from them.

For Outlook replies, consumers with a persisted conversation should pass both `threadId` and the latest stored `replyMessageId`. The adapter validates that message against Outlook before replying. `EmailNotFoundError` means Outlook definitively reports the requested conversation or reply target unavailable. An unscoped inbox or sent-items page that Outlook cannot retrieve raises `EmailMailboxPageUnavailableError`; mailbox sync logs the page, preserves its cursor, and retries it later. Gmail's `get_email_thread` reads a thread too large for one Composio response message by message; `EmailThreadTooLargeError` means a single message is still too large, and mailbox sync quarantines that thread (`threads_failed`) and keeps going. Runtime, connection, and malformed-response failures remain `IntegrationError` for the caller to surface.

An email carries the files of its draft (2026-11-16: up to `MAX_DRAFT_ATTACHMENTS` = 5, `MAX_DRAFT_ATTACHMENTS_TOTAL_BYTES` = 15 MiB together, enforced by simpl_api when they are attached). Gmail sends one file through `GMAIL_SEND_EMAIL` / `GMAIL_REPLY_TO_THREAD` as before; two or more go in a message built here (`imap_messages.build_message`) and posted to `users.messages.send` through Composio's proxy (`ComposioGmailService._send_with_files`): the pinned toolkit takes one file, and its later version appends the Gmail signature. A reply answers the thread's last message (In-Reply-To, References, `Re:` its subject) from the primary mailbox, as the tool does; a new email goes from the alias under its send-as name, else from the mailbox under its own; Bcc rides in the message. Outlook's new email attaches the first file on `OUTLOOK_CREATE_DRAFT` and the others to the draft as a reply does. IMAP/SMTP puts them all in the message. `OUTLOOK_REPLY_EMAIL` cannot attach, so an Outlook reply with files goes through a reply draft, each file added in turn: a file under 3 MiB is added with `OUTLOOK_ADD_MAIL_ATTACHMENT`, a larger one through a Graph upload session (`OUTLOOK_CREATE_ATTACHMENT_UPLOAD_SESSION`), whose bytes are PUT straight to the session's upload URL in chunks of 3 MiB (`simpl_core.services.email.outlook_upload_session`). A failed upload raises `IntegrationError` and the draft is not sent.

`EmailDTO.to_email` is the whole To header (every recipient, as Gmail hands it over; the Outlook adapter joins Graph's `toRecipients` the same way, and fills `cc_email` / `bcc_email` from Graph too). Read it with `parse_email_address_list`, never as one address: mailbox sync files each recipient as a participant of its own, so the mailbox owner among several recipients is on the thread.

Outlook gives a sent draft a new message id once it lands in Sent Items, so the `provider_message_id` that `send_email` returns is not the id mailbox sync later lists. `EmailDTO.internet_message_id` carries the RFC 5322 Message-ID, which survives the move. When persisting an Outlook send, pass it as `record_sent_email_message(..., internet_message_id=...)`: ingestion then recognizes the synced copy in the same thread and does not store the message twice.

A `sales_outreach.journey_log` row is its `(action_type, channel)` pair, and the pair must be in `EXECUTION_KINDS` (`repositories/outreach/journey_dispatch_rules.py`, exported from `simpl_core.repositories.outreach`): `send_message` on `email`, `linkedin_message` or `call`; `linkedin_connect` on `linkedin_connect`; `invite_call` on `calendar`. `classify(payload)` returns `executable`, `non_executable` (a verb in `NON_EXECUTABLE_ACTION_TYPES`, whatever channel it carries) or `invalid`; `execution_kind(payload)` returns the pair or `None`. Write an invitation as `action_type = "linkedin_connect"`, never as `send_message` on the connect channel: approval (`set_pending_with_version`) and the database trigger refuse the row with `invalid_transition: ...` (409 in simpl_api), as they refuse an unknown verb, a missing verb and an email send without `recipient_email`. Mirror the sets (`EXTENSION_KINDS`, `EMAIL_KINDS`, `CALENDAR_KINDS`, `SELLER_EXECUTED_KINDS`, `CONTENT_DELIVERY_VERBS`, `STANDALONE_EXTENSION_TASKS`) with a parity test against this package rather than by hand; every one of them is declared here, the four executor sets partition `EXECUTION_KINDS`, and `(invite_call, calendar)` is a calendar kind run by simpl_api on approval, not a seller-executed one. Its approval is its execution: `invite_call` and `send_calendar_invite_manually` (`APPROVAL_EXECUTED_ACTION_TYPES`) never enter `PENDING`, because nothing claims them there; `set_pending_with_version` refuses them through `assert_queueable` (`invalid_transition: ...`, 409) and the trigger `trg_journey_log_never_queue_approval_executed` refuses raw-SQL writers. Book an invite with simpl_api's `execute-invite-call`.

`JourneyLogRepository.claim_pending_email_actions*` claims only due `PENDING` rows with `channel = 'email'`, an `action_type` in `EXECUTABLE_EMAIL_ACTION_TYPES` (`send_message`, derived from the email kinds) **and** a non-empty `recipient_email`. The outreach agent writes its `schedule_followup` wake-ups and other bookkeeping rows on the lead's channel with no message; they are timeline records, not sends, and the claim leaves them `PENDING`. The per-user claim (`claim_pending_email_actions_for_user*`, the one simpl_flow's email sender uses) also takes nothing while the seller is on vacation, while their customer is in the shortlist stage of the onboarding (`sales.user_outreach_held`, simpl_api migration `20261015150100`), or outside their active hours, read through `sales.resolve_active_hours` (unset or invalid = 07:00-19:00 UTC); a due send waits `PENDING` until the window opens. `claim_pending_email_actions_for_user_with_limits` caps only outreach sends (`first_touch`, `follow_up`, any other or missing `message_type`) by the user's remaining daily email quota. A send to a lead who engaged, `QUOTA_EXEMPT_MESSAGE_TYPES` in `journey_dispatch_rules` -- a reply (`payload.message_type = 'response'`, `REPLY_MESSAGE_TYPE`) or a follow-on (`'follow_on'`, `FOLLOW_ON_MESSAGE_TYPE`: written after a call the lead took, while the lead has not written since) -- is claimed first, whatever the quota says and unpaced (`claim_pending_email_actions_for_user(quota_exempt=True)`; `False` claims outreach only), and `sales_outreach.mark_journey_log_completed` does not count a completed email reply or follow-on towards `user_action_usage` (migrations `20261012_reply_sends_outside_email_quota.sql`, then `20261014_follow_on_outside_email_quota.sql`; a LinkedIn one still counts). On LinkedIn, `sales_outreach.linkedin_pre_send_guard` lets a `follow_on` go out wherever a `follow_up` may once the lead has written: allowed when every message of the lead was known before it was decided, blocked as `inbound_after_follow_up_decided` otherwise (`20261014b_linkedin_guard_allows_follow_on.sql`). Outreach sends are also paced: each claim takes at most the remaining quota divided by the five-minute claims left in the seller's open window (`EMAIL_CLAIM_INTERVAL_MINUTES`), so a day's sends go out through the day rather than in the first run. On top of that, every claim takes an `EmailClaimPacing` (required: no caller claims unpaced), which `services.email.send_pacing.claim_paced_email_sends` builds from the `send_pacing`, `send_warmup` and `send_health` rows of `integration.mailbox_setting` and the seller's delivered `email.message.sent` events (`OutreachEventRepository.email_send_history`): nothing while their last email went out less than `min_gap_minutes` ago, at most `max_per_claim` per run and `max_per_hour` in any hour, replies included (`max_sends`); outreach at most `max_outreach_per_day` (30) a day whatever the stage allows; and while their first email through SimpL is less than `len(daily_caps) * days_per_step` days old, outreach today is capped at `daily_caps[step]` against `used_today` (`outreach_daily_cap`; replies uncapped); and after the provider refused the mailbox (`classify_send_refusal`: `blocked` for SMTP 5.7.x, spam or reputation, `limited` for a quota or rate limit; the journey email flow keeps it as `event_data.send_refusal` on the `email.message.failed` event; a notice that the seller's own mail system refused the sender, recorded by sync as `email.mailbox.blocked`, counts as a block too), outreach pauses per the `send_health` row (block: 72 h, then 7 days at 10 a day; limit: 24 h) while replies still go; and when it bounces (`email.message.bounced`, see Email bounces): at least `bounce_min_count` (5) bounces and `bounce_rate_pause` (3 %) of the emails sent in the `bounce_window_days` (7) up to the latest bounce pause outreach until that bounce leaves the window, then 7 days at 10 a day. Gmail, Outlook and IMAP mailboxes alike; a missing or malformed row raises `MailboxSettingMissingError` and nothing is sent (migration `20261019b_mailbox_send_pacing.sql`). simpl_flow's journey email flow claims through `claim_paced_email_sends`. Before claiming, it hands back to the seller (`PENDING` -> `PENDING_APPROVAL`, reason in `payload.authority.block_reason`, the approval it had in `payload.send_hold`) every due email whose approval no longer stands (`hold_unconfirmed_email_sends_for_user`): an auto-approved one (written by the outreach pipeline, `created_by = 'agent_framework'`, with no `authority.approved_by`) when the seller's auto-approval, ICP override merged, no longer covers email (`auto_approval_off`); any one due or approved more than `SEND_APPROVAL_LAPSES_AFTER_HOURS` (72) ago (`approval_lapsed`), except an auto-approved outreach send (first touch, follow-up: not in `QUOTA_EXEMPT_MESSAGE_TYPES`), which stays queued for the daily quota however long it waits (Raff, 2026-11-10); and an auto-approved outreach send (not in `QUOTA_EXEMPT_MESSAGE_TYPES`: a reply or follow-on is not rebuilt cold backlog) written within `MAILBOX_REBUILD_WINDOW_HOURS` (24) after simpl_api's `channel_activation_gate` cleared that connection's queue when a mailbox was connected (`mailbox_reconnected`). Each `approval_lapsed` hand-back writes
`pending_actions.approval_lapsed` (`APPROVAL_LAPSED_EVENT`) on the row's connection in the same
transaction, `action_id` the journey_log id, dated when the approval lapsed (due or approved
time + 72 h). A person's approval must stamp `authority.approved_by` (`set_pending_with_version`, simpl_api's `approval_stamped_payload_sql`); a pipeline row queued `PENDING` without it is read as auto-approved. If the check fails, nothing is claimed for that seller in that run. A new kind of sendable row must be added to the table before any sender will pick it up. A send with no recipient is never claimed and a sender must never derive one: the row carries its own destination and identity, and from the moment it is `PENDING` or `RUNNING` every payload writer in the repository refuses to change any of `DELIVERY_FIELDS` -- `action_type`, `channel`, `recipient_email`, `cc_emails`, `bcc_emails`, `recipient_linkedin_url`, `recipient_linkedin_urn`, `expected_sender_account` (`assert_delivery_fields_unchanged`, raising `invalid_transition: ...`) -- whatever status the write moves the row to: a claim, a completion or a cancellation that also rewrites one is refused, and so is `admin_override_fields` switching the channel while un-approving. To change a destination, un-approve with the fields intact, edit the draft, approve again. Stamp results with `update_payload` as before; restating the approved values passes, rewriting them does not.

A send that fails on the provider's temporary failure is tried again instead of failing (`services/email/send_retry.py`, since 0.8.379, after the Microsoft 365 outage of 2026-11-13 left 114 Outlook sends `FAILED`). `is_transient_send_failure(error)` is narrow on purpose, because retrying an email that went out sends it twice: a Microsoft Graph code for an internal or unavailable service (`UnknownError`, `ServiceNotAvailable`, `ErrorServerBusy`...), Gmail's `500 Internal error encountered` / `backendError`, a provider HTTP 500/502/503/504 relayed in the tool result, a connection that never opened (`httpx.ConnectError`), an IMAP `MailboxErrorKind.TRANSIENT`. Never a read timeout or a Composio API error (the tool may have run), an Outlook `Failed to send draft` (the draft stays in the mailbox), a mailbox refusal (`classify_send_refusal`, which pauses outreach instead), a dead connection, or anything unrecognised. `plan_send_retry(previous, error=..., now=...)` waits `SEND_RETRY_FIRST_DELAY` (15 min), doubling each time, and gives no next attempt once `SEND_RETRY_WINDOW` (two days) has passed since the first failure; the state rides on the payload under `SEND_RETRY_KEY` (`send_retry`: `first_failed_at`, `attempts`, `last_failed_at`, `last_error` capped at 500 characters, `next_attempt_at`). `JourneyLogRepository.defer_email_send(journey_log_id, retry_at=..., send_retry=...)` moves the claimed row `RUNNING` -> `PENDING` with `scheduled_at = retry_at` (a row that left `RUNNING` meanwhile is left alone, `None`); it re-enters the queue through `trg_journey_log_authority`, so it can come back held or cancelled, and it waits for the lead's send window like every due send. simpl_flow's journey email flow defers on a transient failure and marks `FAILED` (with `email.message.failed`) only on the attempt that fails after the window.

`JourneyLogRepository.set_cancelled(journey_log_id=..., reason=None, commit=True)` rejects a `PENDING_APPROVAL` draft (writing `reason`, when given, as `payload.cancellation`), is idempotent on a `CANCELLED` row, and raises `invalid_transition: <STATUS> -> CANCELLED` for every other status: an approved `PENDING` row is not taken back. A reply Reply Confidence queued on its own is not cancelled either; it goes back to the seller's review (see "Reply Confidence settings and return to review" below).

Every `JourneyLogRepository` writer that checks or rebuilds a row (`set_pending_with_version`, `set_cancelled`, `update_payload_field(s)`, `set_active_version`, `append_regenerated_version`, `update_schedule`, `admin_override_fields`, and `update_payload` when it names a delivery field) re-reads it under its `FOR UPDATE` lock, so a caller may load the row first in the same session (as simpl_api's endpoints do) and the writer still judges the row as it is now. Each of them, `update_payload` aside, raises `ValueError("journey_log_not_found: <id>")` for a row that does not exist.

The send-time recheck (`sales_outreach.recheck_journey_log_authority`) also holds and returns a reply Reply Confidence queued, and every move of such a reply out of the queue, this hold's and the dispatch authority rule's included, is recorded as a return: see "Reply Confidence settings and return to review" below.

The outreach queue (`BatchTriggerQueueRepository`: `get_pending_triggers`, `count_dispatchable_ulcs`, `claim_next_ulc_work`, `claim_ulc_work`) hands out nothing for a seller listed in `sales_view.outreach_held_user` (simpl_api migration `20261015150100`: their customer is in the shortlist stage of the onboarding, until SimpL moves them on), replies included, unlike a vacation; the rows stay queued and run once released. The view is read once per query (`NOT IN`), not per row. `tests/setup_test_db.sql` stands a table in for it.

A customer at its call cap (simpl_api `sales.customer_call_cap_reached`, migration `20261105170000`; Raff, 2026-11-05) gets nobody new: the same four queue seams leave the `lead_claimed` row of its connections queued, neither run nor skipped, until the cap is raised or removed; every other row runs, so open conversations go on. The customer is the connection's work customer (`sales.connection_customer`, a lent identity's borrower). Sends already queued go back to approval: migration `20261105c_call_cap_holds_auto_sends.sql` adds `sales_outreach.call_cap_holds_auto_send(ulc_id, payload)` (no person's approval, `created_by = 'agent_framework'`, and the work customer at its cap), which `trg_journey_log_authority` applies to a send entering or leaving `PENDING`/`RUNNING` (queued, claimed by the extension or the email sender) and `recheck_journey_log_authority` applies before the provider call: `PENDING_APPROVAL` with `payload.authority.block_reason = 'call_cap'` (LinkedIn guard: `authority_call_cap`). `hold_unconfirmed_email_sends_for_user` reads it first (`SEND_HOLD_CALL_CAP`, in `payload.send_hold`). A send a person approved still goes. The call-cancellation message (ENG-956, `created_by = 'agentic_orchestrator'`) is not held.

An `invite_call` row is booked from `payload.invite_call` (simpl_api's calendar executor sends its `proposed_start_datetime` and `timezone`); `call_data` is the copy a version carries. `JourneyLogRepository` keeps the two in step on these rows: activating a version (`append_regenerated_version(..., activate=True)`, `set_active_version`, `set_pending_with_version`) or writing `call_data` / `call_data.*` through `update_payload_field(s)` merges that call data over `invite_call`, and manual versions and the original snapshot record `invite_call`, so the version a seller sees is the invite that goes out. Edit the slot by writing `invite_call`; other rows are unchanged.

A LinkedIn message the pre-send guard blocks for a lead's message with no `message_received` row waits for a check instead of failing: the guard's result carries `recheck_held`, the row is `PENDING_APPROVAL` with `payload.regenerate_pending` (`source: send_recheck`) and `payload.send_recheck` (`state`, `attempts`, `approval`, `missing_message_ids`, `had_auto_send`, `note`; `SendRecheck.from_payload`), and a `seller_regenerate` trigger with `send_recheck: true`. The worker ends it with `settle_kept` (outcome `kept`, `journey_log_status`: the rule may still refuse it), or the usual `settle_rewrite`, `settle_superseded`, `settle_review_request` with `recheck_note`. `send_recheck_facts` gives the message type and whether the lead wrote last, in the guard's scope. Events: `outreach.send.held`, `outreach.send.rechecked`, and `outreach.send.failed` (`event_data.reason`) when the check rejected the message.

Since 0.8.274 (migration `20261103_draft_conversation_recheck.sql`, ENG-1000) the same hold covers every open message whose conversation moved after it was written, on every channel: `PENDING_APPROVAL`, `PENDING` and `RUNNING` rows. News is `sales_outreach.draft_conversation_changes(journey_log_id)` (`{as_of, message_ids, event_ids, changed}`): a message on the lead's threads in the customer's mailboxes (the lead, a colleague, the seller by hand, SimpL from another connection) stored after `draft_conversation_as_of`, or a meeting fact (`call.scheduled`, `call.rescheduled`, `call.declined`, `call.declined_by_lead`, `call.declined_by_lead.withdrawn`, `booking_page.booked`, `calendar.invite.sent`; since `20261112090000_draft_news_not_billing.sql` the pay-per-call customer's `call.accepted` / `call.acceptance.withdrawn` are billing's, not news) on any of the customer's connections to the lead. Since `20261112090100_draft_news_skips_invite_acceptances.sql` a message ingestion marked `provider_metadata.invite_acceptance = 'wordless'` (a calendar app's acceptance the lead wrote nothing in) is not news either. It is checked when the news lands (triggers on `sales_outreach.message` and `outreach_events`) and in `recheck_journey_log_authority` after an allow, which answers `{"outcome": "review", "reason": "conversation_changed", "status": "PENDING_APPROVAL", "recheck": {...}, "changes": {...}}`, or `reason` `lead_message_waits_for_its_walk` (a `message_received` not yet walked: its walk supersedes the draft) or `conversation_changed_unchecked` (the row could not be held; a seller's Send after three spent checks is allowed). `payload.send_recheck` adds `reason` (`SendRecheckReason`: `conversation_changed`, `lead_message_unrecorded`), `held_from`, `release_to` (`SendRecheckRelease`: `queue` only for a row approved before the hold, else `seller`), `changed_since`, `changed_message_ids`, `changed_event_ids`; the trigger adds `send_recheck_reason` (`RegenerateTrigger.send_recheck_reason`). `settle_kept` re-queues only when `SendRecheck.queues_on_keep`. After applying the migration, `sales_outreach.hold_open_drafts_with_news(since)` holds the drafts already out of date. Since 0.8.317 (`20261106_draft_recheck_on_write.sql`, ENG-1118) a message draft is also checked when it is saved, and news reaches a draft by when it read its thread (`draft_conversation_as_of`), not when its row was saved: a reply saved after the seller answered by hand is held as it is written.

Every version in `regeneration_history` can say who produced it and in which run: `append_regenerated_version`, `update_payload_field` and `update_payload_fields` take optional `edited_by` (`user:<uuid>`, `admin:<uuid>`, `agent`) and `pipeline_run_id`, recorded on the entry (null when not passed); manual edits by different authors are never merged into one version. `admin_override_fields(..., edited_by="admin:<uuid>")` records a content or subject override as a manual version by that admin; without `edited_by` it still leaves the history alone.

`JourneyRepository.get_pending_actions(ulc_id, executable_only=True)` returns only the open rows with an execution kind (queued sends and invitations, not placeholders or review cards); `cancel_pending_actions_for_ulc(..., statuses=("PENDING_APPROVAL",))` cancels drafts and leaves an approved `PENDING` row to the sender that will drain it. Both defaults are unchanged. `ensure_journey_run(ulc_id, company_id, journey_id)` returns the ULC's active run or creates it with `ON CONFLICT (ulc_id, journey_id) DO NOTHING`, so a run another writer committed in the meantime (the autopilot upserts it just after the claim) is reused rather than failing on `journey_run_unique`; a conflict with a non-active run for that journey raises `RuntimeError`. `withdraw_drafts(ulc_id, [(action_id, reason), ...], withdrawn_by=..., trigger_ids=...)` cancels drafts of that ULC still `PENDING_APPROVAL` on its active run, each with its own reason in `payload.cancellation` (`withdrawn: true`); approved, queued, sent and other connections' rows are skipped and logged, and it returns the ids it withdrew.

A Gmail, Outlook or HubSpot service built with a `ComposioConnectionService` marks its connection row and raises `StaleComposioConnectionError` (wrapped in the provider's own error) when Composio reports the account unusable: a structured expired/revoked code on a raised error, or a failed tool result relaying the provider's HTTP 401 (`data.status_code`), which marks the row `expired` with reason `COMPOSIO_PROVIDER_TOKEN_EXPIRED_REASON`; or a `GMAIL_GET_PROFILE` result relaying a 403 for insufficient authentication scopes (the user granted no mail access), which marks the row `revoked` with reason `COMPOSIO_PROVIDER_SCOPE_MISSING_REASON`. A missing scope on any other tool stays an ordinary failure: it can be one unticked permission or a scope our auth config never requests. Detect it anywhere in a wrapped chain with `simpl_core.composio.stale_connection_from_error` and treat it as handled: the row is already marked, only the user reconnecting fixes it, and the class sets `__simpl_notify__ = False` so simpl_tracker's default notify skips it.

`sales_outreach.message.provider_metadata` holds identifiers, envelope, and send provenance only: the keys in `simpl_core.services.email.utils.MESSAGE_PROVIDER_METADATA_KEYS`. Subject and body are never copied into it; read them by decrypting `encrypted_subject` and `encrypted_body`. Ingestion drops any payload key outside that set, so a consumer that needs a new field in `provider_metadata` adds it to the set first. `invite_acceptance` is ingestion's own mark, never a payload's: `ingest_thread_messages` writes `provider_metadata.invite_acceptance = 'wordless'` (`simpl_core.services.email.invite_acceptance`: `INVITE_ACCEPTANCE_METADATA_KEY`, `WORDLESS`) in the INSERT of a message not from the owner that `is_wordless_invite_acceptance(subject, body)` matches: a calendar app's "Accettata: ..." / "Accepted: ..." with an empty body, a mail gateway's stamp, or Google's template without a note. An acceptance carrying the lead's words is never marked. Messages stored before the mark existed carry none: readers fall back to the rule (simpl-outreach) or treat them as before. The sync gives `sales_outreach.conversation_thread.metadata` the same allowlist for the thread's head email: read the subject from the thread's `subject` column, never from its metadata. A Gmail message labelled `DRAFT` arrives as `EmailDTO.is_draft=True` and is never stored as a message: an unsent draft is not something the owner said. The thread's newest draft is kept in `sales_outreach.seller_draft` (one row per thread, subject and body in the vault) and returned by `ConversationReadService.fetch_for_ulc` as `seller_drafts`: context for what the seller is writing, never part of `messages`.

A reply on a thread the owner's connection already holds is stored under that connection whoever sends it: the sync no longer resolves such a thread from its newest sender, so a lead answering from another address (a shared mailbox, a longer form of the address, a new employer) is no longer dropped. When the names say the sender is the connection's lead, the address is added to the lead as a `sales.lead_contact` row with `scope='user'` (the owner), `is_primary=false` and `source='email_thread_reply'`: it never outranks the address outreach already writes to. Anyone else at the lead's company, a sender or someone the lead's side copies (To/Cc), is filed under their own lead (the one the customer knows them as, else a placeholder), with the address recorded for the owner (`source='email_thread_reply'` or `'email_thread_copy'`); free-mail, other companies, role inboxes and machines stay unlinked participants. A sender whose display name names someone other than the lead (the lead's surname after another first name) takes an address the thread holds for the lead off the lead: its `sales.lead_contact` rows on the lead get `email_status='another_person'`, which no reader takes as the lead's (`seller_contact_reach(...).visible`). The message still wakes a connection: the row moves to the writer only when the writer has the owner's connection or the lead keeps another row on the thread.

On a mailbox a sales identity sends from with its own alias (`send_from_email` on a connection whose `mailbox_email` is a colleague's), a thread is the identity's only when that alias is on it; every other thread is the mailbox owner's, the user whose address the mailbox is (`SharedMailbox`, `load_shared_mailbox`). Each user's sync stores only its own threads and leaves the others to their user's sync, judging whose a thread is on every address it shows: the new messages' headers, the stored participant rows, and the From/To/Cc/Bcc of its stored messages (a calendar invitation stores no row for the invited mailbox). A thread an identity's sync filed under the identity's conversation with none of its aliases on it is taken back by the owner's sync the next time it stores a message there: the thread moves with its messages, draft and attachments to the owner's conversation, and the identity's owner rows become the owner's or a colleague's (`SharedMailbox.takes_back`, `ConversationThreadRepository.move_to_conversation`, `ConversationParticipantRepository.hand_owner_rows`); the identity's sync writes only as its alias (the mailbox's address is the owner's, so it types as a colleague), and neither sync learns a colleague's address of the mailbox as its own alias. A thread is the user's whose conversation holds it (`sales_outreach.conversation.owner_user_id`). When two users sync one mailbox (a sales identity whose email connection is a colleague's mailbox, sending from an alias of it), the other user's sync leaves the thread's stored participants as they are and files a new address as the owner only when it is the conversation owner's (`ConversationService.upsert_participants`). `ConversationReadService.fetch_for_ulc` gives a connection every thread with its lead that its user is on: one they hold a participant row on, one their conversation holds, or one where any of their addresses is a participant's (profile, connection mailbox and send-as, active aliases; `addresses_of_users`), whoever that row points at. So a colleague copied on a thread reads it, and a row another user's sync once claimed no longer hides it. Pass `viewer_user_id` to get `viewer_thread_ids`: the returned threads that user is on, colleagues' included, which a caller withholding colleagues' messages keeps readable. A message from an internal sender (`participant_type` 3) is read per connection, keyed on the sender row's type rather than `is_sender_owner`: one of the connection's user's own addresses is that user (`is_from_owner`); anyone else is `is_from_internal`, a colleague or the company's mail server (a delivery failure), named by `sender_name` (the colleague's user name when the address is a user's) and never the lead. `compute_stats` leaves such messages out of the user's and the lead's figures. A digest marked `head_snippets_superseded` is left out.

## Email on other providers (IMAP/SMTP)

A mailbox on a provider other than Google or Microsoft (Aruba, Namecheap, OVH, any host) is the `imap` integration (`integration_configuration` row, category `email`, seeded active and beta by `migrations/20261019_mailbox_imap_smtp.sql`; beta keeps the card admin-only until `is_beta` is cleared). Its adapter is `ImapSmtpEmailService` (`services/email/imap_smtp.py`), an ordinary `EmailService`:

```python
service = await get_connected_email_service(
    user_id, "imap",
    db_manager=db_manager,          # required: settings rows and the failed-login count
    composio_api_key=..., composio_toolkit_versions=...,   # unused for imap
    mailbox_cipher=None,            # default: KmsMailboxCipher(MAILBOX_KMS_KEY_NAME)
)
```

- **Install and environment.** The consumer installs `simpl-core[mailbox]` and sets `MAILBOX_KMS_KEY_NAME` (the Cloud KMS CryptoKey resource name, `projects/.../cryptoKeys/...`; not a secret) in Doppler. Its service account needs `roles/cloudkms.cryptoKeyEncrypterDecrypter` on that key.
- **The password** is encrypted by KMS and stored as ciphertext in `user_connection.credentials` (`EncryptedMailboxPassword.to_credentials()`), bound to the seller and the mailbox address (`mailbox_associated_data(user_id, email)`). Never store, log or return it in clear.
- **Looking up an address first** (ENG-915, `services/email/mailbox_lookup.py`): `look_up_mailbox(db, email=)` returns a `MailboxLookup` whose `kind` (`LookupKind`) decides what the seller is asked next, before any password: `use_integration` (Google or Microsoft: `integration_slug`), `known` (a preset: `provider_name`, `app_password_url`, `help_url`, `servers`), `discovered` (no preset, but the domain's own autoconfig, Thunderbird's database or the domain's SRV records name allowed servers: `servers`, `source`, `provider_name`, `help_url`), `manual` (nothing names servers: the seller types them) or `invalid_email`. A read: no row is written.
- **Address-only connect** (ENG-864, `services/email/mailbox_domain_config.py`): `connect_mailbox` / `verify_mailbox` with no preset and no `servers` try the servers `find_address_servers(email, domain=, mx_hosts=, mx_reverse_names=, discover=, fetch_config=)` finds (the domain's own settings first via `fetch_domain_config`, a `DomainConfigFetcher`: HTTPS, public addresses only, pinned to the vetted address with the certificate checked for the name, no redirects, 64 KB, 4 s), and open a provider request with the seller waiting only when nothing is found. The log `detail` names the source (`servers from the domain's own settings (https://...)`). `look_up_mailbox`, `connect_mailbox` and `verify_mailbox` take `fetch_config=` like `discover=`; tests pass a stub so nothing reaches the network.
- **Connecting.** simpl_api calls `connect_mailbox(db, user_id=, email=, password=, cipher=, servers=None)` (`services/email/mailbox_connect.py`), which returns a `ConnectOutcome` whose `status` (`ConnectStatus`) is what the seller is told:
  - `connected`: both logins worked; the password is stored KMS-encrypted and the row is `active`.
  - `use_integration`: the domain is Google or Microsoft (a `mailbox_redirect_rule` row); `integration_slug` is `gmail` or `outlook`. Servers given are ignored.
  - `provider_pending`: no preset or redirect rule matches the domain and no `servers` were given. A provider request is opened or joined (`request_opened` is true once per domain: the caller tells the team). **No password is sent anywhere or stored.**
  - `wrong_password` (with the preset's `app_password_url`), `wrong_servers` (servers the seller confirmed do not resolve, are not public or use a port we refuse), `unavailable`, `rate_limited` (`connect_rate_limit` setting), `invalid_email`. `failed_servers` names which of IMAP and SMTP refused.
  Matching (`mailbox_presets.match_mailbox_provider`) reads the domain's MX hosts, then their reverse DNS names, against the redirect rules first and the presets second. A preset's servers always win. With no preset, `servers` (`MailboxServers(imap=, smtp=, username=None)`: what the lookup found, or the servers of an existing connection) are tried under the same endpoint rules. A connect on servers found for the address (given as `servers` or found by the address-only connect) opens no provider request: the next seller at the domain gets the same servers from the lookup. `update_mailbox_password` reconnects an existing (even revoked) mailbox, on its preset or on the servers it was connected with; `disconnect_mailbox` erases the ciphertext and revokes. Every attempt is a row in `integration.mailbox_connect_attempt` (no password).
- **Onboarding, before the person's row exists.** `verify_mailbox(db, user_id=, email=, password=, cipher=, config=, display_name=, servers=, join_request=False)` is the connect without any row: its `VerifiedMailbox` carries the outcome, the `config` and the encrypted `credentials`. simpl_api parks them with `onboarding_choices.with_mailbox` (metadata key `mailbox`, apart from `connections`) and logs the attempt with `log_connect_attempt`; the finalize's `apply_onboarding_choices` writes the row with `save_mailbox_connection` and erases the parked password from the draft.
- **Mailbox log** (`services/email/mailbox_log.py`, migration `20261021_mailbox_attempt_kind_detail.sql`): `integration.mailbox_connect_attempt` is the mailbox's durable log. `kind` is `connect` (every connect or password update, invalid and rate-limited ones included; the connect rate limit counts only `connect` rows it did not refuse), `sync` (an IMAP read or login that failed during a sync), `send` (a send whose login or Sent copy failed), `revoked`, or `auto_resolve` (one row per waiting seller, `detail` = the run's `as_text()`). `reason` is the machine code; `detail` is the readable account: which server and port (`IMAP imap.example.com:993 tls during list_emails: ...`), what it answered, which step failed, the folders found. Never a password or message content. `record_mailbox_event(db, MailboxLogSubject(...), kind=, outcome=, reason=, detail=)` is best effort; connect rows are not (the rate limit depends on them). `MailboxConnectionHealth.record_auth_failure(kind, detail)` and `record_failure(kind, reason, detail)` are how `ImapSmtpEmailService` reports; `UserConnectionMailboxHealth` needs the `subject`.
- **Provider requests** (`services/email/mailbox_provider_requests.py`): one open `mailbox_provider_request` per domain, its waiting sellers in `mailbox_provider_request_waiter`, and `discovery` hints for the admin (SRV records and Thunderbird's provider database, `mailbox_discovery`; no password involved). A request opens only when nothing names servers for the address (`provider_pending`) or the seller asks (`request_provider(db, user_id=, email=, ...)`, which takes `fetch_config=`, `check_names=` and `transport=` like the connect). An ask whose own address lookup finds servers stores them under `discovery[ADDRESS_SERVERS_KEY]` (`"address_servers"`, `ServerChoice.as_hint()`: `source`, `imap`, `smtp`, `username_format`). `resolve_provider_request(session, request_id, NewPreset(row) | ExtendPreset(key, suffixes) | ExtendRedirect(key, suffixes), settings=, resolved_by=)` applies it, refuses one that would not match the request's MX hosts (`ProviderRequestError`), closes the request and returns the waiters to email; the caller then calls `mark_waiters_notified`. `dismiss_provider_request` closes one without a preset.
- **Auto-resolve** (`services/email/mailbox_auto_resolve.py`): `auto_resolve_provider_request(db, request_id, resolved_by=, transport=None, discover=discover_provider_hints)` builds a preset from fresh public sources (Thunderbird's database, then SRV records), picks the MX hosts' common parent as its suffix, checks both servers without a password (public addresses, certificate verified, a password login offered: `imap_client.imap_server_capabilities`, `smtp_client.smtp_server_auth_methods`), then saves it with `resolve_provider_request`. It never raises for a provider it cannot resolve: the returned `AutoResolveReport` has one `AutoResolveStep` per step, `ok`, `resolved` (the waiters to email, as for a manual resolution) and `as_text()`, the Markdown log an admin pastes into an agent chat. No session is held during network IO. `discovery` also carries `providers` (each database domain's display name and help page). When the public sources know nothing and the request holds `address_servers`, the failed `discover` step says there is nothing to set up (those servers serve the address without a preset; dismiss the request), and `as_text()` lists them.
- **The connection row** is written by `save_mailbox_connection(session, user_id=, email=, config=, credentials=)` (`connect_mailbox`, the onboarding finalize) with `mailbox_connection_config(...)` (`preset` is `None` on servers the seller confirmed) and `external_account_id` = the mailbox address.
- **Settings are rows.** `integration.mailbox_server_preset` (one per provider), `integration.mailbox_redirect_rule` (Google, Microsoft) and `integration.mailbox_setting` (one per global setting; `send_pacing`, `send_warmup` and `send_health` there pace every provider's sends, see the email claim above) are read fresh by `load_mailbox_config(session)` (settings alone: `load_mailbox_settings`) each time a connection is used; a missing setting row raises `MailboxSettingMissingError` (nothing connects), an invalid preset is skipped and logged. Every change is in `integration.mailbox_config_history`. Adding a provider is a preset row; see the ENG-864 plan for the checklist.
- **Security rules are code** (`mailbox_endpoints`): IMAP on 993 with TLS only, SMTP on 465 (TLS) or 587 (STARTTLS required), certificates verified, no address literal as a host, and every address a host resolves to must be public (no private, loopback, link-local or metadata range). The session connects to the vetted address, so DNS cannot change between the check and the connection.
- **Ids.** A message is its RFC 5322 `Message-ID` (`provider_message_id` = `internet_message_id`); a thread is the `Message-ID` of its root, from `References` or, without it, by following `In-Reply-To`. A reply passes `threadId` and `replyMessageId` as for Gmail; the adapter sets `In-Reply-To`/`References` and the subject `Re: <parent subject>`.
- **It never changes the seller's mail.** Reads use EXAMINE and `BODY.PEEK`; the only write is the copy of a sent message `APPEND`ed to Sent (a failure there is logged, never retried by sending again). `search_emails`, `mark_as_read` and `mark_as_unread` raise.
- **Failed logins.** A refused login is counted on `connection_metadata.mailbox_auth_failures`; at the `auth_failures_before_revoke` setting the row is marked `revoked` (reason `mailbox_auth_failed`) and `StaleMailboxConnectionError` is raised: a `StaleComposioConnectionError`, so `stale_connection_from_error` and simpl_flow's handling treat it as "marked, only the seller can fix it, no alert". A login that works resets the count.
- **Connections.** One IMAP connection per service object, closed after `imap_idle_close_seconds` unused or by `await service.aclose()` (a no-op on the HTTP adapters); each send opens its own SMTP connection.
- Which integrations count as email: `integration_configuration.category = 'email'` (`UserConnectionRepository.list_active_email_connections()` and `get_most_recent_active_email_provider()` without `allowed_slugs`), and in code `EMAIL_PROVIDER_SLUGS` (`services/email/schemas.py`) for "is this thread an email thread".

## Entity deduplication

`simpl_core.services.dedup` is the single place identity-matching rules live for
companies and leads. Do not write your own matcher.

```python
from simpl_core.services.dedup import (
    CompanyIdentityClaim,
    CompanyResolver,
    LeadIdentityClaim,
    LeadResolver,
)

outcome = await CompanyResolver().resolve(
    CompanyIdentityClaim(linkedin_url=..., landing_url=..., email_domain=...),
    session=session,
)
if outcome.is_match:
    company_id = outcome.entity_id
elif outcome.should_escalate:
    ...  # enrich, or ask an agent; outcome.proposals / .conflicts say why
```

Resolution is the cheap rung of an escalation ladder. `matched` is the automatic
path; the other three mean *do not auto-link, escalate*, and they escalate
differently:

| action | meaning | next step |
|---|---|---|
| `ambiguous` | several candidates, nothing to choose between them | they are already loaded — often duplicates, so merge. **Never create**: the claim is probably one of them |
| `blocked` | a candidate contradicted the claim on a trusted field | do not link to it — but creating is right, the contradiction is evidence of a *different* entity |
| `insufficient_signal` | nothing found | enrich, then retry; safe to create |

**Branch on `action`, not on `is_match`.** Read as a boolean, three distinct
refusals collapse into "no objection, go ahead" — which is how a refusal to link
becomes a merge, and how "there are several" becomes a fabricated third row.

`blocked` deserves care: it means a weak signal matched but a trusted field
contradicted it. Two companies do share one domain, and one mailbox does forward
for two people, so the right response is to create the new entity and *not* link
to the refused candidate. The exception is a refusal on **scope** rather than
identity — see `require_company_scope` below — where the candidate is the same
entity, merely outside the bounds the caller asked for.

Every non-matched outcome carries `candidates` — `CandidateRef` objects with
name, LinkedIn URL, landing URL and whether the row is a placeholder — so a
caller can render a choice or log a rejection without another query.

**The resolver never picks a winner.** Where several rows match a
non-discriminating key it narrows on evidence the claim carries (exact name,
location, LinkedIn identity) and returns `ambiguous` if that does not reach
one. Ranking on apex-URL, has-LinkedIn or newer `created_at` is not evidence
about which company a message came from, and guessing produces confident wrong
matches.

Rows storing one LinkedIn page under different spellings (`/x` and `/x/`,
`caff%C3%A8` and `caffè`, a country host) are one candidate set. Among rows
naming one page, the row holding `company_linkedin_id` is the page's record and
is kept, even over a duplicate carrying the claim's exact name: the LinkedIn
enricher merges the other rows into it and deletes them.

Identity precedence, strongest first — company: `company_linkedin_id`,
LinkedIn URL, exact normalized landing host, a registrable-domain fallback, then
name+location (proposed, never applied). Lead: LinkedIn URL,
`mini_profile_urn`, email, then name **within an already-resolved company**.
Generic and shared email domains (gmail.com, linktr.ee, …) never resolve a
company — and the blocklist is applied *after* the registrable-domain reduction
too, so `acme.medium.com` cannot reach every company under `medium.com`. The
registrable-domain fallback reads the hosts under the claim's domain through
`ix_company_normalized_host_reversed` (migration
`20261030d_company_reversed_host_index.sql`); without that index each lookup
scans `sales.company`, so a database must have it before running core 0.8.250+.

`LeadIdentityClaim.require_company_scope` bounds the answer by `company_id`
rather than merely giving it context. Leave it off for conversation ingestion:
an email is a per-person identity, and someone who has changed employer is still
the same person, so the answer should not be withheld. Turn it on when asking
"who is named N at company C" — without it the email strategy runs first, never
consults the company, and can return someone employed elsewhere.

`CompanyResolver(domain_policy=...)` picks how far the host strategy may reach:

- `"permissive"` (default) — *attribution*, e.g. filing a conversation under a
  company. A host match is accepted unless it contradicts a LinkedIn identity
  the claim already carries.
- `"strict"` — *linking*, e.g. CRM onboarding, where the answer creates a
  durable bidirectional link. If the claim carries a LinkedIn identity that
  matched nothing, resolution stops rather than falling back to the domain, and
  a host match is only accepted against a candidate with no LinkedIn identity
  of its own.

A company nobody has yet is stood in by `CompanyPlacementService.resolve_or_placeholder`
(a placeholder keyed by domain, then LinkedIn page, then name, recorded in the claim
ledger); never write a `sales.company` row inline. Placement holds
`CompanyPlacementService.lock_lookup_keys(claim, session=…)` before it resolves, so two
writers founding one company at once found it once; a caller that resolves first and
places later takes them itself before resolving.

`LeadIdentityClaim(customer_id=…)` resolves within what that customer may see (addresses
through global rows or rows private to its users, a name only through exactly one fitting
namesake, never a Dummy lead); `LeadPlacementService` keys such a claim per customer and
writes its placeholder no global address. `simpl_core.services.dedup.lead_resolver.profiles_contradict`
is the one rule for a stored LinkedIn profile contradicting a weak match.

A lead whose company is unknown waits on its own stand-in company (origin
`inbound_person`; the shared `(Unknown company)` row is legacy and leads leave it)
(`simpl_core.services.dedup.parking`: `park_lead`, `record_parked_claim`,
`move_parked_lead`), with a pending claim `LeadPlaceholderReconciler` tries on every pass
(current experience, then the claim's company evidence). Run the reconciler, and it moves
the lead with its connections and runs once one resolves.

### Checked examples and replay (entity resolution, phase 1)

The entity resolution service (plan: `entity-resolution-service-plan-2026-11-17.md` in the
workspace) measures every name stage against **checked examples**: mentions whose true
company a key gave apart from the name. The first source is a LinkedIn post's company
tag where Graphia read an organization at the tag's place in the text.

```python
from simpl_core.graphia.log import LogReads
from simpl_core.graphia.tag_examples import build_tag_examples, tagged_post_ids
from simpl_core.services.dedup.checked_examples import CheckedExampleStore
from simpl_core.services.dedup.replay import ReplayStore, replay

post_ids = await tagged_post_ids(db, percent=0.5, limit=20_000, seed=1)
build = await build_tag_examples(db, LogReads(config), variant_name=..., post_ids=post_ids)
run = await replay(stage, list(build.examples.values()), run_id=..., replayed_at=...)
run.report()  # overall, by method, by segment: precision and recall with Wilson bounds
```

- A stage is any object with `name`, `version` and
  `async answer(examples) -> {example_id: ReplayAnswer}` (`NameStage`). It reads the
  example's name and context, never its truth.
- Outcomes: `right`, `right_company` (a twin the stage grouped with its choice), `wrong`,
  `missed` (settled on none), `escalated` (settled nothing).
- Examples (`er_checked_examples`) and outcomes (`er_replay_outcomes`) are row store
  datasets: they grow with data. One name in five is held out (`split`).
- Checked examples lean to clean names (a tag writes the page's own name): their precision
  is an upper bound for free text.
- `simpl_core.utils.proportions.wilson_lower_bound` is the interval every switch reads.
- `read_tagged_post_ids(db, reads, variant_name=..., limit=..., seed=...)` samples among
  the posts a Graphia variant read (`LogReads.served_sources`, the runs' doc keys): a sample
  of `sales.post` (`tagged_post_ids`) yields few examples, since Graphia reads about one
  post in ninety.
- **The service's name stages** (`resolution.name_replay`): `resolve_examples(examples,
  company_name_stages(profiles, cache), policy=...)` runs each example as the mention
  Graphia's run would send, its tag hidden (`example_mention`), every stage in shadow, so
  nothing is applied or written. `mechanism_answers` scores each mechanism on its own
  answers, `pipeline_answers` the stages in order (the first closing answer the policy
  uses); wrap either in `AnsweredStage` for `replay`. `replay_bounds` gives each
  mechanism's precision lower bound per `class_segment`, recorded with
  `ReadingRepository.record_replay` (`resolution.reading.replay_lower`, read back by
  `replay_bounds`); `mechanisms_markdown` is the report. `resolve_examples(...,
  concurrency=n)` runs `n` batches at once, for stages that call a model; each answer's
  `confidence` is kept in its features.
- **The judge and its check** (`resolution.judge_replay`): `judge_stages(profiles, cache,
  judge=, check=, evidence=, cases=)` is the name stages, `JudgeStage`, then
  `JudgeCheckStage`; `replay_judge(examples, stages, cache=, policy=, run_id=, now=)`
  runs them in shadow and returns a `JudgeReplay`: the judge and the check each scored
  alone, on every example and on those the stages before the judge left open
  (`open_before_judge`), each model's answers on a company by confidence band
  (`confidence_bands`) and its verdicts (`verdict_counts`); `markdown(title=)` is the
  report. No rule reads one model's answer through the other's: the gate and its rows are
  gone (the check repeated 76 of the judge's 98 wrong picks). `WithoutTruth(source,
  truth_by_mention(examples))` is a candidate source with each example's true company and
  its group taken out: every company a model then names is a namesake.
  report. A twin of the true row (a row of its group among the mention's candidates,
  `with_twins`) scores as the right company. The host gives the passages.

### The resolution contract (entity resolution, build item 2)

`simpl_core.services.dedup.resolution` is the one contract every consumer will call to ask
"which company or person is this". Nothing calls it yet: consumers move onto it at the end
of the build, and until then today's resolvers are its only stage.

```python
from simpl_core.services.dedup.models import CompanyIdentityClaim
from simpl_core.services.dedup.resolution import (
    ConsumerPolicy, EntityKind, Mention, PolicyRepository, ResolutionService,
)

mention = Mention("crm_pull", row_id, EntityKind.COMPANY,
                  company=CompanyIdentityClaim(linkedin_url=page))
policy = await PolicyRepository(db).active("crm_pull") or ConsumerPolicy(consumer="crm_pull")
service = ResolutionService()
resolutions = await service.resolve([mention], policy, session=session)  # query
same = await service.verify(mention, "company:<id>", session=session)    # query
placed = await service.place(mention, policy, session=session, source="crm_pull")  # command
```

- A **mention** is one consumer's knowledge of one entity: a `CompanyIdentityClaim` or
  `LeadIdentityClaim`, names, the document's companies, passages. `mention_id` is stable
  per (consumer, kind, `source_ref`).
- An **answer** is a typed verdict: `is`, `part_of`, `absent`, `several`, `not_an_entity`,
  `unknown`, with the method that reached it (`hard_key`, `stored_name`, `rule`, `scorer`,
  `judge`, `research`) and the entity as `company:<id>` / `lead:<id>`. There is no human
  verdict.
- `answer_from_outcome` reads today's outcomes: a hard-key match is `is` by `hard_key`
  (the lead resolver matches no name since 2026-11-19); the same person at another
  company than the one asked about is `is` with `scope = out`; `blocked` is `absent`;
  `ambiguous` is `unknown`.
- A **consumer policy** (`ConsumerPolicy`) says which methods it accepts (always
  `hard_key`; add `rule` to take the name rules' matches), whether a miss gets a
  placeholder, and whether an out-of-scope `is` is linked or refused. Policies are versioned
  in `resolution.policy`: `PolicyRepository.publish` adds the next version and makes it the
  active one; a version never changes.
- A **switch** (`off`, `shadow`, `on`) per mechanism and segment (`company/graphia`) lives in
  `resolution.config`; a segment without its own row takes its nearest parent's, else
  `off`. Only an `on` answer the policy accepts is applied; the others are kept on
  `Resolution.answers`. `stage.identifiers` is always on.
- `place` writes only what `CompanyPlacementService` / `LeadPlacementService` write today,
  and never creates for an out-of-scope `is` or beside an answer the policy does not
  accept: those come back `escalated`.
- Requires migration `20261118120000_resolution_schema.sql` before anything reads policies or
  switches.

### Links, the name index and the known path (entity resolution, build item 3)

The net of links lives in the row store; nothing here is in Postgres, and nothing runs yet.

```python
from simpl_core.services.dedup.resolution.links import Link, LinkKind, LinkOrigin, LinkStore
from simpl_core.services.dedup.resolution.name_index import CompanyNameIndex
from simpl_core.services.dedup.resolution.net import NetReader, NetStage
from simpl_core.services.dedup.resolution.rollup import roll_up

links, names = LinkStore(store), CompanyNameIndex(store)
await links.record([link], now=now, run_id=run_id)    # a fact from one source
known = await NetReader(names, links).paths(mentions)  # every path each mention reached
ups = await roll_up(entities, RollUp.OWNER, links=links, at=date.today())
```

- **Links** (`er_links`): `is`, `names`, `part_of`, `same`, `goes_with`. A link a key made
  (origin `key`) is `trusted`; one a rule or the judge made is `weak`; a refuted one is
  `ended` and stays. `Link.key` is the fact and its source: `record` writes a fact from one
  source once, keeps a held link's state, and refuses one whose fact and source were ended.
  `end` ends links, naming the decision.
- **Name keys** (`name_keys`), moved from Graphia: `main_key` (legal forms out, accents
  folded), `sorted_tokens`, `joined`, `acronym`; `person_key`. Both sides of a lookup use
  them.
- **Name index** (`er_company_name_index`): a company's own forms (name, the name before a
  tagline, its LinkedIn vanity name, its site's label) and its learned names from `names`
  links. `build_company_name_index(db, dataset, row_version=, since=)` reads the registry's
  changes; `sweep_removed_companies` tombstones companies the registry no longer holds;
  `index_learned_names` writes the learned ones. `er_person_name_index` holds a person at
  an employer (`build_person_name_index`).
- **Known path** (`net`): a company mention enters by its names; a brand's name reaches its
  owner as `part_of`; trusted `same` links move a path to the company kept, three steps at
  most; a weak link's far end is one more candidate; a name reaching more than
  `CROWDED_KEY` companies is not followed. `KnownPaths.independent(entity)` counts paths that
  share no link or source; `contradicted` says trusted paths disagree. `NetStage` only gives
  candidates.
- **Roll-up** (`rollup`): `exact`, `owner`, `domestic_ultimate` (with a `CountryLookup` such
  as `registry_countries(db)`), `group`, over trusted `part_of` links that hold at the date;
  a company with several owners, a loop or `MAX_DEPTH` stops the walk. `ancestors` gives
  every company above one.
- No backfill: relations and names come only from the service's own decisions, which are
  re-checked. A data set the product does not maintain (a vendor's one-off import) is never
  loaded as links.

### Candidates for a company name (entity resolution, build item 4)

The registry rows a company mention may be, with what the rules read of each. Moved from
Graphia's retrieval, company groups and registry reads. It only reads.

```python
from simpl_core.services.dedup.resolution.candidates import (
    CompanyCandidates, NameQuery, found_on_paths,
)

candidates = CompanyCandidates(db)
query = NameQuery(mention_id, mention.names, companies=document_company_ids)
in_request = await candidates.by_name([query])             # Postgres only
in_worker = await candidates.of_found([query], {mention_id: found_on_paths(known)})
```

- `by_name` asks the registry for each name in any letter case (`lower(name)`), by its
  letters and digits, by its main key with each legal form after it (kept only when the
  row's main key is the name's: "Example Ceramics" reaches "Example Ceramics S.r.l."), and
  as a LinkedIn page when no row has the name. `of_found` takes what the name index found
  (`net.NetReader`) and reads those rows by id.
- Each `Candidate` carries how it was found (`found_by`: `name`, `joined`, `legal_form`,
  `page`, `document`, or the name index's `registry:<form>` / `names:<kind>` and `same`),
  its LinkedIn identity, country (alpha-3), size and followers.
- The document's own companies are always candidates (`document`), first.
- `company_groups.group_rows` puts rows of one company in one group: one page, one site,
  sister sites; two LinkedIn pages stay apart; a bare or misnamed row joins the one group
  of its name. `lead_row` picks a group's row to link.
- Where a mention's rows are two companies or more, or its name is short, each row gets
  its `strength`: what our data holds of it (connections, posts, job offers, people),
  each count capped (`registry.row_strength`).

### Rules, triage and tags (entity resolution, build item 4)

Graphia's rules version 6 as stages of the contract, each with its own switch, sharing one
read of the candidates per mention. Nothing calls them yet.

```python
from simpl_core.services.dedup.resolution.rules import (
    CandidateCache, DocumentCompanyRule, DominantRowRule, LookupStage, NetCandidates,
    RegistryCandidates, TriageStage, UniqueExactRule, class_segment,
)

cache = CandidateCache(RegistryCandidates(CompanyCandidates(db)))  # NetCandidates in a worker
stages = [IdentifierStage(session), TriageStage(), LookupStage(cache),
          DocumentCompanyRule(cache), UniqueExactRule(cache), DominantRowRule(cache)]
resolutions = await run_stages(stages, mentions, switches=board, policy=policy,
                               segment=class_segment)
```

- `stage.triage`: a place or a generic noun (`name_classes.GENERIC_NAMES`) with no
  identifier is `not_an_entity`.
- `stage.lookup`: ranked candidates (same name, same words in another order, strength,
  followers); a document's company whose name shares nothing with the mention's is
  dropped. It only narrows.
- `rule.document_company`: one company the document is tied to has the name (0.98).
- `rule.unique_exact`: one company has the name, of the language's country or large, and
  no site the mention gives contradicts it; common words need a large row, a short name a
  large row our data holds well (0.95).
- `rule.dominant_row`: our data holds one of several namesakes clearly more (0.9).
- Answers carry the method `rule` (a policy must accept it) and the rule in `reasons`;
  `rules.HOLDS_FOR_KEY` names the rules whose verdict holds for every mention of the name.
- `class_segment` reads switches per name class (`company/<class>/<consumer>`):
  `name_classes.name_traits` gives `generic`, `common_words`, `short` or `specific`.
- Tags (`resolution.tags`): `post_tags` reads `sales.post.mentions` (company and person
  tags); `tagged_page(kind, text, tags, passages)` gives the one page a mention is written
  at, and its identity claim, so a tagged name reaches stage 2 as an identifier. Written
  at two pages, it gives none.

### The name's profile and the people ladder (entity resolution, build item 4)

- **Profile** (`resolution.profile`): `NameProfiles(index, links).of(names)` gives each
  name's `NameProfile`: the companies keys confirmed it on (trusted `names` links), those
  a link of it was ended on, and how many registry companies carry it. `safe` (one
  company, none ended elsewhere), `contested` (two or more), `no_entity` (generic) or
  `open`. `StoredNameStage` (`stage.stored_name`) answers a safe name `is` (or `part_of`
  for a brand) by `stored_name`, a contested one `several`. No name is safe until the
  confirm flow writes trusted names.
- **People** (`resolution.people`):
  - `OwnSideStage(session)` (`stage.own_side`): a user's address `is` `user:<id>`
    (`EntityKind.USER`, an answer's entity only); another address on the customer's
    domains is `not_an_entity`. Both by `hard_key`; neither is ever a lead.
  - `NameAtEmployerRule(people)` (`rule.name_employer`): a full name and the claim's
    `company_id` link the one lead who worked there (`RegistryEmployerPeople(db)` for a
    request, `IndexEmployerPeople(store)` for a worker); two are candidates.
  - `PipelineMatchStage(session)` (`rule.pipeline_match`): the one lead the customer's
    seats work, by first and last name at that company or the work address's site
    (`resolution.pipeline_match.find_pipeline_lead`).
- `ResolutionService.verify` also calls a person `different` when the lead's stored
  LinkedIn profile is not the one the mention gives (`profiles_contradict`).

### Cases and decisions (entity resolution, build item 5)

`resolution.cases.CaseStore(store)`, in the row store; nothing runs yet.

```python
cases = CaseStore(store)
held = await cases.register(mentions, now=now, run_id=run_id)  # open_case for a worker
decision, case = await cases.decide(held[m.mention_id], answer, stage=resolution.by,
                                    mention_id=m.mention_id, chain=chain_to(known, answer.entity),
                                    read=resolution.candidates, now=now, run_id=run_id)
```

- A case (`er_cases`) is (kind, name key, partition). `partition_of`: `global` for a
  specific company name; a short, common-word or generic one, and a person's name, only
  inside a customer, an author company or a country, else alone.
- `register` attaches each named mention (`er_case_mentions`) once and counts it as
  support. `of_mentions` reads cases without writing.
- `decide` appends a decision (`er_case_decisions`, id made from its inputs, superseding the
  case's last) and one `er_link_uses` row per link of its chain; a closing verdict
  decides the case, `unknown` and `absent` leave it open. A reused answer is refused.
  `decisions(case_ids)` reads the history; `standing_on(link_keys)` the decisions that
  followed each link.
- `HeldVerdictStage` (`stage.held`, stage 3): a decided case's verdict, `reused`, with its
  decision id and method; a `several` or open case passes its candidates on.

### The judge, lanes and the lane run (entity resolution, build item 5)

- **Judge** (`resolution.judge`, `resolution.judge_agent`): `JudgeStage(model, cache,
  evidence, cases)` (`stage.judge`) asks one typed `JudgeQuestion` per case (up to 8
  companies labelled `C1`..., grouped, in a fixed order; up to 6 passages pooled from the
  case's mentions, one per source first) and maps the `JudgeReply` to an answer by
  `judge`. With no candidate it answers by `rule`: `absent` for a specific name, else
  `unknown`. A case with 20 mentions or more is asked twice, the candidates reversed; two
  answers that differ are `unknown`. The last judged decision on the same reading is
  reused. `LedgerJudge(loader, resolver, db)` runs the ledger agent
  `entity_resolution_judge` (config key `entity_resolution_root`,
  `resolution/resolution_policy.yaml`: DeepSeek's own `deepseek-flash`, a GLM fallback, 90 s).
  `judged_links` writes an applied judged answer back as a weak `names` link (a brand's
  name for `part_of`). The host supplies an `EvidenceSource` (passages by source row).
  `shown_candidates(ctx)` and `question_of(ctx, labelled, passages, mentions=)` build the
  judge's question; `run_ledger_agent(loader, resolver, db, agent_id=, inputs=)` runs a
  ledger agent of the judge's policy and keeps the call as an `agent_runs` row, and
  `policy_loader(db, resolver, config_version=)` opens that policy.
- **The judge's check** (`resolution.judge_check`): the ledger agent
  `entity_resolution_judge_check` (version 2 in version 4 of `entity_resolution_root`):
  TypeSafe's Jev on OpenRouter's decision API, no fallback, asked the judge's own
  question, picking one of `OPTIONS` (`C1`..`C8`, `none_of_them`, `not_a_company`,
  `not_stated`) with the provider's probability of each, and a `relation`
  (`same_company`, `part_of`). `LedgerCheck` / `CheckOnFirstQuestion` return a
  `CheckReply` (`CheckFailed` when the answer carries no probability); `check_answer` maps
  it to `is`, `part_of`, `absent`, `not_an_entity` or `unknown` on the candidates shown,
  the probability as its confidence. `JudgeCheckStage` (`stage.judge_check`) asks the
  check on every company mention with candidates, 8 calls at once, recorded and never
  applied: it runs only in the judge's replay. No gate stands behind the judge: its
  answer is a belief on the claim (`resolution.beliefs`), never a merge.
- **Lanes** (`resolution.lanes`): a lane holds the `workpool` lease
  `lane_resource(shard)` and a `CaseStore(store, shards=n, shard=k)`, which writes only
  its shard (`cases.route` splits mentions by shard). It stages learned links with
  `StagedLinks`; `ensure_compaction` then `compaction_runners(store, shards=n)` fold every
  staging shard into `er_links` and the name index, idempotently (a link refuted before
  the compactor saw it is kept ended: `LinkStore.keep_ended`).
- **Lane run** (`resolution.worker.resolve_in_lane`): registers the lane's mentions, runs
  the stages, records one decision per case for an answer no earlier decision made (with
  its chain and what it read), and stages what the judge learned.

### Re-check, feed and measurement (entity resolution, build item 6)

- **Fixer** (`resolution.fixer`, `resolution.revert`, `resolution.recheck`,
  `resolution.guard`): `recheck_lane(...)` runs one pass on a lane's shard. Plans a stopped
  run left open, what stood on links ended since the last pass, cases decided by an older
  stage version (not answers on keys) and cases on a company the registry merged away are
  marked stale, resolved again as a dry run, and written as a fix plan
  (`resolution.fix_plan`). No person approves, rejects or answers for a plan: a plan under
  20 units is applied at once; a larger one is audited first (`AuditGuard`: a sample of
  the dry run's changed answers checked by `entity_resolution_auditor`) and applied when
  the audit's lower bound passes 0.80, otherwise held (`held_reason`, `audit`) with each
  mechanism the audit found wrong moved back to `shadow`. With no auditor a large plan is
  held. The consumer rebuilds its mentions (`MentionSource`).
- **Feed** (`resolution.feed`): `er_changes`, read by `FeedReader(store).read(consumer,
  after_seq=cursor)`. A decision that moves a case writes one change per attached
  mention; `RegistryRelay(db, store).run()` adds the registry's company merges and undos,
  read below `pg_snapshot_xmin` (cursor in `resolution.relay_cursor`), for every
  consumer. Lanes and the relay stage changes; the compactor folds them.
- **Measurement** (`resolution.measurement`, `resolution.audit`): labels in `er_resolution_labels`
  by kind (`later_key`, `audit`, `revert`), never blended. `next_switch` moves a switch
  `on` at a lower bound of 0.85 (150 later keys, or replay and audit both before that)
  and back to `shadow` under 0.80; `off` never moves by itself. `move_switches` writes the
  moves with their numbers. The audit samples decisions per stage (`audit_sample`) and
  asks `entity_resolution_auditor` (GLM, another family than the judge).
  `summarize_day` and `DailySummaryRepository` write `resolution.daily_summary`;
  `ReadingRepository` keeps each mechanism's last reading per segment in
  `resolution.reading` (later-key and audit labels right and judged, lower bounds), which
  the Entity resolution tab shows beside the switch.
- **Unknowns** (`resolution.unknowns`): `UnknownsReader(cases, describe=, sources=)` is
  what the tab's Unknowns list and drawer read, through the graph explorer service.
  `page(limit=)` lists the open cases whose last answer is `unknown`, most mentioned
  first (`CaseStore.unknown`), with their names, consumers and customers;
  `case(case_id)` gives every decision on one case, the companies they read described
  (`registry_describer`) and the passages of its mentions, read again from their consumer
  and pooled as the judge pools them. Before the cases exist (`CaseStore.stored()`) both
  answer nothing. Nobody settles a case from it.
- **Lanes**: `lanes.LANES` (8) is the shard count of every case, link and change dataset;
  every flow and reader uses it, since another count reads other datasets.
- **Graphia's mentions** (`graphia.resolution_mentions.GraphiaMentionSource(reads,
  document_companies=None, employers=None)`): Graphia's units rebuilt as mentions from
  their unit ids (`LogReads.unit_mentions`, `LogReads.served_runs`), and their passages.
  The resolving side passes each sales document's own companies and `employers` (each
  organization unit -> the company its answer is), so a person is rebuilt as the resolve
  run sent it; a reader that only shows passages passes neither.
- **Graphia's units as mentions** (`graphia.resolution_mentions`): `units_of` gathers the
  organization and person units of a batch's runs, and a site author (an organization
  keyed `host:`) no text names. `mention_of(unit)` is one mention per unit (consumer
  `graphia`, the unit id as `source_ref`; `unit_mention_id(unit_type, unit_id)` gives its
  id from the unit alone). A person is sent only once the documents pair it with exactly
  one company (`employments(roles, runs)` reads the staff and role change facts, a former
  or left job pairs none; `person_mentions(units, employments, answered, runs)` takes the
  company an organization unit is keyed by or its answer, the first title, the latest
  document day). Reading a unit's case: `possible_rows(case)` (open, last answer
  `unknown`: its candidates), `refused_rows(case)` (last answer `absent`: its
  candidates), `open_unit_rows(cases, limit=)` (every Graphia unit whose case is open with
  rows still possible, for promotion).
- Requires migration `20261118140000_resolution_fix_plan_daily_summary.sql` after
  `20261118120000_resolution_schema.sql`.

### Merges and the confirm flow (entity resolution, build item 7)

- **Every merge is journaled and can be undone.** `ReversibleCompanyMerge`
  (`sales.company_merge`) and `ReversibleLeadMerge` (`sales.lead_merge`, migration
  `20261118160000_lead_merge_journal.sql`) merge only when the merge just moves rows, and
  `undo(merge_id, by=)` puts it back in one transaction. A lead merge moves connections (an
  update of `lead_id`: nothing wakes), and with each what hangs on it: the colleague chosen
  to take its call (`sales.lead_call_taker`) and its scored tries (`sales.strategy_outcome`,
  whose `lead_id` follows the merge and its undo); it keeps the source's contacts in full, since a
  contact the target holds is dropped and a primary one demoted, and the undo puts each
  back. A merge that would fold two threads of one user, or drop any other row, raises
  `MergeNotReversible` with every reason. `CompanyMergeService` and `LeadMergeService` are
  no longer exported: they are the private engine of the journaled merges, and
  `tests/test_dedup_merge_surface.py` fails when another module names them.
- **The merge door** (`resolution.merges.MergeDoor(db, links)`): `merge(source, target,
  proof, by=, now=, run_id=)`, or `merge_in(session, ...)` on the caller's transaction
  then `settle(outcome, ...)` after its commit. The proof (`resolution.merge_proof`:
  `MergeProof`, `EntryPath`, `ProofKind`) is two entry points that collide (different
  keys, no shared link or source, one path of keys), the same page on both rows, a
  placeholder's own key (`KEY_LOOKUP`), one pointer (`ONE_POINTER`: a site's path to the
  page it declares, stage 8 under `stage.match.one_way`), or a team's namesake. An answer
  is never a proof: `ProofKind.DECISION` is refused as malformed (`NOT_A_PROOF`). An
  ended `same` link refuses the merge; a merge the journal refuses is held in
  `resolution.held_match` ("matched, not merged"); either way a trusted `same` link is
  recorded. `resolution.merge_proof` needs no row store, so `simpl_core.services.dedup`
  imports without the `row-store` extras.
- **A placeholder's keys stay links**: a merge never copies a placeholder company's
  `landing_url`, `linkedin_url` or `company_linkedin_id` onto the row it merges into
  (`merge.PLACEHOLDER_KEY_FIELDS`; `MergeOutcome.withheld`). `settle` writes each as an
  `is` link from the key (`key_links.site_key`, `page_key`: `site:<host>`,
  `linkedin:page:<name>`) to the row, its source the merge's journal row: weak (a
  consumer's word), or trusted for the site a one-pointer merge read.
  `key_links.LinkedKeyStage` (`stage.linked_keys`, off until switched on, second in
  `company_stages`) reads them: trusted links to one row are an `is` on a key whose
  reasons name the links followed (`link:<key>`), and the settlement puts them in its
  proof's path; weak links bring candidates.
- **A merge can be taken back**: `MergeDoor.undo(kind, merge_id, by=, reason=, now=,
  run_id=, wrong=True)` undoes it through its journal (the source back, its claim
  pending), ends every link the merge wrote (`LinkStore.of_source`), excludes the row for
  the claims that came back, and undoes each merge whose proof followed one of those
  links (`undo_standing_on(link_keys, ...)`, also what a pass calls for links that
  ended). An undo as wrong moves the proof kind's switch (`kind_mechanism(kind)`:
  `merge.<kind>`, segment `company` or `lead`) to shadow: `merge_in` then holds that
  kind's merges (`KIND_STOPPED`). `resolution.merge_reaudit.reaudit_stopped(db, door,
  sites=, now=, run_id=)` checks each stopped kind's last 50 merges again (the page the
  merged row carried against the row kept, the sites read again for a site reading, a
  namesake's name words), undoes the refuted ones and moves the kind back on when none
  is; `one_pointer` returns by its replay alone.
- `LeadTwinMerger(door)` and `LeadPlaceholderReconciler(door)` merge only through the door
  (`MergeIn`): twins on an agreeing LinkedIn identity, or the email every twin holds and
  the name; a placeholder on a key it carries. An identity the people search found by the
  placeholder's name is held (`NAME_SEARCH`), never merged, and nothing is created on it
  (decided 2026-11-18). The company placeholder reconciler is gone.
- **The confirm flow** (`resolution.confirm.ConfirmFlow(links, examples)`):
  `confirm_names` promotes a weak `names` link a checked example (a tag on the same name)
  reaches, ends it for a specific name tagged elsewhere, and counts a contradiction, each
  under the `confirm.checked_example` switch (`shadow` counts only). `compact` writes a
  link on a merged company's survivor (`compact.shortcut`, its path the links it stands
  on) and folds one fact's sources into one link (`compact.fold`). `LinkStore.end` ends
  what stands on an ended link and releases a fold's other links; `LinkStore.promote`,
  `LinkStore.fold`. Links carry `confirmed_by`, `sources`, `folded_into`; readers skip a
  folded link. Checked examples carry `name_key`, read by `of_name_keys`.
- The registry relay copies lead merges and undos into the feed as well as company ones.

### The scorer and the yield (entity resolution, build item 8)

- **Stage 5's facts** (`resolution.facts`): `candidate_facts(ctx)` gives each candidate a
  vector named by `FEATURES` (`FACTS_VERSION`): exact name, how it was found, the name's
  class, country, followers, size, what our data holds, the document's own company, a
  shared site, its group. A stage puts them in `StageOutput.facts`; the worker keeps them on
  the decision (`Decision.facts`, `facts_version`), so a new scorer re-scores the past.
- **The scorer** (`resolution.scorer`): a logistic regression (`fit_logistic`, plain
  Python) on the facts. `ScorerStage` runs after the rules and before the judge; without a
  model it records the facts only. Three uses, three switches: `scorer.order` (the
  judge's candidates in its order, cut to the judge's number), `scorer.drop` (candidates
  under `drop_below` dropped), `stage.scorer` (a candidate alone over `settle_above` is
  the answer, method `scorer`). `ScorerRepository` (`resolution.scorer_model`):
  `save` makes the next version and puts every scorer switch that was on back to shadow;
  `latest`.
- **Training** (`resolution.scorer_training`): `training_rows` builds rows from checked
  examples with the key hidden (`example_mention`), `train` fits on the `tune` split and
  chooses the settling score (precision lower bound 0.85 over 30 settled) and the dropping
  score (1% of true rows), `replay_scorer` replays on the `test` split's later half. The
  measure step feeds the replay's lower bound to the settling switch.
- **The yield** (`resolution.yield_table`): each lane run adds per stage and segment
  (`<kind>/<name class>/<candidates band>/<consumer>`) how many mentions it saw, answered
  and settled (applied, not `unknown`), and its model calls (`resolution.stage_yield`).
  `YieldGate` keeps a gated stage (`run.GATED`: the judge) off a segment where it settled
  nothing over 500 mentions, but on a stable 2% sample; `run_stages(gate=)`.
### The wider search (entity resolution, build item 9)

- **When** (`resolution.wider.WiderSearchStage`): a company mention with no candidate after
  the rules, or whose candidates all score under the scorer's `drop_below`, or whose case
  the judge last answered `absent` or `unknown` with hints (`judge_hints`: `hint:<name>=`
  reasons). Every other mention is passed over (`StageOutput.passed`, not counted as a run
  in the yield). The sources run after the rules and before the scorer and the judge.
- **Four sources, each its own stage, switch and yield row** (`WIDER_STAGES`, all in
  `run.GATED`): `stage.wider.loose_keys` (`LooseKeys`: the name index on looser names, see
  `looser_names`; a looser name more than `PER_SOURCE` companies share is left out),
  `stage.wider.full_text` (`FullText`: BM25 over the companies' descriptions; the name
  column is not searched, as it was not in the companies' Zilliz collection),
  `stage.wider.similar` (`SimilarCompanies`: the companies whose description reads like the
  document around the name, with the hinted sector and country), `stage.wider.site_text`
  (`SiteText`: the companies of the sites whose pages hold the name). Each adds at most
  `PER_SOURCE` companies a mention lacks, tagged `found_by=("wider.<source>",)`, to the
  candidate cache (`CandidateCache.extend`), with their strength.
- **A source searches only where its switch is `on`**: `shadow` is no search, since a
  candidate the judge is not shown measures nothing. The yield counts a source as having
  settled a mention when the applied answer names a company it found first
  (`Resolution.found_first`).
- **Wiring**: `company_stages(..., companies=CompanySearch, sites=SourceSearch)`; without
  them only the looser keys run. `simpl_core.search.companies.open_company_search(
  nebius_api_key=..., queries=...)` gives the companies dataset's search on the row
  store's query service (`by_words`, `like`; `queries` gives the service's client, which
  its owner closes); `SourceSearch.landing_hosts` reads the landing pages' keyword half
  there too. Resolution takes both through protocols
  (`CompanyTextSearch`, `SiteSearch`), so it imports no search module.
- **The judge is asked at most twice on one state of the evidence**: it reuses its last
  answer when it is shown the same companies, in any order (the answer's `candidates`), and
  a hint search that adds companies asks it once more.

### Matches, creation and placeholders (entity resolution, build item 10)

- **Stage 8, research both sides of a match** (`resolution.match`, `resolution.match_research`):
  a question is two company rows we hold (`MatchQuestions`, row store `er_match_questions`,
  keyed by the pair, asked once). The lane worker opens them for the rows a lookup grouped
  as one company (`group_pairs`, bare rows left out), the companies one mention's known
  paths reach (`path_pairs`), and a row stage 9 created beside each candidate of its name
  (`create.created_pairs`). `MatchResearch.run` (the confirm flow) takes the open ones in
  order of what stands on their rows (connections, then leads), `PER_PASS` a pass:
  - each side loaded by id as research loads a subject (`osint.resolution.SubjectResolver`);
    a missing row, a placeholder (`origin = 'placeholder'`) or a row a lead waits on is
    refused;
  - `compare_held`: the same LinkedIn page is one company (`same_page`), two pages are two,
    a site both rows hold with one page between them is one company (`collision`);
  - else each side's own site: the crawl's stored pages (`CrawledSites`, the first
    `IDENTITY_PAGES`), or its landing page asked of the research API as a fetch
    (`HomePageFetches`: one page, internal, source type `entity_resolution`, purpose
    `entity_confirm`; a refused fetch is asked again next pass), read for their structure only
    (`page_keys`: links and schema.org `sameAs`). `compare_sites`: both sides pointing at
    each other (a home that moved to the other's site, a declared page) is one company, and
    so is a site declaring the page of a row with no site of its own (it has nothing to
    point back with); one way between two sites, or a page only linked, is `one_way`
    (never merged); a side with no page whose site names another page is `different`;
    nothing is `weak`;
  - `same` goes through the merge door (the row without a page into the one with it, else
    the one less stands on into the other), journaled, or is held.
- **Stage 9, create** (`resolution.create.CreateStage`, `stage.create`, last): a mention
  nothing closed that carries a site naming a company or a LinkedIn page
  (`writers.hard_identity_key`) gets a real row keyed by it through
  `CompanyPlacementService.resolve_or_create` (resolved again under the key locks; origin
  `entity_resolution`; recorded resolved in the claim ledger, so a key never creates twice).
  Only while the stage's switch is `on`, and only for a consumer whose policy creates
  (today company-from-domain's claims): `run_stages` skips a stage marked `creates` under
  any other policy, and the fixer's dry run never runs one. A name never creates.
- **Name stages by consumer** (`resolution.claims.NAME_STAGES`, 2026-11-23): a consumer that
  matches by name runs by default only the name stages with a counterpart in its matching
  before entity resolution; `rule.dominant_row` and `stage.stored_name` had none and act
  only once switched on. `ConsumerPolicy.name_stages` carries the set (`None`: all of
  `NAME_MATCHING`).
- **One-way pointer** (`match.ONE_WAY_MERGE`, `stage.match.one_way`): a row with no site of
  its own, whose page another row's site declares, merges only once that switch is on;
  off, the pair stays `one_way`. A reconciler merge of an address-only placeholder into the
  one lead its team works under the same name (ENG-1285) is journaled with its own proof
  kind, `team_namesake`, never as a key lookup.
- **Placeholders** are settled through their claim's case (build item 11 below), not by a
  reconciler of their own.

### The product's claims (entity resolution, build item 11)

- **Pending claims are cases** (`resolution.claims`): `PendingClaims.read` turns the pending
  rows of `sales.unresolved_company_claim` and `sales.unresolved_lead_claim` (oldest
  first, `CLAIMS_PER_PASS` of each) into mentions of their ledger `source`, the consumer
  (`inbound`, `crm_blocklist`, `conversation_processor`...), named by the claim's own name
  or its placeholder's. Each consumer runs under its published policy, else
  `claim_policy(source)` (`CLAIM_POLICIES`, from the plan's "Accepts": identifiers only
  for a source with none).
- **Settling** (`ClaimSettlement(door, holds=).settle(..., decisions=)`, on the caller's
  session; the caller commits, then settles the merges with the door). **Only a key
  merges; every other answer is a belief**: `is` on a key merges the placeholder into
  the row the key reaches through the merge door (a key lookup from the claim's own key,
  journaled), or resolves a claim with no placeholder. A rule's, a stored name's, the
  scorer's or the judge's `is` is recorded on the claim (`believed_company_id` /
  `believed_lead_id`, `believed_at`, `belief`: stage, method, confidence, decision id,
  evidence hash; migration `20261128_claim_beliefs`), counted `believed`: nothing is
  merged and a claim with no placeholder is not resolved, whatever a switch or a
  confidence says. No belief on a row whose own LinkedIn page (a lead's profile) is
  another than the claim's. A belief the ladder no longer gives ends (`belief_ended`); a
  failed stage keeps it. `not_an_entity` by a rule retires the claim (`resolution_state =
  'retired'`), a model's does not; an answer naming the placeholder itself waits. A
  placeholder gone abandons its claim, one promoted resolves it on itself, a lead
  placeholder parked on a company a lead waits on is left to the conversation backfill's
  lead reconciler. `holds`: each consumer's `BeliefHolds` (`believed(session, claim, row,
  now=)`, `ended(session, claim, landed=, now=)`), by claim source.
- **Beliefs and rungs** (`resolution.beliefs`, no row store): `Belief`, `Exclusion` (a row
  a rung refuted for a claim: the row, the rung and its version, the evidence hash; it
  lapses when either changes and no merge reads it), `FoundKey` (a key a rung found, with
  its `Grade`), `RungRecord` (a rung's last run on the claim), `owed(rungs, evidence,
  now=, retry_after=)`, `evidence_class(...)` (what stands behind a belief). The rungs:
  `STAGE8`, `CLAIM_SITE`; `UNDONE` excludes the row of a merge undone as wrong.
  `ClaimMention` carries `evidence` (`claims.evidence_key(mention)`), `belief`,
  `found_keys`, `rungs`, `held_out_connection_id`; the rows refuted for a claim on its
  evidence are its mention's `Mention.excluded`: no stage's answer names one and none is
  a candidate (`stages.never_it`). `PendingClaims.believed()` reads the believed claims;
  a believed row gone from the registry ends its belief on the next read.
- **The pass's policy** (`claims.pass_policy(policy)`): a source of `BELIEVED_BY_NAME`
  (`crm_blocklist`) is also resolved by the name rules and the judge in the worker's
  claims pass, as beliefs; its requests keep `claim_policy` (keys alone).
  `crm.flow.blocklist_holds.BlocklistHolds(switches)` is the blocklist's `BeliefHolds`:
  while a claim believes a row, the scope's seller gets a claimed placeholder connection
  on it at Not In Target (recorded as `held_out_connection_id`), unclaimed when the
  belief ends; a connection the customer's sellers already hold there is left as it is
  and counted (`connections_on(session, customer_id, company_ids)`). Off until
  `claims.blocklist_hold` is on for `company/crm_blocklist`.
- **Stage 8 on a claim's pair** (`resolution.match`, `match_research`):
  `MatchOrigin.BELIEF` / `FOUND_KEY` (`CLAIM_ORIGINS`) let a placeholder be a side when
  its pending claim carries a key (`claim_side(claim)`). `ask_beliefs(db, questions,
  now=, run_id=)` opens the pairs that owe the rung (`MatchQuestions.reopen` for one
  settled on older evidence or a week ago). `same` merges the placeholder into the row;
  one pointer does so only under `stage.match.one_way`, on a `ONE_POINTER` proof;
  `different` is an `Exclusion` on the claim and ends its belief (no link between the
  rows; `linked_only(finding)`: a merely linked page ends the belief and excludes
  nothing); `one_way` and `weak` leave it believed. `MatchResearch(..., holds=)` tells a
  consumer's hold where a claim landed. `preview_claim_pairs(db, pairs, sites)` says what
  a pass would find, written nowhere.
- **The claim's own site** (`resolution.claim_sites.ClaimSites(db, questions, sites,
  fetches).run(now=, run_id=)`): a pending claim with a site and no page has its site
  read once (the crawl's pages, else a fetched landing page); a page it declares is a
  `FoundKey` on the claim, never a field and never a row, and a real row holding it is a
  pair asked of stage 8. It merges nothing. `preview_claim_sites` counts what the crawl
  alone would find.
- **What is owed** (`resolution.owed`): `owed_report(db, now=)` counts the beliefs by
  consumer and age, the rungs each still owes and the oldest unpaid one, refutations,
  retirements, held-out rows and the merges by proof kind with their undos;
  `OwedReport.alerts()` names a rung whose oldest unpaid belief passed `ALERT_AFTER` (14
  days); `rows(day)` and `blocklist_standing(db, day)` are the Entity resolution tab's
  rows (`resolution.daily_summary`). `reopen_retired(db)` makes a retired company claim
  that now carries a key pending again.
- **Measured against a known answer**: `resolution.site_reading` (`registry_examples`,
  `replay_site_reading(db, examples, sites, fetches)`: a registry row's own site, on a
  site no other row holds, scored against the registry by grade and reader: the row's own
  page is right, a page another row holds is wrong, a page no row holds scores nothing; a
  fetch that stored no page is `failed`, and the sites whose home moved are also counted
  apart in `moved`; `start_rule(registry, claims)`: the one-pointer reading starts only
  when both populations' lower bounds clear `MERGE_BAR` 0.99 on `MERGE_SAMPLE` 1,000
  scored pages; `apply_start_rule` moves the switch then, never otherwise) and
  `resolution.hidden_key` (`claim_examples`, `blocklist_examples`, `hide`,
  `replay_hidden_keys`: claims a key settles resolved with that key hidden, per consumer
  right / wrong / open, and what their own sites declare).
- **Planning before settling** (`ClaimSettlement.plan(session, claim, resolution)`, a
  query): the `SettlementPlan` `settle` acts on, `action` (`Settled`, `believed` among
  them) and `target`, a `PlannedMerge` for a merge, else `reason` (`Unsettled`:
  `no_answer`, `open`, `not_an_entity_by_a_model`, `page_conflict`, `excluded`,
  `row_gone`, `other_kind`, `its_placeholder`, `parked`). The claims dry run
  (`resolution.claims_dry_run`) reports beliefs and what stands behind each
  (`ClaimsDryRun.beliefs()`).
- **A claim nothing settled is not asked again for nothing**: `settle(..., tried_as=)`
  (the ladder version each consumer's claims ran under) records on a claim the whole ladder
  left unsettled its `Tried` (`er_tried_version`, `er_tried_mention`, `er_tried_at`,
  migration `20261127_claim_er_tried`), counted `unsettled`; a resolution with a failed
  stage (`Resolution.failed`, from `StageOutput.failed`: the judge's model call) is not
  recorded. `worth_trying(claim, version, now=)` asks it again once
  `ladder_version(stages, policy=, board=, judge=)` (stage versions, policy, every switch
  moved, `judge_agent.judge_version`) or `mention_key(mention)` changed, or after
  `RETRY_AFTER` (7 days). `run.stage_list(stages_for, store, shards=)` gives the stages
  for their versions alone.
- **No placeholder is a candidate** (`rules.CandidateCache`): a company of origin
  `placeholder` is a claim's stand-in, no identity, so no rule, scorer or judge ever answers
  it, for any mention. `CompanyResolver` drops a placeholder for a real row holding the same
  key before it filters on the claim's name or place (a placeholder is named and placed
  after its claim), so a placeholder whose site a real company holds settles by key at
  stage 2. **A user is never a lead**: a lead claim the own side answers
  `is user:<id>` is retired, like `not_an_entity`. The people rules' `is` carries
  `people.PERSON_RULE_CONFIDENCE` (0.9), the pipeline match's included.
- **The judge sees what the mention gives besides its name** (`JudgeQuestion.about`,
  `JudgeMentionFacts`: the kind of record, `judge.RECORDS`; its site, LinkedIn page, email
  domain, country, location, language) and each candidate's LinkedIn page. Prompt
  `entity_resolution/judge-system` 2 holds those facts against the candidates' own and
  answers `unknown` when only the name agrees. An `is` on a mention with a stand-in (a
  claim, whose answer can merge its placeholder) is asked twice, the candidates reversed;
  two answers that differ are `unknown`. `LedgerJudge` keeps each call as an
  `agent_framework.agent_runs` row (question, reply or error, model, prompt), unless the
  definition says `audit: false`. `JudgeOnFirstQuestion(..., config_version=)` asks a given
  ledger version, for a replay before it switches on.
- **A claim's stand-in** (`Mention.stand_in`, set by `company_claim_mention` and
  `lead_claim_mention`): the row the claim stands on, its placeholder or its caller's lead.
  `run_stages` keeps an answer naming it unapplied and drops it from the candidates, and
  `CandidateCache` never offers it, so a placeholder its own key finds goes on to the
  stages that can name the row it stands for. Requests and Graphia's mentions have none.
- **The claims pass as a dry run** (`resolution.claims_dry_run.dry_run_claims(db, store,
  stages_for=, shards=)`): every pending claim (`PendingClaims.peek`, never stamped)
  through the whole ladder, each consumer under `full_policy` (every method, every name
  stage, nothing created), every mechanism on in memory (`FULL_LADDER_BOARD`, stage 9
  off), then the settlement's plan for each. It writes nothing: stages run through
  `run_stages`, never a lane; the case store is read with `shard=None`. The judge's and
  the searches' calls are paid and recorded with sub-process `claims_dry_run`.
  `ClaimsDryRun.markdown(run_id=)` and `.rows()` report it.
- **People in the lanes**: `company_stages(..., people=True)` adds the people ladder (own
  side, a name at its employer from the person name index, the customer's pipeline); the
  two stages that read on a session run through `people.SessionPerRun`, a session per run.
- **The name indexes** (`resolution.index_refresh.refresh_name_indexes`): the first run
  builds the company and person name indexes from the whole registry, later runs read what
  changed since the cursor (`name_index`), and the sweep tombstones companies gone. The
  resolve run finds companies by name only through them.
- **Callers resolve through the service, with no name matcher of their own.** Name matching
  is a consumer's opt-in (`ConsumerPolicy.name_matching`; the name stages act by default
  for it, a measured switch still winning: `name_matching.opted_in`). A request reads the
  switch board on its own session (`switches.read_board(session)`). Moved so far: inbound
  (`ingestion.company_placement.SignalCompanyPlacement`, also simpl_api's call linker),
  the conversation's named company (`ingestion.conversation_company`, consumer
  `conversation_processor`, the person's current roles as the mention's
  `document_companies`), email thread people (`email.thread_people`), inbound's lead
  lookup by name, CRM pull and link (`crm.crm_entity_onboarding`: `CRM_POLICY`, hard keys
  over the resolver's strict linking policy; a miss gets a real row on its own site or
  page with the CRM's origin, else a placeholder; several rows fitting raise
  `crm_company_unplaced`) and the CRM blocklist (`crm.flow.blocklist`). Placeholders go
  through `ResolutionService.place`, which also takes a company claim carrying only a
  name under a placeholder policy (the caller decided the name is free), and creates on
  the mention's own hard key under a policy with `create` (`resolve_or_create`, origin
  the caller's `source`). An identifier's `is` names the key that matched first in its
  `reasons` (`linkedin_url`, `landing_host`, `email`...). `Placement.identity_key` is the
  ledger key a placeholder or created row stands on. The conversation processor's company
  lookups go through `resolve`. Still direct, on purpose: meeting facts' and email sync's
  pipeline lookups (the corroborated matcher itself: meetings want the customer's pipeline
  lead over an address lead no seat holds, and email sync needs the pipeline row's
  company); lookups by address or domain alone (the email lane, the processor's person,
  which is unscoped because the lane writes its people's addresses globally); and company
  import (`resolve_prepared_identities`, a name breaking host ties; its callers are
  simpl_api's, and the dialog is an open product decision).
- **The person name index** (`er_person_name_index`) also holds the leads whose own row
  places them at a company they hold no experience at (`sales.lead.company_id`, key
  `lead|company|placed`, incrementally by `updated_at`), so the worker's people ladder sees
  them by name. Such a row is never tombstoned: a stale one only ever makes a candidate.
- **A name is not enough for a person** (decided 2026-11-19; `resolution.person_evidence`,
  facts read by `resolution.person_facts.PersonFacts`). `rule.name_employer` (experiences
  at the employer, or the lead's own `company_id`) and `rule.pipeline_match` (the
  customer's seats' leads there) return the leads a full name reaches as candidates. The
  one candidate nothing contradicts is `is` only when a role at the company is open on the
  mention's day (`Mention.seen_on`, today by default; with no experience there, the lead's
  own row or the customer's connection) and one more fact agrees: the mention's title
  (`Mention.title`; acronyms read as their words, rank words alone never agree), its
  country, or an address on the company's site or a sister domain. Another LinkedIn
  profile, every role there ended, or another country rule a candidate out. A customer's
  mention is offered only the leads the customer may see (`PersonFacts.visible`). The lead
  resolver matches no name: its two name paths (`leads_named_at_company`, and
  `find_lead_by_name_and_company`'s `ILIKE '%name%'` first row) and the email lane's
  `resolve_lead_by_name_and_company` are gone. Both rules are version 2.
- **A referred colleague** (consumer `outreach_referral`, decided 2026-11-19): a name at a
  company with no address gets a real stand-in lead when the caller passes `origin` to
  `place` (`writers._stand_in_by_name`), its claim keyed by the mention
  (`unkeyed:<source>:<source_ref>`), never by the name: the same referral finds it again,
  another one naming the same person gets its own. A search's profiles are weighed by the
  person rule (`resolution.profile_pick.pick_profile`: the name, then a role at the company
  now and an agreeing title); several plausible go to the one judge as a person question
  (`person_question`, `JudgeQuestion.kind == "person"`, candidates `P1`...), and only its
  `is` at the consumer's `min_lower_bound` names one (`judged_profile`). The judge's
  question and reply live in `resolution.judge_question` (no row store: simpl-outreach has
  no pyarrow); `NameKind` and `Relation` in `resolution.vocabulary`; the lazy ledger judge
  in `judge_agent.JudgeOnFirstQuestion`.
- **The old verdicts as cases** (`resolution.legacy`, worker-only: pyarrow through `cases`,
  never imported by `resolution/__init__`; plan §15 before cutover, step 3):
  `legacy_method(decided_by)` maps Graphia's `er_assignment.decided_by` (author_anchor,
  strong_key, key:* → hard_key; teacher, nuextract, model:, recheck: → judge;
  `er_service:<m>` → m; the rest → rule); `legacy_answer` gives one case's answer (one row:
  `is` by the weakest method; several rows, or a row beside a held nil verdict: `several`; a
  nil verdict alone: `absent`; nothing held: no decision, never `unknown`).
  `import_legacy(...)` registers mentions as a live run does and decides each case at most
  once at `stage.legacy@0`: a case another stage decided is kept, a legacy-decided one is
  widened (batches commute, a re-run writes nothing), shards without a store are `skipped`.
  `SettledClaims` pages the ledgers' resolved and merged claims; `claim_placement` puts each
  on its row (hard_key for domain, LinkedIn and email keys). `LEGACY_VERSIONS`
  (`stage.legacy` at 1), merged into the fixer's current versions, makes every non-key
  import stale, so the fixer re-plans it.
- **The fixer re-plans by impact** (`recheck.by_impact`: most mentioned first, then longest
  decided, then case id), at most `STALE_PER_PASS` (twice `AUDIT_SAMPLE`: 80) stale cases per
  pass and shard; the rest wait, and a plan re-selected by version is capped the same way.
  `recheck_lane` and `Reverter.dry_run`/`apply` take `policy_of(consumer)`: mentions are
  grouped by consumer and each group resolved under its own policy (`revert.by_policy`).
  `apply` leaves out a mention rebuilt under a name whose case lives on another shard
  (`CaseStore.on_own_shard`, logged), rather than failing the plan on every pass.
  `measurement.measured()` drops `stage.legacy` decisions, so the import gets no audit,
  reading or switch. `PolicyRepository.active_all()`; `legacy.claim_row(row)` is the row a
  claim stands on now.
- **A name a person gave for their own company** (`Mention.own_company`: a form's typed
  company, the company a reply says it answers for): `rule.unique_exact` links the one
  company carrying it whatever its size, and `rule.document_company` also takes a document
  company whose name the words named start or that starts them ("GMB Finance" names "GMB
  Finance Solutions Srl"). For any mention, a LinkedIn page it gives contradicts a row with
  another page. Rules version 7.

### Email word lists

Three tables in the `mappings` schema hold the words simpl_core's email checks
use. Add or remove a word as a row; running services pick it up within ten
minutes.

- `mappings.email_provider_domain`: the domains that name a mail provider, never
  a company. `free_mail` (gmail.com, libero.it…) and `certified_mail` (Italian
  PEC hosts: pec.it, legalmail.it…). The domain after the `@` is matched exactly.
- `mappings.email_role_local_part`: local parts of a mailbox a team shares
  (info, sales, fatture…), matched against the whole part before the `@`.
- `mappings.email_automated_pattern`: text marking a mailbox nobody reads
  (noreply, mailer-daemon…), matched anywhere in the address.

The checks in `simpl_core.services.email.domain_policy` (`classify_domain`,
`is_generic_domain`, `should_resolve_company_by_domain`, `is_role_address`,
`is_automated_address`, …) and the colleague rule in `internal_identity`
(`internal_domains_of`) read an in-memory copy of the tables. Core's own
services load it. Code that calls these checks directly loads it first, or they
raise `AddressListsNotLoaded`:

```python
from simpl_core.services.email.address_lists import (
    ensure_address_lists,
    ensure_address_lists_from,
)

await ensure_address_lists(session)   # with a session
await ensure_address_lists_from(db)   # with a DatabaseManager
tenant_domains = internal_domains_of(tenant_emails)
```

Both are free while the copy is under ten minutes old. Tests install a copy with
`use_address_lists(EmailAddressLists(...))`.

### A customer's own addresses

`simpl_core.services.email.tenant_identity.load_tenant_identity(session,
customer_id)` answers which addresses and domains are a customer's own people's:
each user's profile email, what their email connections record (the synced
mailbox and the send-from alias), and the active rows of
`sales.customer_email_identity` (aliases, with the user each belongs to, and
domains). Every such address's domain counts too, unless a mail provider owns
it, and so does any host under it (`team@sales.acme.io` is acme.io's). Email sync files a participant on one of them as the owner (the owner's own
aliases, whose messages are the owner's) or a colleague, never a lead; the
conversation backfill never takes one for the counterpart.

```python
identity = await load_tenant_identity(session, customer_id)
identity.contains("someone@alias-domain.example")  # one of the customer's
identity.user_of("nico@alias-domain.example")      # the user an alias is recorded for
identity.alias_match(address, display_name)        # AliasMatch(user_id, sure) or None
```

A row's `source` is `manual`, `provider` (the mailbox's verified send-as or
proxy addresses), `sent_mail` (the From of a message in a user's Sent folder) or
`name_match`; its `status` is `active`, `proposed` (waiting for someone to set it
active or rejected) or `rejected`. Email sync fills it as it runs:

- once a day per connection it reads the mailbox's verified send-as addresses
  from the provider (Gmail's send-as settings, Outlook's proxy addresses) and
  records each one no user holds yet as that user's (`provider`; the
  connection's `config.send_as_checked_at` holds when);
- the From address of every message the provider files as sent (Gmail's SENT
  label, Outlook's Sent Items) is the owner's: it writes as the owner for the
  rest of the run and is recorded as theirs (`sent_mail`), unless it is
  another user's or a machine's;
- an outside address whose local part and display name are both a user's is
  recorded as that user's (`name_match`, active); one with only one of the two
  is proposed.

Add an alias domain by hand with a `manual` domain row.

### Handing a lead to a colleague

`simpl_core.services.account_coordination.lead_handoff.LeadHandoffService.hand_over`
records a lead-scoped `sales.account_handoff` (company and dummy connections NULL)
with one `account_handoff_ulc` mapping: the source seller's connection with the
lead to the target colleague's, each reused or created (origin `account_handoff`).
The source's drafts waiting for approval are cancelled (`account_handed_off`), and
`sales_outreach.evaluate_dispatch_authority` (rule 1b,
`migrations/20261015_lead_scoped_account_handoff.sql`) cancels any later send from
the source connection, coordination or not; outreach's trigger guard forwards the
lead's replies on it to the target as `handoff_inbound_received`. The newest
lead-scoped hand-off a connection takes part in decides: handed back, it writes
again (`LeadHandoffRepository.silenced_ulc_ids` is the same test). The target's
new connection gets no cold first touch: `wake` (a `LeadMessage`) queues
`handoff_inbound_received` with the lead's message (and, for a forward,
`trigger_data.forwarded_original`: sender, subject, text, note), unless that
message already waits on the source (the guard forwards it) or the source wrote
to the lead since it (its walk answered). Idempotent on `idempotency_key`; the
lead is locked for the transaction. Logs `account.handoff.initiated` and
`.completed` on both connections. Company-wide hand-offs are unchanged.

### Opted out and Not In Target stop every send

`sales_outreach.evaluate_dispatch_authority` rule 1s
(`migrations/20261030_dispatch_refuses_excluded_stages.sql`, Python reference
`account_coordination.authority`, facts `connection_category` and
`connection_came_inbound`) cancels any LinkedIn invitation, LinkedIn message,
email, call or calendar invite on a connection whose stage is in category -1
(`opted_out`) or 8 (`not_in_target`). Two exceptions (fact
`row_is_closing_reply`): the farewell or thank-you a walk queued beside a close or
a redirect (a row whose `payload.batch_id` is that of a `close_conversation` or
`change_target` row on the same connection; simpl-outreach's close handler
inserts it before it applies the close's status), and, at Not In Target, a
person who came in on one of the seller's channels (an `inbound_signal` row in
`sales_outreach.batch_trigger_queue` for the connection, simpl-outreach's
`came_inbound`), whom outreach keeps answering. Rule 1l
(`migrations/20261112090200_dispatch_refuses_first_message_to_lost.sql`, fact
`writes_first`, helper `authority.writes_first(payload)`) cancels as `lost_after_call`
a `send_message` or `linkedin_connect` row on a connection in category 13 (Lost) whose
`payload.message_type` is not `response` (a missing one included) and that carries no
`payload.lost_reopen` (the 90-day reopening the seller approves): an answer to the lead
and the reopening go. Not Interested, Non sales and Disqualified are left to outreach's
own rules. The rule runs wherever the authority does: when a
row is queued, approved or claimed (the extension task claim delivers only a row
still `RUNNING` after it) and in `recheck_journey_log_authority` right before a
LinkedIn message or an email goes out, so moving someone to one of these stages
stops what was already queued for them. A LinkedIn invitation the extension
already claimed is not rechecked before it is sent.

### Judging a conversation thread

`simpl_core.services.conversation_processing` judges one stored thread and, for a
sales thread, opens one `sales.user_lead_connection` at the funnel category the
conversation reached. That insert is the whole outreach handoff.

```python
from simpl_core.services.conversation_processing import (
    ConversationProcessor,
    SalesDecision,
    counterpart_identity,
    load_thread_bundle,
    render_transcript,
)

processor = ConversationProcessor()
bundle = await load_thread_bundle(session, thread_id)
reading = await processor.read(bundle, session=session)   # writes nothing
if reading.skip_reason is None:
    transcript = render_transcript(
        bundle,
        counterpart=reading.counterpart,
        own_participant_ids=reading.own_participant_ids,
    )
    # Who the transcript's "counterpart" is (name, email, LinkedIn profile):
    # give it to the sales check beside the transcript, or an app's
    # notification quoting a sale reads like the buyer.
    who = counterpart_identity(reading.counterpart)
    decision = SalesDecision(verdict="sales", stage="messaged", reason="...")  # your sales check
    if decision.verdict == "sales":
        result = await processor.process(
            bundle, reading, decision, session=session,
            mode="incremental",   # or "backfill" / "report_only"
        )
        result.outcome    # connection_opened / connection_existed / no_lead / ...
        result.issues     # what a person has to settle
```

`read` settles, without any model call, every thread that could not open a
connection anyway, and a LinkedIn sponsored message the owner never answered
(`reading.skip_reason`). That includes a counterpart the owner already has a
connection to (`connection_existed`, with `reading.existing_ulc_id`): the lead
ingestion linked, or, for a counterpart linked to no lead or to a dummy, the
single lead its email or LinkedIn identity matches; a name alone never counts.
On email, a LinkedIn profile link in the counterpart's own messages (signature,
quoted replies cut) is part of that identity. It also settles a counterpart
working for one of the owner's company's active customers (`existing_customer`):
anyone on the owner's team holds their lead or their company at Customer, or,
for the platform's own team, the counterpart's address is a user of any of the
platform's customers, whatever its subscription (a reactivation or a move may be
under way), or the platform's accounts hold their company domain with a running
subscription or managed engagement. A thread of a colleague's mailbox the owner
reads as a sales identity, with none of the aliases the owner sends from on it,
is settled as `colleague_mailbox_thread`: it is the mailbox owner's. A LinkedIn thread
read from another SimpL user's account (its owner-flagged messages, or a teammate's
profile row that wrote, show that user's profile and never the owner's) is settled as
`another_users_linkedin`: a thread is the owner's only when the owner wrote on it from
their own profile. A colleague's thread the owner was only copied on is
settled as `colleague_thread`: the owner never wrote on it, a teammate did, and a
colleague of the owner's customer still works the counterpart's lead (a connection
not silenced by a lead hand-off). A second connection there would make the copied
owner a second voice to the lead, woken by its own copy of every reply.
A thread among colleagues with no counterpart where a colleague forwarded the
owner, in the last two weeks (`FORWARD_HANDOFF_WITHIN`), an outside person's
message is settled as `colleague_forward` (`reading.forward`: the forward
message, who forwarded it, to which users, and the original from
`forwarded_original(body, is_internal=...)`, which reads past the forwarder's own
reply down to the outside person's message). Only the receiver's copy counts,
and only a message that is itself a forward (`is_forward_subject`: `Fwd:`, `Fw:`,
`I:`, `WG:`, after a `[EXT]` tag); a reply quoting a forward is not one. Email
sync stores such a thread only for backfill-enabled mailboxes and only under a
forward subject or a reply to one (`is_forward_thread_subject`: `Re: Fwd:`,
`R: I:` ...). `process_forward(bundle, reading, decision)`, for a forward a check
judged a sale handed over, resolves or creates the lead and hands it from the
forwarder to the one colleague the forward went to (see "Handing a lead to a
colleague"); several colleagues, an inactive colleague, no status, a lead a third
colleague still works, or a company handed to someone else are issues. Backfill
and incremental runs hand over alike; `report_only` reports `would_hand_off`.
`attach` queues no reply on a connection handed off lead by lead since the
message.
`vendor_pitch_gate(bundle, reading)` says whether a thread `read` left unsettled may
become a vendor's cold pitch the owner answers with a pitch of their own (ENG-775):
the counterpart wrote first and last, nobody on the owner's side wrote, their newest
message is at most 14 days old, the address is a person's, a LinkedIn counterpart is
1st-degree, and nobody on the team ever wrote to that address, profile or company
(`ConversationRepository.team_wrote_to`; a freemail or PEC domain names no company).
A pitch the owner already answered by hand (the counterpart wrote first, the owner
after) passes as `answered` on the gate: only the identity checks apply, and only
what the team wrote before the pitch counts.
`process_vendor_pitch(bundle, reading, reason=...)` opens its connection as `process`
does (Connected, no ICP, origin `conversation_processor`) with the counterpart's
message queued flagged `vendor_pitch` and `seller_approval_required`, under an
advisory lock per customer and person: the first teammate processed answers, the
others get `vendor_pitch_duplicate`; a closed gate is `vendor_pitch_skipped:<why>`.
On an answered pitch it is `vendor_pitch_answered`: the pitch is recorded as a
handled `message_received` flagged `vendor_pitch` (so outreach keeps the lane), no
follow-up is queued (a vendor gets one answer), and a reply of theirs since is
queued unflagged.
`process` opens the
connection for the counterpart: the lead ingestion linked (never a dummy), or the
lead its email or LinkedIn identity matches; one it creates when none exists (from
a LinkedIn identity the lead itself, from an email a placeholder with its claim);
or, for a mailbox people share (a role address, or two LinkedIn accounts behind one
address), the company's connection to its dummy lead
(`company_connection_opened`). Among several leads holding the address, the one
whose full name the counterpart's row or own messages carry is chosen; with
nothing to choose between them they come back `no_lead` with a `lead_ambiguous`
issue. A digest message (`head_snippets`, several messages run together) is no
evidence of who its participant is: neither its signature links nor its names
count. `EmailSyncOrchestrator.refill_thread(user_id, provider, thread)` reads such
a thread (`MessageRepository.digest_threads`) again from the mailbox, stores each
message on the thread's own record and marks its digests superseded, which the
reader treats as absent; it returns how many messages the thread gains (counted,
not written, on a dry-run orchestrator), or `None` when the mailbox no longer
holds the thread. The
connection takes the lead's company (`company_id`, else its current role), and the
conversation is never written. Its origin is `conversation_processor`
(`PROCESSOR_SOURCE`), or `inbound` (`INBOUND_ORIGIN`, `result.inbound`) for a
decision with `inbound=True` -- the counterpart came interested in what the
owner sells: how it works, what it costs, a trial, a purchase or a demo -- on a
thread whose first message is the counterpart's. Such a connection writes
`inbound.lead.received` in the same transaction, dated at the counterpart's first
message, with `event_data` `origin` `conversation_backfill`, `company_id`,
`conversation_id` and `thread_id` (`record_conversation_inbound` in
`conversation_processing.inbound_event`; never a second one on a connection that has the
event). `mode="report_only"` decides exactly the same and writes nothing
(`incremental` and `backfill` behave the same here: the caller decides which
threads to pass). `process` takes only a sales decision and raises `ValueError`
on any other.
On a thread that waits on the counterpart it opens at Messaged and queues a
`planner-follow-up-check` with it, so outreach proposes a follow-up. On one at
Connected or Messaged whose newest message is the counterpart's it queues that
message as `message_received`, so outreach answers it
(`result.reply_message_id`); an active
stage on a thread older than 90 days opens at Monitoring, and so does a
`deferred` stage ("maybe later") at any age. A deferral that named its day
(`SalesDecision(..., stage="deferred", recontact_on=date(2026, 7, 1))`) opens at
Messaged instead, with the follow-up due that day (`result.follow_up_at`),
queued the way outreach queues a callback the lead asked for
(`deferral_kind: scheduled_callback`) at the start of the owner's working day; a
day more than 90 days past, before the counterpart's newest message, or more than
two years after it parks at Monitoring like an undated one. Every connection it
opens without an immediate follow-up has its own `lead_claimed` closed with
`BatchTriggerQueueRepository.settle_lead_claimed(ulc_id, reason=...)`, which any
caller opening a connection that must not start with a first touch can use:
outreach's connect fast path would otherwise send an invite at any stage. Every
connection it opens also gets the lead's earlier LinkedIn messages recorded as
handled with `BatchTriggerQueueRepository.record_prior_lead_messages(ulc_id=...,
user_id=..., lead_id=..., reason=...)`; any caller opening a connection on a
conversation that already exists needs the same, or the LinkedIn pre-send guard
refuses every send on it (`inbound_message_missing_trigger`).
`simpl_core` never imports a model runtime: the sales check is the caller's.

### Unknown companies never block

Resolution can miss; ingestion must not stop. Use the placement service when you
need to proceed regardless — email ingestion, imports:

```python
from simpl_core.services.dedup import CompanyPlacementService

placed = await CompanyPlacementService().resolve_or_placeholder(
    claim, session=session, source="email_ingestion", customer_id=customer_id
)
placed.kind        # "matched" | "placeholder" | "unresolved" | "escalated"
placed.company_id  # None for "unresolved" and "escalated"
```

`"escalated"` is the `ambiguous` path: several real rows match, so the claim is
most likely one of them and a placeholder would be a third. The claim is
recorded under its identity with the candidates attached, and nothing is
created. `identity_key` is unique and reused, so getting this wrong would pin
every future sighting of that identity to the fabricated row permanently. An
`insufficient_signal` outcome that still carries candidates escalates the same
way — the resolver found rows it declined to link (an exact name match under
`propose_by_name`), and creating alongside them has the same effect.

Two tiers, decided by what the claim carried. **Company evidence present** (a
corporate domain, or a name) creates a real `sales.company` row with
`origin='placeholder'`, recorded in `sales.unresolved_company_claim` under a
unique `identity_key` — so ten thousand import rows from one domain reuse one
placeholder. **No company evidence** records the claim and fabricates nothing:
inventing a company on no evidence asserts something false and, having no key,
could never be enriched or merged away. Nothing blocks either way —
`user_lead_connection.company_id` is nullable and the `lead_claimed` trigger
never reads it.

`LeadPlacementService.resolve_or_placeholder` does the same for people, and the
`origin` it uses is the whole point: `queue_batch_trigger` returns early for any
lead with `origin='Dummy'`, so the old fallback — parking an unidentified person
on the company's dummy lead — produced a ULC that never fired a single outreach
trigger. A placeholder lead uses `origin='placeholder'` and is treated like any
other. Journeys still wait, because `trg_ulc_create_journey_run` needs both
`full_name` and `linkedin_url`; AI triggers fire immediately.

A placeholder lead is keyed on the email address (or a name inside a resolved
company), always carries a name — a nameless one would collide with
`unique_dummy_lead_per_company` — and records the address as a `lead_contact` so
the next sighting resolves to the same person.

The conversation backfill (`ConversationProcessor.process`) is the live
caller: it creates the lead for a counterpart nobody is yet, and links the
participant row, only once its sales check has read the thread and judged it a
sale. Email sync creates leads only on a thread a connection already holds, for
people at the lead's own company (`services/email/thread_people.py`); elsewhere
`ConversationService.upsert_participants` links a participant to the lead
`LeadPlacementService.resolve` finds (writing nothing) and leaves anyone else
`participant_type=2`, unlinked. Standing a placeholder in at sync for any sender
made a lead of every notification, receipt and newsletter sender before anything
had read them. Automated mailboxes
(`is_automated_address` — `noreply@`, `bounce@`, `mailer-daemon@`) are never
resolved at all, and a message a service delivered to the owner again
(`services/email/redelivery`) is left out before anything is stored. Dummy leads are still
used for *company* pipeline placement
(`get_or_create_company_dummy_ulc`), which represents a company rather than a
person — that is a different job and is unchanged.

`get_or_create_lead` is the lower-level writer, and only creates against an
identity the database enforces (`linkedin_url`, `mini_profile_urn`). An email or
a name can *find* a lead but is not safe to invent one from directly; that is
what the placement service's ledger exists for.

`PlaceholderReconciler` closes the loop: once enrichment writes identity onto a
placeholder, the next pass resolves it to the real company and merges it away,
retiring the `identity_key`. Placeholders are meant to liquidate, not accumulate.
A claim with no entity — tier 2, or an escalated one — is retried from its
recorded payload, so an `ambiguous` verdict settles itself once the duplicates
behind it merge. Each row is handled inside its own savepoint, and the queue
leases with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, stamping every row it hands back so a pass
always advances past claims that cannot resolve yet.

`LeadPlaceholderReconciler` is the lead side. Enrichment records a discovered
LinkedIn URL or URN on the placeholder's **claim**
(`UnresolvedLeadClaimRepository.record_identity`), since `sales.lead.linkedin_url`
is unique and the real person may already hold it. The pass merges the
placeholder into the lead holding that identity; when no lead holds it yet, it
creates the person first (`get_or_create_lead`, origin
`lead_placeholder_reconciler`, exported as `RECONCILED_LEAD_ORIGIN`). Only the
identity recorded on the claim creates a lead.

`LeadTwinMerger.merge_if_twins(lead_ids, session=...)` merges leads that are
one person stored twice, as `judge_lead_twins` decides: their LinkedIn
identities agree (at most one profile id, at most one profile URL, and a row
holding both when both appear), and every row without LinkedIn carries the
survivor's full name. Two ids, two URLs, a URL and an id no row links, no
LinkedIn at all, a Dummy or a placeholder: nothing is merged. Nor across
connections of more than one customer.

Retiring the claim is the **merge's** job, not the caller's. Any caller can
merge a placeholder away — `simpl_flow`'s LinkedIn enricher does — and one that
forgets leaves a row whose entity the foreign key nulls while
`resolution_state` stays `pending`: a claim with nothing to merge and nothing to
resolve, sitting at the front of the queue forever. `MergeResult.claims_retired`
reports how many were repointed.

`CompanyMergeService` / `LeadMergeService` fold a duplicate into a survivor:
nullable fields are backfilled, every known reference is repointed, colliding
rows are dropped, and the source is deleted only after a sweep proves nothing
*registered* still references it. The sweep iterates the hand-maintained
registry, not the FK catalogue, so a table missing from the list is invisible to
it; `tests/test_dedup_merge_completeness.py` checks the registries against
`pg_constraint` to keep that drift detectable. `MergeResult.skipped_tables`
lists reference tables absent from the current database — check it, because a
skipped table is also skipped by the safety sweep.

A company merge also rewrites the source's id to the survivor's in every
`sales.icp_filters.wish_list` that names it (`IcpFilterRepository.repoint_company`;
count in `MergeResult.wish_lists_repointed`). Company-list imports pin targets
and exclusions with `company.id IN ('…')` / `NOT IN` there, which no foreign key
reaches.

A company merge folds an owner's conversations on both sides into one before
repointing them, because `uq_conversation_owner_company` holds an owner to one
`sales_outreach.conversation` per company (`ConversationFoldService`, in
`simpl_core.services.dedup.conversation_fold`). The oldest keeps every thread,
message, draft, attachment and trigger log of the others; the folded count is in
`MergeResult.conflicts_resolved["sales_outreach.conversation"]`. Two that hold
the same thread or message raise `MergeConflictError`.

A merge runs in its own savepoint and is all-or-nothing: it blanks the source's
uniquely-indexed identity columns and flushes *before* repointing references, so
a failure partway through must not leave the source stripped. A company merge
never deletes the surplus dummy lead either — it folds it through
`LeadMergeService`, because `user_lead_connection` cascades from `sales.lead`
and `conversation_participant.participant_id` is polymorphic with no FK at all,
so a plain delete destroys connections and orphans participants, silently.

A company merge can be undone when it goes through `ReversibleCompanyMerge(session)`
(`simpl_core.services.dedup.reversible_merge`). It merges through `CompanyMergeService`
only when the merge would only move rows, and records everything it changed in
`sales.company_merge` and `sales.company_merge_step` (migration
`20261112170000_company_merge_journal.sql`, applied before the first such merge):

```python
merges = ReversibleCompanyMerge(session)
checked = await merges.check(source_id, target_id)  # .reversible, .reasons, .references
merge_id = await merges.merge(source_id, target_id, by="graphia-identity", proof={"page": page})
result = await merges.undo(merge_id, by="admin:<uuid>")  # UndoResult; .report() for logs
```

`merge` raises `MergeNotReversible(reasons)` and changes nothing when the merge would
drop or fold anything: a reference row that would collide with one of the target's, an
owner with a conversation on both companies, more than one placeholder lead on a side,
the source's placeholder lead referenced by anything (it would be merged, not deleted),
a pending LinkedIn enrichment request on both, or a reference table with rows to move
and no primary key. Putting those back would replay what their triggers did when the
rows were made, so they are left to `CompanyMergeService` and a person.

`undo` runs once (`ValueError` "was undone"), and refuses whole when the source's id is
taken again ("exists again"). It gives the target back the fields it took, each only
when unchanged since the merge (`target_fields_restored`), re-inserts the source with
its own id and its own placeholder lead, moves back each row that still points at the
target, and restores the claims and the wish lists it rewrote. A row changed since is
left as it is and counted in `left_as_they_are`. Data written to the target after the
merge stays there.

`merge_page_twins(session, pages, by=, dry_run=True)`
(`simpl_core.services.dedup.company_twins`) merges registry twins by their LinkedIn
company page (the `/company/<name>` part, e.g. `"example-works"`). `page_rows` reads
every row on the page in every spelling, and on the numeric page of the id its rows
hold; `judge_company_twins` decides: exactly one row holds a `company_linkedin_id`, so
it survives and the rows with none are its twins. A page with several ids, or none, is
`unproven` and merges nothing. A name is never proof, but a name that fits neither the
page nor the other rows stops a merge (`name_fits_page(name, page)`, `names_alike(a, b)`:
one holds the other's letters, a word in common, or one abbreviates the other, legal
forms set aside: "unicatt" is Università Cattolica, "nber" the National Bureau of
Economic Research; a page name under four characters or all digits fits anything): a
page whose id row fits neither its page nor any twin's name is `unproven` (2026-11-13,
"Comune di Venezia" merged into a row named "SpaceX" on the city-of-venice page), and a
twin that fits neither the page nor the survivor's name is `skipped`. Each twin merges reversibly in
its own savepoint; one that is not reversible is `skipped` with its reasons and the run
goes on. `dry_run` checks only, and reports what each merge would move.
`TwinReport.counts()` gives pages, merged, skipped, unproven and failed; `merged` entries
carry the `merge_id`.

`merge_page_twins_each(db, pages, by=, dry_run=True)` does the same with a
`DatabaseManager`, each twin in its own transaction, committed as soon as it is merged.
Use it for any batch: a merge moves rows across dozens of tables and takes seconds, so
one transaction over many twins holds their locks and the database's vacuum horizon for
the whole batch (2026-11-12: 182 merges in one transaction took 36 minutes). A twin's
statements may run `MERGE_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT` (10 minutes, `SET LOCAL`); a twin whose merge
or page read still fails is rolled back alone, listed in `failed` with the error and
logged (`company_twins.merge_failed`), and the batch goes on (2026-11-16: one merge cut
at two minutes moving 1,556 job offers failed a whole identity run).
`merge_page_twins` stays for a caller that must merge inside its own transaction.

Lead merges preserve customer scope: `sales.lead_contact` rows keep their
`scope` and `user_id`, are never promoted from `user` to `global`, and are
deduplicated only within a single scope and owner. One customer's privately
sourced contact details never become visible to another.

Which contact rows a seller may use is one rule,
`simpl_core.repositories.lead_contact.seller_contact_reach(user_id)`: the seller's
own rows, a colleague's (`scope='user'`, owned by another user of the seller's
customer) and global rows; never another customer's private rows, and a seller with
no customer has no colleagues. It returns SQL conditions (`own`, `colleague`,
`shared`, `visible`) and the ranking (`rank`: primary first, then own, colleague,
global). `LeadContactRepository.find_best_contact`, `record_user_contact` (a
colleague's row is `VISIBLE`; `BLOCKED_PRIVATE` means another customer's seller holds
it) and `EnrichmentService.check_contact_availability_with_scope` read through it, and
simpl-outreach's address choice must too, so a sender and the decision that wrote the
send agree. The rows stay as they were written: nothing is promoted to `global`.

A shared coordination objective follows a lead merge. When a seller is
connected to both leads and one connection is dropped, the objective's
`source_ulc_id` / `owner_ulc_id` (and its participants' `ulc_id`) move to the
surviving connection, and its `lead_id` / `company_id` follow lead and company
merges. At most one objective per source connection and responsibility may be
open, so when both connections carry an open one the merge raises
`MergeConflictError` rather than dropping live work: close one, then merge.
`FkReference(on_conflict="refuse")` is how a registry entry asks for that
instead of the default drop.

A connection a lead merge drops into the surviving one (same seller on both
leads) is recorded on the survivor as `ulc.merged` (`ULC_MERGED_EVENT`) in
`outreach_events`, with `from_ulc_id`, `to_ulc_id`, `from_lead_id`,
`to_lead_id`, in the merge's transaction. A connection that only moves to the
surviving lead records nothing.

## CRM providers

`get_crm_service` / `get_crm_export_provider` (`services/crm/factory.py`) build
a provider from `integration.integration_configuration.provider`, which must
equal the row's `slug` and the Composio toolkit slug: `hubspot` or `attio`. Both
run through Composio and the customer's `customer_connection`, and both pin a
toolkit version in `composio/client.py`. Callers never branch on the provider;
every provider returns the same shapes:

- `search_deals` rows: `{"id", "properties": {"dealstage", ...},
  "associations": {"companies": {"results": [{"id"}]}}}`.
- One stage-id string everywhere: `get_pipelines()` stage ids, `dealstage`,
  `get_deal_stages()` values and what `upsert_deal_stage_for_company` echoes.
- Fatal failures raise `CrmAuthError` / `CrmQuotaExceededError` /
  `CrmRateLimitError` (the scope's circuit breaker); per-record ones raise plain
  exceptions.
- `resolve_owner_id(email)` answers `None` when the CRM has no such owner or
  the connection cannot read owners (logged as a warning with the cause); quota
  and rate-limit failures propagate.
- `upsert_deal_stage_for_company` (the stage-mapping push) is one
  `CrmExportProvider` method built on `create_process_object` /
  `update_process_object`, the writes CRM flows use: a deal an earlier run
  created under the same `simpl_deal_key` is adopted, never created twice, and
  a rejected stage update raises.
- The Composio plumbing (connection binding, error classification, retries) is
  `services/crm/composio_provider.py`, shared by both providers.

**Attio** (`attio.py`, `attio_export.py`), as it differs from HubSpot:

| Concern | Attio |
|---|---|
| Objects | `companies`, `people` (Simpl leads), `deals`; flows name them so |
| Pipeline | the deals `stage` status attribute; pipeline id `stage`, stage ids are status ids, written by title |
| Matching | LinkedIn URL (any spelling), domain, email; a uniqueness conflict on `domains` / `email_addresses` links the existing record without writing to it |
| Values | formatted per attribute type from the live schema; attributes the workspace lacks are dropped |
| Deal | `owner` is required: the seller's workspace member, else the member who connected Attio; with neither the deal fails before writing; `associated_company` / `associated_people`; unique `simpl_deal_key` text attribute (visible, Attio has no hidden attributes) |
| Associations | a person's `company` is set only when empty; a flow association `label` names the deal's record-reference attribute |
| Messages | a note on the person (else company, else deal); found again by parent, timestamp, title and body, since notes carry no custom fields (a failed lookup raises rather than writing a second note); Attio cannot edit notes, so an update keeps the note and recreates it only if deleted |

## CRM sync scopes

A `crm_export_config` row is a *scope*. It is born in `dry_run` mode, and the
scheduled `crm_sync` flow in `simpl_flow` never claims it in that mode:
`CrmExportConfigRepository.acquire_next_config` filters on `execution_mode =
'live'`, so neither the hourly run nor the manual backfill touches a scope that
has not been promoted. Everything before promotion is API-driven:

1. `CrmDryRunService.run` evaluates the scope and stores an immutable plan in
   `integration.crm_dry_run_report`, without mutating the CRM or Simpl state.
2. `CrmDryRunService.apply` performs that plan once, through
   `ReconciliationService.run_scope_sequence` — the same sequence the scheduled
   flow runs for a live scope — over the window the report was evaluated with,
   then stamps the report (`applied_at`, `applied_by`, `applied_summary`). The
   scope stays `dry_run`; the customer verifies the writes in their CRM.
3. Promotion (in `simpl_api`) flips `execution_mode` to `live` only when the
   *latest* report is applied. A re-run after a mapping change therefore has
   to be applied and verified again before it can go live.

`apply` takes the scope lock through `CrmExportConfigRepository.claim_config`,
the only lock path that ignores execution mode, and releases it whether the
sequence succeeds or fails. It raises `CrmDryRunNotFoundError` for an unknown
scope or report and `CrmDryRunConflictError` for a live scope, an
already-applied report, a disabled scope, a scope with no workflow or with
neither a pipeline nor a flow, or a scope whose lock is held — the same preconditions the scheduler's claim
query imposes, so applying never performs writes promotion would not. A report
is applied at most once; a fresh plan needs a fresh dry run.

**Each live scope syncs on its own schedule.** `gcp_crm_sync` ticks every 15
minutes (`SYNC_TICK`); a scheduled tick calls `acquire_next_config(...,
due_only=True)` and claims only scopes whose `next_sync_at` is null or past. The
claim books the next slot: every `sync_interval_minutes` (default 15, at least a
tick), inside the active hours of the scope's seller (`scope_seller_id`: whoever
connected the CRM, else the customer's first user; unset hours fall back to
07:00-19:00 UTC, the window sends use), starting one tick before the window opens
so a run lands before the first send. Seller hours apply every day, like sends.
A targeted or backfill run (`due_only=False`) still takes every eligible scope.
`request_sync(config_id)` makes a scope due on the next tick. The columns come
from migration `20261102_crm_scope_sync_schedule.sql`; apply it before running
this core.

## Strategy contract: plans, directives, facts

The strategy layer plans; the outreach walk does. They meet only in core tables
(`migrations/20261104f_strategy_contract.sql`, design in
`.account-strategy/STRATEGY_REWRITE.md` of the workspace):

- **Plans** (`sales.company_plan`, one row per version, and `sales.plan_branch`) are
  written only through `services.strategy.plan_writer.PlanWriter.write`, which runs the
  deterministic `plan_check.check_plan` first (staleness per person, a 14-day commitment
  horizon, patterns, account caps, one branch per person, refusals, identities, the exit)
  and returns the refusals instead of writing. A `PlanDraft` with `close=True` (no branch)
  closes the company (`not_worth_it`) and drops its review; one with `rest_until` (no
  committed branch) rests it until then; any other plan wakes a resting company. The next
  review is the earliest of the plan's date, an intent's date less the commitment horizon,
  and a callback date a person at the company gave (`PlanWriter.callbacks_ahead`).
- **Directives** (`sales.outreach_directive`) are what the walk reads:
  `repositories.strategy.directive.OutreachDirectiveRepository.open_for_ulc(ulc_id)`.
  One live directive per identity and person; content never changes (a newer one has
  `epoch + 1`); states move in core only. `services.strategy.directive_emitter.DirectiveEmitter.reconcile`
  writes them from the current plan (idempotent), and wakes the walk with a
  `strategy_directive` trigger (or `lead_claimed` for a connection claimed now) whose
  `trigger_data` carries `directive_id` and `epoch`.
- **Facts** go back as `outreach_events` the walk writes: `directive.acknowledged` and
  `directive.cannot_follow` (with `directive_id` and `epoch`), `lead.declined` (with
  `decline_class`), `lead.referral.named`, `lead.callback.requested` (with `callback_at`,
  an ISO 8601 date or date and time), `lead.opening.found` (with `text`), plus the
  existing replies, acceptances and `call.scheduled`
  (`models.strategy.facts.StrategyFact`). `services.strategy.plan_facts.PlanFacts.scan`
  reads them and moves directives, branches and the review queue (`sales.strategy_review`).
  The walk never writes a directive, a branch or a plan.
  - A decline, an opt-out and a booked call end what they end at once and are reviewed at
    the end of the window; a referral, a directive that cannot be followed or expired, and
    a finished people read re-plan at once (`models.strategy.review.REPLAN_AT_ONCE`) and
    are claimed first (`StrategyReviewRepository.claim_due`: those, then first plans, then
    the rest). A callback is due on its date, an opening at the end of the window.
  - `stop_when: refusal` fires only on a `refusal` or `opt_out` decline, from anyone at the
    company; a softer decline stops nobody else's branch.
  - A material fact holds back only the branches on the person it is about (the emitter's
    `held_back`); nothing pauses a whole company.
- **Entry**: a company offered to a seller of a customer with an active
  `sales.pool_scope` enters `sales.pool_company` and gets a review
  (`services.strategy.pool_entry`). `emits_directives = false` is shadow.
- **Jobs** for simpl_flow to deploy: `services.strategy.jobs.admit_offers`,
  `scan_facts`, `reconcile_directives`, `observe_outcomes` (scoring, below) and
  `report_health` (overdue or failed reviews alert).
- **Scoring** (`services.strategy.scoring`, 0.8.357): `observe_outcomes` writes one
  `sales.strategy_outcome` row per person tried once the try's 30 days have passed, never
  earlier, whatever happened to the branch: `directive` (a strategy directive opened on the
  person; a directive opened inside an earlier try's window on the same connection belongs
  to it) or `history` (a thread of a customer with an active scope that no directive led,
  from its first outbound touch, within 180 days: the customer's own base). Each row has the
  try's shape (objective, purpose, the first move made and its channel) and the first
  acceptance, reply, decline and booked call in the window. A positive reply is a reply with
  no decline, known only when the window started after the walk began writing declines
  (`declines_recorded`). `outcome_rates` gives N and the rates per customer, source and
  shape; `prediction_skill` scores the planner's four predictions per customer and pattern
  against the customer's own history rate (`SkillRow.base_rate`), `pooled_prediction_skill`
  across all. `sales.branch_outcome` is retired: nothing reads or writes it.
- **Ask tags and the exchange guard**: `journey_log.ask_objective`, `ask_purpose`,
  `directive_id`, `plan_branch_id` (migration `20261104g_journey_log_ask_tags.sql`,
  stamped by the walk's persist step). `services.strategy.ask_guard.exchange_ask_allowed`
  says whether an exchange call may be asked of a connection's person now: none in 90 days,
  no opt-out, fewer than two refusals, over every connection of the customer to them.
- **Planner run**: `services.strategy.planning_run.run_planning(db, planner, now=...)` claims
  due reviews, reads each company's `dossier.PlanningDossier`, asks the planner (the model,
  behind `PlannerPort`, implemented in simpl-outreach), writes through `PlanWriter`, and
  sends refusals back to the planner at most twice. A spent `daily_plan_budget` defers the
  review to the next UTC day.
- **Company people read**: a branch may target a role (`plan_branch.target_role`) instead of
  a lead. When its window opens the emitter queues one `sales.people_search_request` on the
  branch's identity (`services.strategy.people_reads.PeopleReads.request_for_branch`) and
  the branch waits (`people_read`). `services.strategy.people_search_runs.run_people_searches(db,
  operations=DatabaseLinkedInOperations(db), now=...)` runs the queue on the sellers'
  extensions, one company people search per keyword (`search_company_people`, paced as
  `company_people_search`; the search autopilot runs, not the global `people_search` capped
  at three results): every profile read becomes a `sales.lead` with a current `lead_experience` at
  the company (autopilot's `AutopilotLeadService.bulk_upsert_from_linkedin`), and the company
  is reviewed at once (`people_read`). People at a company are those leads, whoever read them
  (the dossier lists them with the headline read). A role read within 30 days (failed within
  7, or still open) is not read again: the plan check refuses it (`role_already_read`).
  Search batch helpers shared with autopilot: `services.linkedin_proxy.search_batches`.
- **Standard's work and languages**: `services.strategy.standard_work.standard_state` says
  whether `standard` still works a connection (`in_flight`, `replied`, `done`,
  `not_standard`: 3 unanswered messages on a channel, 30 quiet days, a booked call or a
  reply keeps it standard's). A directive supersedes `standard` (approval class
  `supersede_standard`) only while it is `in_flight` or `replied`. `services.strategy.languages`
  reads a company's languages from its country (ISO alpha-2 or alpha-3); the plan check
  refuses an identity who speaks none of them when a colleague does (`language_mismatch`,
  from `sales.user.spoken_languages`). The dossier carries both.
- **Email reach**: `services.strategy.reach.email_reach(session, lead_ids=..., identity_user_id=...,
  company_id=..., now=...)` says per person `on_file` (an address the identity may use),
  `lookup` (a name plus the company's site or a LinkedIn profile, and no provider answer in
  90 days) or `none`. The plan check refuses an email-only branch to a `none`
  (`email_unreachable`); the dossier shows each person's reach for the customer's sellers.

- **Scope settings and scoring for the dashboard**: `services.strategy.scope_settings.ScopeSettingsService`
  starts a customer's scope (shadow by default, one active per customer), updates only the
  fields given (`ScopeSettings`), and ends it; refusals carry `code` `already_active`,
  `no_active_scope` or `invalid_setting`. `services.strategy.scoring.pooled_prediction_skill`
  scores each measure across customers and patterns. There is no skill gate before emission
  (2026-11-04): the strategy runs as a bet and is scored from production branches.
- **Directive lifecycle rules** (review of 2026-11-05):
  - A plan branch with `continues_branch_id` on a committed live branch carries it on as it
    is (`PlanWriter`): identity, person, dates, moves, channels, budget, ask, guidance and
    predictions come from the live branch, so a replan keeps its directive. A change is a
    new branch; its directive supersedes the old one, keeps the chain's
    `touches_counted_from` and waits for approval again when its class needs one. A
    directive re-created on a carried branch finds its predecessor through the branch
    lineage: it keeps the budget start, and the approval when content and class are
    unchanged.
  - A directive still awaiting approval never opened: whatever ends it withdraws it
    (`models.strategy.directive.ended_state`). Each fact runs in its own savepoint; one that
    raises is logged (`strategy.fact_failed`) and passed.
  - Opening records the connection as it was (`prior_claimed`, `prior_status_id`; NULL when
    standard was working it). Ending for a reason that is not the person's
    (`directive_delivery.RELEASING_REASONS`) cancels the directive's wakes and queued rows
    and gives a claimed connection back, unless the person engaged on the thread since the
    chain opened (`ENGAGEMENT_FACTS`: then only its wakes go and the walk answers as
    standard); a call, referral, reply or decline keep the thread.
  - Shadow (`emits_directives` false) withdraws live directives; an approval after the
    timeout expires, one on a shadow or ended scope or a closed company withdraws.
  - An opt-out or a refusal ends the person's live directives on every identity, and gives
    back the other identities' threads with their queued rows cancelled.
  - Pattern support counts the customer's `directive` outcomes per branch pattern (every
    row is a full window); a branch planned in shadow, refused or timed out is no try.
  - Reconcile takes the least recently reconciled companies first
    (`pool_company.last_reconciled_at`); branches only on open companies of active emitting
    scopes. A plan's next review is never sooner than the scope's review window.

## Light moves: profile views, reactions, comments

A light move is a `journey_log` row with `action_type` `light_move` on channel
`profile_view`, `post_reaction` (with `payload.activity_urn`, optional `reaction_type`),
`post_comment` (with `activity_urn` and `message`; a draft the seller approves) or
`post_repost` (an instant repost without a note, with `activity_urn`; only a post whose
thread is an activity, else it fails as `repost_needs_activity_thread`). They are
`PROXY_KINDS` (`repositories.outreach.journey_dispatch_rules`); no channel starts with
`linkedin`, so the extension's task queue never selects them, and the dispatch trigger
(`20261104i`) checks their fields and freezes `activity_urn` once approved.

`services.light_moves.executor.run_light_moves(db, operations=DatabaseLinkedInOperations(db),
now=...)` runs them through the LinkedIn proxy on the seller's own extension: one move per
seat per run, the seat's daily limits from simpl_api's `sales.linkedin_light_move_limits`
(warming ramp, steady views 15, reactions 8, comments 2, reposts 1; a profile view counts
OSINT research's seat reads too, below), a reaction, a comment
or a repost only after a profile view in 14 days and on the person's own post at most 21
days old. A view stores the
person's posts in `sales.post` (`author_kind` person, `source` `linkedin_profile_activity`,
`models.post.Post`, `repositories.post.PostRepository`), each with `thread_urn`: the post's
social thread (an activity, `ugcPost` or `share` URN, not always the activity's), where a
reaction or a comment goes. `payload.activity_urn` names that thread: a producer takes it from
`sales.post.thread_urn`, never builds it from `post_id`. A post the person reposted as it is
(its actor is not the person) or reshared with their words is `is_repost`, never their own.
The port takes the thread: `react_to_post(thread_urn=...)`, `comment_on_post(thread_urn=...)`,
`repost_post(thread_urn=...)`. Events: `models.light_moves.LightMoveEvent`
(`linkedin.profile.viewed`, `linkedin.reaction.sent`, `linkedin.comment.sent`,
`linkedin.repost.sent`, `event_data.activity_urn` the thread); a reaction, a comment and a
repost count as touches. The proxy operations (`profile_activity`, `post_reaction`,
`post_comment`, migration `20261104h`; `post_repost`, `20261110c`) shipped disabled; on a live
seat (2026-11-10) `profile_activity` revision 2 (`20261110`, moduleKey's colon encoded),
`post_reaction`, `post_comment` and `post_repost` answered. The three writes are enabled by
their revision 2 (`20261110d`); `profile_activity` stays disabled. The flag is per operation,
not per customer: a consumer that must not send for some seats checks that itself (simpl_ops
admits only SimpL's own colleagues). `post_repost` calls LinkedIn's server-driven
UI action `createInstantRepost` (flagship-web), not the voyager API. `post_comment` and
`post_repost` set `redispatch: false` (never handed to an extension twice); one that times
out fails as `outcome_unknown`, never retried.

**One count of a seat's profile views** (`services.light_moves.seat_views`, extension plan
X8, Q17 (a); the review of simpl_core#549, I1): every profile a seller's extension opens is a
`profile_positions` proxy request, whoever asked (light-move views, employment checks,
simpl_flow's fresh profiles, research's seat reads), and they all spend the one
`view_daily_limit`. `seat_views_today(session, user_id, now=)` returns `SeatViews(limit,
spent)` (`.left`): the seat's `profile_positions` requests since the start of the UTC day,
any `source_type`, plus a light-move view claimed (`RUNNING`) whose request is not written
yet, once (`extension_proxy.seat_profile_views_since`, an owner-rights function that returns
a number; `research_read_repository.seat_profile_views_since(session, user_id, since)`;
partial index `extension_proxy_request_profile_views_idx`, migration `20261119150100`).
`LightMoveQueue.done_today` is not added: it would count light-move views twice. The
light-move executor calls `lock_seat_views(session, user_id)` (a transaction advisory lock)
before reading the count and keeps it until its claim commits; research's admission takes
the same lock inside its function. Employment checks and fresh profiles are counted but do
not check the count yet (their repositories' follow-up). `seat_of_customer(session,
user_id, customer_id)`: the user is active and the customer's, or lent to it through an open
`sales.identity_loan` (admission's seat check). A seat read checks the same rule again, and
the seller on duty (not on vacation, not held at the onboarding shortlist; the lead's sending
window is a message's rule, not a read's), in SQL under the seat's lock
(`extension_proxy.research_seat_refusal`).

**Research's seat reads in the proxy ledger** (`repositories.extension_proxy.
research_read_repository.ResearchReadRepository(session)`, migration `20261119150200`): the
research login holds no grant on the `extension_proxy` tables, which have no row-level
security; it calls `SECURITY DEFINER` functions that take the profile read's id and never a
seat or a profile: each finds the seat (the read's parent's `seat_user_id`), the profile
(the read's own URL, its public identifier) and research's share (the pinned policy's
`SeatReader.max_view_share` of the limit, rounded down, at least 1; default
`DEFAULT_MAX_VIEW_SHARE` 0.25, which is also its bound, in the model and in SQL; version
1's `max_views_per_seat_per_day` caps it too) itself,
and touches research's rows only (source type `RESEARCH_PROFILE_READ_SOURCE`,
`osint_profile_read`; idempotency key `osint_profile_read:<read id>`), on the caller's
session: `admit(read_id=, linkedin_account_id=, timeout_seconds=, presence_stale_seconds=)`
(the read's seat, customer, URL and policy read once, its parent row kept `FOR SHARE`;
`existing`, else under the seat's lock, at the clock read after it (`clock_timestamp()`, also
the request's `created_at`), a refusal, else the request for the seat's live extension
signed in to that account: `admitted`, `extension_offline`, `linkedin_account_mismatch`; the
device rule is `admit_request`'s, kept in step by hand), `admit_read(read_id)` (migration
`20261123130000`, for a login that may read nothing of the seller, such as the fetchers'
`scraping_worker`: the same admission, its arguments derived in SQL and never passed: the
seller's account from `sales."user".linkedin_url` by `utils.linkedin_identity.
linkedin_account_id_from_url`'s rule, `extension_proxy.linkedin_account_id_from_url`; the
active runtime revision's presence window; the read's seat reader's `wait_s` capped by the
`profile_positions` revision's `request_timeout_seconds`. One more outcome,
`seat_not_handed_out` (kill switch on, operation disabled, no readable profile URL), then
`slot_not_reached` (the read reserved no slot, or its slot is more than a second ahead),
after `no_read`, `no_seat` and `existing`; only the outcome and the request id come back,
and a login granted `research_read_admit(uuid)` cannot call the four-argument one),
`refusal(read_id)` (a pre-check:
`no_read`, `no_seat`, `seat_not_the_customers`, `seller_off_duty`, `seat_views_spent`,
`research_share_spent`, or `None`). The rows they trust are kept by osint's triggers
(`20261119210000`): a research request's `seat_user_id` never changes after admission
(only cleared by the user's delete), and a profile read's customer and lead are its
parent's and its URL the lead's stored LinkedIn URL in the platform's one spelling; `of_read(read_id)`, `next_slot_at(read_id)` and `reserve_slot(read_id)` (the seat's
`profile_positions` pacing slot, as `reserve_operation_slot` reserves it, once per read:
since `20261123130000` a repeat returns the read's slot, kept in
`extension_proxy.research_read_slot`, and moves the seat's slot no further; since
`20261123170000` `next_slot_at` answers that slot too when the read has one, else the
seat's next slot, and reserves nothing), `get`,
`time_out`, `consume`, `seat_online`, and the SQL conditions
`research_read_answered(request_id)` and `research_read_unconsumed(request_id)`.
`DatabaseLinkedInOperations.read_profile_positions(request)` parses a `profile_positions`
answer and acknowledges nothing, so a caller can keep the raw answer before its
acknowledgement lets the ledger drop it. `services.linkedin_proxy.profile_identity`:
`names_profile(positions, identifier=, mini_profile_urn=)` (the answer's public identifier
compared unquoted and case-folded, or the lead's URN) and `is_member_id(identifier)` (a
`/in/ACoAA...` member id, which `profile_positions` cannot read by).
`positions_to_experience(..., jobs_only=True)` (a research read): only
`sales.lead_experience` rows, companies found by their LinkedIn id and never created,
attached or promoted, no claim resolved, no lead or connection company filled; a position of
a company SimpL does not know is written with no `company_id`, its LinkedIn id, URL and name
in `raw` (`PositionsWritten.unknown_companies`), told apart by that id
(`LeadRepository.add_experience_if_absent(company_linkedin_id=)`).

## CRM flows

A scope may declare a *flow* in `crm_export_config.config["flow"]`: which CRM
records Simpl creates and moves through a pipeline, when each is created, which
stage it sits in, and what it is named and stamped with. A new customer's CRM
process is then configuration rather than code. A scope without a flow keeps
the stage-mapping push (`crm_pipeline_config.stage_mappings`) unchanged.

```json
{
  "flow": {
    "version": 1,
    "objects": {
      "companies": {"kind": "identity"},
      "contacts": {"kind": "identity"},
      "deals": {
        "kind": "process",
        "grain": "company",
        "stages_from": "status_mapping",
        "associations": [
          {"to": "companies", "label": "deal_to_company"},
          {"to": "contacts", "label": "deal_to_contact"}
        ],
        "name": "Simpl - {company.name}",
        "create_properties": {"source": "Simpl"}
      }
    }
  }
}
```

- **Objects** are keyed by the provider's object type. `identity` objects
  (HubSpot `companies`, `contacts`) are matched and linked through
  `ensure_external_entity_link_without_overwrite`, never overwritten. `process`
  objects are Simpl-owned; HubSpot supports `deals` today. HubSpot `leads` needs
  OAuth scopes the Composio-managed app cannot grant, so it has no spec yet.
- **Identity options**: `"create_missing": false` only links records the CRM
  already holds (found by the scope's `identifier_fields`; add `"domain"` there
  to match companies by domain). A process object whose company is missing is
  skipped with reason `<object>_not_in_crm`, recorded as no error and retried
  only when its facts change; a missing contact is left out of the
  associations. `"write_properties": [...]` limits a record Simpl creates to
  those CRM properties, the owner (`hubspot_owner_id`) included.
- **`skip_when_open_exists`** (company grain): `same_pipeline` or
  `any_pipeline`. When creation comes due and the company already has an open
  record of that type (HubSpot: a deal with `hs_is_closed` false), Simpl
  creates nothing and touches nothing, reason `open_record_exists`. Nothing is
  recorded, so once their record closes Simpl creates its own. The dry run
  cannot see the CRM, so its create entry carries `unless_open_exists`.
- **`grain`**: one record per Simpl contact (`contact`) or per company (`company`).
- **`stages_from: "status_mapping"`** takes the pipeline and stages from the
  scope's own status mapping (`crm_pipeline_config`), read on every plan and
  resolved with `stage_for_status` exactly as the standard push does. Prefer it
  whenever the customer's stages follow Simpl statuses: the mapping people edit
  on the scope stays the one answer to which status lands in which stage, and
  the flow adds only names, stamps, associations and `create_when`. Without
  `create_when` the record is created once the mapping puts the status in a
  stage. Only the object that mirrors the company deal (HubSpot `deals`) can use
  it, a scope with no pipeline or no mapped status is refused, and
  `forward_only` is refused because a mapping has no order.
- **`stages`** (with `pipeline`) are the alternative for stages the mapping
  cannot express. They are ordered most advanced first; the first rule that holds wins.
  `stage_policy` is `follow_status` (every change applies), `forward_only`
  (never moves to a rule listed lower) or `set_on_create`.
- **`driven_by`** (company grain): `latest_contact_change` follows whichever
  connection changed status last, as company sync always has;
  `qualifying_contacts` follows the contacts that met `create_when`, and a
  recorded driver keeps moving the record after it stops qualifying.
- **Conditions**: `status_in` (workflow status names or `status_mapping_id`s: a
  name matches that one stage, a mapping id matches its whole category,
  customer sub-stages included; archived stages are refused),
  `outreach_sent` (an outbound message in a thread the contact shares with its
  owner; a connection request is not a message), `replied` (an inbound one),
  and `all` / `any` / `not`.
- **Templates** (`name`, string property values): literal text plus `{path}`;
  `{a|b}` takes the first non-empty value. Paths are listed in
  `simpl_core.services.crm.flow.TEMPLATE_PATHS`. A name that renders empty skips
  creation with reason `name_unresolved`. Names and `create_properties` are sent
  only at creation; `on_enter` properties go with a stage change.

**Blocklist.** `"blocklist": [...]` lists CRM records Simpl must not contact,
read on every run and never written to. A flow may hold a blocklist and no
process object at all, which reads the CRM and exports nothing: its scope is a
`pull` scope.

```json
{"version": 1, "blocklist": [
  {"object_type": "contacts", "list_id": "4021", "blocks": "person"},
  {"object_type": "companies", "property_name": "do_not_contact", "equals": true,
   "blocks": "company"}
]}
```

- A source is a list's members (`list_id`, a HubSpot list or segment; needs the
  `crm.lists.read` scope) or the records whose `property_name` equals `equals`
  (has any value when `equals` is left out). `blocks` is `person` or
  `company`; a blocked company always blocks the company, and a contact
  blocked with `company` blocks the person too.
- Each run reads every member id, fetches identifiers only for ids it has not
  seen (`integration.crm_blocklist_entry`, migration
  `20261029_crm_blocklist_entries.sql`), and marks members that left. A person
  matches a Simpl lead by the scope's CRM link, LinkedIn URL, then email; a
  company by its CRM link, the matched lead's company, then LinkedIn page or
  domain; one Simpl has none of goes through dedup placement
  (`CompanyPlacementService.resolve_or_placeholder`, source `crm_blocklist`): a
  placeholder company in the claim ledger, merged into the real company by the
  reconciler once Simpl meets it. An ambiguous match places nothing and is
  retried on the next run.
- Every connection of the customer's sellers to a blocked person, or at a
  blocked company, that is still before a meeting (Monitoring to Messaged)
  moves to its workflow's Not In Target status (history `crm:<provider>`,
  reason `crm_blocklist`), and its queued actions and wake-ups are cancelled
  in the same transaction. Later stages are left alone. A blocked company also
  gets a claimed placeholder connection at Not In Target, so opportunity
  generation skips it. A person Simpl has not met is matched again each run.
- Leaving the list does not move anyone back: nothing records the earlier stage.
- The scope's workflow must have a Not In Target status. The dry run reads the
  CRM and gives each member the first thing applying does for it (see below);
  `scripts/check_crm_flow.py` validates the blocklist but does not read it.

Flows are configured internally; the status mapping stays editable on the
scope, and a flow with `stages_from` honours it. To give a customer a custom setup:

1. Write the flow as JSON (the file may hold the flow or `{"flow": {...}}`).
2. Check it read-only against the scope and preview what it would do:
   `python scripts/check_crm_flow.py --config-id <scope> --flow flow.json --since <date>`.
   It prints `VALID` with the flow hash, or `INVALID` with the offending path,
   then counts every outcome and lists the proposed writes with names and
   stages. It never calls the CRM and stores nothing.
3. Store it with the statement `--print-sql` prints (a `jsonb_set` on
   `crm_export_config.config` that also sets the flow's `sync_direction`),
   after review.
4. Run the scope's dry run from the admin screens, apply it, verify in the
   CRM, and promote, exactly as for a standard scope.

Check a flow before saving it: `parse_flow(raw)` checks structure, and
`validate_flow(flow, provider_class=..., status_names=..., status_mapping_ids=...,
sync_direction=..., status_mapping=...)` checks it against the provider, the
scope's workflow and, for `stages_from`, the scope's status mapping.
Both raise `FlowConfigError`, whose `path` names the offending part. A flow
scope's `sync_direction` must be the flow's, `FlowConfig.sync_direction()`:
`push` when it has process objects (it writes to the CRM), `pull` when it only
reads a blocklist, `bidirectional` when it does both.

`ReconciliationService.run_scope_sequence` runs a flow scope through
`CrmFlowEngine` alone: the stage-mapping pull and push never run beside a flow,
and a `pull` flow scope syncs no emails or messages. `CrmDryRunService.run`
plans it with the same planner. The report lists `would_create_object`,
`would_set_object_stage`, `would_associate_object` and `would_skip_object`
entries (each with
`object_type`, `reason`, and for writes the stage before and after and the
rendered name), counts not-due outcomes as `would_skip_object_not_due`, and
stores the flow and its `flow_hash` in `configuration_snapshot`. A blocklist
gives every member one entry, each with a `reason`:
`would_mark_not_in_target` (its connections still in outreach stop),
`would_hold_blocked_company` (its company gets the claimed Not In Target
placeholder), `would_place_blocked_company` (Simpl does not know the company: a
placeholder company is placed and held, at most `COMPANY_MATCHES_PER_RUN` per
run), and the counted skips `would_skip_blocklist_entry` (matched, nothing left
to do) and `would_skip_unmatched_blocklist_entry` (nothing in Simpl, and nothing
identifying to place). Every member's connections are read in one query. A live run returns one
`ReconciliationResult` with `entity_type="blocklist"` whose metadata counts
what it read, matched, created and stopped.

Owned records live in `integration.crm_object_link` (migration
`20260916_crm_flow_object_links.sql`), keyed by a Simpl key also written to a
hidden unique CRM property, so a create that was never recorded is adopted on
the next run. A company's deal is mirrored into
`crm_export_record.external_deal_id`, which pull, engagement sync and the link
screens still read; a deal created before the scope had a flow is adopted from
there, and one without a Simpl key belongs to the customer and is never written.

## Transient database failures

Supabase's Supavisor pooler occasionally drops a connection handshake under
load. asyncpg surfaces it as a bare `TimeoutError` (the pooler logs
`ClientHandler: Timeout while waiting for message in state SCRAM final`), and
SQLAlchemy may re-raise it wrapped in a `DBAPIError`. The database is healthy,
so the same operation succeeds seconds later.

```python
from simpl_core.db.retry import is_retryable_db_error, run_with_db_retry

result = await run_with_db_retry(
    partial(repo.get_by_id, lead_id),
    attempts=4,
    label="email_sync.resolve_lead",
)
```

- `run_with_db_retry` re-awaits the factory from the start, so only wrap work
  that is safe to run twice.
- Retries are always bounded. Pair them with an outer scheduling guarantee (a
  Prefect schedule, a released claim) rather than raising `attempts`.
- `is_retryable_db_error` unwraps `DBAPIError.orig` and `__cause__`; use it when
  a caller needs to branch on transient-vs-permanent instead of retrying.
- `is_pool_exhaustion` is the narrower verdict: *our own* `QueuePool` refused a
  checkout (`sqlalchemy.exc.TimeoutError`), rather than anything the database or
  the pooler did. A direct-mode signal only — under Supavisor the engine runs on
  `NullPool`, which has no queue to time out on.
- Both classify; neither decides. `run_with_db_retry` retries pool exhaustion
  under its multi-second backoff, which suits coarse-grained work. Filter it back
  off with `is_pool_exhaustion` when your caller cannot afford the wait: when the
  retry is fine-grained enough that replaying it is itself load on the saturated
  pool, or when the caller holds a deadline (a renewable lease, a request budget)
  it can lose while queueing. `simpl_core.agent_framework.retry` does exactly
  that.
- `simpl_core.sales_domain.db_retry` is a back-compat alias for this module.

## Scraping contracts, object storage and operator notifications

Three small public surfaces back `simpl_scraping`; any consumer may read them.

### Scraping contracts

`simpl_core.models.scraping` and `simpl_core.repositories.scraping`, schema in
`migrations/20260911_scraping_page_contracts.sql`,
`migrations/20260915_scraping_page_deletion.sql`,
`migrations/20261105_scraping_extraction_publications_unlogged.sql`,
`migrations/20261105b_scraping_drop_page_versions_storage_object_idx.sql`,
`migrations/20261110_scraping_drop_extraction_publications_config_idx.sql`,
`migrations/20261110b_scraping_pages_extraction_marker.sql`,
`migrations/20261112160000_scraping_drop_extracted_entities.sql` and
`migrations/20261120120000_scraping_drop_page_versions_observed_idx.sql`:

- `ScrapingPage` (`scraping.pages`): one row per `(canonical_url, representation)`.
  `latest_version_id` names the newest observed raw response. `extraction_queued_*`
  (version id, config key, time) name the last extraction job simpl_scraping queued for
  the page, all set or all NULL; simpl_scraping writes them, nobody else should.
- `ScrapingPageVersion` (`scraping.page_versions`): immutable. It stores a reference to
  the object holding the response (bucket, key, version id, stored SHA-256 and size),
  never the body. The database rejects UPDATE. Its only indexes are `id`,
  `(page_id, id)` and `(page_id, artifact_kind, content_sha256)`: read a page's versions
  by `page_id` and sort them (about 1.7 per page), and reach the newest through
  `pages.latest_version_id`. Nothing indexes `observed_at` since `20261120120000`.
- `ScrapingExtractionPublication` (`scraping.extraction_publications`): one receipt per
  extraction job. The table is unlogged: a Postgres crash or a backup restore empties
  it, so treat a receipt as a recent record, never as proof a version was extracted
  (the extraction job's status is that record). A read replica cannot read it.

`ScrapingExtractedEntity` is gone (0.8.381), and `20261112160000` drops
`scraping.extracted_entities`, archived to Parquet first. simpl_scraping's README says
where the archive is and how to read it. What the product used of it is in
`sales.company`.

The repositories take an `AsyncSession` and never commit, so a caller can record an
observation inside its own transaction:

```python
async with db.session_scope() as session:
    pages = ScrapingPageRepository(session)
    page_id = await pages.ensure_page(canonical_url=url, host=host)
    observation = await pages.record_observation(page_id=page_id, version=new_version)
    await session.commit()
```

Identical content reuses the existing version. The latest pointer only moves forward
in `observed_at` order, so a late commit of an older fetch cannot roll a page back.
`PageObservation.extraction_queued` carries the page's last queued extraction job
(`ExtractionQueued`, or None), read from the row the observation locked;
`record_extraction_queued` replaces it in the caller's transaction.

Pages and versions may be deleted. Deleting a page deletes its versions; deleting the
version `latest_version_id` names moves the pointer to the newest remaining raw
response, or clears it. A receipt's `page_version_id` and an entity's
`source_page_version_id` read `None` once their version is gone. Stored objects are
not deleted with the rows.

`simpl_scraping`'s queue schema depends on these migrations being applied first.

### OSINT research contracts

The `osint` schema is OSINT research's request ledger (`simpl_core.models.osint`,
`migrations/20261112100000_osint_research_schema.sql`, seeds in
`20261112100100_osint_policy_seeds.sql`, changed by `20261117100000_osint_request_changes.sql`,
`20261117100400_osint_keep_what_research_found.sql` and, for search and internal requests,
each request's policy key and purpose, every submit's record and entity resolution's words
for a page's identity, `20261118130000_osint_search_requests.sql` with its seeds
`20261118130100` (search) and `20261118130200` (version 2 of each request policy, for the
real callers), and for heartbeat deadlines `20261119140000_osint_heartbeat_deadlines.sql`
with its seed `20261119140100` (version 3 of person, company, topic and search, inactive)).

**Four rules (Jackie, 2026-11-17)** shape the ledger:

1. **A customer only enforces limits** (daily budgets, seat views). It never separates
   information: no per-customer copy, no per-customer read filter. Every customer reads
   every request. `customer_id` is on `osint.request` (and `osint.search_call`) for
   budgets only, as a plain uuid with no foreign key: **T3 checks at submit that the
   customer exists**, since that is where the id comes in from outside.
2. **Nothing research found is ever deleted:** no expiry, no sweep, no cascade from a
   customer. A customer's delete works and leaves its research, whose requests keep the
   deleted customer's id.
3. **Facts don't expire.** `scraping.research_fact` has no `delete_after`
   (`20261117100500`).
4. **Postgres stores identifiers, not page text.** Research, fetch and crawl return page
   version ids and where each page is stored (bucket, key), never its text: no request
   asks for text (no `want_text`, `20261117100000`) and no table holds it
   (`scraping.research_page_text` dropped, `20261117100600`).

**Research is a retrieval tool (plan v10).** The basic research API returns identifiers:
the found pages, references to SimpL's own records about the subject, index hits and
profile reads. It builds no answers: there is no `research_answer` table and no
`answer_updated` event; the caller does its own processing. A store of facts built from
what research finds is later work. "Questions" (`request.questions`, a policy's
`questions[]`) are the **fact kinds** extraction looks for; the names stay.

- `OsintRequest` (`osint.request`): one research, fetch, crawl, profile_read or search request.
  - **The customer is optional** (`20261118130000`): `customer_id` NULL is an internal
    request (staff or a SimpL service asking for no customer). A source type listed in the
    policy's `budget.internal_by_source_type` counts its internal requests in a bucket of
    its own (requests or pages, and dollars); every other internal source type shares one
    internal bucket under the per-customer caps (plan Q42 (a)). The idempotency key is
    unique per customer and, through `request_internal_idempotency_key`, on its own among
    internal requests.
  - **`policy_key`** (`20261118130000`): the key of the policy version the request pins,
    filled by the insert trigger from `policy_id` (callers never write it) and fixed. A
    research request may run under any active policy of its subject type (an identity run
    beside the news default), so the run in flight is one per `(subject_type, subject_key,
    policy_key, questions)` (`request_research_in_flight_key`).
  - **`purpose`** (`20261118130000`): why the request was made, a key the pinned policy's
    `purposes` accept, set at submit and fixed; NULL only on a profile read (its parent's
    purpose applies).
  - **`RequestSubmission`** (`osint.request_submission`, `20261118130000`, service_role
    only): one row per submit that admitted a request, whatever it found: `outcome`
    `created`, `joined` (the run in flight) or `fresh` (a done run within freshness), with
    `customer_id`, `source_type`, `source_id`, `purpose` and `idempotency_key`. The key is
    unique per customer (`request_submission_customer_key`) and among internal submits
    (`request_submission_internal_key`). A retried submit reads it and gets the request
    its first submit got, whatever the outcome; the retry writes nothing. Not a budget
    charge: budgets count `osint.request` rows. Requests written before the file have no
    row (none exist in production).
  - **search** (`20261118130000`): `submit_search` writes one, the research worker
    runs it (T5's search: SimpL's landing index, then the web) and keeps its hits in
    `osint.research_source` (`decision` `landing_index`, or `search_hit` for a web hit,
    never fetched). Columns, search only and fixed at admission: `queries` (the caller's
    order, trimmed, distinct, 1-2,048 characters each), `engines` (sorted; the policy's
    when the caller names none), `timelimit` (`d`, `w`, `m`, `y` or NULL). States `queued ->
    searching -> done | failed | timed_out`, `queued -> failed | timed_out`, and the
    hand-back `searching -> queued`; no `refused` (a submit refuses without writing). No
    inbox row. **One row per URL** (`research_source` is unique on `(request_id, url)`): a
    URL several queries or engines find is kept once, by the first query and engine to find
    it. The worker writes hits in the order it runs the searches (queries in the request's
    order; for each, the landing index before the web; each call's hits by rank) with `ON
    CONFLICT (request_id, url) DO NOTHING`, so the row's `search_call_id`, `engine` and
    `rank` are the first find's and a call's `hits_kept` counts only the rows it wrote.
  - **Submit, in order:** (1) read the submit's record by `(customer_id,
    idempotency_key)`: a retried submit gets the request the first one got and writes
    nothing; (2) research: the **fresh-facts rule**: any customer's latest research
    request for the same `subject_type`, `subject_key`, `policy_key` and question list
    (`request_subject_idx`, newest first) with status `done` and `finished_at` within the
    policy's `freshness_days`. A hit is returned and recorded (`fresh`), not counted in the
    daily budget. A failed, refused or running request is no hit; (3) research: time out
    an expired in-flight twin with the same in-flight key, any customer's; (4) `INSERT ...
    ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` with no conflict target (a target of `(customer_id,
    idempotency_key)` would raise on an in-flight index), then read back by idempotency
    key, then by the in-flight key `(subject_type, subject_key, policy_key, questions)`,
    across customers, for every subject type (`request_research_in_flight_key`). If none
    finds a row (the twin finished in between), start over. A second caller who shares an
    in-flight request, whichever customer, gets that request (`joined`); only its submit's
    record is written. Every customer may read every request and its events; the run is
    charged once, to the customer whose request started it (Q26).
  - Research stores its question list in one spelling, sorted: a caller that names no fact
    kind gets the policy's whole list. **No request asks for page text** (rule 4):
    `osint.request` has no `want_text`, and no inbox row asks for text.
  - **Nothing expires** (`20261117100400`): `osint.request` and `osint.search_call` have no
    `delete_after`, and no sweep deletes a request, its events, sources or search calls.
    Their `customer_id` has no foreign key, so a customer's delete (`simpl_api`'s
    `delete_customer_cascade`) leaves them; the budget indexes on it stay
    (`request_customer_kind_created_idx`, `search_call_customer_idx`).
    `osint.er_label.customer_id` keeps its ON DELETE SET NULL.
  - **profile_read is internal and automatic** (T24): a lead's profile on a profile
    platform, read for its positions and activity. From X58 (`20261123120000`) the
    research worker queues one scraper inbox row per reader it tries (kind
    `profile_read_<reader type>`), so a fetcher reads and the extractor parses.
    - **No caller asks for one:** T3's public submit refuses kind `profile_read`. The
      research search pass (T4/T5) starts one, as a child of a person research request
      (`parent_request_id`, no action on delete: a parent with a read is never deleted),
      when the person has a profile URL of an enabled platform, given in the request or
      stored on the lead. A trigger holds that the parent is a person research request, of
      any customer (runs are shared).
    - **Platforms are data** (`ProfilePlatform`, `osint.profile_platform`: key, anchored
      `url_pattern`, `canonical_form`, `is_enabled`; seeded `linkedin`,
      `https://www.linkedin.com/in/<id>` as `normalize_lead_linkedin_url` writes it). A
      read names its `profile_platform`; the trigger refuses an unknown or disabled
      platform, a URL its pattern does not match, and a pinned policy other than
      `profile_read_<platform>`. A new platform (Instagram, say) is a row, a policy key and
      its readers' code, never a schema change.
    - **The child read** has its own claims, deadline and reuse rule: any customer's ready
      read of the same platform and `lower(url)` younger than the platform policy's
      `reuse_max_age_s` is reused (`request_profile_read_reuse_idx`). Its budget is the
      platform policy's daily cap per customer (and `by_source_type`), counted on its own
      `customer_id` (usually the parent's), not the parent's research budget. It takes the
      parent's `source_type` and `source_id`, the lead, the parent's `company_id`, and an
      idempotency key from the parent (`profile_read:<parent id>:<platform>`). What it read
      is kept by identifier (`raw_page_object_key`), with the job rows it inserted
      (`lead_experience_ids`, X58); other facts built from it are later work.
      The relay writes its progress into the parent's log (event type `profile_read`, dedup
      key `profile_read:<child id>:<status>`); the parent does not wait for it.
    - **Starting one** (`RequestAdmission.profile_read(parent, profile, policy)`, the
      research worker's search step, extension plan A4): `parent` is a `ProfileReadParent`
      (request id, customer, source type and id, lead, company), `profile` the subject's
      `ProfileUrl`, `policy` the active `profile_read_<platform>` version. It returns a
      `ProfileReadAdmission(read, outcome)`: `reused` (a ready read younger than
      `reuse_max_age_s`), `joined` (the read in flight) or `created` (written with its
      first `status_changed` event; no `osint.request_submission` row). It raises
      `RequestRefused` before writing: `profile_refused` (the platform is unknown or
      disabled, or the URL is not its profile in its one spelling: checked first, so the
      insert trigger never fails the parent's transaction), `source_type_refused` (the
      policy's `source_types` are the research source types it reads for) or
      `budget_spent`. The caller writes the parent's `profile_read` event, which names the
      read (`queued`/`reading` when created or joined, the read's own event when reused);
      `request_event_profile_read_key_idx` (`20261119150300`, built `CONCURRENTLY`) finds
      every parent whose log names a read, so the worker copies the read's end to each.
    - **Version 2 of `profile_read_linkedin`** (`20261119200000`, inactive): version 1 plus
      `entity_resolution` in `source_types`, and `budget.internal_by_source_type` giving
      internal reads of `agent`, `planner` and `entity_resolution` a bucket each (100 reads,
      5 dollars a day), as person research's versions 2 and 3 do; `staff_endpoint` and
      `cli` share the internal bucket.
    - **Version 3** (`20261120110000`, inactive; the one to activate): version 2 with the
      Olostep reader's `timeout_s` 120 instead of 60. A lead's guest page took 70 to 105 s
      through Olostep (2026-11-20), and simpl_scraping calls Olostep in a step of its own
      that allows it. It also writes out the reader's `country` "US" and `parser`
      "@olostep/linkedin-profile".
    - **`OlostepReader.country`**: the residential country of the Olostep call
      (`OlostepCountry`; default `DEFAULT_OLOSTEP_COUNTRY` "US", so versions 1 and 2 read as
      US). Only countries verified live are usable; today US. RU and RANDOM never: LinkedIn
      is blocked in Russia, and a read with no country got a Russian ISP's block notice
      (2026-11-20). Another country goes in by its own change, after a re-test shows real
      profiles through it.
    - **`OlostepReader.parser`**: the Olostep parser the call asks for (default
      `DEFAULT_OLOSTEP_PARSER` "@olostep/linkedin-profile", which answers the profile as
      JSON). simpl_scraping reads a profile through a parser only; the guest page's HTML is
      no longer read (Jackie, 2026-11-20).
    - It carries `lead_id` (required at admission), the platform, `url` in the platform's
      one spelling, the parent and optionally the company; no research, fetch or crawl
      column. One read in flight per platform and `lower(url)`, across customers
      (`request_profile_read_in_flight_key`): a second parent's pass, whichever customer,
      gets the running one, so a seat view is never spent twice on one profile.
    - **What the read did, by identifier** (T24 writes them; refused on every other kind,
      but a person research carries the seat its submit named in `seat_user_id`):
      `seat_user_id` (the `sales."user"` whose seat read the profile), `proxy_request_id`
      (the `extension_proxy.request` the seat's read went through; the relay's "the seat's
      answer has arrived" rule reads it), `raw_page_object_key` (where the raw page is
      stored; the page is not in Postgres) and `lead_experience_ids` (X58, `bigint[]`: the
      `sales.lead_experience` rows the read inserted, never the rows the lead already had;
      `{}` when none was new, NULL when it wrote none; no foreign key; each id positive,
      none NULL, none twice: `osint.is_lead_experience_id_list`). The
      research worker (`scraping_research`, UPDATE on the whole table) writes it in the
      update that ends the read, copied from the extraction's result; no trigger limits
      it, and `osint_caller` has no UPDATE. A research read selects it from 0.8.461, so
      `20261123120000` is applied before any service runs that version. A seat's
      light-move profile views and its
      research reads are counted in one place (`services.light_moves.seat_views`), against
      the seat's `view_daily_limit`, with the policy's `max_views_per_seat_per_day` as
      research's share.
      States: `queued -> reading | ready | refused | failed | timed_out`, `reading ->
      ready | refused | failed | timed_out` (`reading` holds no claim; `queued -> ready`
      reuses a read).
    - **For T24:** claim a `reading` request only when a reader's answer has arrived or
      its wait has ended (then move to the next reader in the policy's `readers`), never
      to poll: every claim counts against `max_attempts`.
  - A lead request (person research, profile_read) may carry a company in `company_id`: a
    person's employer as research loaded it (the lead's own company; subjects are loaded by
    id, never pinned), or the company a profile read was asked for. A change feed per
    company is later work. The planner submits as `source_type` `planner`.
  - The request pins an `osint.policy` version of its kind (person, company, topic, fetch,
    crawl, profile_read) and sets `pages_due_at` and `deadline_at`. What admission writes
    never changes, except `lead_id` and `company_id`, which follow merges, and
    `pages_due_at` under a heartbeat (`20261119140000`, X18): on a research, crawl or
    search request whose pinned version has a `heartbeat`, the claim holder may move it
    forward, before it has passed, in an update that keeps the claim, never past
    `deadline_at` less the pinned version's `finish_window_s` (the trigger reads the
    version; each refusal has its own message). `deadline_at` never moves. The research
    worker (simpl_scraping `research/relay.py`) moves it on each progress with
    `SET pages_due_at = GREATEST(pages_due_at, LEAST(now() + idle_s, deadline_at -
    finish_window_s)) WHERE id = :id AND claim_token = :token AND pages_due_at > now()
    RETURNING pages_due_at`, so a progress that comes early or after the request is due
    writes nothing instead of failing its transaction, and moves the request's open
    `scraping.research_inbox` rows' `deadline_at` to the returned time.
  - **Claims:** the database enforces each kind's state machine and the claims: a new
    `claim_token` only before `deadline_at`, on an open row, never from a holder younger
    than `reclaim_after_s`; each claim adds 1 to `attempt_count`, capped at
    `max_attempts`. SKIP LOCKED does not skip a committed claim, so every claim query
    filters `finished_at IS NULL AND deadline_at > now() AND attempt_count < max_attempts
    AND (claim_token IS NULL OR claimed_at <= now() - make_interval(secs =>
    reclaim_after_s))`: one row the database refuses makes a batch claim raise. A long
    pass renews with `UPDATE ... SET claimed_at = now() WHERE claim_token = <its token>`.
  - **What resets `attempt_count`:** only a release with `attempt_count = 0` after the
    pass committed at least one event or a forward status move. A hand-back
    (`searching -> queued`, `resolving -> waiting_for_pages`, claim cleared) is not
    progress and keeps the count, as does a crash, an error or an empty pass. The search
    pass, the relay and the resolving pass share the one count, so a relay pass that made
    progress also clears a search pass's failures; the deadline still bounds them.
  - **What the claim fences:** `append_request_events` refuses any token but the row's.
    A status UPDATE or a `research_source` verdict is fenced
    only inside the transaction of a fenced append, or with `AND claim_token = <token>`.
    A writer without a claim (the submit, the timeout) appends with a NULL token, which
    works only while no claim is on the row: the timeout clears the claim in the update
    that times the request out. No human decides a page's identity: there is no staff
    review and no `page_reviewed` event (`20261118130000`).
  - **The relay claims only what has something new**, never every open request on a
    timer: an inbox row whose state has no event yet (anti-join on the event's
    `dedup_key`; the migration header maps inbox states to keys, and `queued`/`seeded`
    have none), an extracted page with no `page_resolved`, crawl pages past
    `relayed_crawl_sequence`, or an open request whose `pages_due_at` has passed and whose
    row has not been written for 20 s (`updated_at <= now() - interval '20 s'`, so a
    hand-back is not claimed again on the next tick; `request_open_idx` serves that scan).
    Woken by `pages_due_at`, the pass ends the wait with what is there: research
    `waiting_for_pages -> resolving` with the pages that arrived (still `queued`:
    `failed`), a crawl `seeded -> ready` with its pages, a fetch `timed_out`. The table's
    fillfactor is 70, so claims, renewals, releases and `last_sequence` updates stay HOT.
  - **Timeouts:** the worker times out expired requests on every 5 s tick, through
    `request_open_idx`, with an update conditional on `finished_at IS NULL`. Nothing
    waits for a daily sweep: an expired research request keeps its in-flight twin out
    until it is timed out.
- `RequestEvent` (`osint.request_event`): each request's numbered, append-only event log.
  - Write it only with `SELECT osint.append_request_events(request_id, claim_token, events)`:
    the caller's claim token (NULL only for a writer that holds no claim, the submit and the
    timeout), which must be the row's, so a pass whose claim was taken over cannot write;
    and a JSON array of `{"type", "dedup_key", "payload"}`, each payload at most 4,096 bytes
    of JSON text (`octet_length(payload::text)`).
  - It locks the request row (`FOR NO KEY UPDATE`, the lock any update of the row takes)
    to the commit, so sequences are gap-free in commit order, and updating the request
    before appending in the same transaction cannot deadlock with another append.
  - It drops events whose `dedup_key` the request has, sets `request.last_sequence` (the
    only way it moves), and refuses to run outside READ COMMITTED.
  - A `status_changed` event gives `payload.to` and no `dedup_key`: the function assigns
    `status:<to>:<n>` (the n-th visit to that status). It drops a status event that
    repeats the latest logged status (two sweepers, a retried transaction), so one move
    is one event and a real second visit after a hand-back is still logged, and it
    refuses a batch whose last status event is not the row's current status: append a
    status event only in the transaction whose `UPDATE ... RETURNING` moved the row.
  - A direct insert, update or delete is refused.

**OSINT policies** (`simpl_core.services.osint`): one versioned document per policy key in
`osint.policy`, of kind person, company or topic (research), fetch, crawl, profile_read or
search. The seed migrations' headers explain every key (`20261112100100`, `20261117100100`,
`20261118130100`).

- **Models** (`services/osint/policy.py`): `parse_policy_document(kind, document)` returns a
  `PersonPolicy`, `CompanyPolicy`, `TopicPolicy` (all `ResearchPolicy`), `FetchPolicy`,
  `CrawlPolicy`, `ProfileReadPolicy` or `SearchPolicy`, or raises pydantic's
  `ValidationError` naming each key at fault.
  - **search** (`SearchPolicy`, key `search`, seeded version 1 inactive): `budget`
    (`RequestBudget`: requests per customer per day, `by_source_type`, cost 0 while ddgs is
    free), `engines` (at least one; a request may name only these), `landing_index`,
    `max_queries` (a request with more is refused), `max_results_per_query`, and the common
    keys. No `inbox_priority`.
  - **crawl** (`CrawlPolicy`): `pages_per_crawl`, the most pages one crawl may take (25, the
    scraper policy `research_crawl`'s `traversal.max_pages`; keep them equal). Added after
    version 1, whose versions read it at 25. The daily page budget counts each crawl at the
    active version's `pages_per_crawl` (or the pages its run admitted, when more) until its
    inbox row finishes, then at the pages its run admitted, at least 1.
  - **profile_read** (`ProfileReadPolicy`): **one policy key per platform,
    `profile_read_<platform>`** (kind `profile_read`; seeded `profile_read_linkedin`
    version 1, inactive). A platform's key must exist and be active for its reads to run.
    Keys: `use_limits`, `budget` (the platform's own daily cap per customer, counted on
    the child reads), `reuse_max_age_s`, `readers`, and the common keys (`source_types`:
    the source types whose research may start a read). `readers` is an ordered list of
    typed readers (`ProfileReader`, told apart by `type`): `seat`
    (`max_views_per_seat_per_day`, `wait_s`; the seat's own view limit still applies) and
    `olostep` (`timeout_s`). A new reader is a new type in code and a policy version, no
    schema change. At least one reader, each type once, their waits and timeouts together
    within `pages_due_s`. No `inbox_priority` (it writes no inbox row: that key is the
    research, fetch and crawl kinds' only).
  - **Heartbeat deadlines** (research, crawl and search; X18): `heartbeat: {idle_s,
    max_s}`, `null` by default (fixed times, the behaviour before the key). With it,
    `pages_due_s` is the first wait; each progress (a new hit, a stored, extracted or
    resolved page, a crawl page; never a failed page) before `pages_due_at` passes moves
    it to `idle_s` after the progress when that is later, never back and never past
    `max_s` after the submit; admission sets `deadline_at` to `max_s` plus
    `finish_window_s` after the submit. Every policy model has `pages_due_cap_s` (the
    latest `pages_due_at`, in seconds after the submit) and `pages_due_idle_s` (`None`
    without a heartbeat), so the worker reads any pinned version alike. A
    fetch and a profile read have no `heartbeat` key. Version 3 of person, company and
    topic (180 / 120 / 900 s) and of search (30 / 20 / 120 s) is seeded inactive by
    `20261119140100`. Activate it only when two things hold. First, every service that
    loads OSINT policies (the research worker, the `osint_policy` command, any caller
    that submits or reads requests) runs simpl_core 0.8.427 or later: an older one
    refuses a document that names `heartbeat` (`extra="forbid"`), so every submit and
    read under it fails.
    Second, the research worker moves `pages_due_at`: before it, research ends at its
    first wait, 180 s (version 2: 600 s), and search at 30 s (version 2: 60 s), whatever
    each still finds.
  - **Push-down host lists** (research; next plan X68): `search.push_down_host_lists`,
    `osint.host_list` keys, empty by default and not in the defaults, so a version stored
    before the key never changes. The research worker (simpl_scraping 0.8.191 or later)
    decides a hit on a listed host, or a subdomain of one, after every other hit before
    the page caps: still fetched when the caps leave room, never dropped. Version 4 of
    person and company (version 3 with `["research_push_down"]`) and the list's first
    hosts (pitchbook.com, crunchbase.com, dealroom.co, scholar.google.com) are seeded by
    `20261123150000`, inactive. Activate it, or any later research version that names a
    list, only once every service that loads OSINT policies runs simpl_core 0.8.464 or
    later: an older one refuses the key (`extra="forbid"`). A version that names no list
    leaves the key out of its document, so it loads on any simpl_core. Topic policies
    refuse the key: topics keep the engine's order. A host goes on or off the list with a
    row, no new version.
  - **Per source type budgets** (research, profile_read and search):
    `budget.by_source_type: {source_type: {max_requests_per_customer_per_day}}`, empty by
    default. A listed source type's requests count against its own cap and the kind's, so
    the kind's 0 still stops everything; every listed source type must be in
    `source_types`. The planner's budget (`planner`) is a later policy version, no deploy.
  - **Internal budgets per source type** (every kind):
    `budget.internal_by_source_type: {source_type: {max_requests_per_day,
    max_cost_usd_per_day}}` (fetch and crawl: `max_pages_per_day`), empty by default: a
    listed source type's internal requests count in a bucket of their own, alone; the
    others share the one internal bucket under the per-customer caps (the behaviour
    before the key). A cap set to `null` is no cap: the bucket is counted and never
    refused for it. Every listed source type must be in `source_types`.
  - **Purposes** (every kind): `purposes`, the list a request's `purpose` must be in;
    `null` by default, which takes any purpose (the behaviour before the key).
  - **Version 2 seeds** (`20261118130200`, inactive): person, company, topic, fetch, crawl
    and search take `staff_endpoint`, `cli`, `agent`, `planner` and `entity_resolution`,
    with purposes `staff_review`, `agent_task`, `outreach_planning`, `entity_resolve`,
    `entity_confirm`, `entity_recheck`, and internal buckets for `agent`, `planner` and
    `entity_resolution` (research and search at the kind's per-customer cap in requests and
    that cap times `max_cost_usd_per_request` in dollars, search 1 dollar; fetch and crawl
    500 pages and 5 dollars). Activate them per key with the command line (Jackie's yes in
    production). `profile_read_linkedin`'s version 2 is `20261119200000`'s (entity
    resolution added, with the same internal buckets), and its version 3
    `20261120110000`'s (Olostep's call 120 s).
  - **Entity resolution uncapped** (`20261123170000`, inactive): the `entity_resolution`
    bucket's caps all `null` (uncapped) over the version before, on the same six keys:
    version 5 of person and company (over the push-down seed's version 4), version 4 of
    topic and search (over the heartbeat seed's version 3) and version 3 of fetch and crawl
    (over version 2); the agent's and the planner's buckets unchanged. Version 2's caps were
    guesses that would stop entity resolution's volume on its first day, and the worker
    records no cost yet, so no dollar cap measures anything. Activate a key only once every
    service that loads research policies (simpl_scraping's worker, the `osint_policy`
    command, each caller that submits or reads requests) runs a simpl_core that takes a
    null cap: an older one cannot parse these versions. The file fails, writing nothing,
    unless all six versions end uncapped. Not copied into `tests/setup_test_db.sql`.
  - Research's `freshness_days` is the fresh-facts window. No kind has a page text key:
    Postgres keeps no page text.
  - **Schema versions:** a stored version is never rewritten, so a key leaves through a
    schema version. Version 1 (a document without `schema_version`: the T1 seeds) carried
    `retention_days`, for research `anchor_required`, and for fetch and crawl
    `want_text_default`, `max_text_chars` and `text_retention_days`; version 2
    (`SCHEMA_VERSION`) has none of them, since nothing research finds expires, a subject is
    loaded by its id, and Postgres keeps no page text. The model reads a version 1 document
    with them dropped, and refuses a version 2 document that names one, as any unknown key.
    New versions are stored resolved, at version 2; `profile_read_linkedin`'s version 1 is
    seeded at schema version 2. The fetch and crawl seeds' `purpose` still says "with its text when asked",
    as stored.
  - **Reworded free texts:** the research defaults' `use_limits` start "Shared by every
    customer" and the person's `who_may_be_researched` is "Any lead, by its id". No code
    reads either, and every stored version holds them written out, so no stored version
    changes; the T1 seeds keep "Requesting customer only" and "Anyone ... with at least
    one anchor". The new wording reaches a new version only from the defaults:
    `osint_policy add` merges its file over the key's active version, so a version added
    over an active seed keeps the seed's wording unless the file names `use_limits` and
    `who_may_be_researched`; with no version active it takes the defaults.
  - **Defaults:** every key has one, its kind's value in `POLICY_DEFAULTS`
    (`services/osint/policy_defaults.py`, version 1 as seeded except the reworded free
    texts, read at schema version 2).
    They resolve a stored
    version and fill keys added later; objects merge key by key, a list or
    `er.signal_weights` given replaces the default whole. Versions are stored resolved
    (every key, `schema_version` included). `merge_policy_document(kind, base, document)`
    merges a partial document over a whole one the same way.
  - **Strict:** an unknown or misspelled key is refused, and so is a value of another type.
    The rules between keys hold on the resolved document: among them
    `claim.reclaim_after_s < finish_window_s`, engines only from
    `SUPPORTED_SEARCH_ENGINES` (yahoo,
    yandex; the search code imports the same constant), query templates with only
    `QUERY_PLACEHOLDERS[subject_type]`, the subject's name as one quoted phrase and no `OR`
    or `|`, every question named by a query, `vat_code` never in `er.hard_keys`, and for
    fetch `server_wait.max_s < pages_due_s` (crawl: `server_wait` null), and with a
    heartbeat `pages_due_s` and `heartbeat.idle_s` at most `heartbeat.max_s`. So are the
    database's limits: seconds and `claim.max_attempts` at most 2,147,483,647, and never
    `page_text` as a research `extraction_profile`. A daily budget of 0 is valid.
  - **Immutable:** a parsed policy is frozen to the leaves (tuples, read-only mappings).
  - **A key added later** must get a default that reproduces the behaviour before it
    existed (on its model field, or in `POLICY_DEFAULTS` for every kind when it differs by
    kind), so every stored version keeps passing: the active one, those running requests
    pinned, the rollback targets. Never make a new key required, never change a value in
    `POLICY_DEFAULTS`, and bump `schema_version` when a model change must tell old
    documents from new ones.
- **Load** (`services/osint/policy_loader.py`): keep one `PolicyLoader` per process.
  - `await loader.load(session, policy_key, kind)` returns the active version as a
    `LoadedPolicy` (`id`, `policy_key`, `kind`, `version`, typed `document`; its type
    narrows with a literal `kind`), or `PolicyNotActive(policy_key)` when the key has no
    active version. It raises `InvalidPolicyError` when the version is of another kind or
    its model refuses it: on every load, never a fallback. Each key's result, a refusal
    included, is cached 60 s, so an activation reaches every process within 60 s with no
    deploy, and a broken policy costs one read per window and raises the same exception
    each time.
  - `await loader.load_any(session, policy_key)` is `load` for a key of any kind: a caller
    that refuses another kind itself (a research request's `policy_key`).
  - `await loader.load_version(session, policy_id)` returns the version a request pinned,
    read and validated once per process (versions never change); `LookupError` when it
    does not exist.
  - Pin `LoadedPolicy.id` on the request (`request.policy_id`). For T3: `PolicyNotActive`
    cannot be stored as a refused request row, because `request.policy_id` is NOT NULL, so
    the submit refuses without writing a row.
- **Write** (`services/osint/policy_repository.py`, on the caller's session, never commits):
  `add_version(policy_key=, kind=, document=, created_by=)` appends the key's next version,
  inactive and resolved over the kind's defaults, under a per-key advisory lock; `activate(policy_key=, version=)`
  makes it the key's one active version and returns an `Activation` (`policy`, `previous`,
  `changed`). Both refuse a document its model refuses and a host list
  (`search.excluded_host_lists`, `search.push_down_host_lists`) with no host in
  `osint.host_list`; `add_version` also
  refuses a key that already holds another kind. A version's content never changes.
- **Stop a kind** by activating a version whose daily budget is 0
  (`budget.max_requests_per_customer_per_day` for research, profile_read and search,
  `budget.max_pages_per_customer_per_day` for fetch and crawl). There is no deactivation,
  as for simpl_scraping's resource policies. **Roll back** by re-activating an older
  version.
- **Command line** (writes are a dry run, rolled back, unless `--yes`; exit codes 0 done,
  1 refused, 2 usage, 3 database error):

  ```bash
  python -m simpl_core osint_policy show [--key person]
  python -m simpl_core osint_policy validate --kind person --file person_v2.json
  python -m simpl_core osint_policy add --key person --kind person --file person_v2.json \
      --by jackie.wang@thesimplplatform.io [--yes]
  python -m simpl_core osint_policy activate --key person --version 2 [--yes]
  ```

  `add` takes a partial file: the keys it leaves out come from the key's active version
  (resolved, as stored), or from `POLICY_DEFAULTS` when no version is active; objects merge
  key by key, a list or `er.signal_weights` given replaces the active value whole. Its dry
  run prints the new version as a diff against the active one, which then shows only what
  the file changes (the whole resolved document when none is active). `show --key` prints
  the active document as the loader resolves it. `add` requires `--by`, stored as
  `created_by`. As for simpl_scraping's resource policies, nothing records who activated a
  version: `activate` takes no `--by`, and each write with `--yes` logs one plain line (key,
  versions; for `add`, `created_by`). Activating in production needs Jackie's yes.

**OSINT requests** (`simpl_core.services.osint.requests`, plan T3): submit and read
research, fetch, crawl and search requests. There are no HTTP endpoints (simpl_api#897 was
closed): SimpL services and agents call `OsintRequests` directly, in process, on the
service's own database login. It admits and reads; the research worker runs every request.

- **The login's access** (`migrations/20261118160000_osint_caller_role.sql`, narrowed by
  `20261119160000_osint_writes_with_owner_rights.sql`): grant the calling service's login
  the role `osint_caller` (`GRANT osint_caller TO <login>`, in production with Jackie's
  yes, once the service calls research). The role may submit and read, never do the
  worker's writes:
  - `osint.policy` SELECT; `osint.request` SELECT, INSERT (admission writes the request);
    `osint.request_event` SELECT; `osint.request_submission` SELECT, INSERT;
    `osint.search_call` and `osint.research_source` SELECT; `scraping.research_inbox`
    SELECT, INSERT of fetch and crawl rows only (a RESTRICTIVE rule,
    `osint_caller_inbox_kinds`, `20261123120000`); `scraping.research_crawl_page`, `scraping.pages` and
    `scraping.page_versions` SELECT (page metadata of every page, since research may reuse
    the landing crawl's copy; never text).
  - EXECUTE on two SECURITY DEFINER functions, which run with their owner's rights:
    `osint.append_request_events` (every event, and the row's `last_sequence`) and
    `osint.time_out_request` (`OsintRequestRepository.time_out`: a submit that finds a run
    past its deadline ends it). So the role holds no INSERT on `osint.request_event` and no
    UPDATE on `osint.request`: a caller cannot write an event that skips the function's
    claim and numbering checks, nor claim, end or move the times of another customer's
    request (extension plan X38, since simpl_core 0.8.429; before it the role had both).
    `20261119160000` needs the role (`20261118160000`) first, and runs again after either
    `20261118160000` or `20261112100000` runs again: the first grants the INSERT and the
    UPDATE back, the second recreates `append_request_events` with the caller's rights. It
    fails when a role still holds either right through another grantor, PUBLIC or a
    membership.
  - Not granted: `osint.host_list`, `osint.profile_platform`, `osint.er_label`,
    `scraping.research_fact`, and any UPDATE or DELETE.
  - Each of those tables has row-level security, and the role's rule lets every row through.
    A login with grants but no rule reads zero rows and gets no error, so give a login the
    role, never the table grants alone.
- **What the login needs besides:** SELECT on `sales.customer`, `sales.lead` and
  `sales.company` (admission checks the customer, lead or company exists and is no
  placeholder; service logins have it already), and a Wasabi read key for the page bucket to
  read the pages a request returns (`services/osint/pages.py`).
- **Tests:** a login holding only the role submits and reads every kind
  (`tests/services/osint/test_osint_caller_role.py`); callers test against
  `FakeOsintRequests` and `WorkerSimulator` (below).

```python
from simpl_core.services.osint.policy_loader import PolicyLoader
from simpl_core.services.osint.requests import OsintRequests
from simpl_core.services.osint.submissions import FetchSpec, RequestOrigin, ResearchSpec

POLICIES = PolicyLoader()  # one per process: it caches the active policies 60 s
requests = OsintRequests(db.session_scope, POLICIES)
# One key per request: a key reused for another subject is refused. An agent's company
# research is taken by version 2 of the company policy (20261118130200) once it is active.
origin = RequestOrigin(customer_id=None, source_type="agent", purpose="agent_task",
                       source_id=run_id,
                       idempotency_key=f"run:{run_id}:research:company:{company_id}")
admission = await requests.submit_research(origin, ResearchSpec(company_id=company_id))
# admission.pages_due_at, .deadline_at (and .fresh_until for a fresh run): when to come back
read = await requests.read(admission.request_id, after=cursor)   # never waits
async for read in requests.follow(admission.request_id, max_wait_s=120):
    ...  # reads every 1 s (at least retry_after_s); yields reads with news; stops when
         # read.complete or the time is up
heads = await requests.heads(open_request_ids)  # many states in one query
```

- **Answer within a time budget** (picked-fixes plan X73): `follow` already stops at
  `max_wait_s`, and the request keeps running after it. A caller that must answer in, say,
  10 s follows with that budget, answers from the last read, keeps the cursor and reads
  the rest later. There is no other method to call:

  ```python
  async for last in requests.follow(admission.request_id, max_wait_s=10):
      ...  # a crawl's pages come with each read's events: keep them as they come
  answer_now(last)  # a research read's pages are every page found so far
  if not last.complete:
      keep(admission.request_id, last.next_cursor)  # the request is still running
  # Later, from the kept cursor: one read, or follow again with a new budget.
  rest = await requests.read(request_id, after=kept_cursor)
  ```

  `follow` always yields its first read, so `last` is set. The budget starts when `follow`
  starts, not at the submit, and is not a hard limit: `follow` checks it before each
  sleep, then reads once more, so its last read can end up to one read's query time after
  `max_wait_s`. For a hard limit, pass the budget less the time the submit took and about
  0.5 s for that last read. Expect a thin first answer from research: hits arrive at about
  5-7 s and a page takes about 5 s more to be stored, so in the 2026-11-25 benchmark (8
  requests) 28% of the final pages were stored by 10 s, and three requests had none.

- **Submit** (`submit_research`, `submit_fetch`, `submit_crawl`, `submit_search`): one
  transaction each, which returns an `Admission` (`request_id`, `kind`, `status`,
  `outcome`, `retry_after_s`, `pages_due_at`, `deadline_at`, and for `fresh` `fresh_until`:
  the done run's `finished_at` plus the policy's `freshness_days`) or raises
  `RequestRefused` (`reason`, `detail`, and for a spent budget `limit`) with nothing
  written. `RequestOrigin`: `customer_id` (None: internal; a given one must exist),
  `source_type` (one of the policy's `source_types`), `source_id` (free), `purpose`
  (required, key format: one of the policy's `purposes`, any while it lists none),
  `requested_by_user_id`, `idempotency_key` (1-256, no control character; one per request:
  name the subject in it, or use a fresh UUID per submit). Specs: `ResearchSpec` (a
  `ResearchSubject`: exactly one of `lead_id`, `company_id`,
  `topic=Topic(text, synonyms, exclude)`; plus `policy_key`, an active research policy of
  the subject type, None for the subject type's own key; the question list is that
  policy's whole list; `seat_user_id`, a person research only: the seller whose own
  LinkedIn seat its profile read may spend, an active user of the customer or lent to it
  through an open `sales.identity_loan`; an internal request (no customer) names none; None:
  the read goes to the public page, never a seat (Q16 (a)); stored on the request, not
  compared on a retry, and a joined or fresh run keeps its own), `FetchSpec(url, max_age_s)`, `CrawlSpec(start_url, max_age_s,
  policy_key)` (`max_age_s` None: the policy's `default_max_age_s`; `policy_key`, default
  `crawl`: an active crawl policy, `book_probe` for the book discovery run's probes; the
  request pins its version, whose `scraper_policy` the drainer seeds under once
  simpl_scraping reads it), `SearchSpec(queries, engines,
  timelimit)`. URLs must be http or https with a host. No text a request carries (key,
  topic, query, word to exclude, URL) may hold a control character (`NO_CONTROL_CHARACTERS`
  in `policy.py`; a query's tabs and newlines are folded to spaces first).
- **Order** (`admission.py`): lock the budget bucket (policy kind and customer, or
  internal); the idempotency key, read from `osint.request_submission` (`outcome`
  `existing` when the retry asks for the same: research the same `subject_key` and
  `policy_key`, fetch and crawl the same URL, `max_age_s` and policy key, search the same `queries`,
  `engines` and `timelimit`, every kind the same `purpose`; a default left out is resolved
  under the version the first submit pinned. It returns the request the first submit got,
  created, joined or fresh, even when that run has failed or stopped being fresh since; a
  key naming another kind, or asking for something else, is refused
  `idempotency_key_reused`); the customer, the policy key's active version (of the
  subject type's kind for research), its source types and purposes, then the kind's own
  checks (research: the lead or company exists and is no placeholder, with #513's rule,
  `SubjectResolver.refusal`, from the row alone; the seat it names); research: the fresh rule (`fresh`: any
  customer's done request for the same subject, policy key and questions within
  `freshness_days`) and the run in flight (`joined`; one past its deadline is timed out
  `failed` there); the daily budget over the last 24 hours; the insert (`created`), its
  first `status_changed` event and, for fetch and crawl, its inbox row; the submit's record
  (`created`, `joined` or `fresh`). `fresh` and `joined` count nothing. A lost insert race is
  read back by key, then by the in-flight key.
- **Budgets:** a customer's requests count in its bucket: research and search in requests
  (the kind's cap, and a source type's own cap from `by_source_type`); fetch in pages (one
  each); crawl at the policy's `pages_per_crawl` until its inbox row finishes, then the
  pages it admitted, at least 1. A page budget counts only the requests under its own
  policy key, so `book_probe`'s crawls never spend `crawl`'s buckets. Only page counts
  are per key: request counts and the dollar cap still count every request of the kind. An internal request of a source type listed in
  `budget.internal_by_source_type` counts in that source type's own bucket, alone: requests
  (`max_requests_per_day`; fetch and crawl: pages, `max_pages_per_day`) and dollars
  (`max_cost_usd_per_day`: a finished request at its `cost_usd`, a running one at least at
  the policy's `max_cost_usd_per_request`). Every other internal source type shares one
  internal bucket under the per-customer caps. A refusal for a spent budget carries
  `limit` (`BudgetLimit`): `unit` (`requests`, `pages`, `usd`), `cap`, `used`, `window` (24
  hours) and `retry_at` (when the oldest counted request leaves the window; None when none
  was counted, a cap of 0).
- **Refusals** (`RefusalReason`): `unknown_customer`, `not_found` (no lead or company has
  the id), `placeholder` (a placeholder company or a Dummy lead: #513's own check),
  `no_active_policy`, `policy_kind_refused` (a research or crawl `policy_key` of another
  kind),
  `source_type_refused`, `purpose_refused`, `budget_spent`, `private_address` (a private,
  loopback, link-local or otherwise non-public IP, `localhost`, `.local`, `.internal`,
  `.home.arpa`, a one-label host or an all-digit last label; no DNS lookup, `addresses.py`
  keeps simpl_scraping's `is_public_address` rule; `url_rejections` and excluded hosts are
  the drainer's), `too_many_queries`, `engine_refused`, `idempotency_key_reused`,
  `seat_refused` (a person research's `seat_user_id` is no active user of the customer nor
  lent to it, or the request is internal). There is no submit for `profile_read`.
- **Read** (`read(request_id, after=0, limit=200, kind=None)`): one `REPEATABLE READ READ
  ONLY` transaction; `RequestNotFound` for an id that is no request of `kind`. Every read
  (`RequestRead`): status and reason, `final` (the stored status is final), `expired` (past
  `deadline_at` while the stored status is not: no worker may claim it, so it has ended),
  `complete` (final or expired, with no event left after `next_cursor`; `follow` stops
  there), `pages_due_at` (when it stops waiting for pages; under a heartbeat it moves
  forward while the request finds pages, so a later read may show a later time),
  `deadline_at` (fixed at admission: the latest it can end, so a `follow` that should see
  the end passes a `max_wait_s` that reaches it), `policy_key`, `purpose`, the typed
  events after `after`, `next_cursor`,
  `last_sequence`, `retry_after_s`. `ResearchRead`: `pages` (each hit to fetch or held by
  the landing index: `state` pending, stored or failed with its `reason`, `page` =
  `StoredPage` with `page_version_id`, `host`, `final_url`, `http_status`, `media_type`,
  `charset`, `last_observed_at` (when the page was last fetched: a fetch's page and a web
  hit from `research_inbox.observed_at`, else, and for landing-index and crawl pages,
  `pages.latest_observed_at`, which may be of newer content when the page changed since),
  `content_first_seen_at` (the version's `observed_at`: the scraper keeps one version per
  content, so an unchanged page keeps it), `storage_bucket`, `storage_key`,
  `stored_content_encoding`, `stored_sha256` and `content_sha256`; `identity`: `status` in
  entity resolution's words, `same`, `different` or `unknown` (its two-way check,
  `Verification`), or `unresolved` while nothing decided, with `decided_by` (`hard_key`,
  `rule` or `judge`, ER's `Method` spellings; no human decides an identity) and the
  `hard_key` that matched), `skipped_hits` (each hit not fetched, with its `decision`:
  `excluded_host`, `identity_key_only` (a LinkedIn profile kept as the lead's identity key,
  never fetched), `other_profile` (another LinkedIn profile, for a lead with a stored one:
  only the stored profile is the lead's key), `no_name_in_hit`, `same_page` (a copy of a
  page the request already fetches: the same site name, the host's main label, and the
  same path once a leading language or locale segment is removed; the query counts),
  `document_link` (a URL whose path ends in one of the `research_page` scraper policy's
  `skip_extensions`: a PDF, an office file, an image), `over_host_cap`, `over_page_cap`;
  `SourceDecision`. `same_page` and `document_link` since simpl_core 0.8.522, migration
  `20261125210000`: a reader on an older simpl_core fails on a request that holds either),
  `resolved_subject` (#513's `ResolvedSubject`,
  fields a newer worker added ignored; None until the worker writes it), and
  `profile_reads` (its children and the reads its `profile_read` events name: status,
  reason, raw object key, `lead_experience_ids`: the `sales.lead_experience` rows a read
  inserted, `[]` when none was new, None when it wrote none; never the seat or its proxy
  request, which are one customer's).
  `FetchRead`: the inbox row's status and the page. `CrawlRead`: `counts` and the pages of
  the `crawl_page` events after the cursor, each with the `media_type` and `http_status`
  `scraping.research_crawl_page` holds. simpl_scraping's drainer fills them for stored
  pages only, so failed and skipped pages read None today; book corpus plan P2 copies the
  fetch job's HTTP status onto failed pages (no failed page's media type is recorded). `SearchRead`: every hit with its query and
  source.
- **Without events:** `heads(request_ids)` (at most 500, one query by primary key): a
  `RequestState` per id (`status`, `final`, `expired`, `last_sequence`, `deadline_at`,
  `policy_key`, `purpose`, times), an unknown id left out; read in full only the requests
  whose `last_sequence` passed your cursor. `latest(subject, policy_key=None)`: the
  subject's newest done run and the run in flight (not past its deadline), any customer's,
  under one policy key or any (`LatestResearch`). `history(subject, limit=20)`: its runs,
  newest first (1 to 100). All three write nothing and count against no budget.
- **A page's content** (`services/osint/pages.py`): `StoredPageReader(session_scope,
  store, bucket=store.config.bucket)` over simpl_core's `S3ObjectStore`.
  `page_text(page_version_ids)` (at most 50) reads each version's object, checks
  `stored_sha256`, gunzips per `stored_content_encoding`, checks `content_sha256`, and
  returns `StoredPageText(page_version_id, text)` with `page_text()`'s output
  (`search/landing/text.py`: title, visible lines, their block paths), in the order asked;
  a body gone from storage reads as `text=None` (logged). `page_bytes(page_version_id)`
  returns the body as fetched (`ObjectNotFoundError` when it is gone). The landing index
  reads its pages the same way (`search.landing.sources.read_page_body`). simpl_core runs
  in the caller's process, so **the calling host needs a read key for the scraper's page
  bucket on Wasabi** (`ObjectStorageConfig.from_env` with the variables the host sets); no
  session is held while objects download. A typed profile-read result waits for T24.
- **Events, the worker's contract** (`events.py`, plan Q43): one model per event type
  (`StatusChanged`, `HitsKept`, `PageStored`, `PageFailed`, `PageExtracted`,
  `PageResolved`, `CrawlPage`, `CrawlProgress`, `ProfileRead`), written only through
  `append_request_events(session, request_id, claim_token, events)`; each derives its
  `dedup_key` from T1's map, and payloads carry ids and counts. Writes refuse a field the
  model does not know; reads ignore it (a newer worker's), so one row never breaks every
  read of its request. `research_source.er_verdict` holds `IdentityStatus`'s value
  (`same`, `different`, `unknown`; NULL while unresolved) and `er_decided_by` a `DecidedBy`
  (`hard_key`, `rule`, `judge`). A hard-key match writes `research_source.er_signals` as
  `HardKeySignals(hard_key, value)`; a hard-key row with no `er_signals` reads as no hard
  key. `resolved_subject` holds #513's `ResolvedSubject`; a landing-index hit's
  `page_version_id` is set by the search step. `ProfileRead(child_request_id, status,
  lead_experience_ids, seat_answer_key)` is written when a profile read ends, first in the read's own log,
  then copied by the worker to the parent's once the parent holds no claim;
  `lead_experience_ids` are `sales.lead_experience`'s bigint ids (they were typed UUID
  before 0.8.428, which no stored event used) and name the rows written and the rows
  already there together, where the read's `osint.request.lead_experience_ids` names only
  the rows it inserted; `seat_answer_key` is where a seat's answer
  that could not be used is kept, when the read went on to its next reader.
- **Test doubles** (`services/osint/testing.py`), shipped with the package:
  - `OsintRequestsApi`: the API as a `Protocol` (`submit_*`, `read`, `follow`, `heads`,
    `latest`, `history`); type your dependency with it. `OsintRequests` implements it.
  - `FakeOsintRequests(clock=FakeClock(), retry_after_s=1, pages_due_s=600,
    finish_window_s=120, freshness=timedelta(days=1))`: in memory, for unit tests. The
    same spec models and `RefusalReason` values (a private address and a reused key are
    refused as the real API refuses them; queue any other refusal, with its `limit`, by
    `refuse_next(reason, detail, limit=)`). `script(request_id, (seconds, event), ...)`, or
    `script_next(...)` for the next request the code under test submits: each event shows
    once `fake.clock.advance(...)` reaches it, and the status follows its
    `StatusChanged` events; `follow` waits on the clock, so nothing really sleeps. Research
    joins the run in flight and gets a fresh done one. `set_details(request_id, pages=...,
    resolved_subject=..., hits=...)` sets what the worker's rows would fill.
  - `WorkerSimulator(session_scope, bucket="osint-test-pages", store=None)`: **test
    databases only, never production** (nothing research finds is deleted, so its rows
    would stay). It writes what the research worker will write, through the real path:
    `start` (research and search claim and move to `searching`; fetch and crawl are
    seeded), `store_page(request_id, url, html=, title=)` (a page version in
    `scraping.pages` and `scraping.page_versions` with a test bucket and key, and the
    research hit and inbox row, the fetch's page or one more crawl page; uploaded too when
    `store` is given, so `StoredPageReader` reads it), `add_hit` (a search hit),
    `resolve_page` (a research page's identity), then `finish`, `fail(reason)` or
    `time_out` (research has no `timed_out`: it fails with `RESEARCH_TIMED_OUT`). Events go
    only through the typed models and `append_request_events`; the caller reads with the
    real `read()` and `follow()`. It may drift from the real worker once that exists.

`simpl_core.models.scraping_research` defines the three scraping tables OSINT research and
the scraper share. Other packages read them, so simpl_core owns and migrates them, as it
owns pages and page versions: `migrations/20261112100200_scraping_research_tables.sql`
(applied after simpl_scraping's `034`, whose `scraping_app` role it grants), changed by
`20261117100500_scraping_research_facts_kept.sql` and
`20261117100600_scraping_drop_research_page_text.sql`. The extraction
job reason `research` they are extracted with is simpl_scraping's
(`088_extraction_jobs_research_reason.sql` there).

- `ResearchInbox` (`scraping.research_inbox`): the outbox from `osint.request` to the
  scraper, one row per URL a request wants (research, fetch and crawl rows unique per
  request and URL, `research_inbox_request_url_key`, partial since X58; a plain
  `research_inbox_request_url_idx` serves lookups by request), written in the
  transaction that writes the request. Kinds `research`, `fetch`, `crawl`, and one per
  profile reader, `profile_read_<reader type>` (`PROFILE_READ_SEAT`,
  `PROFILE_READ_OLOSTEP`; `20261123120000`, X58): the research worker writes one per reader
  a read tries, naming the read's own request and its profile URL, so the Olostep row can
  follow a seat row the seat refused; each reader runs once per read
  (`research_inbox_profile_read_key` on request and kind). Only the scraper's logins
  insert them (`osint_caller` is refused), and the drain seeds one only for an open
  profile read of that URL whose policy lists the reader. The check takes
  any reader's kind by its pattern, so a new `ProfileReader` type needs a value in
  `ResearchInboxKind`, no migration. A profile read row names its extraction profile (the
  extractor config for its reader's answer), moves as a research row does but never
  `reused`, and ends `extracted` naming its `extraction_job_id`. A research row
  carries its `subject_type` and research profile and no retention: research facts are
  kept (`20261117100500` dropped `facts_needed_until`). A fetch or crawl row has no
  profile and no text columns: it asks for no text (`20261117100600` dropped
  `retention_s`, `text_needed_until` and `text_failure_reason`, and the `page_text`
  profile is refused on every row). The scraper moves it through `ResearchInboxStatus`,
  only forward along its kind's path, and `RESEARCH_INBOX_TERMINAL` states are final; on a
  research row, `extraction_job_id` names the extraction whose facts answer it. Finished
  rows stay: T1's 30-day sweep is gone, and `20261117100500` dropped the index that
  served it (`research_inbox_finished_idx`).
- `ResearchFact` (`scraping.research_fact`): per extraction of a page version with a
  profile version, one `kind = 'page'` row (url, host, identity keys, names, page date;
  no page text: `20261117100600` dropped its context windows, so a judge reads the page
  from its stored version) and one row per fact (kind, fields, quote of at most 500
  characters, the one bounded copy of a fact's evidence).
  **Kept:** no `delete_after` (`20261117100500`), so a fact id stays valid for anything
  that names it later.
- `ResearchCrawlPage` (`scraping.research_crawl_page`): the pages of one crawl
  (`sequence`, the relay's watermark), written by the drainer once a page's outcome is
  final; a stored page names its page version, never a text. A trigger refuses a page
  whose inbox row is not a crawl. There is no page text table: a caller reads a page by
  its page version's stored object.

No foreign key crosses between `scraping` and `osint`: `request_id` is a plain id.

### OSINT internal retrieval

What SimpL already holds about a research subject (plan §4.1 step 3, task T4): the subject
loaded by id, SimpL's records about it as references, and hits from SimpL's own search indexes as
raw data. Free (no search vendor, no fetch); no tables of its own. A customer limits what
research may do, never what it sees (2026-11-17): resolution and records are the same for
every customer, the asking customer's own rows treated like anyone's. **References only to
SimpL's shared tables; rows owned by a customer come back only as facts about the person,
copied** (an address a seller saved, a "contact left" event), never as a reference to the
row, its id, its connection or its seller. Modules in `simpl_core.services.osint`:

- **Subject in** (`subject.py`): `parse_subject(subject_type, request.subject)` returns a
  `CompanySubject` (`company_id`, required), a `PersonSubject` (`lead_id`, required) or a
  `TopicSubject` (`text`, `synonyms`, `exclude`); strict (unknown keys, a missing or
  malformed id and a value with no letter or digit refused, pydantic's `ValidationError`). **The id is the
  subject** (2026-11-17): research never pins one from a name, a website, a LinkedIn URL,
  an email or an employer; a caller that has only those finds the id first.
- **Load** (`resolution.py`): `await SubjectResolver().resolve(session, subject)` returns a
  `Resolution` (`subject: ResolvedSubject`, `records: tuple[RecordRef, ...]`, `facts:
  PersonFacts | None` for a lead) or a `SubjectRefusal` (`reason`, `detail`). Reads only, on
  the caller's session.
  - `not_found`: no row has the id (never existed, deleted, or merged into another row: a
    merge deletes the source, and its old id is not followed).
  - `placeholder`: the row stands in for something else: a placeholder company
    (`PARKING_COMPANY_ORIGINS`) or a Dummy lead (`origin = 'Dummy'`, a company's stand-in).
  - Company: the row by key. Person: the lead by key; its employers are the companies of
    its current roles (`LeadRepository.current_position_company_ids`: no end date or one
    still ahead, open-ended first, then the latest started), placeholders left out, so a
    recorded departure (`end_roles_at`) drops the company. `sales.lead.company_id` counts
    only for a lead with no current role and no ended role there: a "contact left" reply
    ends the roles but moves the lead only when its connection moves. Topic: nothing
    loaded; the text and hints.
- **`ResolvedSubject`**: `subject_type`, `lead_id` (always set for a person), `company_id`
  (the company subject, or the lead's first employer), `names` (the person's
  `NameForms`; `None` for a company subject, whose names are its `company`'s, and for a
  name with no letter or digit), `company` / `employers` (`ResolvedCompany`: `names`,
  `websites` (its own host), `linkedin_url`; never a stand-in alias's name or site),
  `identity_keys`, `profiles`, `topic`, `records` (`RecordKey`s: table and key only, from `record_keys.py`),
  `index_hits` (research's posts from SimpL's own indexes, `InternalHit`s from
  `index_hits.py`; empty until that search ran, and for a topic), `index_searched_at` (when
  that search ended, found, empty or failed; `None` before), `resolved_at`. T5 builds
  queries from it, T11a matches hard keys against it.
  - `NameForms`: `name`, `surname_first` (a tuple: the first given name moved to the end,
    and for three words or more the first two, "Rossi Maria Grazia"), `accent_free`,
    `without_legal_form` (a company's name without the legal forms at its end, only when
    two words or more are left: "AS Roma" and "Lavazza S.p.A." get none), `.variants`,
    `.tells_a_person_apart` (two words or more: a first name alone is not searched for).
  - `identity_keys`, from stored data only: `linkedin_urls` (the lead's profile or the
    company's page), `company_linkedin_id`, `emails` (`identity_emails`: the lead's stored
    addresses, never a role or automated mailbox, one the lead's rows give to another
    person, or one another lead holds; the holders come from
    `LeadContactRepository.lead_ids_by_emails`, which reads through the SECURITY DEFINER
    function `sales.lead_ids_by_emails`, so the login needs EXECUTE on it, granted by
    `migrations/20261120100000_sales_lead_ids_by_emails.sql` to `service_role` and by
    simpl_scraping's migration 113 to the research login; since 0.8.444), `domain` (only
    when no other company has the host),
    `registrable_domain` (only when the host is it), `vat_code`.
  - `profiles`: `ProfileUrl(platform, url)` from the lead's stored data (today its LinkedIn
    profile, `platform="linkedin"`, in the spelling `normalize_lead_linkedin_url` writes,
    which the `linkedin` row of `osint.profile_platform` matches, simpl_core#510).
- **Save it** (`subject_store.py`, never commits): `await save_resolved_subject(session,
  request_id=, claim_token=, subject=)` writes `osint.request.resolved_subject` only while
  `claim_token` holds the request (False otherwise); `lead_id` and `company_id` go to the
  request's columns, which follow merges, and stay in the document; `updated_at` moves
  (the table's trigger). `await load_resolved_subject(session, request_id)` returns the
  saved subject while the columns match its ids; once a merge or a delete moved one, it
  resolves the subject again by the columns' ids (not saved: no claim). `None` before a
  save, when the company or lead subject was deleted, or when the surviving row is refused
  (logged, `osint.subject_reload_refused`). A key it does not know (a newer worker's) is
  ignored, as the read ignores it; a subject resolved again keeps the saved
  `index_hits` and `index_searched_at` (since 0.8.537). The save leaves those two keys out
  of the document: a worker before 0.8.537 loads the subject strictly.
  - `await save_index_hits(session, request_id=, claim_token=, hits=, searched_at=)` writes
    only `index_hits` and `index_searched_at` on the saved document (JSONB `||`), while
    `claim_token` holds the request and a subject is saved (False otherwise). Only it
    writes them: a new `save_resolved_subject` replaces the document and so clears both. `index_searched()` is the condition "that search
    ended" on `osint.request`, for a claim query (`resolved_subject->>'index_searched_at'
    IS NOT NULL`). Since 0.8.537.
- **Record keys** (`record_keys.py`): `RecordTable` and `RecordKey`, apart from `records.py`, which
  imports SimpL's search packages: `subject.py` imports only this module, so the subject models
  load where the search stack is not installed (simpl_scraping's research worker).
- **Records** (`records.py`): a `RecordRef` is `table` (`RecordTable`), `key` (the row's key
  columns as text), the `fields` a caller shows and `flags` (`RecordFlag`); never a copy of
  the row. Every read is by key or an indexed column:
  - company: `sales.company`, its `sales.company_alias` links (their ids only mark job rows
    `at_employer`; the stand-in companies are not read), `sales.company_funding`,
    `sales.company_headcount`, and its website signals `sales.website` /
    `sales.website_technology` by host (the website facts index projects these two
    tables, so one company's facts are read here, not searched);
  - person: `sales.lead`, every `sales.lead_experience` row of the lead, its global email
    rows of `sales.lead_contact`, and each employer's row and alias links.
  - Person facts (`PersonFacts`, copies, never references): `saved_emails`, one
    `SavedEmail(address, flags)` per address only sellers saved (`lead_contact` rows not
    global, any customer's); `moves`, one `CompanyMove(old_company_id, new_company_id,
    learned_on)` per "contact left" event (`sales_outreach.outreach_events`,
    `lead.company.left`) on any connection to the lead. Nothing else of those rows is read:
    no row or event id, connection, seller, source, outcome or reply text. Every address,
    saved or global, feeds `identity_emails`.
  - Job-history traps are flags, never dropped: `start_unknown` (1900-01-01 or no start),
    `open_may_be_past` (every row with no end date: most are Crustdata's mid-2025 import),
    `live_read` (`raw.source` is `profile_positions`: an extension read through SimpL's
    LinkedIn proxy), `employer_lost` (no company), `at_employer` (a current employer or one
    of its alias links). Addresses keep `bounced` and `another_persons_address`.
- **Index hits** (`internal_search.py`; the hit models in `index_hits.py`, which
  `subject.py` imports, since 0.8.537): `InternalSearch(db, landing: LandingSearch | None,
  posts: PostIndex, passages: PassageFinder | None = None, *, named_posts=True)`, then
  `await search.hits(resolved, hard_keys=policy.er.hard_keys, passage_questions=[...])`
  returns `InternalHits`: `hits`, `candidates` (posts the posts index returned for the
  subject's names, at most `POST_CANDIDATES`) and `candidates_full` (it returned all of
  them, so tags and reposts ranked below the cut were not checked). Each hit is an
  `InternalHit`: `source`
  (`landing_pages`, `posts`, `post_mentions`, `company_passages`), `relation`
  (`own_site`, `mention`, `by_subject`, `reposted`, `tagged`, `passage`, `topic`), `url`,
  `record` (a post's `RecordKey`, `sales.post` by `id`; a post passage's too, from
  `Passage.post_id`, since the index holds no link for it), `index_key`, `host`, `title`,
  `date` (a post's day; landing pages have none), `snippet`, `author_company_id` /
  `author_lead_id` (who wrote a post's text), `score`, `question`, `identity`.
  - `identity` is `unresolved` unless a hard key the policy lists proves it `same`
    (`status` `same`, `method` `hard_key`, `key`; a research page's identity words,
    `IdentityStatus` and ER's `Method`, never `different`, `unknown`, `rule` or `judge`;
    an unresolved hit names no method or key): a landing page on the company's granted
    host (`landing_host`) or under its granted registrable domain (`registrable_domain`);
    a post the subject wrote
    (the index's author is the subject; a page's plain repost of it is that post), or one
    it reposted (a company on its own page, a person with a comment of theirs); a post
    tagging one of the subject's pages in `sales.post.mentions`; a company passage. A name
    never is.
  - The posts index's `post_id` is `sales.post.id`; a plain repost (`r:<id>` key) carries
    the original's id, so its record names the original post, whose text it shows. Text,
    links, authors and tags are read from `sales.post` by id; a quote's text is its comment
    and then the quoted post's (by `reshared_post_id`), as the index holds it. An index hit
    whose `sales.post` row is gone is skipped and logged
    (`osint.internal_search.post_missing`).
  - Each post comes back once (its reposts share the record; the subject's own relation
    first), and each landing page once, as its best-ranked chunk (by URL).
  - Company: its own site's pages naming it, one chunk per other site naming it or its
    domain (phrases checked), its latest own posts and reposts (read, below), posts
    naming, tagging or reposted by it, and `PassageFinder`'s passages per passage question
    (its site only when its host is a hard key). Person: one chunk per site naming them
    (ranked and checked by the name alone), the employers' sites' pages naming them,
    their latest own posts and reposts, posts naming, tagging or reposted by them; a
    one-word name is not searched for, so such a lead gets only its own posts, its reposts
    and the posts tagging it.
  - Own posts and reposts are read from `sales.post` by the subject's company or lead,
    newest first (`ix_post_company_posted_at`, `ix_post_lead_posted_at`): the latest
    `OWN_POSTS` (20) own posts and `OWN_REPOSTS` (10) reposts (`is_repost` or a
    `reshared_post_id`), not searched for. A repost's text is mostly its original author's,
    so the subject's names rarely rank it (production sample: 20% of company reposts held
    the company's name, 33% of person quotes the surname). A repost read so is
    `reposted`, as the name search's hit of it: its record and link, its comment then the
    quoted post's text, the quoted post's author, its own day; `same` by the LinkedIn key
    when the policy lists it. The name search may find the same post: each record comes
    back once, the one read first. A page's plain repost (`sales.post_repost`) is found by
    the name search only (since 0.8.537). Topic: landing pages and posts for the text and synonyms, minus
    hits holding an excluded word; hybrid when the searches have `embed_query`,
    keyword-only without.
  - Post mentions have no index (`sales.post.mentions`, no GIN): the posts index finds the
    candidates by the subject's names, their mentions are read by id, and a tag whose URL
    is one of the subject's pages (slug decoded and case-folded, or the company page's
    numeric id) matches. A tag under another text is missed.
  - Without `landing` no landing page is searched; `named_posts=False` keeps only the
    hits a hard key proves (`same`): no post that only names a person or a company, and no
    own post, repost or tag when the policy's `er.hard_keys` lists no LinkedIn key. The ranked query leaves out one-letter
    words (a legal form's "S.r.l.") unless nothing else is left.
  - Research's search: `seek_post_search(db, queries)` is `InternalSearch(db, None,
    KeywordPostSearch(queries), named_posts=False)`: posts only, keyword only, without
    simpl_ia, only what a hard key proves (own posts and reposts, read; tags, found by the
    name search, whose only job there is tags). `queries` gives
    the caller's `SeekClient`, which the caller closes. `internal_search` loads without
    SimpL's search stack (the passages load only when a `PassageFinder` is passed). `posts`
    is any `PostIndex` (`PostSearch` or `KeywordPostSearch`).
  - Wiring: keyword-only needs the query service alone (`RowStoreLandingSearch(queries)`,
    `RowStorePostSearch(queries)`); topics' dense half and passages need the Nebius
    embedders at each dataset's `QUERY_DIMS` (`embed_query=embedder.embed_query`;
    `PassageFinder(queries, landing_embedder, posts_embedder)`). Limits per search are
    module constants (`OWN_SITE_HITS`, `MENTION_SITES`, `POST_CANDIDATES`, ...).

### Object storage

`simpl_core.object_storage` talks to S3-compatible storage (Wasabi, AWS S3, MinIO) over
`httpx` with SigV4 signing and no SDK dependency. Every write sends its real payload
SHA-256 so the server verifies it. Every read verifies a digest from the `ObjectRef`,
an explicit value, or the object's stored `sha256` metadata.

```python
config = ObjectStorageConfig.from_env()
async with S3ObjectStore(config) as store:
    ref = await store.put_bytes(key, gzipped, content_type="text/html", content_encoding="gzip")
    body = await store.get_bytes(ref)
```

`from_env` reads `OBJECT_STORAGE_ENDPOINT_URL`, `OBJECT_STORAGE_REGION`,
`OBJECT_STORAGE_BUCKET`, `OBJECT_STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID`,
`OBJECT_STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` and optional `OBJECT_STORAGE_ADDRESSING_STYLE`.
Persist the reference's fields, never a signed URL. A missing object raises
`ObjectNotFoundError` and a corrupt one `ObjectChecksumMismatchError`; neither comes
back as empty content. Keys with `.` or `..` segments are rejected.

A body too large to hold in memory goes up as a multipart upload. Each part is signed
with its own SHA-256; the caller hashes the whole body as it goes and passes the digest
to `complete_multipart_upload`, because the object's metadata is fixed before any byte
is known and so carries no `sha256`:

```python
upload = await store.create_multipart_upload(key, content_type="video/mp4")
parts = [await store.upload_part(upload, number, chunk) for number, chunk in numbered_chunks]
ref = await store.complete_multipart_upload(upload, parts, sha256_hex=whole_body_digest)
# on failure: await store.abort_multipart_upload(upload.key, upload.upload_id)
```

Every part but the last must be at least 5 MiB. `list_multipart_uploads` pages through
uploads never completed or aborted, which are billed until aborted.

### Database rows as Parquet objects (`parquet_archive`)

`simpl_core.parquet_archive` writes rows read from a Postgres table to object storage as
one Parquet file per batch, for history moved out of the database. Install
`simpl-core[parquet]` (pyarrow); nothing imports it at `import simpl_core`.

```python
rows = (await session.execute(select(FetchAttempt.__table__).where(...))).mappings().all()
arrow = arrow_table(FetchAttempt.__table__, rows)
ref = await put_parquet(store, "history/<table>/<yyyy>/<mm>/<batch>.parquet", arrow,
                        metadata={"rows": str(arrow.num_rows)})
```

- `arrow_schema(table)` derives the Arrow schema from the SQLAlchemy table: every column,
  in order, with its nullability; the schema metadata names the source table
  (`source_table`).
- Integers, floats, booleans, timestamps (UTC when the column has a time zone), dates,
  times, intervals (durations), bytea and arrays map to their Arrow types. uuid, inet,
  numeric and text are strings; JSON and JSONB are their serialized document as a string.
  A column type with no mapping raises `TypeError` naming the column.
- `put_parquet` builds the file in a worker thread (zstd) and uploads it with
  `S3ObjectStore.put_bytes` (content type `application/vnd.apache.parquet`), replacing
  any object at the key. The file is held in memory: keep each object to one batch.

### Parquet datasets for training readers (`parquet_dataset`)

`simpl_core.parquet_dataset` keeps a dataset as plain Parquet in one folder on GCS (or a
local disk), for readers that take Parquet as it is: Hugging Face datasets, pyarrow,
BigQuery external tables, a GPU job. Writers on many machines add part files; merges
fold them into capped shards with one row per key. Nothing writes to a database. Install
`simpl-core[parquet]` (pyarrow, google-cloud-storage).

```python
spec = ParquetDatasetSpec(name="opus_llmgraph_v1", schema=LABEL_SCHEMA,
                          key="label_id", order="labelled_at")
labels = ParquetDataset.at(spec, "<bucket>/distillation/opus_llmgraph_v1", gcs_fs,
                           writer="jackie-mbp", token=os.environ.get("GOOGLE_STORAGE_TOKEN"))
part = await labels.write_part(rows)   # parts/<UTC time>_<writer>_<8 hex>.parquet
merged = await labels.merge()          # shards/, keys/, manifest.json
```

- **Layout** (simpl_flow's `og-merge`, T13: the same files, names and rules, so its
  folders are read and extended as they are): `parts/*.parquet` (kept, never rewritten);
  `shards/<name>-<nnnnn>.parquet`, exactly the schema, zstd, row groups of 5,000 rows,
  about 256 MB each (`shard_bytes`); `keys/<name>-<nnnnn>.parquet`, each closed shard's key
  index (`key`: the first 16 bytes of each key); `manifest.json` (`format` 1, `group`,
  `shard_bytes`, `shards` with name, rows, bytes, closed, and the `parts` merged);
  `merge.lock`; `tmp/`, files being written, never read. The folder's last segment is
  the dataset's name. **Readers read the shards `manifest.json` lists** (`shards[].name`)
  as one table, not a glob of `shards/`: while a merge writes, or after one stopped half
  way and until the next merge, `shards/` can hold a shard not listed yet.
- **Spec.** `key` is a non-null string column of lower-case hex digests (a SHA-256 in
  hex): its first 16 bytes index it. `order` is non-null; among a merge's new rows a key
  keeps the row with the lowest value, and a key already in a shard keeps its stored row.
- **Rows are checked before a write** (`spec.table(rows)`), because Arrow would let them
  through: an unknown key at any depth, a missing value or a null in a non-null column at
  any depth, a value of another type (a naive datetime for a column with a time zone
  too), a key that is not a lower-case hex digest (checked whole: a trailing newline is
  refused, as the merge refuses it), a key repeated in one write. Each raises
  `ParquetRowError` naming the row's key, the column path and the problem. The dataset's
  name and the `writer` are checked whole too.
- **A part** is written whole or not at all, never over an existing path, in a worker
  thread under `write_timeout_s` (180 s; a `TimeoutError` is an `OSError`).
- **A merge** returns at once when no part is new. Otherwise it holds `merge.lock` (on GCS
  an object created only if absent, holding its owner and an expiry, 30 min, after which
  another merge takes it over; locally an `flock`), streams the open shard into its new
  version followed by the new rows, closes a shard at the cap (and writes its key file),
  and replaces the manifest conditional on the version it read (GCS generation, or
  inode, time and size). Right after taking the lock it writes the manifest again as it
  is (its claim), and its last write is conditional on that new version: a merge that
  lost the lock cannot land its manifest over the merge that took it over. Before it
  writes, it deletes the shard and key files numbered past the manifest's last shard
  (`Manifest.remove`, conditional on their version; logged
  `parquet_dataset.unlisted_file_deleted`). Once the manifest exists, its `shard_bytes`
  wins over `ParquetDataset(shard_bytes=...)` (warning `parquet_dataset.shard_bytes_kept`),
  so every merge closes shards at the dataset's first cap. Every shard and key file is written under `tmp/` and put on its
  name only once complete and only if that name is still at the version it had when the
  merge began (`Manifest.publish`: a server-side rewrite on GCS, a rename locally); the
  lock is renewed before each of these and before the manifest, and while the merge reads
  and writes between them, before each part, key file and row group, once
  `LOCK_RENEW_AFTER` (a third of the expiry, 10 min) has passed since the last renewal
  (`Lease.renew_if_due`): a merge whose reads outlast the expiry keeps the lock. A failure
  mid-file leaves the old shard; a lock taken over (the merge slept past its expiry) or a name another
  merge wrote raises `MergeConflict` and the merge writes nothing more (no retry: the next
  merge starts from the manifest). `MergeBusy` when the lock stays held for `lock_wait_s`
  (120 s): the parts wait for the next merge. A part that cannot be read (not Parquet,
  another schema, keys that are not hex digests) is logged (`parquet_dataset.part_skipped`),
  named in `MergeResult.skipped`, not listed as merged, and tried again by the next merge;
  the other parts are merged. A closed shard's columns are never read again; the key index
  grows with the dataset (16 bytes a key). A manifest of another format or dataset in the
  folder is refused (`ValueError`).
- **Manifest by filesystem**: `manifest_for(folder, fs, token=...)` gives `GcsManifest` on
  `GcsFileSystem` (the token, an OAuth access token, else Application Default
  Credentials; no project needed) and `LocalManifest` on `LocalFileSystem`; any other
  filesystem is refused: no conditional write is known for it.

### Processed rows (`row_store`)

`simpl_core.row_store` keeps rows a process derives from Postgres (embeddings, detected
languages, extracted fields) in a Lance table per dataset version, on GCS. The table is
the source of truth for derived rows, and searches read it through the query service
(`simpl_core.seek`). LanceDB and DuckDB's `lance` extension read the table as it is. Needs the `row-store` extra (`pylance`) and the catalog migration
`migrations/20260929_row_store_catalog.sql` (schema `row_store`).

```python
store = RowStore(RowStoreConfig.from_env(), RowStoreCatalog(db))
posts = store.dataset(SPEC)                  # DatasetSpec: fields, required, indexes
await posts.ensure()                         # catalog row, table, indexes
await posts.commit(rows, run_id=run_id)      # key, row_version, deleted?, fields
rows = await posts.get(keys, ["text"])       # by key; tombstones included

await posts.maintain()                       # indexes, bounded compaction and merges, cleanup
await posts.build_index("vector")            # sections of a declared vector or text index
```

- **A plain copy of a source table** (nothing to embed) is a `copy_spec(name, version,
  fields)` dataset written with `RowCopy(dataset)`: `copy_range(rows, after=, upto=,
  version=)` (every source row of a key range: changed or new rows written, keys gone
  tombstoned), `copy_rows(rows, version=)` (rows by key, no tombstones),
  `copy_keys(keys, after=, upto=, version=, read=)` (keys only: new keys read and
  written, keys gone tombstoned), then `flush()`. Rows hold the source's columns and
  `row_hash`; an unchanged row is never written. `version` is `read_version()` (the
  read's time, so a later read wins). `sales_datasets.TableCopier` drives it page by
  page with cursors.
- **A commit upserts by `key`, and a row replaces the stored one only if its
  `row_version` is not older**, so a replay is harmless. A deleted source row is a
  tombstone (`deleted=True`, fields null), so a reader of changes (`Dataset.changes`)
  sees the delete. Mark source
  work done only after `commit` returns; a `LeaseLostError` means it is not done.
- **One writer per dataset.** Commits run under a Postgres lease that Lance's commit
  hook re-checks, which also numbers them; other writers wait up to `lease_wait`, then
  get `LeaseBusyError`. Every Lance write runs off the event loop. The lease lives
  `lease_ttl` (90 s, renewed every 30 s), so a writer that dies frees the dataset
  before the others give up.
- **A commit rides out a short stall.** When its write fails on storage IO (a Lance
  `LanceError(IO)`, such as a GCS request that hung) or the commit hook's lease
  renewal fails or does not answer in 30 s, and none of its rows reached the table,
  it confirms the lease and writes again after 5 s, 30 s and 120 s
  (`WRITE_RETRY_PAUSES_S`, log `row_store.write_retry`), holding the lease. A lease
  found taken, any other error, or the fourth failure is raised as before.
- **No commit or read scans more as the table grows.** A commit finds the stored rows
  of its keys through the key index (key and row_version only, `KEYS_PER_LOOKUP` (10k)
  keys a read) and replaces them in one Lance commit. The lookup's memory still grows
  with the key index: about 1.6 KB a key on an empty table, 6 KB on a 1.9M-row one
  with 11 segments, so a commit's keys, not its bytes, size it. Reads find rows on a
  narrow column and take the others by row id;
  once `INDEX_NEW_ROWS_AT` (10k) rows are outside the indexes, the commit indexes them
  as new segments, so no read scans more than that. `build_index` and
  `consolidate_index` index them too, under their commit's lease, before they commit
  a section: no version has a vector or keyword section holding a fragment a scalar
  index does not (a search filtering through one that is behind is five times slower).
- **`maintain` costs the same however big the table grows.** It merges fragments
  smaller than `COMPACTION_TARGET_ROWS` (100k) into ones of about that size, never
  rewrites a full one, and rewrites at most `COMPACTION_MAX_ROWS` (250k) per run, one
  bin at a time, the oldest whatever its size; what is left waits for the next run. It merges an index's newest
  segments like a binary counter, at most `INDEX_SEGMENT_MAX_ROWS` (2M) rows and
  `INDEX_MERGE_MAX_BYTES` (64 MiB) from `INDEX_SEGMENTS_PER_MERGE` (64) segments a
  merge (its memory is about six times the bytes it reads); segments the size of a commit's
  always merge, and a backlog of them merges over successive runs. It also heals, best
  effort, one segment over those caps a run (built before them, or of unknown size):
  rebuilt from its fragments' rows as capped pieces, which frees the fragments
  compaction held for it. With none, it rebuilds instead the oldest run of
  commit-sized segments stranded behind a larger, newer one (a merge of the newest
  segments never reaches them) into one, within the same caps
  (`MaintenanceResult.segments_healed`; a failure is `heal_error` and the rest of the
  run goes on). It drops indexes
  the spec no longer plans and builds a missing one per run (a new index reads its
  whole column).
  Its memory does not depend on fragment size or row width either: compaction reads
  `COMPACTION_BATCH_BYTES` (16 MiB) of rows at a time, and importing the row store
  caps Lance's read-ahead at `IO_BUFFER_BYTES` (128 MiB, `LANCE_DEFAULT_IO_BUFFER_SIZE`
  unless the process sets it). Each step logs `row_store.maintain_step` as it starts.
- **A spec may declare columns the store computes itself** (`DatasetSpec(computed=...)`):
  `NarrowedVector("vector", 512)` is the row's vector cut to its first 512 components and
  re-normalised (column `vector_512`), `HasValue("company_id", "has_company")` whether a
  column holds a value. The store fills them at every commit from the row it is given:
  **no writer sends them**, and they are outside the input schema and the fingerprint,
  so declaring one is no new dataset version.
  - A new table has them from the start. A table that holds rows takes them in place:
    `Dataset.add_computed_columns()` (Lance records the columns, no data is written),
    then `Dataset.fill_computed()` until `rows_left` is 0, a part a call, the lease held
    for each commit alone. `DatasetHealth.computed_missing` and `computed_unfilled_rows`
    say what is left.
  - Until a column is filled, compaction leaves its unfilled fragments alone and no
    index is built on it (a scalar index waits in `maintain`; `build_index(create=True)`
    and `consolidate_index` raise).
  - **Deploy order**: add them only after every writer and every maintenance of the
    dataset runs a release that declares them. A release that does not still writes
    the table once they are added: its rows lack the columns, its compaction merges them
    into files that hold the columns empty (where `fill_computed` and the health no
    longer see them), and its maintenance drops an index on a computed column.
    `Dataset.computed_gaps()` finds such rows by value (each fragment that holds the
    columns read on its own, the computed column and `deleted` only, no filter; then the
    source vector of their null rows only, read by row id), and
    `fill_computed(fragments=gaps)` fills them; `ComputedFill.column` and `.fragments`
    say which it committed.
  - `searched_vector(stored, query)` gives a kind its computed column and its one vector
    index: on the vector cut to the width its queries have. Every searchable kind uses
    it (`vector_1024`; `vector_512` for posts and landing pages), and posts declare
    `has_company` (`posts.spec.ON_A_COMPANY_PAGE`). The stored 4,096 components are
    indexed nowhere: measured, an index on them found 71% of the true best 100 companies
    for a query of 1,024, an index on `vector_1024` 97.7% in a quarter of the time.
- **Vector and full-text indexes are declared on the spec; on a filled table they are
  built on request.** Like the scalar indexes they are outside the fingerprint, so
  declaring one is no new dataset version.
  - `DatasetSpec(vector_indexes=(VectorIndex("vector_512"),))`: `IVF_RQ` (1 bit a
    dimension, cosine) on a fixed-size float list, a computed one included.
  - `DatasetSpec(text_indexes=("text",))`: a BM25 (`INVERTED`) index under
    `TEXT_INDEX_OPTIONS`, one neutral analyzer for every language (split, lower case,
    accents folded; no stemming, no stop words), the analyzer the Zilliz collections
    searched with, so keywords are cut as they were.
  - They are kept as **sections**: segments each built from the rows of a run of
    fragments, by `Dataset.build_index(column)` alone. Lance's own update and merge of
    such an index open every segment (every section's centroids, every section's word
    dictionary), so their memory grows with the table; a section build reads only its own
    rows. A section holds at most `SECTION_MAX_ROWS` (1M) rows.
  - `build_index` is incremental. Without the index it trains the vector centroids on a
    sample (`VECTOR_TRAIN_SAMPLE_BYTES`) and builds the first sections in fragment
    order; with it, it builds the rows no section holds yet, together with the small
    sections (`SECTION_SMALL_ROWS`, 100k), into larger ones, removing those in the same
    `CreateIndex` commit. On a vector or full-text index, a section from 100k up to
    half `SECTION_MAX_ROWS` is partial: once the partial sections hold `SECTION_MAX_ROWS`
    rows together, a call first rebuilds the oldest of them as one full section (one
    such fold a call), so an index keeps about a section a million rows (0.8.289). A
    vector section is built in pieces of `VECTOR_PIECE_ROWS`
    (50k) merged into it, on the centroids of an existing section and a rotation of its
    own (each section searches with its own model). One call builds from at most
    `SECTION_ROWS_PER_BUILD` (4M) rows, from a snapshot without the writer lease,
    which it takes only for the `CreateIndex` commit (0.8.166); `IndexBuild.rows_left`
    is what the next call has. Call it on a schedule: rows no section holds are still
    found by searches, which read them without the index. `build_index(column,
    create=False)` returns None for an index the table does not have yet: a schedule
    builds sections of the indexes a table has, and a first vector build, whose
    training holds a sample of up to 2 GiB of vectors, is a run of its own with the
    memory for it.
  - Commits and `maintain` never touch these indexes: commits index new rows into the
    scalar indexes only; maintenance does not update, merge or split a section, and
    compaction leaves the fragments of a large section as they are (a small one's are
    compacted, its remap is cheap). It never builds a vector index, and builds a
    full-text index only on an empty table. An index on an undeclared column is dropped.
  - `DatasetHealth`: `unbuilt_indexes` names a declared one not built yet;
    `section_unindexed_rows` and `section_partial_rows` say how far a vector or full-text
    index's builds are behind (rows in no section, rows in partial sections not folded),
    `most_sections` how many sections each of its searches opens, `ngram_unindexed_bytes` and `ngram_small_sections` a
    substring one's (bytes on disk, as its sections are sized: a large table has many
    full sections by design, so only those under `NGRAM_SECTION_SMALL_BYTES` count), and
    `problems()` reports each past its threshold.
  - Search a vector index with Lance's `nearest` (`refine_factor`), a full-text one with
    `full_text_query`; filter `NOT deleted` with `prefilter=True`.
- **Which datasets a host runs, and how, is a table: `row_store.dataset_configs`**
  (migration `migrations/20261009_row_store_dataset_configs.sql`). One row per dataset
  version: `kind` (the host code that builds and writes it), `dims`, `status`, and
  `params`, a JSON object of settings by section.
  - `status` is `active` (written by its kind's runs and maintained; one per kind),
    `maintained` (maintained only: a version being built, or the one just replaced) or
    `off`.
  - `RowStoreCatalog.dataset_configs(kind=None, statuses=("active", "maintained"))` and
    `active_dataset_config(kind)` return `DatasetConfig`s. `config.section("index")` is
    one section's settings; the reader checks their keys.
  - Adding a dataset, moving a kind to a new version or changing a setting is an
    `INSERT`/`UPDATE`, picked up by the next run, with no deployment.
- **A reader without the spec opens the table by name.** `StoredTable(config,
  "sales_posts__v2")` gives `schema()`, `read(columns, where=...)` and `version()`: live
  rows only, `where` a pyarrow expression; with `pinned=True` all three see the version
  it first opened. It never writes, so it takes no lease and needs no catalog. The agent
  framework's `data_producer` reads the row store through it (`source: row_store`).
  `read(..., limit=n)` reads at most `n` matching rows.
- **A `simpl_foundry` recipe reads the row store as a source.**
  `row_store.source_reader.RowStoreReader` is the `row_store` kind of the
  `simpl_foundry.sources` entry point group: `{row_store: {dataset, version}, columns:
  [...], where: [...]}` reads the live rows of `{dataset}__v{version}` under
  `ROW_STORE_URI`, at one Lance version. Needs the `row-store` and `foundry` extras.
- **A serving process keeps its tables on one Lance session.** `PinnedTables(config,
  names, session=lance.Session(...))` opens one pinned `StoredTable` per name as a set,
  in `names`' order, before any of them is read (`await tables.table(name)`);
  `prewarm({name: columns})` loads the indexes on those columns once at start, and
  `await refresh(max_age_s)` opens a new set at the latest versions once the current
  one is that old and swaps it in when all are open, reusing what the session holds.
  Put first the table a writer commits last (a commit marker). Opened per request, a cold BTree lookup on a
  large table loads its whole index from GCS (hundreds of MB in the Graphia log).
- **Join many filter terms with `any_of(terms)` / `any_range(column, ranges)`**
  (`row_store.filters`), never a chained `a | b | c ...`: Lance serializes the
  expression to Substrait at a cost exponential in a chain's length (50 ranges never
  finished); the helpers join a balanced tree.
- `RowStoreConfig.from_env()` reads `ROW_STORE_URI` (`gs://<bucket>`, optionally with
  a prefix). Lance reaches the bucket with Application Default Credentials (the job's
  service account on Cloud Run, `gcloud auth application-default login` locally); no
  key is configured. Lance prefers `GOOGLE_*` credential variables in the environment,
  so set none for another account.

- **A vector index is consolidated into one section over the table.**
  `await dataset.consolidate_index(column)` builds one section from a snapshot without
  the lease and commits it under the lease, removing every section the index had
  (`IndexConsolidation`, log `row_store.index_consolidated`, with the search speed it
  measured). `await dataset.consolidation_state(column)` reads the manifest only: `due`
  when more than 5% of the rows sit outside the largest section. The 15-minute
  maintenance keeps building small sections for new rows around it; a `build_index`
  overtaken by a consolidation plans again once (`row_store.index_build_replanned`).
  Nothing compacts the fragments under a consolidated section in `maintain`: clear dead
  rows by hand with `Dataset.compact_held(max_rows=...)` (`HeldCompaction`, log
  `row_store.held_compacted`) when `DatasetHealth.held_dead_share` grows.
- **Logs to alert on**: `row_store.query.retried` (warning: a call retried a service
  away for a moment), `row_store.query.service_fault` (error: a crash, a refused token,
  an unreadable answer), `row_store.query.walk_restarted` (warning: a walk's ranking
  expired, started again once), `row_store.query.walk_truncated` (warning: a walk cut
  at `ABOVE_MAX_ROWS` or the reader's page cap, with its dataset and floor),
  `row_store.query.groups_short` (warning: a grouped read came back full with fewer
  groups than asked), `row_store.index_consolidated` (info: a consolidation committed,
  with its measured search speed).
- **Backup** (`row_store.backup`, runbook `docs/row-store-backup.md`):
  `snapshot_tables(catalog, store, backup)` copies each table's data and manifest (no
  index files), only the files the backup lacks, with create-only writes;
  `prune_tables(catalog, store, backup, keep=30)` keeps the newest 30 records per table;
  `restore_table(backup, target, record)` restores one version and drops its indexes;
  `commits_after_snapshot` names the commits a restore lacks. `version_files(dataset,
  indexes=...)` lists every file a version needs, its manifest last.

### Searching the row store's tables (`row_store.table_search`)

`AsyncLanceStore` (extra `row-store`; from `simpl_core.row_store.table_search`) searches the row store's Lance
tables in worker threads: by meaning, by keywords and both, paginated. The query
service (simpl_seek) reads every table through it; a batch job may open one in
process. Its writes and index builds serve tests and one-off loads: `RowStore` writes
and maintains the tables. Its results are its own types (`SearchResult`,
`PaginatedSearchResult`, `GroupBy`, `VectorPoint`); it depends on no simpl_ia vector
store.

```python
from simpl_core.row_store.table_search import (
    AsyncLanceStore, LanceStoreConfig, LanceTableConfig, LanceHybridSearchConfig,
    LanceDenseLeg, LanceTextLeg,
)

config = LanceStoreConfig(
    root_uri="gs://bucket/tables",
    tables={"companies": LanceTableConfig(id_column="key", live_filter="NOT deleted")},
    read_only=True,
)
store = AsyncLanceStore(config)
page = await store.search_paginated("companies", vector, limit=50, filter={"country": "IT"})
more = await store.search_paginated("companies", vector, limit=50, filter={"country": "IT"},
                                    cursor=page.next_cursor)
```

- **Order and pages.** Every search orders rows by score, then id (Milvus's tie rule),
  asks Lance for the whole window `offset + limit` and widens it while the boundary score
  is tied: two reads of a page agree and pages concatenate. `max_window` (16,384) bounds
  `offset + limit`; walk deeper with `next_cursor` or an iterator.
- **Cursors** pin the table versions the first page read, so commits between pages never
  repeat or skip rows. On an exact search (no vector index, or `use_index=False`) the next
  page reads only rows past the last one, and pages concatenate to one search. Through an
  index the next page holds the best rows after the last `(score, id)` returned, of a
  search as deep as the window: a cursor walk never repeats a row and comes in score
  order, but misses a row a deeper page's candidates find above the last one returned
  (each page takes its candidates from a search of its own depth). A caller that needs
  every row of a walk asks them in one page, or through an exact search. Pages asked by
  `offset` alone are windows of separate searches: through an index they can repeat or
  skip rows; use the cursor.
  **Grouped cursors are the exception**: with `group_by` the cursor pins the versions but
  goes on by offset, so through an index a walk can repeat or skip a group between pages;
  it is complete only when each page is exact (a filter keeping at most `exact_filter_rows` rows).
- **Dense** scores are cosine similarity at the stored width. `score_dims=N` on the table
  re-scores candidates on the first N components, re-normalised (a migration comparison
  tool), also per call. Knobs: `LanceSearchParams` per call, table and store (`nprobes`
  100 per index segment; `rescore_factor` 2: the store reads the vectors of the best
  `k × 2` candidates in sorted batches and scores them exactly; Lance's own
  `refine_factor` off; `prefilter` on; the metric is always passed). A probe count is a
  ceiling too: a filtered search that comes back with fewer rows than it asked, and than
  its filter keeps (counted up to the page, rows outside the index included), is asked
  again at 4 and 16 times the count, then with the count as a floor and no ceiling
  (Lance then probes every partition), stopping once it holds them; each widening is
  logged at info (`lance.probes_widened`, counts tried and `elapsed_ms`).
  A filtered search whose filter keeps at most `exact_filter_rows` (4,096) rows scores
  them all exactly without the index. Counting them costs the whole filter through its
  scalar indexes (54 ms for an `IN` of three values on 23.9M rows, 143 ms for a range
  keeping most of 19.8M), so a filter found to keep more is remembered for the opened
  version of its table: the conditions every search carries are counted once (a search
  of 100 went from 354 to 215 ms on posts and from 183 to 130 ms on landing pages). A
  ceiling set alone (`maximum_nprobes`) keeps the
  inherited count as its floor; a ceiling you set can leave a filtered page short.
  `score_dims` on a search that reads every filtered row (no vector index yet, or an
  exact search) refuses past `max_fetch` rows with `LanceExactScanTooLargeError`, a
  size cap and no `LanceIndexError`: its `index_missing` says whether the table has no
  vector index (a reader answers an index not built) or exact scoring was asked over
  too many rows (the caller narrows or splits its filter). A search that filters
  after ranking (`prefilter=False`) still filters first wherever it scans listed
  fragments (the rows outside the index), which pylance requires.
- **Rows outside the index**: `unindexed` on every dense, text and hybrid search:
  `include` scans them (complete), `skip` leaves them out (as fast as the index), `refuse`
  raises `LanceUnindexedRowsError` when there are any. `include` scans at most
  `LanceStoreConfig.max_unindexed_rows` of them (100,000; `None` lifts it): the scan
  reads every such row, so its cost grows with them and not with the rows asked. Past
  the bound the search raises `LanceUnindexedScanTooLargeError` and scans nothing: a
  `LanceIndexError` (an index too far behind its table), not a
  `LanceUnindexedRowsError` (nothing the caller asked is refused). A column with no
  index has every row outside it, so a table larger than the bound is searched there
  only with `use_index=False`, which asks for every row and is not held to the bound.
  A row counts as outside when an
  index the search reads does not hold it: the vector or text index, and every scalar
  index its filter (or the table's live filter) reads; a vector or INVERTED index on a
  filter column is not one a prefilter reads. While such a scalar index lags,
  the search lists its fragments itself instead of trusting Lance's `fast_search`,
  which would drop the lagging rows from an equality filter and keep tombstones through
  a negated one. That read is correct but slow: Lance then reads the filter fragment by
  fragment (460 ms against 60 for the scan of 200 candidates on 11.6M rows, a tombstone
  index 1,346 rows behind being enough), on every search of the table. The writer
  keeps it from happening (simpl_core indexes the waiting rows before it commits a
  vector or keyword section); the store logs `lance.filter_index_behind` (warning, once
  per opened version: table, fragments, rows) when it does.
  `unindexed_rows(collection, column=None, filter=None)` and
  `index_stats` count them from the manifest.
  `has_index(collection, column=None)` says, from the manifest too, whether a column has
  a vector or keyword index at all: a search of one without still answers by scoring
  every row, so a caller that serves a column only through its index asks first.
- **Rows that store no vector** have no score. Wherever the store scores stored
  vectors itself (re-scored candidates, a filter that keeps few rows, named rows scored
  exactly, any `score_dims` search) it leaves them out, as Lance's own exact scan
  does, and a read `with_vectors=True` returns `vector=None` for them. Before, one such
  row among those scored failed the whole search (`cannot reshape array`); a fifth of
  the companies store no vector.
- **Keyword**: `text_search(..., sparse_field=column or [columns])` with `LanceTextParams`
  (or/and, boosts, fuzziness, phrase). A column without an INVERTED index raises
  `LanceIndexError` (Lance alone would scan every row). The store writes every query
  itself, so it knows the columns one reads: it takes no ready `FullTextQuery`.
- **Hybrid**: `hybrid_search(table, LanceHybridSearchConfig(legs=[...]))` fuses as Milvus 2.6
  does, in float32: RRF `sum(1/(k+rank))`, weighted with `norm_score`; each leg contributes
  `max(3 * limit, 100)` rows unless set. `column_aliases` maps other names (Milvus's
  `dense`, `*_sparse`) to the table's columns.
- **Grouping**: `group_by=GroupBy(field, size, strict)` on search, text and hybrid; `offset`
  and `limit` count groups. Grouped dense search picks a path with
  `LanceSearchParams(scoped=...)`: `exact`, `index` or `approx_then_exact`.
- **Filters**: the Milvus filter dict grammar (`LanceFilterTranslator`) plus `or` / `and`; a
  string is raw Lance SQL. Lists longer than 1,024 values become balanced `IN` groups.
- **Shards**: `LanceTableConfig(tables=[...], route=...)` makes one logical table of several;
  dense results merge exactly, keyword results by raw BM25 score (each shard's statistics)
  or by rank (`text_merge="rank"`).
- **Reads**: `count` (raises on failure, never 0), `get_by_id`, `retrieve`, `scroll` (id
  order, cursor), `query_iterator` (one version, storage order), iterators for search,
  text and hybrid (they log `lance.walk` with the rows read).
- **Memory**: one `lance.Session` per store bounds the index (512 MiB unless
  `index_cache_bytes` says) and metadata (128 MiB) caches, and scans buffer at most
  128 MiB; Lance's defaults are 6 GiB, 1 GiB and 2 GB. A serving deployment sizes the
  index cache to its indexes: smaller, a search costs seconds, and the store warns at a
  table's first open. Stores share one cache, of one cap, with
  `AsyncLanceStore(config, session=...)` (`.session`). Lance keys the cache by table URI:
  a table under two roots (bucket and local copy) is held twice, and the warning counts
  every store on the session by URI. `cache_bytes()` reports what the caches hold;
  `list_indexes` the bytes of each index. `AsyncLanceStore(config, executor=...)` runs
  that store's blocking pylance calls on its own `concurrent.futures.Executor` (default:
  the loop's), so a slow root's stuck reads cannot take every other store's threads.
- **Versions and restores**: reads inside `at_versions({table: version})` open that
  version from its own manifest (cached per table and version), never checked out of the
  dataset open at the latest. `forget(table)` drops what the store keeps of a table
  replaced under it (a restore): the next read opens it again, and a read already opening
  it when it is forgotten keeps nothing of what it opened.
- **Writes** (`create_collection`, `upsert*`, `delete*`, `create_vector_index`,
  `create_text_index`, `create_scalar_index`, `optimize`) are refused with
  `LanceReadOnlyError` when `read_only=True`: keep tables another process maintains
  read-only.

### simpl_seek, the row store query service (`simpl_core.seek`)

One query service answers searches of the row store over HTTP; a reader never names a
table, a version or SQL.

- **The contract** (`seek.contract`, re-exported by `simpl_core.seek`):
  `nearest`, `words`, `fused` (reciprocal rank with `rank_constant` and `candidates`,
  60 and 1,024 by default: the Zilliz rankers'), `best_per_owner` (the best rows of each
  company or site of a list), `best_per_owner_queries` (up to `QUERIES_MAX` queries by
  meaning over one list of owners, each with its own `id`, floor, rows per owner and
  `where`: each answered as `best_per_owner` answers it alone, the owners' rows read once;
  a query whose conditions the service cannot check is in `refused`, by id, and the
  others are answered), `rows` (by condition, paged by key), `lookup` (by key),
  `above` (every row over a score by meaning or by keywords, paged, at most
  `ABOVE_MAX_ROWS`). A `Condition` is a value, a list or a `Between` per column, all
  holding; `None` asks for no value, alone or in a list; a time is ISO 8601 text.
  `unindexed` says what a search does with rows outside the index: `include`, `skip` or
  `refuse`. `precision` says how much of the vector index a search by meaning reads
  (`nearest`, `fused`, `above` with a vector). `standard` is the dataset's settings (400
  probes, five times the rows re-scored, fewer times for a deep page): measured, 32 to
  68 ms for the best 100 and 97% to 99% of the true ones found, 95.5% of the best 5,000
  companies at twice the rows. A caller sets its own multiple with `rescore` on the
  request (`RowStoreSearcher.with_rescore(n)`, or `rescore=n` on `RowStorePostSearch`
  and `RowStoreLandingSearch`): twice the rows found 89% to 95% for 200 stored vectors
  read on a page of 100, five times reads 500, ten times 1,000 for under a point more
  where it was measured (100 probes); on the best 5,000 companies ten times found 97%
  in 0.82 s against 95.5% in 0.24 s. The service keeps a caller's multiple while the
  search re-scores at most 65,536 vectors. `full` reads every partition and finds
  99.9% to 100%: **costly**, 0.3 s (companies) to 0.8 s (landing pages) of one processor
  for the best 100 and more for a deeper page, and the service answers two at once (a
  third gets `QueryUnavailableError` and the client tries again). Use it for a question
  asked now and then, never for a page a user waits on or a loop; a request that names
  its rows is exact without it. A table is served by meaning once its vector index is
  built (`IndexNotReadyError` until then). The same error answers a search, by meaning
  or by keywords, of a table with more than 100,000 rows outside an index it reads:
  the service scans those rows only up to that bound (`unindexed="skip"` is still
  answered, without them). Caps are
  named in the contract (`ABOVE_MAX_ROWS`, `EXACT_MAX_ROWS`,
  `PAGE_MAX_ROWS`, `KEYS_MAX`, `OWNERS_MAX`, `PER_OWNER_MAX`, `QUERIES_MAX`,
  `FUSED_CANDIDATES`):
  readers import them, never copy them. `AboveRequest.max_rows` is required.
- **Errors, siblings named by what the caller does**: `QueryUnavailableError` (away:
  retry later; the only one the client retries), `IndexNotReadyError` (an index being
  built, or too far behind its table), `QueryServiceFaultError` (token refused, a crash, an unreadable answer) and
  `QueryRefusedError` with `UnknownDatasetError` and `WalkExpiredError`. A reader never
  catches them by class (a test fails on an `except` naming one outside `seek`):
  `query_outcome(error)` reads any failure, groups and wrapped errors included, as
  `Outcome.AWAY`, `NOT_READY`, `FAULT` or `REFUSED`. Interactive: simpl_api's handler
  answers it. Batch: `BatchBreaker(step=...)`, `async with breaker.item(id,
  dataset=...) as item`, asking `breaker.skips(dataset)` first; the service away stops
  the step (the item left as it was), an index not ready stops only the items of its
  dataset (`IndexNotReadyError.dataset`, which crosses the wire), a fault fails the item
  (`item.failed`) and the step goes on, anything else is raised; `optional=True` for a
  part the step can go without. A part of a search over several
  goes through `answer_or_not_ready`; calls run together through `in_task_group`.
- **The client**: every host calls `check_seek_settings()` once at start
  (`ROW_STORE_QUERY_URL` and `ROW_STORE_QUERY_TOKEN` set, neither with spaces or a line
  break around it, the URL `http(s)://host`; else `ValueError`, values never in the
  message). `SeekClient.from_env(profile)` makes a client; `open_seek(profile)` opens
  one for a piece of work (a client belongs to the event loop that made it). The
  `Profile` is what the reader is, never a timeout of its own: `INTERACTIVE` (retries
  an outage 10 s, a call 20 s at most), `INTERACTIVE_WALK` (10 s, 120 s), `BATCH` (150 s,
  300 s), `HEALTH` (no retry, 2 s). A reader never wraps a call in a timeout of its own:
  an outage would reach it as a bare `TimeoutError`. A request that cannot be sent
  (httpx's `LocalProtocolError` / `UnsupportedProtocol`) is a `QueryServiceFaultError`,
  never retried, logged by type only (its message quotes the token).
- **The client's pace** (`seek.pace`): every answer, a refusal included, carries the
  caller's budget (`wire`: `X-Seek-Places-Reserved`, `-Held`, `-Available`, over all the
  caller's processes; `X-Seek-Load`, the engine's CPU over its cores; `X-Seek-Server-Ms`;
  `X-Seek-Owners-Max`, `X-Seek-Queries-Max`; `Retry-After` on a 503 over the limit).
  `SeekClient` keeps a limit of searches in flight from it (`Pace`: 2 before any answer,
  then the reserved places; one more every 2 s while searches wait, the load is under 0.7
  and a place is free; ×0.7 on a refusal or a load over 0.9, once every 5 s; never above
  held plus available) and waits `Retry-After` (with jitter) before trying a refused
  search again. `client.budget` is the last answer's `Budget`, `client.limit` the limit.
  `client.stream(calls)` runs `(key, call)` pairs (an iterable, or an async one read as
  calls are needed: pages read from a database) at the client's limit and yields
  `(key, answer)` as each comes back; the first failure cancels the rest and is raised. A
  service built before the headers leaves the limit at 2.
- **Several signals over one page**: `RowStoreSearcher.best_per_group_queries(queries,
  group_by=, groups=)` (`GroupQuery`: id, dense vector, floor, conditions) splits groups
  by `OWNERS_MAX` and queries by `QUERIES_MAX` and answers each query as `best_per_group`
  does by meaning; a refused query raises `QueryRefusedError`.
  `PostSearch.best_per_company_queries(queries, company_ids=)` (`PostQuery`) and
  `LandingSearch.best_per_company_queries(db, queries, company_ids=)` (`LandingQuery`;
  the companies' sites looked up once) answer each query as `best_per_company` answers it
  alone, by meaning, keyed by the query's id. Keyword and hybrid searches stay one
  request each.
- **Walks**: `walk_above(client.above, request, reader=...)`, with `request.max_rows`
  the most the reader wants (`ABOVE_MAX_ROWS` for every row the service walks), ranks in
  `WALK_STAGES` (10,000, then 100,000, then the cap) so the first page never waits for
  half a million rows, yields each row once, and ends with `walk.cut`: None, or a `Cut`
  saying why (`limit`, `service_cap`, `page_cap`, `deadline`) and where. A reader that
  must tell a whole answer from a partial one reads `walk.cut`, nothing else.
  `walk_pages` reads one request's pages (a `rows` walk).
- **The service's database role**: `simpl_seek_reader`
  (`migrations/20261123100000_row_store_seek_reader_role.sql`) reads
  `row_store.dataset_configs` and nothing else, through a row-level security rule of its
  own; a maintainer gives it its login and password by hand.
- **The service is the only system for these searches**: there is no other backend to
  fall back to. An interactive reader answers 503 on `QueryUnavailableError`; a batch
  step retries. The service is its own repository, simpl-techs/simpl_seek: the engine,
  the local mirror and the app live there. simpl_core gives what it and the readers
  share: the contract, the wire, the client, and the datasets served by kind
  (`search.served.SERVED`, versions from the active `row_store.dataset_configs` rows).
  The kinds' readers are `search.index.row_search.RowStoreSearcher` and the kinds'
  `RowStore*Search` classes (posts, landing pages, website facts), each query scored at
  its kind's `QUERY_DIMS`.
- **The evaluation** (`seek.evaluation`): every reader is gated on every run,
  per dataset and operation, against exact answers: recall by meaning through the index
  mean / worst 0.90 / 0.50, by meaning on stored vectors (`best_per_owner`, or at most
  `EXACT_MAX_ROWS` rows named) and by keywords and fused 0.98 / 0.80, precision 0.98 /
  0.80, every score by meaning within `SCORE_TOLERANCE` of the exact cosine, a fused
  answer over listed rows ranking its word matches where its keyword half puts them, at
  least 5 measured cases, p95 under 1 s for interactive readers. An empty walk page
  that names a next one fails the case. A failing reader logs
  `row_store.query.evaluation_failed`, alerts #data-lake, and the host fails its run.
- **Agent memory and ops files stay on Zilliz**: three small collections whose source
  is Postgres, not row store tables.

### Work pools: queues, leases, instances (`workpool`)

`simpl_core.workpool` is the queue and lease machinery for every process that claims
work from Postgres. Its own tables are in the `workpool` schema
(`migrations/20261103_workpool.sql`: `resource_leases`, `instances`, `incidents`); a
queue table and a counters table belong to the consumer's schema and migration.

```python
spec = QueueSpec(table="knowledge_graph.model_requests",          # selector column: model
                 payload={"request": "jsonb"}, results={"response": "jsonb", "release_id": "text"})
queue = WorkQueue(spec)
async with db.session_scope() as s:                                # requester
    await queue.enqueue(s, [NewRow(selector="encoder", requester=token, request_id=rid,
                                   deadline_at=lease_end, size_hint=len(text), payload={...})])
async with db.session_scope() as s:                                # consumer
    claim = await queue.claim(s, "encoder", token=t, ttl=ttl, batch_units=400_000,
                              instance_id=instance)
async with db.session_scope() as s:
    await queue.complete(s, t, [RowResult(row.id, {"response": ...})])  # fenced on token + lease
rows = await queue.wait_done(db, rid, token, expected=n, deadline=d)    # requester reads ...
async with db.session_scope() as s:
    await queue.delete_read(s, rid, token)                               # ... and deletes
```

- **Every queue table has `REQUIRED_COLUMNS`** plus its selector column; the caller
  declares payload columns (written at enqueue) and result columns (written by the
  completion). `verify_shape(session, spec, unlogged=...)` lists what a table lacks.
- **States:** `queued` -> `leased` (claim, counts an attempt) -> `done` | `failed`, or
  back to `queued` (`fail` with a retry left, `release`, `recover`). `done` and `failed`
  are terminal; only the requester deletes them, and `sweep` deletes every row past its
  `deadline_at`. Every write of a holder is fenced on its token and an unexpired lease
  (`clock_timestamp()`), so a lost lease writes nothing.
- **Claims** take lanes in rank order, then priority, eligibility and age, with
  `SKIP LOCKED`; bounded by rows (`limit`) or `size_hint` (`batch_units`, a row counting at
  least 1), with per-kind `shares` so one kind cannot starve another, taken in claim
  order so no later lane goes first; a kind with no share only fills. A row whose lease expired twice with its
  instance alive (`crash_suspect`) is claimed alone; at three it fails.
- **`recover`** gives the attempt back when the holder's instance is gone from
  `workpool.instances` or stopping, and keeps it otherwise.
- Every method runs on the caller's session and never commits, except `wait_done`,
  which polls with a short session per read and raises `RowsGoneError` at once when rows
  are missing, `WaitTimeoutError` at the deadline. A requester that does not wait reads its answered
  and failed rows later with `finished(session, requester, limit=...)` (each `RowState`
  with its `request_id`) and deletes them with `delete_read`.
- `LeaseKeeper` renews every lease a process holds in one statement per third of the
  ttl. `ResourceLease` holds one named resource, arbitrated by `PostgresArbiter`
  (with `fence` inside a transaction) or `ObjectStoreArbiter` over a `GenerationStore`
  (`GcsGenerationStore`, the `parquet` extra). `InstanceRegistry`, `ClaimPacer`,
  `CounterTable`/`MinuteCounters` (one row per key and minute) and `IncidentOutbox` with
  `deliver_due` (one open incident per dedup key, delivered after commit, each send in
  a worker thread so a blocking notifier never holds the event loop; a notifier that
  raises is a failed delivery) complete it. `workpool.incidents` is shared: `deliver_due`
  takes `key_prefix` (only those keys) or `skip_key_prefix` (all but those).
  `RESEARCH_KEY_PREFIX` (`research.`) starts every key the OSINT research pool records and
  delivers itself (simpl_scraping `research/incidents.py`); every other deliverer skips it.
- `load_topology(path)` reads a fleet's pools (`Topology`, `Pool`, `Scaling`, `Runtime`,
  `Quota`): role (`gpu` with `depth`, `cpu` with `concurrency`), substrate, region and GPU
  type, runtime binding (`in_process` or `server`), `batch_units`, lanes, per-kind
  `shares`, timings and scaling bounds. It refuses a heartbeat slower than a third of the
  lease, a drain past the stop grace, connections over `db_connection_budget`, and GPU
  pools whose `Σ max_instances` (or floors) exceed their region's quota. A pool with
  `yields: true` (a backfill pool) is left out of that sum: it has no floor, the yielding
  pools' maximums together fit in what the others' floors leave, and every tick caps it at
  `Topology.left_for(pool, held)` (the quota less each other pool's floor or new count),
  the yielding pools decided after the others; wanting more opens an
  `autoscaler-quota-short` warning a day. A pool that wants more than its
  `max_instances` opens an `autoscaler-at-max` warning only while
  `Topology.room_above_max(pool)` is above 0 (the quota leaves room to raise the
  maximum); at the whole quota only more quota grows it, and the tick records the
  decision's `at_max` reason alone. The release a pool serves is its deploy
  argument, never a topology field.
- `Substrate` reads and sets a pool's instance count (`get`, `set_instances` with the
  version it read; a stale one raises `StaleVersionError`); `CloudRunWorkerPools` does it
  through the Cloud Run Admin API v2 (`scaling.manualInstanceCount`, etag, no new
  revision), `ComputeEngineMigs` through the Compute API v1 on the regional managed
  instance group named as the pool (`targetSize`, its `fingerprint`; topology
  `substrate: compute_engine_mig`), and `fleet_substrate(topology, http, tokens)` sends
  each pool's calls to the one its topology names. `workpool.deploy` rolls Cloud Run
  pools only: a group's instances come from its own instance template, and every deploy
  command reports it skipped. `workpool.deploy` checks, rolls (`redeploy`) and creates (`provision`) the
  pools from the topology's `deployment` section, each instance running `command --pool
  <name> --release <release>`: the release is a per-pool deploy argument, and a pool
  serving another one reads as drift. `InstanceRegistry.record_serves`/`served` keep what
  each pool's runtime serves (`workpool.pool_serves`, migration `20261103b`), so a
  requester names a pool's model while it has no instance.
- `workpool.autoscaler`: `decide(pool, demand, current=, memory=, pressure=)` is the pure law
  (`wanted = ceil(backlog_units / (units_per_s × drain_window × target_utilization))`,
  sleep after `sleep_after_s` at empty whatever `min_instances` says, wake on enough work,
  three rising ticks or a row of its lanes near its deadline: `Scaling.wake_after_s` old, by
  default the drain window less the wake lead, `starved` holds a pool whose ready
  instances idle with a backlog, cooldown before shrinking, no growth under database
  pressure). `Autoscaler(db, substrate, topology, demand_reader, steps=...).tick()` runs
  the caller's tick steps, prunes stale instances, samples `db_pressure`, and sets each
  pool's count with the version it read, recording `workpool.scaling_operations` and
  `workpool.pool_states` (migration `20261103c`); failures and at-max, starved and
  deadline wake-ups open incidents.
- A claim can be restricted to `kinds` (an empty tuple claims nothing), so a budget-bound
  pool keeps a reserve for one kind. A `Demand.cap` bounds a pool's count from above (a
  budget-bound pool's calls the money left allows); a cap of zero keeps it asleep (`capped`).
- A topology names its fleet's queues (`queues:`, each a `FleetQueue`: table, selector,
  payload and result columns, `counters`, `failures_kept_in`) and each pool names its
  queue. A new queue needs its table, its entry, a pool claiming from it and the runner
  its instances run; it needs no demand reader, tick steps or autoscaler of its own.
  `serving_counters(table, selector)` is its per-minute counters table (`SERVING_COUNTERS`:
  rows and units written, answered and failed, batches, busy and ready seconds).
- `workpool.autoscaler.tick_fleet(db, topology, substrate, caps=, routes=, dry_run=)` runs
  one tick over every pool at once, since they share the quota, under a Postgres lease
  named for the fleet's queues (a tick that finds it held does nothing). `QueueDemand`
  reads each pool's backlog, arrivals per tick, the oldest row of any of its lanes, its
  rate and its cap; `queue_steps` recover expired leases (rows failed for good after
  crashing a pool's instances are one `dead-rows:<pool>:<day>` warning), sweep and prune,
  reported as `<queue>.recover` and so on; then the due incidents go to the topology's
  alert route, all but the research pool's (`skip_key_prefix=RESEARCH_KEY_PREFIX`). A pool's `cap` and the topology's `alerts` are names: `ALERT_ROUTES` holds
  core's (`data_lake`: `DataLakeNotifier`, errors and warnings on #data-lake, info only
  logged), `caps=` and `routes=` add the caller's, and a name nobody registered raises
  `ValueError` before the tick.
- `workpool.Requester(db, queue, counters)` is a queue's requester side: `submit` writes
  rows and counts them as arrivals in one transaction, `sweep` deletes rows past their
  deadline. `workpool.status.pool_status` and `render` show a fleet now.
- `workpool.cli.run_cli(argv, topology=, database=, prog=, caps=, routes=)` is a fleet's
  command line (`status`, `scale`, `check`, `redeploy`, `provision`, `autoscale`);
  `database` opens the repository's database. `open_fleet_substrate(topology)` is
  `fleet_substrate` for the length of a block. A pool's `runtime.command` replaces the
  deployment's `command`, for a fleet whose pools run different entry points.
- `workpool.consumer.Consumer(db, queue, counters, runner, settings)` is a pool instance's
  loop over a `JobRunner` (`self_check`, `serves`, `jobs` splitting a batch, `run`
  answering one, raising `RuntimeDown` when it stops answering): register, self-check,
  record what it serves, claim with a fencing token per batch, keep `depth` batches in
  hand, write each batch's answers fenced, and heartbeat with the counters. On the stop
  signal it reports itself stopping at once, starts no job, gives the running one what is
  left of `drain_timeout_s` from the signal, gives back the rest and deregisters.

### Search index for any kind (`search.index`)

`simpl_core.search.index` turns a kind of object into a searchable index: a ledger of
one row per document, documents built into rows of a row-store dataset (embedded once
per distinct text, stored vectors reused by key), searched through the row store's query
service (`RowStoreSearcher`). Posts are the first kind (`PostsKind`); a new kind
implements `DocumentKind` and reuses the rest.

```python
indexer = DocumentIndexer(
    ledger=IndexLedger(db, "sales.my_kind_index", prioritized=True),
    kind=MyKind(...),            # load(claims), build(claim, loaded), stored_keys(...)
    dataset=dataset,             # needs text_hash, embedding_model and vector columns
    embedder=NebiusEmbedder(api_key=..., dims=4096, query_task="..."),
    name="my_kind",              # log events: my_kind.batch, my_kind.rows_failed
    companion=notes,             # optional: a dataset without vectors, with doc_hash
)
result = await indexer.run_batch(batch_size=100)

# or overlapped: the next batch loads and the previous one commits while one embeds
async with BatchPipeline(indexer, run_id=run_id, depth=3, on_result=record) as pipeline:
    while not pipeline.stopped:
        claimed = await pipeline.submit(100)  # returns once claimed, loaded and built
        if claimed.claimed + claimed.released < 100:
            break
# leaving the block waits for every batch to commit (or gives them back on an error)

# searched through the query service, a hit made by the kind's HitShape
MY_KIND = ServedDataset(name="my_kind", shape=HitShape(columns=(...), fields=...),
                        owner="company_id", query_dims=512)
searcher = RowStoreSearcher(queries, MY_KIND, embed_query=embedder.embed_query)
groups = await searcher.run_hybrid_groups(dense, text, {"language": ["it"]}, limit=10,
                                          group_by="company_id")
```

- `RowStoreSearcher` (`search.index.row_search`): `run` (dense, keyword or fused,
  grouped here on a read `HITS_PER_GROUP` times deeper), `run_hybrid_groups` (each half
  grouped, fused by group: `FusedGroup` with each half's `HalfRank`),
  `chunk_similarities` (exact cosines of given keys), `best_per_group` (the best hit of
  each owner: exact for a list of owners, from the best of the dataset otherwise).
  `with_precision("full")` gives a searcher whose searches through the index read all
  of it (costly, see `precision` above); the default is `standard`.
  `with_rescore(n)` gives one that re-scores `n` times the rows it asks for instead of
  the dataset's five (`search.served.DEFAULT_RESCORE`); not with `full`, which sets its
  own.
  Conditions are said on a hit's fields (`ServedDataset.condition` sends a time as ISO
  8601 text, and a value a hit shows for a null asks for the null too). Its concurrent
  requests run in a task group (`in_task_group`): the first failure cancels the rest
  and is raised itself. Walks read `PAGE_MAX_ROWS` (10,000) rows a page, `MAX_WALK_PAGES`
  pages at most (`walk_pages`), and start again once when their ranking expired.
- A keyword-only kind (typed tests and words, no embeddings) has a dataset without
  `vector` (and needs no `embedding_model` or `text_hash`) and no embedder:
  `DocumentIndexer(ledger=..., kind=MyKind(...), dataset=dataset, name="my_kind")`.
  Without an embedder the indexer skips the vectors step (nothing is embedded, and
  `max_text_chars` does not apply); claim, build, the unchanged check, tombstones,
  commit and settle are the same, and `BatchPipeline` still groups the commits. Its
  readers read rows through the service (`rows`), as the website facts do.
- A hit's `content` is cut at `CONTENT_MAX_BYTES` (`cap_utf8`), as the collections cut
  it, so a snippet or a judge's passage keeps its length.

- A document of several rows keys them `row_key(doc_key, part, ...)`; its stored keys
  come from `stored_row_keys(dataset, doc_keys)`, one read through the key index.
- `BuiltDocument.text_hash`/`meta_hash` equal to the ledger's (same table) settle the
  document without writing; rows a new version no longer has get tombstones.
- `BuiltDocument.companion` rows (`CompanionRow`, whatever the outcome) go to the
  indexer's `companion` dataset in the same batch, rewritten only when
  `companion_hash` differs from the `doc_hash` stored with them: a change to them alone
  never rewrites the document's own rows.
- A scoped run indexes exactly a selection: a kind's `ScopeWalk` stages a page of the
  scope (`ScopePage.pending` = its documents due now) and
  `run_batch(batch_size=..., keys=page.pending)` claims only those, then
  `walk.advance(page)`. Each scope has its own cursor (`ScanCursors`); scoped and
  general runs share the ledger, so neither redoes what the other did or holds.
- A kind whose ledger is a queue with a record (`IndexLedger(db, table,
  record=IndexRecord(...))`) is walked page by page against the record:
  `ListingWalk(listing=...)` for a kind listed by key (every company's job offers:
  `marking.job_offer_listing`, every offer whatever its age; the conversation scope's
  keep one year), `sales_datasets.positions_walk.PositionsWalk` for every
  lead's positions (a page of leads, each with the company the product's rule gives;
  a lead with none stays out). An existing ledger moves onto a queue and its record with
  `move_ledger(db, source="schema.table", queue=ledger)`, the kind paused: stored rows
  are not embedded again.
- `BatchPipeline` overlaps batches (up to `depth` in hand; `depth=1` prepares one at
  a time) and commits embedded batches together: one row store commit once the bytes
  of their Arrow tables reach `commit_bytes` (`COMMIT_BYTES`, 64 MiB) or the first has
  waited `commit_wait` (`COMMIT_WAIT_S`, 60 s); `commit_bytes=0` commits each batch
  alone. `commit_bytes` counts bytes (the batches' row tables plus their companion
  tables), not rows: a kind of narrow rows (website facts, about 350 bytes a row) fits
  about 190k rows in 64 MiB, and a commit's key lookup costs per row, so such a kind
  passes a smaller one (`BatchPipeline(..., commit_bytes=16 * 2**20)`). A batch waits
  for its group with its
  tables only: `embed` lets its built documents go (`PreparedBatch.writes` and
  `companions` are empty afterwards; `to_store` keeps their count). A batch with
  nothing to write settles at once. `on_result` sees each committed batch (a group's
  share one `commit_seq`); `drain()` waits for every batch in hand and commits the
  group (a scoped run drains before `walk.advance`); `deferred`/`stopped` say why it
  stopped. An embedder that cannot answer for now raises `EmbedderUnavailableError`
  (`NebiusEmbedder.embed` does on simpl_ia's retryable errors: timeout, connection,
  rate limit, 5xx, once its retries are spent): the pipeline defers instead of failing.
  The batch goes back with no failure counted, the waiting groups still commit, nothing
  more is claimed, nothing is raised, and `embedder_unavailable` holds the first such
  error for the caller to report. Other embedder errors still raise.
  The embedder never gets more than `embed_concurrency` requests at once.
  A commit never carries a document twice: a batch holding a document already
  waiting commits the group first.
- A scanner's mark must leave a claim in place (never `claim_token = NULL`): the batch
  holding the row gives it back after its commit, and it is claimed again from its new
  state. Clearing it lets one run hold the document twice.
- `Dataset.health()` reads, from the manifest, what maintenance has left
  (`DatasetHealth`: small and held fragments, rows outside the indexes, the most
  segments in an index); `problems()` names what is past normal.
  - **What it costs:** each fragment's id sequence lives in the manifest, which every
    commit rewrites.
  - **Index types:** use BTREE, BITMAP and NGRAM only; zone map, bloom filter and FM
    indexes don't survive compaction on such a table.
- **Reading a whole table: no filter, a fragment part per reader (0.8.368).** A
  filtered `scan` goes through the scalar indexes, which fetch each matching row on
  its own: one request a row on GCS. 400 owners' posts across 922 fragments took
  26,105 requests and 38 s for 4,569 rows (2026-11-12). Read every row with
  `scan(columns, include_deleted=True, version=v, fragments=part)`, drop tombstones
  and unwanted rows in memory, and give each reader a part of
  `await dataset.fragment_ids(version=v)`: one version, read once between them.
  pylance's `to_batches` ignores a `fragments` argument; `scan` passes it to the
  scanner.
- A `DatasetSpec` can declare `ngram_indexes` (string columns) for substring filters;
  `Dataset.scan(where=...)` takes a Lance SQL string such as
  `"contains(markup, 'pricing')"` to use them, since Arrow expressions cannot express a
  substring match. Indexes are not part of `spec_hash`, so adding one keeps the dataset
  version. An NGRAM index is a section index (0.8.159): `build_index(column)` builds it
  in sections of at most `NGRAM_SECTION_MAX_BYTES` (32 MiB on disk, about 1.5 GB to
  build), never a commit or `maintain()`; rows outside its sections are still found by
  a scan. Such a table's fragments are written and compacted to that size (compaction
  re-encodes them, 0.8.165), and `maintain` rewrites an older fragment over 1.5 sections
  into section-sized ones (`MaintenanceResult.rows_rewritten`), which `build_index`
  leaves out until then.

### Landing search (`search.landing`)

Company websites, as the crawl stores them (`scraping.pages` rows of representation
`default` only; OSINT research's copies, `research`, never enter the index or mark a site
changed), in the row store (the `landing_pages` dataset, searched through the query
service): one document per site (`h:{host}`), its pages' text
chunked along each page's elements (1,200 to 1,800 characters: `CHUNK_SOFT_CHARS`,
`CHUNK_HARD_CHARS`), its template (menu, footer) once, copies of a line or a chunk left
out, every page with text kept however short. Its markup goes to its own dataset without
vectors (`landing_markup_spec`, `landing_indexer(markup=...)`), one element per line: a
start tag with every attribute where the text is kept (e.g. `<input type="email"
name="EMAIL">`), the whole source where it is not (scripts, CSS, SVG, hidden elements,
comments; only embedded binary file bytes are cut). Markup rows share the text rows' keys
(each chunk's and the template's markup), plus one per page, `h:{host}#p:{page id}`: the
page's `<html>`, `<head>`, `<body>` and trailing markup (a line on many of the site's pages
only on the first page that has it) and the markup of what its text rows leave out. A site
without text keeps its markup. Markup is neither embedded nor searched by meaning, and a
change to it alone rewrites markup rows only. Sites enter the index through scoped runs
(e.g. Italian companies, or `all`: every company); the general run keeps them in step
with the crawl.

```python
scanner = LandingScanner(db)

# a scoped run: exactly the scope's sites, a page of companies at a time
walk = scanner.walk_scope(CompanyScope(name="italy", countries=("ITA", "IT")))
while True:
    page = await walk.next_page(2000)        # rows at priority 1, changed sites marked
    for keys in batched(page.pending, 20):
        await indexer.run_batch(batch_size=len(keys), keys=keys)
    await walk.advance(page)
    if page.done:
        break

# the general run: the sites in the ledger whose pages the crawl changed
await scanner.follow_crawl()                 # adds no sites
await indexer.run_batch(batch_size=20)       # by priority

sources = LandingSourceRepository(db, bodies, bucket=bodies.config.bucket)  # S3ObjectStore
indexer = landing_indexer(db, sources=sources, dataset=landing_dataset,
                          embedder=NebiusEmbedder(api_key=..., dims=4096,
                                                  query_task=landing.QUERY_TASK),
                          detect_language=detect)

# embedder at landing.QUERY_DIMS (512); queries: the query service's client accessor
search = RowStoreLandingSearch(queries, embed_query=embedder.embed_query)
hosts = await hosts_for_companies(db, company_ids)       # host -> company ids
hits = await search.search(text="impianti fotovoltaici",
                           filters=LandingSearchFilters(hosts=list(hosts)),
                           one_per_host=True, limit=50)
```

- **Which companies' sites match** (`best_per_company(db, company_ids=..., ...)`): a
  topic (vector + `min_score`), keywords that must be in the chunk text
  (`KeywordFilter`, as for posts), or both. It maps the companies to their hosts, searches
  the sites (`best_per_host`, complete for the listed hosts) and returns company id ->
  the site's best chunk (`url`, `title`, `section`, `content`; `page_id` is null for the
  site template). `company_ids=None` searches the best 1,024 sites and maps them back.
  Pages carry no date, so there is no window. `KeywordFilter.locate(text)` gives the
  span of the first keyword, for a snippet; `landing_evidence(hit, keywords)` is what a
  match shows (`url`, `title`, `section`, `page_kind`, `snippet` around the first keyword).

```python
found = await search.best_per_company(
    db, company_ids=ids,
    vector=topic_vector, min_score=0.5,                   # omit for keywords only
    keywords=KeywordFilter(["ISO 9001", "UNI EN ISO 9001"]),
)
```

- **Preview a website signal** (`open_landing_preview`): the same search, without
  storing anything, on the ICP's companies (`PreviewScope.ICP`, read from
  `opportunity.icp_company_matches` 1,024 at a time, 6 pages at once, no page started
  after 20 s: `cut_short` and `companies_searched` / `icp_companies` say how far it got)
  or the whole index (`PreviewScope.ALL`: the best 1,024 sites or chunks), read through
  `queries` (the query service's client, which the caller owns); the topic is embedded
  at `QUERY_DIMS`. A failed search fails the preview with the service's error, raised
  itself.

```python
async with open_landing_preview(db, nebius_api_key=key, queries=queries) as previewer:
    preview = await previewer.preview(
        WebsiteSignalQuery(topic="ISO 9001 certified", min_score=0.5,
                           keywords=KeywordFilter(["ISO 9001"])),   # either or both
        scope=PreviewScope.ICP, icp_id=icp_id, limit=100,
    )
preview.companies_matched, preview.matches[0].company_name, preview.matches[0].hit.url
```

- `landing_indexer(..., html_bytes_per_batch=64 * 2**20)` sizes batches by the HTML
  their sites read (`LandingSourceRepository.html_bytes`); `run_batch`'s `batch_size`
  caps the sites. Claims past it are released (`released_keys`) and processed by a later
  batch; the first site is always kept. A site reads its pages in URL order up to
  `max_pages` and `max_site_bytes` (64 MiB of HTML, the first page always): pages past
  either are left out. (0.8.160; `pages_per_batch` and `page_counts` are gone.)
- A scope with no conditions must be named `all` (priority 0); other scopes need a
  condition and a priority of at least 1. Redefining a scope under the same name
  starts its walk over.
- Every visible text is kept; legal pages are excluded from search unless
  `page_kinds` names `legal`. Hits carry the chunk (`content`), its page `url`, `title`
  and `section`.
- The ledger and cursors are simpl_flow migration 075; bodies need an `S3ObjectStore`
  over the crawl's bucket (`simpl-web-pages`). Install `simpl-core[search]`: the row
  store and lxml.

### Company search (`search.companies`)

Every company with a description, in the row store (the `sales_companies` dataset,
searched through the query service: simpl_api's company search): one row per company,
keyed by its id, its text the description (as `sales_companies_hybrid` embedded it).

```python
scanner = CompanyScanner(db)
await scanner.discover(walk_pages=1)     # created/updated since last time, then the walk
await scanner.sweep()                    # nightly: merged-away companies, one key slice

indexer = company_indexer(db, dataset=companies_dataset,   # companies_dataset_spec(dims=4096)
                          embedder=NebiusEmbedder(api_key=..., dims=4096,
                                                  query_task=companies.QUERY_TASK),
                          detect_language=detect)
await indexer.run_batch(batch_size=1000)
```

- `DiscoverResult.found` is what a discovery added or marked; `walk_done` once every
  company has been walked. A flow passes `discover` as the `refill` of its batch loop.
- A company hit (`COMPANY_HIT`, `search.companies.row_search`: `SALES_COMPANIES`,
  `company_columns`, `company_result`) keeps the replaced collection's field names
  (`description` and `content` both the description, `name`) beside `industry_id`,
  `industry_name`, `country`, `city`, `location`, sizes, `follower_count`, URLs,
  `created_at` (epoch seconds) and `language`. A query is scored at `QUERY_DIMS` (1,024).

### Website facts search (`search.website_facts`)

What each company site runs and shows (simpl_scraping's `sales.website` and
`sales.website_technology`), in the row store (the `website_facts` dataset, read through
the query service): no embeddings; a condition's typed tests and keywords are judged on
each site's rows. One document per site (its `sales.website` host): `{host}#site` with every
site fact, `{host}#tech#{digest}` per technology. A site can have technology rows and no
site row. What moves on every sighting (`last_seen_at`, `seen_on_url`, `evidence`) is
not indexed; a match reads it from Postgres.

```python
scanner = WebsiteFactsScanner(db)
await scanner.follow_extraction()   # every run: succeeded website_signals jobs -> hosts
await scanner.sweep()               # nightly, and the first fill: every site by host

indexer = website_facts_indexer(db, dataset=facts_dataset)  # website_facts_dataset_spec(version=...), no embedder
await indexer.run_batch(batch_size=500)

errors = await check_condition(db, condition)       # before ANY save; [] = valid
# A signal on an ICP: check_condition, then the ICP's company filters (simpl_api's save rule)
errors = await website_facts_signal_errors(db, filter_json, icp_id=icp_id)
condition_in_words(parse_condition(condition))       # "sites running PHP past end of life"
await technology_names(db, search="apache", category=None,
                       listing_tool="list_website_technologies", save_tool="create_icp_signal")
search = RowStoreWebsiteFactsSearch(queries)         # the query service's client accessor
found = await search.matching_companies(db, parse_condition(condition), company_ids)
shown = await website_facts_evidence(db, parse_condition(condition), list(found.values()))
```

- **Two dataset shapes, by version** (0.8.532): `website_facts_dataset_spec(name=,
  version=)` gives version 1 and 2 today's columns (spec hashes pinned in
  `test_spec.py`), and version 3 on (`FAMILIES_VERSION`, `holds_families(version)`) adds
  the site row's `families` (`DERIVED_SITE_FIELDS`) and drops the technology rows'
  `max_severity` and `vulnerability_count` (`VULNERABILITY_FIELDS`, versions 1 and 2
  only; `technology_fields(version)`). Build each version only with its own shape: pass
  the config's version to the spec (simpl_seek's `search.served` and simpl_flow's
  `DatasetRef` already do), and `website_facts_indexer(db, dataset=)` reads and builds for
  `dataset.spec.version` (`WebsiteFactsSources(db, version=)`, `build_site_facts(host,
  sources, version=)`; both default to version 1's shape). A version-3 build reads the
  internal facts (`home_page_kind`, `missed_security_release`) and the family rules on
  every load: its login needs SELECT on `sales.website_fact_families` **and** to see its
  rows past row level security (the owner, or BYPASSRLS such as `service_role`). With no
  enabled row readable it does not fail: it logs `website_facts.no_families` (once per
  state of the rows, per process, as `website_facts.family_refused`) and stores an empty
  `families` list on every site, so switching every family off never stops the index.
  Only `build_site_facts` given sources read without the rules (`family_rules` None) for
  version 3 raises: that is a caller's mistake.
  `TECHNOLOGY_FIELDS` is now the four columns every version holds (`technology`,
  `categories`, `version`, `eol_date`), and so is `MATCH_COLUMNS`'s technology part: a
  reader of `TECHNOLOGY_FIELDS` that wants version 2's pair adds `VULNERABILITY_FIELDS`.
  The scanner's fingerprint and `TEXT_PREP_VERSION` (2) do not change in this release.
- **Signals on an ICP** (0.8.322): `WEBSITE_FACTS_KIND` is how every prompt that lists
  signal kinds describes this one, word for word (simpl_api's wizard and chat, which
  import it, and the Sales Domain skills); `NO_COMPANY_FILTERS` is the refusal on an ICP without an enabled
  company filter; `condition_key` tells when two conditions are the same signal;
  `technology_names` is the listing behind simpl_api's `list_website_technologies` and
  the Sales Domain agent's, moved here from simpl_api (which imports it). See "Signal proposals" under the Sales
  Domain approval queue.
- **Field groups** (0.8.359): each `SiteField` has a `group` (`FieldGroup`), and
  `FIELD_GROUPS` maps each group to its label in display order: Security, Mobile & UX,
  SEO, Freshness, Server, Performance, Crawl. Display data only, for a list a person or
  an agent picks a field from (the Sales Domain skill; simpl_api's catalog route and the
  dashboard's signal form once they read it): show the groups in `FIELD_GROUPS` order and the fields in
  `SITE_FIELDS` order within each group. `SITE_FIELDS` keeps its order, which is the
  dataset's, so groups change no index. A reader built against an
  older simpl_core may meet a group it does not know: show its fields anyway (under
  their key or an "Other" heading), never drop or refuse them.
- **What the follow pass sees, and what waits for the sweep** (0.8.304): the follow pass
  reads finished `website_signals` jobs through `extraction_jobs_succeeded_idx`, so the
  page writer's changes (content, technologies) reach the index within minutes. A
  change only the crawl writer makes (the site row: HTTPS and TLS, security headers,
  response times, the header technologies it writes with it) or `website-risk apply`
  waits for the nightly sweep: up to a day, then the next nightly matching run.
  `follow_sites` (by `sales.website.updated_at`, which no index covers: every round read
  and sorted the whole table) was removed; drop any call to it. Since 0.8.314 each page
  reads about `page` jobs however far behind the cursor is and whatever the table's
  statistics say: 277k jobs behind, a page had read and joined all of them and hit the
  statement timeout (2026-11-06, 2026-11-07).
- **`matching_hosts(db, condition, hosts, *, today, sites=None)`**: what a parsed
  condition matches in Postgres, by the condition's own rules: most site tests in SQL
  (`SITE_TESTS`, one per operator), versions on the site row as the index builds it
  (`BUILT_ROW_TESTS`, `version_prefixes`), technology tests
  per row (version prefix by `version_prefixes`, `required` / `covers`), keywords
  through `KeywordFilter` on the rows' keyword texts. It reads the built rows itself
  when a condition needs them; pass `sites=BuiltSites.of(sources)` to reuse them.
- **`site_match(condition, rows, *, today)`** (`row_match`, 0.8.352): one site judged on
  its rows as the website facts dataset holds them (`MATCH_COLUMNS`: a day as a
  `date`): what `RowStoreWebsiteFactsSearch` and simpl_flow's opportunity matcher v2
  ask. A `SiteMatch` or None; its keyword texts are every row holding the keywords,
  each once. `match_columns(condition)` (0.8.530) is the columns it reads for one
  condition: `families` only for a families part. **`MATCH_COLUMNS` never holds
  `families`**: a reader selecting it would fail on a dataset version without the column;
  select `match_columns(condition)` per condition instead.

- **Problem families** (0.8.530, the website signals plan's S5 ported onto the query
  service; **inert in this release**): named groups of website facts kept as data in
  `sales.website_fact_families` (simpl_api `20261117130000`, seeded by `20261117130100`:
  `placeholder_or_parked` tier 1, `old_platform` and `dated` tier 2 enabled;
  `insecure`, `hidden_from_google`, `not_mobile_friendly` seeded off). A condition asks
  for them with a `families` part:

```json
{"collection": "website_facts", "families": {"names": ["old_platform", "dated"], "min": 2}}
```

  - Holds when the site row's `families` holds at least `min` (default 1, at most the
    number of names) of `names`. `SiteMatch.families` names those the site holds, in the
    part's order. `condition_key`, `condition_in_words` ("sites with all of the problem
    families old platform and dated") and the DSL's `WebsiteFacts.families` know the part
    (an unset one is left out of the dump, so `spec_hash` of a spec without families is
    unchanged). No error message names `families` to whoever writes a condition until
    the reason-to-contact rule is switched on.
  - **`check_condition` refuses a families part until the index served has a
    `families` column** (the active `website_facts` row of `row_store.dataset_configs`,
    read through `served_shape(db)`, kept a minute): "families: a signal cannot ask for
    problem families yet: the website facts index served now (version 2) does not hold
    the sites' problem families, ...". The shape is read first; while it refuses, nothing
    about families is read. From 0.8.532 version 3's spec holds `families`, so the move of
    the active row to version 3 lifts the refusal with no further release (0.8.530 alone
    cannot: its version 3 has no such column). Once served, a name that is not an enabled
    family is refused, with its spelling. The reason-to-contact rule is **not** applied by
    any save path yet.
  - `family_rules(db)` (kept a minute) loads the enabled rows into `FamilyRules`; a row
    whose members do not all parse is refused whole and logged
    (`website_facts.family_refused`). Members are conditions in the signal grammar parsed
    with `parse_condition(member, internal=True)`, which also takes the internal facts
    (`home_page_kind`, the risk `missed_security_release`, the op `is_null`); a seller's
    condition naming one is refused. `judged_rows(sources)` builds a site's rows with
    those internal facts; `FamilyRules.held(rows, today=)` the families it holds.
  - Rules as functions, for the release that switches them on: `hook_refusal(condition,
    rules)` / `hook_eligible` (one tier-1 family, or at least 2 families), and
    `is_stack_condition` / `stated_problems` (a condition that only names technologies
    needs no family, unless a family names one of them on its own as a problem, such as
    Adobe Flash in `dated`).
  - Callers of `check_condition` with a families part need SELECT on
    `sales.website_fact_families` and `row_store.dataset_configs` too.

- **The condition** (a signal filter's condition, `collection: "website_facts"`; every
  part given must hold, at least one):

```json
{"collection": "website_facts",
 "technologies": {"names": ["WordPress"], "categories": ["CMS"], "match": "any",
                  "version_prefix": "4.", "risk": "past_eol"},
 "site": [{"field": "uses_https", "op": "is", "value": false},
          {"field": "estimated_last_update", "op": "before", "value": "2022-01-01"},
          {"field": "security_headers", "op": "lacks", "value": ["strict-transport-security"]}],
 "keywords": ["ecommerce"], "keyword_match": "any"}
```

  - `technologies`: one technology row passes every test given: `names` (exact, e.g.
    `"Apache HTTP Server"`), `categories` (any of; the active rules' categories),
    `version_prefix` (whole parts: `8.1` finds 8.1 and 8.1.x, not 8.10; needs `names`),
    `risk` `past_eol` (end of life before today), the only risk. `risk: "vulnerable"`
    and `min_severity` were removed (0.8.390): most known vulnerabilities the data held
    were wrong or meaningless to a seller. `parse_condition` refuses a condition that
    still names either with `NO_VULNERABILITIES`, so a stored signal holding them is
    unreadable: the readers of stored signals (summaries, the duplicate check, the
    matcher's conversion) log or refuse it and go on. `match: "all"` needs a passing
    row for each name (each category when no names). No test for a technology being
    absent.
  - `site`: at most 20 tests (`MAX_SITE_TESTS`) on the site row, by field kind
    (`SITE_FIELDS`, `OPERATORS`): `is` (yes/no); `in`, `not_in` (one value);
    `starts_with` (`php_version`, whole parts); `has_any`, `has_all`,
    `lacks` (lists); `before`, `after` (`YYYY-MM-DD`); `lt`, `gt`, `between`
    (`[low, high]`, inclusive). A text or list value must be one some site holds now,
    of any length; a fact no site holds at all is refused as not collected yet. A list held empty (`[]`) counts as collected: it says nothing was
    found.
  - `keywords`: words or phrases (`KeywordFilter` rules) in the keyword text of the
    site's rows (a technology's name and categories; the site's clue names and
    languages). `keyword_match: "all"` is site-wide: each keyword must be somewhere on
    the site, in any of its rows, not necessarily the same one (a website signal's
    "all" needs every keyword in one chunk).
  - A fact never observed is null and fails every test, `is false` and `lacks`
    included.
  - **ICP-only.** `restrict_to_icp: false` is refused ("website facts signals search
    the ICP's companies only; remove 'restrict_to_icp' or set it to true"); `true` or
    absent is accepted (the matcher reads the key, default true). Post and website-text
    signals left unrestricted keep the best 1,024 matches by relevance score; website
    facts have no score, so an unrestricted signal would match every passing site
    (tens of thousands, possibly) on every run.
- **`check_condition(db, condition) -> list[str]`**: the one check every save path
  calls (simpl_api routes and wizard, the chat agent's re-plan, the background agent's
  proposals, an approved proposal). First the format (`parse_condition`: the grammar
  in code), then the values against the data, none of them copied into simpl_core;
  the reads it needs run at once:
  - technology names: `sales.website_technology`, index probes on every check, and
    spellings for the first 5 unknown names from one more statement (a loose index
    scan per first letter), the whole lookup under `NAMES_TIMEOUT_S` (5 s);
  - categories: the active row of `scraping.technology_rule_versions` (its open list's
    categories and its own rules' `cats`, picked out by jsonb path; a payload `format`
    other than 1 raises), kept a minute (`CATEGORIES_TTL_S`), as the extractors reload
    rules;
  - small vocabularies (`VOCABULARY_FIELDS`: TLS errors, fetch outcomes, estimate
    sources, clue lists, security headers): the distinct values
    `sales.website` holds, one scan (30 s timeout; ~2 s on 160k synthetic rows), kept
    an hour (`VOCABULARY_TTL_S`);
  - free text (`FREE_TEXT_FIELDS`: `languages`): only the named values, looked up on every check in one
    statement (a filtered read per fact, 10 s timeout; ~0.1 s on 160k synthetic rows).

  The kept lists are per database and process. Once held, a list is served past its
  time to live while one refresh runs in the background; a failed refresh logs
  `website_facts.<list>_read_failed` and the held list serves at most
  `CATEGORIES_MAX_STALE_S` (15 min) / `VOCABULARY_MAX_STALE_S` (6 h) more; after that,
  and in a cold process, a check waits for the read. Concurrent checks share one read
  and get its value or its error; a failed read caches nothing. A condition is never
  passed unchecked.

  **What every caller needs:** SELECT on `sales.website`, `sales.website_technology`
  and `scraping.technology_rule_versions`; USAGE on the schemas `sales` and `scraping`;
  and to see their rows past row level security (as owner, with BYPASSRLS, or through
  a policy). simpl_scraping grants the `scraping` schema to its own logins only (034,
  044, 065), so a `service_role` caller likely fails with "permission denied for
  schema scraping". Row level security hides rows without an error: a read that finds
  no row logs `website_facts.no_active_rules` or `website_facts.no_site_values`.

  Every problem comes back at once, each naming the part and its fix, e.g.
  `technologies.names: no site runs a technology named 'Wordpress'; did you mean
  'WordPress'?`, `site test 2 ('tls_error_kind' in): no site has 'Expired' for
  'tls_error_kind'; did you mean 'expired'?, so asking for it matches no site; values
  sites have: ...`; for `not_in` / `lacks` a value no site holds "excludes no site".
  Suggestions are worked out for at most 5 unknown values of a test. `parse_condition`
  raises `ConditionError` (`.errors`); `active_categories(db)`, `site_vocabulary(db)`
  and `free_text_values(db, asked)` give the lists themselves.
- **`RowStoreWebsiteFactsSearch(queries).matching_companies(db, condition, company_ids,
  today=None)`** -> company id -> `SiteMatch(host, technologies, keyword_texts)`;
  `matching_sites(condition, hosts)` the same by host. Companies map to hosts as for
  landing (`hosts_for_companies`, `www.` ignored); companies sharing a site share its
  match. Every row of the sites asked is read through the service (`rows`, by host,
  `HOSTS_PER_READ` hosts a walk of 10,000-row pages; only the row kind and columns the
  condition tests) and judged by `site_match`; no score. Search errors raise (a failure
  is never "nothing matched"). `hosts_per_search(condition)` is how many companies or
  hosts a caller paging them should pass at once: 4,096 without keywords, 1,024 with
  (the opportunity matcher's page size).
- **`website_facts_evidence(db, condition, matches)`** -> host -> plain JSON: `host`,
  `url`, `technologies` (10 of the matched, `MAX_TECHNOLOGIES`, the worst first:
  highest `max_severity`, then end of life passed, then by name (`worst_first`);
  each with version, categories, `eol_date`, `max_severity`, `vulnerability_count`,
  up to 10 `vulnerability_ids`, `last_seen_at`, `seen_on_url`, `evidence`; the three
  vulnerability values stay until simpl_api drops their columns, though no condition
  tests them any more),
  `technologies_matched` (how many matched), `site` (the tested facts), `keywords` (the
  texts that held them). One read per table by primary key, hosts and names sent as
  arrays: a page of any size is a few parameters. A families part (0.8.530) adds
  `families`: `names`, `min`, `held` (what the index said: `SiteMatch.families`) and
  `facts`, one entry per held family, strongest first (tier 1, then 2, then one no
  longer enabled), judged again on Postgres now (`family_facts`). For that condition the
  site row is read whole, one more read takes all the site's technology rows, and the
  family rules are read (kept a minute):

```json
"families": {"names": ["old_platform", "dated"], "min": 2, "held": ["old_platform", "dated"],
  "facts": [
    {"family": "old_platform", "label": "Old platform", "tier": 2, "holds_now": true,
     "facts": [{"kind": "technology", "technology": "PHP", "version": "7.2.34",
                "eol_date": "2022-11-28", "read_on": "2026-03-02"}]},
    {"family": "dated", "label": "Dated", "tier": 2, "holds_now": true,
     "facts": [{"kind": "site_field", "field": "copyright_year", "value": 2017,
                "read_on": "2026-11-19"}]}]}
```

  Each fact has a `kind` (`technology`, `site_field`, `keyword`) and always a `read_on`,
  the day the value was read: a version (prefix, end of life, missed security release)
  when that version was seen (`version_seen_at`); a technology named alone when it was
  last seen (`last_seen_at`); a page fact when its home page version was stored
  (`content_observed_at`); a TLS fact, `home_outcome` or `home_http_status` on the
  latest crawl run (`last_crawled_at`). **Null means the day is not known** (the crawl
  facts of the latest answered run, the news date, the estimate): never claim one.
  `SITE_FACT_DATES` maps each site fact to its column. A list fact shows only the values
  asked for that the site holds (`missing` for a `lacks` test). Not listed: an `is_null`
  test (an absence) and a crawl date compared with a cutoff. `read_on` moves with every
  crawl, so a stored match must drop it (simpl_flow does); the draft reads freshness
  when it is written. A family that no longer holds on Postgres (the index lags up to a
  day) has `holds_now: false` and no facts. Quote one fact, not all (the plan's S6).
- **Preview** (`open_website_facts_preview(db, queries=queries)`): `previewer.preview(condition,
  scope=PreviewScope.ICP, icp_id=..., limit=...)` on the ICP's companies, or
  `PreviewScope.ALL` on every site of the ledger stored at least once (one marked
  again or failing included), to estimate a signal's reach beyond its ICP (signals
  themselves are ICP-only); 1,024 a page, 6 pages at once,
  no page started after 20 s (`cut_short`, `companies_searched` / `sites_searched`).
  Matches by host then company, with their evidence; `companies_matched`,
  `sites_matched`.
- **Hosts the ledger cannot key are skipped.** A host that is not lowercase or holds
  `/` or `#` (the ledger's `website_facts_search_index_key_shape`;
  `sales.website_technology` checks nothing, `sales.website` allows `#`) is left out
  of every pass with a warning, `website_facts.scan_host_skipped` (`host`,
  `scan_pass`, `rule`), and counted in `FollowResult.skipped` / `SweepResult.skipped`.
  The cursors move past it, so the passes never stop on it. Fix the source row. An
  empty host is never read: the follow pass drops it, and the sweep never returns it.
- **`ledger_key_refusal(key)`**: the ledger's key check as a SQLAlchemy expression over
  any text column or value: `"empty"`, `"not lowercase"`, `"contains /"` or
  `"contains #"`, or null when the ledger takes it. The scanner uses it; use it rather
  than a copy wherever source hosts must match what the index can hold, e.g. a sample
  of hosts to check against the index (`WEBSITE` from `search.website_facts.tables`):
  `select(WEBSITE.c.host).where(ledger_key_refusal(WEBSITE.c.host).is_(None))`.
- `COLLECTION` (and `WEBSITE_FACTS_ALIAS`, the same value) is the `collection` a website
  facts condition holds, `"website_facts"`: a value saved signals carry and the matcher
  dispatches on, not a collection anything reads.
- The ledger and cursors are the simpl_flow ledger migration `website_facts_search_index`
  (`tests/setup_test_db.sql` holds the DDL to copy). Install `simpl-core[search]`.

### Post search (`search.posts`)

LinkedIn posts and plain reposts, one record per appearance on a page, stored in the
row store (the `sales_posts` dataset, searched through the query service). Nothing is added
to `sales.post` / `sales.post_repost` (no trigger, no index): `PostSearchScanner` reads
them and marks rows of `sales.post_search_index` (simpl_flow
`migrations/065_post_search_index.sql`) pending. `discover()` (every run) adds ids above
the cursor in `sales.post_search_scan`; `sweep()` (nightly) re-marks posts whose
record columns changed (among those whose `updated_at` moved, compared by
`sales.post_search_post_hash`, simpl_flow migration 200), reposts whose tracked columns
changed, rows whose source is gone, and records built from a changed post
(`depends_on`). `PostSearchIndexer.run_batch`
claims them, builds records (`build_record`), embeds each distinct text once
(`NebiusPostEmbedder`), commits, and settles the rows. Searches read the dataset through
the query service.

```python
# embedder at posts.QUERY_DIMS (512); queries: the query service's client accessor
search = RowStorePostSearch(queries, embed_query=embedder.embed_query)
hits = await search.search(
    vector=company_embedding,                       # or text=..., keywords=...
    filters=PostSearchFilters(company_ids=ids, is_repost=False, languages=["en", "it"]),
    one_per_company=True,
    limit=len(ids),
    min_score={"en": 0.55, "*": 0.5},               # dense-only or keyword-only
)
```

- **Without simpl_ia:** every `RowStoreSearcher` search builds simpl_ia's `SearchResult`,
  whose package needs its vector store extra. `KeywordPostSearch(queries).search(keywords=,
  limit=)` sends one `words` request, on the dataset's text columns
  (`ServedDataset.request_text_columns`, as every searcher), asking for every hit column
  but the text (`KEYWORD_HIT_COLUMNS`: up to 32 KB a row, 500 rows a search), and builds
  `PostSearchHit`s from the hits' fields (`ServedDataset.fields`,
  `PostSearchHit.from_fields`): the same hits as `PostSearch.search(keywords=...)` with
  `content` None, for a reader with none of SimpL's search stack that reads the text from
  `sales.post` (the research worker). Since 0.8.537.
- **Records:** `p:{sales.post.id}` (a quote carries its comment, a blank line and the
  quoted text) and `r:{sales.post_repost.id}` (exactly the reposted post's text, so the
  same vector). `company_id` is the page the row is on; `is_company_post` is about the
  original author; reposts are dated by their activity id.
- **The dataset's vector width is a parameter** (`posts_dataset_spec(dims=...)`); a new
  width is a new dataset version. A query is scored on the stored vectors' first
  `QUERY_DIMS` (512) dimensions, a wider query vector (a 1,024-d company embedding) cut
  to them, so no width needs embedding again.
- **Search:** the keyword half is sent as given (the analyzer is language-neutral, no
  stemming); pass `keywords` to add word forms and translations without changing the
  dense query. `one_per_company` keeps each company's best post; lists over 1,024
  companies are searched in chunks. `min_score` is refused on hybrid (fused) scores. A
  date window (`posted_after`, `posted_before`) is sent as ISO 8601 text.
- **Which companies have a matching post** (`best_per_company`): a topic (vector +
  `min_score`), keywords that must be in the text (`KeywordFilter`), or both. With
  `company_ids` the answer is complete (every listed company with such a post, and its
  best post); without, it comes from the best 1,024 of the dataset, a person's own
  posts left out (they belong to no company).

```python
hits = await search.best_per_company(
    vector=topic_vector, min_score=0.5,                     # semantic (omit for keywords only)
    keywords=KeywordFilter(["SDR", "SDRs", "account executive"], match_all=False),
    filters=PostSearchFilters(company_ids=ids, posted_after=since),
)
```

- **Keywords** are words or phrases matched on the post text the way the full-text
  index's analyzer reads it (`analyzer_words`: letter/digit runs, lowercased, accents folded):
  whole words, a phrase's words together and in order, no stemming (list "SDR" and
  "SDRs"). Hits from these searches carry `content`. Keywords that are all single words,
  matched `any`, are one keyword `best_per_owner` per list of companies (no candidate
  check); phrases or `all` take the checked path, which reads every candidate's text.

### Company passages (`search.company_passages`)

Each company's passages for a question, read the way simpl_api's feature check reads them
(moved here from `simpl_api/services/feature_check/passages.py` and `code.py`, so simpl_flow's
opportunity verifier reads the same evidence). `PassageFinder.find(question, companies,
vectors)` takes `CheckedCompany` rows (id, name, site host, and `about`: what SimpL's company
database says, which the finder does not read) and returns, per company, up to
`CLOSEST_PASSAGES` passages by meaning across website and posts, up to `KEYWORD_PASSAGES` more
holding the question's words (BM25), then its site's code lines holding the question's
markers. Searches are `best_per_owner` on the query service, `COMPANIES_PER_SEARCH` (256)
companies at a time, at most 4 in flight per process, run in a task group: the first that
fails cancels the rest, and its error (`QueryUnavailableError`, ...) is the caller's.
`embed(question)` embeds once per dataset at its `QUERY_DIMS` and, with a `CodeReader` and a
`markers` writer, writes the code markers (`write_markers(question, client)`, the caller's
model). `passage_finder(db, marker_client=None, queries=queries)` opens one on the production
datasets through `queries` (the query service's client, the caller's) and, with a marker
client, on the active `landing_markup` dataset (`ROW_STORE_URI`); without a row store it
reads text and posts only (logged `company_passages.code_off`). The code reader caches
the markup table's key index in Lance's default caches, which grow toward the whole index on a
long walk (about 0.9 GB for 360 sites). A caller short of memory passes
`passage_finder(..., code_session=lance.Session(index_cache_size_bytes=...,
metadata_cache_size_bytes=...))` (or `open_code_reader(db, session=...)`) and reads slower;
callers that pass none keep the defaults. Needs the `search` extra and `NEBIUS_API_KEY`.

### Sales datasets (`sales_datasets`) and text anonymization (`anonymization`)

What Graphia reads about the companies we hold outreach conversations with, as row
store datasets. **Nothing is anonymized here:** names, emails, phones and profile links
are as Postgres holds them, because Graphia links identities on the real data first
and anonymizes after, before what it builds goes into the graph (user decision
2026-11-05). Five are entities like their tables: columns named as there, foreign keys
kept (`lead_id`, `company_id`), only columns that serve our pipelines left out. All but
the skills are chunked and embedded (`CHUNK_SPECS`), and so are the messages: one row
per chunk of the entity's text, keyed `<id>#<chunk_no>`, each with the entity's
columns, its id column (`ID_FIELDS`), `chunk_no`, `text` (the chunk), `language`,
`vector` (Qwen3-Embedding-8B at the config's `dims`, 4096) and `embedding_model`; the
text chunked is not a column of its own.

| Kind | Table | Key | Chunked text | Left out |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `conversation_messages` | `sales_outreach.message` | `<id>#<n>` (`message_id`) | the message's own text (no quoted reply), in Graphia's parts | the whole body with quotes (one row per part, with its conversation's company and its sender: `sender_side`, `sender_user_id` or `sender_lead_id`, `sender_name`, `sender_email`) |
| `sales_leads` | `sales.lead` | `<id>#<n>` (`lead_id`) | the profile (job title, tagline, summary), in Graphia's parts; one empty chunk without | images, `origin`, `score`, `summary` |
| `sales_lead_skills` | `sales.lead_skill` | `<lead_id>\|<skill>` | — (plain, `SPECS`) | — |
| `sales_lead_experiences` | `sales.lead_experience` | `<id>#<n>` (`experience_id`, a bigint) | `description` | `raw`, `fts`, `embedded_at` |
| `sales_job_offers` | `sales.job_offer` | `<id>#<n>` (`job_offer_id`) | `description` | checker bookkeeping |
| `sales_articles` | `sales.article` | `<id>#<n>` (`article_id`) | `raw_text`, else `summary` | `scraping_status` |

`sales_leads.company_id` is the lead's resolved company (below), not the raw
`sales.lead.company_id`; skills and positions carry it too. Each chunked entity is one
Graphia source, `GRAPHIA_PREFIXES[kind] + id` (`m:`, `l:`, `e:`, `a:`, `j:`), and its
`n`-th chunk (from 1) one Graphia document: the source, then `#<n>`.

```python
store = RowStore(RowStoreConfig.from_env(), RowStoreCatalog(db))
scope = await conversation_scope(db)
datasets = {kind: store.dataset(spec()) for kind, spec in SPECS.items()}
result = await build_sales_datasets(db, datasets, scope=scope, run_id=run_id)
# The chunked kinds (needs the search extra): mark the ledger, then index. Every kind
# but the messages reads the scope (SCOPED_KINDS); the messages take scope=None.
articles = store.dataset(CHUNK_SPECS[ARTICLES_KIND](dims=4096))
await mark_sales_documents(db, ARTICLES_KIND, scope)
indexer = sales_document_indexer(  # simpl_core.sales_datasets.documents
    db, ARTICLES_KIND, scope=scope, dataset=articles, embedder=embedder,
    detect_language=detect_language,
)
await indexer.run_batch(batch_size=500, run_id=run_id)
```

- **Chunks** (`chunking.document_chunks`, positions, job offers, articles, as landing
  pages are chunked): simpl_ia's `chunk_blocks` at the landing sizes (1,200 soft, 1,800
  hard characters), each non-empty line a block, under a header (`<company> | <title>`;
  a position: its own company and title) that the embedded text puts above the chunk.
  Every line of a text is in a chunk, however long the text; an entity with no text is
  chunked from its header alone.
- **Parts** (`chunking.document_parts`, messages and lead profiles): Graphia's
  documents. A text of at most `PART_CHARS` (1,800) once stripped is one part, as
  written; a longer one is cut like a body above at 1,800/1,800. Each part is exactly
  the text Graphia extracts, so a search hit on a row is a Graphia document. Header: a
  message's company and subject, a lead's name and company.
- **Ledgers** (`LEDGERS`; simpl_flow migration 171 for positions, job offers and
  articles, 183 for messages and leads, each rebuilding version 1 of its datasets):
  `sales.article_chunk_index`, `sales.job_offer_chunk_index`,
  `sales.lead_experience_chunk_index`, `sales.lead_chunk_index`,
  `sales.conversation_message_chunk_index`, one row per entity. `mark_sales_documents`
  lists every entity with a fingerprint (`marking`) and marks the new, changed and
  gone ones; the shared `DocumentIndexer` then rewrites only entities whose texts or
  fields changed and embeds only new chunk texts. Bump `TEXT_PREP_VERSION` when the
  chunking changes: every entity is marked once and unchanged chunks keep their
  vectors. A message's fingerprint holds its conversation's message count and newest
  message, so a new message rebuilds its whole conversation (same company and people
  on every row); unchanged texts keep their vectors.
- **Searched through the query service**, each kind's dataset by its name: a hit
  (`sales_datasets.hits.HIT_SHAPES`) has the entity id to group by, `company_id`,
  `title` (`TITLE_COLUMNS`), `dated_at` (epoch seconds), `language` and each kind's
  filter ids; a query is scored at `QUERY_DIMS` (1,024). A lead is found by name through
  its name column and its text (the leads' text columns).
- **Every row has a company** (`companies`). A lead: their most current experience
  (`LeadRepository.current_experience_company_ids`), else their user-lead connections;
  never `sales.lead.company_id`. A conversation: its `primary_company_id`, else the
  owner's connection with its leads, any connection with them, their most current
  experience. A lead or conversation none of these gives a company to cannot be
  resolved and stays out of every dataset. Columns: `company_id` on
  messages, leads, skills, job offers and articles; `lead_company_id` on positions
  (beside the position's own `company_id`, which may be empty).
- **Scope (which rows):** the resolvable leads of every conversation with a message,
  their skills and positions; the job offers (posted, else stored, in the last 365
  days) and articles of those leads' companies, of every company their connections
  give, and of the conversations' `primary_company_id`.
- **Texts as written:** message bodies (HTML rendered to plain text), profiles,
  descriptions and articles keep every name, email, phone and link. Treat the datasets
  as personal data; anonymize downstream (`simpl_core.anonymization`) before anything
  leaves Graphia's identity linking.
- **Dates:** every row but a skill has `dated_at` and `dated_at_basis`: the source's
  real time when it has a clear one (`sent`, `posted`, `published` for a full ISO
  article date, `started`), else `stored` (Postgres `created_at`).
- **Every company's articles** (0.8.346): `marking.article_listing` lists them by id for
  a `ListingWalk` (any age; an article with no company left out), and
  `ArticlesKind` with no scope builds them, as for job offers (simpl_flow's
  `sales_articles` version 2 fill).
- **Tables copied as they are** (`TABLE_COPIES`, 0.8.346, for matcher v2): `locations`
  (`geolocation.locations`), `company_headcount`, `company_funding` and
  `company_crustdata`, one dataset row per source row keyed by its id, no vector. A
  `CopiedTable` is a fingerprint walk by key (`rows_after`) or a change cursor with a key
  sweep (`changed_after`, `keys_after`, `rows_of`); `TableCopier(db, dataset, table,
  cursors=ScanCursors(db, "sales.table_copy_scan"), settings=...)` runs a pass
  (`await copier.run(deadline=...)`, a `CopyRun` of what it read and wrote), writing
  through `row_store.RowCopy` (only changed rows, tombstones for keys gone).
  `COPY_SETTINGS` names the settings: `page_size`, `read_rows`, `commit_rows`,
  `pause_s`, `overlap_minutes`, `walk_rows`.
- **Runs:** skills compare a fingerprint of every field; the chunked kinds go through
  their ledgers (above). Only rows that changed are rewritten. What left Postgres or
  the scope becomes a tombstone. An unreadable message body or a conversation with no
  company is skipped and logged (`sales_datasets.messages_left_out`). `ROW_VERSION` is
  the skills' `row_version` and part of the chunked kinds' fingerprint: bump it when
  what a row holds changes.

### Opportunity matcher specs (`opportunity_matcher`)

The DSL matcher v2 reads (ICP and signal specs, stored in simpl_flow's
`opportunity.icp_spec` / `opportunity.signal_spec`), and the conversion of today's
definitions into it. Moved from simpl_flow `pipelines/opportunities_v2/dsl.py`
(0.8.343); simpl_flow's evaluator still holds its own copy until it imports this one.

- `opportunity_matcher.dsl`: `IcpSpec`, `SignalSpec`, the `Predicate` tree (`company`,
  `similar`, `text`, `fact`, `entity`, `job_offers`, `rows`, `website_facts`,
  `all`/`any`/`not`, `min_matches`, `within_days`), `spec_hash`. `website_facts`
  (0.8.352) holds a website facts condition in its own grammar (`technologies`,
  `site`, `keywords`, `keyword_match`), checked by
  `search.website_facts.parse_condition`; `.parsed()` gives the parsed condition. New in core: `Company.location` (by `geolocation`
  names), `size_min`/`size_max` (the company's own bracket), `linkedin_size`,
  `linkedin_urls`, `unknown`, `description_regex` (RE2); `IcpSpec.include_ids`
  (candidates optional when given); `job_offers` with a row-level `where`
  (`JobCondition`: `roles`, `words`, `about`, `like`, `regex`, `seniority`,
  `employment_types`, `countries`/`regions`/`provinces`/`cities`, `active`, `unknown`,
  `all`/`any`/`not`), `days`, `older_than_days`, `min_offers`.
- `opportunity_matcher.word_queries`: the `words` syntax (words, `"phrases"`, `OR`,
  `-not`, `prefix*`, parentheses), `from_websearch` / `from_tsquery` (Postgres'
  readings under `simple`), `matches(query, text_words(text))`: whole words, case and
  accents ignored, no stemming.
- `opportunity_matcher.legacy_specs`: `read_legacy_icps(db)` (active ICPs, enabled
  filters and signals) and `convert_icp(icp)` -> `IcpConversion` (spec or `Refusal`,
  each signal's `SignalConversion`; keys `legacy_signal_<hex>`). An ICP filter it cannot
  read refuses the ICP; a signal it cannot read is refused alone.
- `python -m simpl_core opportunity_specs_report [--json PATH] [--connection-mode
  session]`: what converts and why the rest does not. Read-only.

Does NOT: write specs (simpl_flow's matcher run does, through its spec store) or
evaluate predicates (simpl_flow `opportunities_v2/evaluate.py`). An article signal with
no `embedded_text` is refused (matcher v2 embeds the text, never a stored vector).

### Search over Graphia's sources (`search.graphia_sources`)

`open_source_search(nebius_api_key=..., queries=queries)` gives a `SourceSearch` over
seven datasets through the query service (`queries`, the caller's client): the five
chunked sales kinds', the posts' and the landing pages'. `search(text,
filters=SourceSearchFilters(kinds, company_ids, hosts, languages), per_kind, limit)`
embeds the text once per kind with its query instruction and at its `QUERY_DIMS`, runs
each kind hybrid (dense and BM25) grouped by source, and fuses the kinds by RRF on their
ranks. The kinds are searched at once in a task group: the first failure cancels the
rest and is raised itself. Each `SourceHit` names its Graphia `source` and `document` (a landing
chunk: its page's first document, `landing_document_key(host, url)`), the chunk's row
`key`, `title`, `content`, `company_id` or `host`, `dated_at` and its `rank`. Its
`score` only encodes that rank; what was measured is `similarity` (the exact cosine
between the query and the chunk shown, from its stored vector; None if the row left
the dataset), `dense_rank` and `dense_score` (the source's place in the semantic half
and its closest chunk's exact cosine, maybe another chunk), and `keyword_rank` and
`keyword_score` (BM25, no fixed scale).
Cosines compare well within a kind, only roughly across kinds.
`SourceSearch.resolve_leads(name)` finds leads by name in the lead profiles' dataset;
`resolve_companies(db, text)` the companies entity resolution answers for a URL, a
domain, a LinkedIn company URL or a name (`ResolutionService.resolve` under the live
switches, then `ResolutionService.candidates` for a name: any letter case, legal forms,
letters run together, LinkedIn pages), `matched_by` the answer's method (`hard_key`,
`rule`...) or how the lookup found the row (`name`, `joined`, `legal_form`, `page`);
`resolve_lead_by_linkedin(db, url)` a lead by profile URL, whatever host, trailing slash,
query or case it is pasted with (`lead_linkedin_url_variants`). The last two read
Postgres only. `resolve(db, search, text)` is what one search box needs, as a
`Resolution(companies, leads)`: a profile URL gives a lead, another LinkedIn URL or a URL
with a scheme a company, a word that looks like a domain (`anna.rossi`) both a site and a
name lookup, any other text a company name and the lead name search.

`simpl_core.anonymization` is the anonymizer on its own, for any text whose people are
known, for the step after identity linking:
`TextAnonymizer([Person("[LEAD_1]", ("Example Name",))], keep=("Example Co",))`. One
way only; it keeps no map back to the values. The sales datasets do not call it.

### Graphia read model (`graphia`)

Reads what simpl_flow's Graphia pipeline extracted (its log tables in the row store,
`kg_*__v1`) back as a small graph, for an explorer. Read-only; needs the `row-store`
extra, and `identifiers` the `search` extra. The ontology model lives here too
(`graphia.ontology`: `parse_ontology`, `Ontology`, `EdgeRuleDef`...; its fingerprint is
the sha256 of the document's bytes); simpl_flow imports it from here.

```python
import lance

from simpl_core.graphia.document import document_passage
from simpl_core.graphia.identifiers import SourceIdentifiers
from simpl_core.graphia.log import LogReads
from simpl_core.graphia.neighbourhood import NeighbourhoodLimits, neighbourhood
from simpl_core.graphia.ontology_store import load_ontologies

ontologies = await load_ontologies(db)  # {fingerprint: Ontology}, every registered version
session = lance.Session()               # one for the process's life
reads = LogReads(config, session=session)
identifiers = SourceIdentifiers(config, db, session=session)
await reads.prewarm()                   # ~1 GB of index into the session, once
await identifiers.prewarm()

await reads.refresh(300)                # before a request: newer versions, same session
found = await identifiers.of_company(company_id, per_kind=10)
graph = await neighbourhood(
    reads,
    ontologies,
    variant_name="graphia-v2-nuextract-joint",
    sources=found.sources,          # or doc_keys=[...]
    limits=NeighbourhoodLimits(),   # 50 seed docs, 25 units, 10 facts a unit, hub at 500 rows
    locations=True,
)
passage = await document_passage(reads, ontologies, doc_key, variant_name=...)
```

- **Current run.** A document is read through its variant's latest `extracted`,
  `served` run that no whole-run retraction takes back (`log.latest_runs`, highest
  `(finished_at, extraction_run)`); rows of other runs are left out.
- **Source and doc keys** (`graphia.source_keys`): `m:` message, `l:` lead profile,
  `e:` position, `a:` article, `j:` job offer, `p:` post, `h:` site; a source's
  documents are the doc keys in `[source, source + "$")` (`m:<id>`, `m:<id>#2`...).
- **Identifiers** (`SourceIdentifiers`): a company id to its job offers, articles,
  positions (`company_id` or `lead_company_id`), messages, leads, posts and site host;
  a lead id to its profile, positions, sent messages and posts. Newest first by
  `dated_at` (posts `posted_at`), at most `per_kind` each, `truncated` naming the kinds
  cut. At most `READ_LIMIT` (5,000) rows are read a kind; `capped` names the kinds
  whose read stopped there, whose newest sources may be missing. A chunk key `<id>#<n>`
  counts as its entity. Names to ids are not resolved here.
- **Graph** (`models.Graph`): `nodes` are units (what the extraction names, before
  identity), named by the author's name or the text naming them most often; `facts`
  with their roles (a unit, a literal, a choice or plain text); `edges` are the
  relations the forms' edge rules derive (`relations.fact_relations`): unit endpoints
  only, every source to every target but itself, `when`/`unless` on the fact's choices,
  `properties` copied. `endpoints_without_unit` counts the endpoints left out. The
  hop follows only units that cross authors (`units.crosses_authors`: any type but an
  organization or a person, or an author keyed `company:`/`lead:`/`user:`); a unit
  with more than `hub_rows` role rows is a hub, flagged and not expanded. The seeds'
  runs are read up to `seed_run_rows` (5,000) rows (`truncated.seed_runs` when cut: a
  seed document may be missing or shown through an older run). `truncated` says what
  each cap left out; `timings_ms` each step's time. No sources and no documents: an
  empty graph.
- **Offsets** are code points into the document's `model_text` (a JavaScript client
  converts from UTF-16 before slicing).
- **Serving** (`LogReads`, `SourceIdentifiers`): without a `session` every table opens
  cold and loads its indexes for that request alone (a graph took 18 to 44 s on prod),
  so keep that for tests and scripts. A serving process holds both on one
  `lance.Session` for its life, calls `prewarm()` at start (about 14 s and 1 GB for
  the log from a workstation) and `await refresh(max_age_s)` before each request:
  graphs on documents never read before then took 2.6 to 3.7 s from a workstation
  (10/11/2026). The log's tables open as one set, runs first, so a request never sees
  a run whose facts its tables lack.
- **Ontologies** (`ontology_store.load_ontologies(db)`): every version in
  `knowledge_graph.ontology_version` (simpl_flow's registry writes it), retired ones
  included, by fingerprint; a document that does not hash to its stored fingerprint is
  refused. A run whose fingerprint is not loaded raises `LookupError`
  (`log.ontology_of`): reload after a new version is registered.
- **Steps** (`steps`): what identity and promotion make of a graph, for an explorer.
  `read_identity(db, graph.nodes, cases=None)` reads each unit's entity
  (`er_assignment`), follows redirects to the canonical entity, and reads its registry
  anchor (`company:<id>` / `lead:<id>`); the link state is the case of the unit's mention
  (`CaseStore.of_mention_ids`): `link_method`, `link_p`, `link_anchor` from its answer
  (none while it has none or is `unknown`), `open_review` when it is open with rows still
  possible, `ruled_out` the rows an `absent` refused. Pass the explorer's warm
  `CaseStore(store, shards=LANES, shard=None)` as `cases`; without it a store is opened on
  `ROW_STORE_URI` for the call (cold), and with no row store named the link state is
  empty. Before the cases exist it is empty too. `read_promotion(db)` reads the newest `promotion_config` (as
  `promotion_rules.PromotionRules`, also simpl_flow's) and the newest `done` or
  `measured` run's report (a measurement that wrote no golden row, simpl_flow 187). `graph_steps(graph, identity, promotion)` gives `GraphSteps`: `extracted`
  counts, `identity` (units folded into entities: per entity type units, entities,
  merged, anchored, unplaced, pending; per relation type edges, folded, loops; the
  merge groups), and `promotion` (the bars, and each entity and relation type shown or
  why not: `type_off`, `type_unmeasured`, `type_below_precision`, or `not_measured`
  when no run measured it; each with its precision and the 95% lower bound the bar
  reads, `precision_lower`). `units` maps each unit to its node after identity
  (`node_id`: the canonical entity, else the unit itself).

  ```python
  from simpl_core.graphia.steps import graph_steps, read_identity, read_promotion

  steps = graph_steps(graph, await read_identity(db, graph.nodes), await read_promotion(db))
  ```

### Operator notifications

`simpl_core.observability.TrackerOperatorNotifier` delivers `OperatorEvent`s to
dedicated Discord webhook URLs through tracker (>= 0.5.0 reads URLs, not webhook
blocks) and returns whether delivery succeeded, which an outbox needs before marking
an incident delivered. Context passes `redact_context` first, and the event's
`dedup_key` is tracker's fingerprint. `register_config_fallback_notifier` routes
agent-framework config fallback to operators, and `unavailable_prefect_blocks` checks
tracker JSON config blocks at startup without sending anything. Importing the module loads neither tracker, Prefect nor the agent framework.

**The data lake's alerts go to #data-lake.** `data_lake_alert(level, message, *, event,
dedup_key=None, error=None, **context)` (and `data_lake_alert_async` for async code,
since delivery is a blocking HTTP call) is the one way the row store, the search
indexers, their maintenance, the knowledge graph extractor and the opportunity matchers
alert. Levels are `"error"` and `"warning"` only: success and progress never go there.
It delivers synchronously to `DISCORD_DATA_LAKE_WEBHOOK_URL` (read on every call) and,
when that is unset or Discord does not take the message, falls back to the process's
#error or #warning through tracker, so an alert is not lost. It logs each alert once
(`data_lake_event`, `data_lake_channel`, the traceback of an exception) and never
raises. A delivered exception is marked announced, so tracker's default policy does not
post it again to #warning. Pass a `dedup_key` per problem (a document, a job): repeats
edit one message, within one process. A problem many runs raise (each Cloud Run job a
process that starts empty) also passes `shared_dedup=<database URL or DatabaseManager>`.
Its repeats are then counted in `agent_framework.alert_fingerprints` by every process
(`PostgresDedupStore`, via `simpl_core.agent_framework.alerting.shared_alert_dedup_store`),
so the problem is one message, edited as it recurs. When the store cannot be had, the
alert still goes out, deduplicated in process. `TrackerOperatorNotifier(...,
dedup_store=...)` takes any tracker `DedupStore` the same way.

## Decks in a company's look (`decks`)

Needs the `decks` extra (python-pptx, pillow, lxml, resvg-py). A deck is slides on fixed
layouts (`decks.deck.LAYOUTS`: cover, statement, quote, names, rows, image, hero, figures,
chart, table, steps, flow, closing and more), written as PowerPoint and typeset to PDF by
DocRaptor, in the kit it is given. Photographs of a company's work have their own layouts:
`case` (the picture fills the right of the slide to its edges, a label, a title of up to
three lines and rows or a body on the left), `gallery` (two or three pictures cut to one
frame, a caption under each) and `image` (full-bleed or under a title). A caption on a
photograph sits on a dark band. Pictures are passed to `make_deck(pictures=...)` by the
names the slides use; `deck.picture_refs(slides)` lists the ones to read.
A deck looks like the company that presents it, as its site does (Nico chose this,
2026-11-06): a phrase of a title written `*like this*` is drawn in the brand's colour; a
`cover` may lie on a picture of its work (`image`), in colour or in black and white as the
site shows its photographs (`BrandStyle.photo_tone`: `natural`, `mono`); the brand's colour
shows on labels, marks and marked phrases wherever it stands out by
`brand.ACCENT_SHOWS` (2.5) and gives way to the ink where it does not (a pale yellow).
A company whose work is not something people see (software, a service) gets no photographs;
its cover may carry the brand's own art instead (`cover.art`: a render or shape from its
site's hero, such as SimpL's carbon shards, kept whole beside the words, never veiled). The
picture reader keeps such art as a PNG with its transparency (`PictureFound.cutout`, a cut-out
at least `pictures.ART_MIN_SIDE` on its short side; smaller cut-outs are logos and marks), and
knows a picture served through an image proxy (`/_next/image?url=...`) by its file.
`decks.pictures.read_pictures(site, http=...)` reads a company's photographs from its own site
(the home page and the pages it links to, plain HTTP, no Firecrawl credit): logos, marks,
thumbnails and other sizes of the same upload are left out, each kept picture is a JPEG at
most 1600 px long with its page, title, headings, alt text and caption.
`tag_pictures(found, senses=..., company=...)` has a vision model (the same `senses.ask`
interface `read.complete` uses) say what each shows, its project and line of work, and how
well it carries a slide (`full_bleed`, `under_title`, `weak`); `TaggedPicture.entry(url)` is
what a product keeps once it has stored the JPEG. Generic: nothing here knows any company. Each company's
brand (logo, colours, face, how its pages draw) is kept where its product keeps it and
passed in, or read from its site; a deck is always in the look of the company that presents it.

```python
from simpl_core.decks.brand import BrandKit
from simpl_core.decks.make import make_deck

look = BrandKit.model_validate(stored_brand)        # the customer's kept brand
made = await make_deck(slides, look=look, logo=logo_bytes, http=http,
                       organisation="Acme · Rossi", title="Acme per Rossi", lang="it",
                       docraptor_key=docraptor_key)
made.pptx, made.pdf, made.pdf_is_test, made.notes
```

- `brand.kit_from_brand` turns a `BrandKit` into the engine's `kit.DocumentKit`: colour
  roles that hold their contrast, the logo (SVG or not) drawn for the paper and the dark
  slides at its own proportions, the face fetched from Google Fonts (Poppins, which
  ships, stands in when it cannot be).
- `make_deck` raises `deck.DeckRefused` naming every slide that does not fit (fix them all
  and call again), `brand.BrandUnusable` for a brand that cannot be drawn, and
  `typeset.RenderFailed` / `OverDocumentLimit` from DocRaptor. Without a DocRaptor key the
  PDF is a watermarked test document (`pdf_is_test`): never send it.
- `make.deck_shape(slides)` says how many slides are words only and which show something,
  for the maker to weigh.
- A company's look from its site: `read.complete(BrandKit(site="acme.it"), firecrawl=SiteReader(key),
  senses=None, http=http)` returns the kit, the logo's bytes and notes (one Firecrawl credit,
  cached 6 hours). It runs `measure.js` in Firecrawl's browser and reads colours by painted area,
  the logo's own colour, faces from Google Fonts, cards, corners, labels and the hero. Fields the
  kit names win. `senses` (a vision check) and `voice_reader` are optional; without `senses`
  no screenshot is asked for or read. A logo is kept only when it is a picture a deck can draw
  (`brand.check_logo`); otherwise the read has none and says so in the notes.
  `brand.logo_png(data)` turns a logo read from a site into a PNG to keep (an SVG can carry
  scripts) and raises `BrandUnusable` for anything that is not a picture.
- Every picture is refused above `brand.PICTURE_MAX_PIXELS` (40 million) from its header,
  before it is decoded, and an SVG before it is drawn. `brand.fetch_bytes` reads only https
  addresses on public hosts, every redirect checked again, and stops at its size cap.
  `site.FirecrawlError.status` is Firecrawl's HTTP status (`site.OUT_OF_CREDITS`, 402: the
  account has no credits left).

## Who takes the meetings: team hand-off

`simpl_core.services.account_coordination.TeamHandoffService(session)` writes the team
table (`sales.responsibility_assignment`) from two kinds of intent, the same for every
customer (migrations/20261010b_team_handoff.sql):
- **A member's own hand-off**, `sales.user.handoff_to_user_id`: whatever is theirs to take
  goes to that colleague, who may hand it on. A sales identity (role `sales_identity`,
  `SALES_IDENTITY_ROLE`) always has one and never owns a meeting; anyone else may.
- **A team rule**, `sales.responsibility.owner_user_id` (`ResponsibilityDefinition.owner_user_id`):
  one member takes that responsibility for everyone. Rules come first.
- **The table is their projection** (`handoff.project`): for a ruled key everyone hands
  off to its taker; otherwise each member's own taker owns it. An identity nobody can take
  for gets no row, so it books nothing while colleagues own `calendar_booking`. Every rule
  carries `calendar_booking`.
- **Call cover** (`sales.call_cover`, migrations/20261104_call_cover.sql,
  `account_coordination.cover`): who takes a member's calls when that member is busy at the
  time the lead agreed (SimpL: Nicolò covers Davide). `sync` projects
  it as one more responsibility per `calendar_booking` rule and cover,
  `cover_key(rule, cover)` = `<rule>__cover_<12 hex of the cover id>`, owned by the cover
  (`owner_user_id`), handed to them by every member whose taker of the rule they cover. The
  authority rule, walks and `redirect_targets` read it like any other key; `snapshot`
  never lists cover keys as rules (`is_cover_key`, `cover_base`) and gives each member
  `cover_user_ids`. Write with `set_member_covers(customer_id=…, user_id=…,
  cover_user_ids=[…], actor_user_id=…)` (`invalid_handoff` for an identity, oneself, a
  non-member or a repeat); `save_rule`/`delete_rule` refuse a cover key (`not_found`).
  simpl-outreach `invite_slot_free` sends a walk to the cover key only when the usual
  taker is busy and the cover free at an accepted time.
- **Writes:** `set_member_handoff(customer_id=…, user_id=…, handoff_to_user_id=…,
  actor_user_id=…)`, `save_rule(customer_id=…, key=None|key, description=…, owner_user_id=…,
  actor_user_id=…)` (a new key is a slug of the description), `delete_rule(...)`, and
  `sync(customer_id, actor_user_id=…)` after any change of role, activity or customer.
  Each saves through `ResponsibilityPolicyRepository.save` (a narrowed cell bumps its
  revision) and then runs `reconcile_policy`. The caller commits.
- **Refusals** (`CoordinationError`): `identity_needs_handoff`; `invalid_handoff` (a
  colleague who is inactive, another customer's, an identity, or who hands back along the
  way); `invalid_policy`; `not_found`.
- **First table:** a customer gets one the first time someone hands off (or is an
  identity), or a rule is added: it starts with the catch-all `meetings`
  (`DEFAULT_RESPONSIBILITY`). Nobody handing off: no table, and walks stay on the
  ordinary path. A team with no active member (billing paused it) is left as it is.
- **Safety net:** the `trg_user_fill_responsibilities` trigger gives a member who joins or
  comes back to a customer with a table the rows an ordinary member has (owns, or hands
  off to the rule's taker). It only adds rows and skips identities and personal hand-offs.
- **Reads:** `snapshot(customer_id)` returns the members (own choice, resolved
  `taker_user_id`, `needs_colleague`), the rules and the revision;
  `colleagues_for(user_id)` lists who someone may hand off to; `colleague_by_email(...)`.

`simpl_core.services.onboarding_choices` is what an onboarding parks before the person's
`sales.user` row exists: `with_handoff(metadata, to_user_id=…|to_email=…)` (the colleague;
by address for an invitee of the same setup) and `with_connection(metadata, slug=…,
external_account_id=…)` (a mailbox or calendar connected while onboarding). The finalize
calls `apply_onboarding_choices(session, user_id=…, customer_id=…, role=…, metadata=…)`
once the row exists: it writes the hand-off, syncs the team table, attaches the
connections (`integration.user_connection`) and promotes a mailbox's owner to the email
stage (`simpl_core.services.email_stage_promotion`). Each part runs in a savepoint and
never fails the finalize; what could not be applied is in `AppliedChoices.problems`.

`simpl_core.services.onboarding_member` is how that person becomes a working member,
shared by simpl_flow's finalize (every onboarding) and simpl_api (a member joining a team
that already exists is activated when they press Finish, without the finalize container).
Both run, in one transaction: `mark_customer_finalized` (the customer's `onboarding_state`,
`onboarding_completed_at`, `metadata.finalized_*`), `upsert_member` (the `sales.user` row;
a new row keeps the database defaults, the login email is passed in), `assign_icp` per ICP
(`member_icp_picks(flow_type, metadata, team_icps(...))`: a salesperson or identity gets the
invite's `icp_assignments`, or every active ICP when it names none; an admin or target
manager only the ICPs its invite names), then
`apply_onboarding_choices`, then `start_autonomous` (autonomous outreach prefs; no mailbox
connected puts a person on the default stage onto `Only LinkedIn`). A step every
activation needs goes there, so both callers get it.

`simpl_core.services.email_stage_promotion.promote_user_to_email_stage(session, user_id)`
runs wherever an email connection becomes active (simpl_api's Composio and IMAP connects,
`apply_onboarding_choices`). It moves the seller to `established` when their stage sends no
email (`action_limit` email row at 0 or missing) and no person chose it: no stage row,
`new`, or a stage with `assigned_by` NULL such as onboarding's `Only LinkedIn`. A stage a
person picked (`assigned_by` set by the dashboard or guided prefs) and a stage that already
sends email are kept. `established` only has to allow one email a day
(`MIN_EMAIL_DAILY_LIMIT = 1`); volume is the claim's job (`services/email/send_pacing`). It
runs in a savepoint and never fails the connection: a missing target is an error log and
`None`. It changes the stage only, not `outreach_prefs.auto_approval`, so a seller whose
auto-approval lists no `email` still approves each email.

## SimpL identities lent to a customer: the work customer

A colleague decides a seller's drafts (2026-10-28): `approvable_by(user_id)` in `repositories/outreach/journey.py` is `owned_by` OR a colleague's own connection (never one stamped with a loan) whose seller is in `sales.approvable_sellers(user_id)` (simpl_api migration `20261028150000`: owners and ICP managers for the team by default, the owner's setting otherwise; never a billing_admin or sales_identity; same current customer). Or a connection passed to `user_id` for approval (`sales.approval_passed_connections(user_id)`, simpl_api migration `20261102120000`: an open `sales.lead_approval_pass`, while the colleague is of its customer; 2026-11-02). Only the decision paths use it, through `JourneyLogRepository.load_decidable_for_user_locked` (`DecidableRow(row, seller_user_id, as_colleague)`, row locked in the caller's transaction); a colleague is refused with `ValueError` `already_decided: <STATUS>` once the row left `PENDING_APPROVAL` and `colleague_not_allowed: <verb>/<channel>` outside `COLLEAGUE_DECIDABLE_KINDS` (LinkedIn connect, LinkedIn message, email, call invite: simpl_api books a colleague's invite as the seller, in the seller's calendar; a call the seller makes is refused). The draft's file too (Raff 2026-11-04): whoever decides a draft attaches, replaces and removes its file through the same loader (simpl_api `conversation_attachment_service`), a colleague only while it is `PENDING_APPROVAL`; the file stays the seller's (`conversation_attachment.owner_user_id` = the connection's `user_id`, `uploaded_by_user_id` = who attached it), since the email sender (`resolve_journey_email_attachments`) and the extension accept only the sending account's files. A draft's files are `payload.attachment_ids` in the order the lead receives them, with `payload.attachment_id` mirroring the first (or null) for readers that only ask whether a file is there and for writers that know one file (a deck simpl_outreach stamps); read them with `draft_attachment_ids(payload)` and write them with `attachment_ids_update(ids)` (`repositories/outreach/draft_attachments.py`). The answer to a question card too (Raff 2026-11-11): `JourneyLogRepository.load_answerable_for_user_locked` locks a `request_human_review` row on the same `approvable_by` connections and returns a `DecidableRow`; for a colleague the card must pass `colleague_questions.assert_colleague_may_answer(payload)` (`ValueError` `colleague_not_allowed: <why>` for a row that is not a question, a `review_type` in `SELLER_ONLY_REVIEW_TYPES` — `call_recap`, `call_outcome_proposal`, `not_in_target` — or a card offering any of `SELLER_ONLY_OPTION_IDS`: Meeting Booked, a meeting link, Close the conversation, Not in target, Not interested, the call outcomes, a drafted question sent at once); `colleague_may_answer(payload)` is the same rule for a list. The status is the caller's to judge under the lock (simpl_api answers a second answer, the seller's included, with who gave the first). Everything else (manual sends and their confirmations) stays on `owned_by`. A colleague's edit changes only the text: `assert_colleague_may_edit(fields)` raises `ValueError("colleague_not_allowed: only the seller changes <fields>")` for any field outside `COLLEAGUE_EDITABLE_FIELDS` (`message`, `content`, `email_body`, `subject`, `email_subject`, `invite_call.summary`, `invite_call.description`), and `colleague_version_fields(payload, version_index)` names what a version switch would change (text, `DELIVERY_FIELDS`, a version's own `action_type`/`channel`, `invite_call.<key>`, `call_data` off an invite) so the same rule judges it; the API calls both. "Approved by a colleague" is `approved_by_a_colleague()` (a predicate for a select joining `JourneyLog` to its `UserLeadConnection`): not a loan's connection, not a chat message (`created_by = seller_chat`), `payload.authority.approved_by` another person of the owner's current customer, and that person's latest `user.action.approved` on the row has `via = 'app'` (Managed, admin bulk/override and the Agent API are not colleagues). The seller takes a send a colleague approved back before it leaves: `JourneyLogRepository.take_back_approval(journey_log_id, owner_user_id)` (own connection only; `approved_by_a_colleague()`; row locked, `PENDING` only, else `ValueError` `already_sending: <STATUS>`) moves it to `PENDING_APPROVAL` as a lapsed approval is moved (`SEND_HOLD_TAKEN_BACK`: `payload.send_hold` `{reason: taken_back, held_at, held_by, approved_at, approved_by}`, `authority.block_reason`, the approval keys removed), commits nothing and returns `TakeBack(row, previous_status, approved_by, approved_at, taken)` (`taken=False`: already back with the owner, unchanged); the caller writes the event (simpl_api `user.action.taken_back`). `set_pending_with_version` drops a `taken_back` `send_hold` (and its `block_reason`) when the row is approved again; other holds stay. `sales_domain/colleague_acts.py`: a colleague's edits and decisions are not the seller's in message calibration and user statements.

A SimpL sales identity can be lent to a pay-per-call customer (`sales.identity_loan`,
model `IdentityLoan`; DDL in simpl_api `20261016100000_identity_loan`). Each connection it
opens for the loan's ICP is stamped by the database (`UserLeadConnection.identity_loan_id`,
`trg_ulc_identity_loan_stamp`, never written by callers except
`get_or_create_ulc_for_lead(identity_loan_id=…)` for a referral of a stamped thread) and works
for the loan's customer for the rest of its life, after the loan ends too.
- **One definition:** the *work customer* is `coalesce(loan.customer_id, user.customer_id)`:
  SQL `sales.connection_customer(ulc_id)` / `sales.work_connections(customer_ids)`, Python
  `UserLeadConnectionRepository.work_customer_id(ulc_id)`.
- **Read on the work customer:** account coordination (`_source` returns `customer_id` =
  work, `home_customer_id`, `identity_loan`; the identity may act on a customer's objective
  it was lent to; owners and targets are members), the colleague lookups
  (`find_colleague_ulc_by_tokens` / `_by_name`, `find_lead_ulc_by_email`,
  `find_existing_emails_in_customer`), `seller_contact_reach` (an identity's row on a lead it
  works for the seller's customer), `LeadHandoffRepository.company_handoff_target`
  (`account_handoff.customer_id`), `LeadTwinMerger`, and `evaluate_dispatch_authority`
  (`migrations/20261016_identity_loan_dispatch_authority.sql`). `context_for_ulc` returns no
  remembered answers when work ≠ home.
- **`ConversationReadService.fetch_for_ulc(…, colleague_scope="home"|"work")`:** a walk passes
  `work` (the customer's sellers are the thread's colleagues); the app keeps `home`.
- **Team table:** `TeamHandoffService.sync(C)` projects every identity ever lent to C as a
  `hands_off` row (C's rules win, else the latest loan's `handoff_to_user_id`);
  `HandoffSyncResult.unrouted_identity_ids` names those nobody takes calls for (a loan writer
  refuses `loan_without_route`). `validate_loan_handoff(C, identity, target)` (codes
  `handoff_required`, `invalid_handoff`, `not_an_identity`), `lent_identities(C)`,
  `customers_lent_to(identity)`, `sync_identity(identity, actor)` (home + every borrower).
  `snapshot`/`members` never list a lent identity.
- **Notifications:** what an identity lent now would be told goes to whom she hands off to at
  the borrower, in its workspace. `services.notifications.lent_identity.open_loan_takers(conn,
  identity_ids) -> {identity: LentIdentityTaker}` (asyncpg; `customer_id`, `customer_name`,
  `taker_user_id`, `taker_role`): `sales.meeting_booking_seller` on a connection stamped with
  the open loan (the borrower's own rule, else the loan's hand-off), the loan's hand-off when
  none is stamped yet. `GovernedUserNotificationCommand.dispatch` redirects a notification
  addressed to her there before every gate (role, quota and audit are the taker's and the
  borrower's; `caller_jsonb.lent_identity_user_id` names her) only when it is about the
  loan's work (`is_loan_work`): the caller's workspace is the borrower's, `metadata.icp_id`
  is the loan's ICP, or `metadata.ulc_id` is a connection stamped with the loan. A SimpL ICP,
  a SimpL thread, nothing named or an ended loan: hers, as before.
- **Refusals:** `LeadHandoffService.hand_over` raises `ValueError` across work customers;
  `process_forward` answers `forward_lead_lent` for a lead the forwarder works for another
  customer; `LeadMergeService` raises `MergeConflictError` when one user's two connections
  carry different loans.
- **Moving an identity off an ICP:** `services.icp_pipeline.release_open_pipeline(conn, *,
  user_id, icp_id, reason, change_ref, dry_run=False) -> PipelineRelease` (asyncpg, the caller
  owns the transaction). Companies whose stage over all the user's connections there is open
  and that nothing binds: the person connections on the ICP are unclaimed and marked
  (`user_lead_connection.released_at`, written by `release_connections`; no stage change, no history
  row; the placeholder stays claimed), their `PENDING`/`PENDING_APPROVAL` rows cancelled,
  `RUNNING` rows get `cancel_requested_at`, unclaimed wake-ups cancelled; the user's live
  autopilot work units on the ICP expire. `dry_run` counts without locking or writing;
  `PipelineRelease.as_dict()` is the admin move's `release` shape. A first contact planned
  afterwards on such a connection is cancelled by the dispatch authority
  (`identity_released`: an identity ever lent, a connection marked released, still
  unclaimed, never contacted and not on an ICP the identity is assigned to now). A
  connection made after the move (a referral on a thread that continues, an inbound
  person) carries no mark and is worked.
- **Releasing given connections:** `services.icp_pipeline.release_connections(conn,
  connection_ids, *, reason, change_ref, cancelled_by=RELEASE_CANCELLED_BY) -> ConnectionRelease`
  (asyncpg, the caller owns the transaction) is the per-connection half of the move: unclaim,
  set `released_at` where it is missing, cancel `PENDING`/`PENDING_APPROVAL` rows (open
  reviews included), flag `RUNNING` ones, cancel unclaimed wake-ups. No stage change, no
  history row; running it again cancels nothing. simpl_api's "not a lead" uses it with
  `reason="not_a_lead"`. `UserLeadConnection.released_at` exposes the mark; read it with
  `claimed` (claiming again clears it, `trg_ulc_reclaim_clears_release`). simpl-outreach's
  admission gate ends a walk on a released connection without a review.
- **A contact who left the company** (2026-11-07, ENG-941,
  `services.outreach.contact_left.record_contact_left(session, *, ulc_id, source, evidence,
  learned_at, ended_on=None, new_company_id=None) -> ContactLeft`; the caller owns the
  transaction): never Not In Target, the target is the company. Ends the person's open roles
  at the connection's company (`LeadRepository.end_roles_at`), then moves the connection to
  `new_company_id` when it is in the connection's ICP pool (`in_icp_pool`:
  `opportunity.icp_company_matches` minus `icp_verification_exclusions`), else releases it
  (`release_connections`, reason `contact_left`, stage kept). A connection with a call (a
  call stage, Disqualified and Lost included, a booking or a charge) is left as it is
  (`ContactLeftOutcome.KEPT_FOR_CALL`: the caller asks the seller). Writes one
  `lead.company.left` event (`source` reply/profile/backfill, `evidence` capped at 500
  characters, `outcome`, `research_queued`) timed at `learned_at`. After a move or a
  release the old company goes back on simpl_flow's recheck lane
  (`services.autopilot.company_recheck.queue_company_recheck`): only when the seller's
  autopilot is on for the ICP under the flow runner, a recheck attempt is left (1 or 2),
  and once the seller's placeholder is in Monitoring `autopilot_recheck_still_monitored`
  holds (no teammate works the company outside Monitoring; ended roles do not count since
  simpl_flow migration 178); otherwise the placeholder change is rolled back (savepoint).
  A moved person's lead is put on the new company when it still named the old one
  (`LeadRepository.replace_company`). It does not re-arm a walk: callers do.
- **Borrower switched off:** the dispatch authority cancels anything a connection stamped
  with a loan (open or ended) still has queued once the loan's customer is switched off
  (`metadata.is_active = false`) or has no active pay-per-call subscription
  (`loan_customer_inactive`, at insert, approval and the pre-send recheck), the same test
  as simpl-outreach's `lent_customer_guard`.
- **LinkedIn only:** a connection stamped with a loan (open or ended) never writes email: the
  dispatch authority cancels any row on the `email` channel there, whatever its verb
  (`lent_identity_email`; a lent identity's mailbox is SimpL's). The Python reference reads
  it from `AuthorityFacts.email_on_lent_connection` (`is_email_channel(payload)` on a stamped
  connection).

## Meetings on the team's calendars, and bookings made by hand

`simpl_core.services.account_coordination.LeadMeetings(session).for_lead(customer_id=…, lead_id=…)`
lists the team's meetings with a lead, from Recall's copy of each connected calendar
(`integration.recall_capture_job`). It covers 30 days back and 60 ahead. It is read-only:
Recall and the calendar providers are never called.
- **Matching:** a lead matches through any email on file for it, whichever seller's
  `lead_contact` row holds the address.
- **Deduplication:** a meeting on several colleagues' calendars counts once, keyed on the
  invite's iCal id and start (`meeting_key`).
- **Each meeting carries:**
  - `status`: `upcoming` / `in_progress` / `past` / `cancelled`;
  - `recorded`;
  - `lead_spoke`: `true` when simpl_api's speaker links (`capture_metadata.call_lead_links`)
    hear this lead on the recording, so the call took place even if the lead wrote before
    it that nobody had joined; `false` when the recording was linked without them; `null`
    when nothing is recorded or linked yet;
  - `call_type`: the sales classifier's label of the recording (`discovery`, `demo`,
    `no_show`, ...), or `null`. Only these two paths of `capture_metadata` are read, never
    the transcript;
  - the `organizer` (resolved to a user of the customer);
  - `lead_attendee {email, response_status}`;
  - `capture_job_ids`, with the organizer's row first.

`LeadMeetings(session).last_held(customer_id=…, lead_id=…)` returns the latest call this
lead spoke on that started before `for_lead`'s window: between 365 days
(`HELD_CALL_MEMORY`) and 30 days back. The meeting has the same shape as above, with every
calendar's row of it included. It returns `None` when there is no such call. A finished
recording counts only when its `call_lead_links` hear the lead. A caller that keeps
`for_lead`'s window uses it so a call that took place is not forgotten once the window
moves past it.

`AccountCoordinationService.request(...)` for a `calendar_booking` key needs the call to
book under `evidence["booking"]`; `booking_problems(booking, now=...)` is the check.
- **Shape:** `call_medium` (`invite` / `phone`), `lead_email`, `lead_phone`,
  `proposed_start_datetime`, `timezone`, `timezone_source` (`lead_stated` / `location` /
  `seller_default`), `slot_precision` (`exact` / `window`), `window_label`,
  `source_message_id`.
- **Rules:** an invite needs a valid bare `lead_email`, a phone call a `lead_phone` and an
  `exact` slot. The start is ISO 8601 with a UTC offset, strictly in the future, and its
  offset is the one the IANA `timezone` has at that instant.
- **Refusal:** `CoordinationError` with code `booking_not_ready`, naming every problem;
  nothing is written. A replay on the same idempotency key still returns its objective.
- **Corrections:** `supply_information(answers=..., booking={...})` lays the given keys
  over `objective.context.booking` in the same update, and checks the result the same
  way. The owner's invite still queued for the objective (`PENDING` / `PENDING_APPROVAL`
  `invite_call` on the owner's connection) moves with it: start, zone,
  `timezone_source`, `needs_user_to_pick_slot` (a `window` slot) and the lead's address,
  in `invite_call` and `call_data`, stamped `payload.rescheduled`. A `RUNNING` row is
  left alone.
- **Counter-proposal:** an owner who cannot make the lead's slot calls
  `need_information(questions=[...], proposed_times=[{start, timezone}, ...])`.
  The times are 1 to `MAX_PROPOSED_TIMES` (3), each held to the start rules above, with
  no two at the same instant (`proposed_times_problems(times, now=...)`). They are stored
  on the event payload under `PROPOSED_TIMES_KEY` (`{start, timezone}` only), for the
  source to offer the lead. A refusal is `invalid_event` and writes nothing. The
  objective's booking is unchanged until the source supplies the time the lead picks.
- Other capabilities, and `record_observed_booking`, need no booking.

Objective commands and who they wake:
- **`supply_information`** is the source's, from any open state. From
  `waiting_information` it resumes the work (`active`); from `requested` or `active` it
  hands the owner news and keeps the state. `next_state(command, state)` answers which
  state a command leaves.
- **Wake-ups:** a participant's `accept`, `need_information`, `supply_information`,
  `cancel` and `fulfil` wake the other participant (`coordination_updated`,
  `WAKING_COMMANDS`). `progress` and `schedule_check` wake nobody: they are the actor's
  own record and timer. Lifecycle commands wake both sides, and delivery receipts wake
  the other side through `wake_coordination_on_receipt` on `outreach_events`.

`AccountCoordinationService.redirect(customer_id=…, objective_id=…, actor_user_id=…,
to_user_id=…, reason=…, idempotency_key=…)` passes an open objective to another colleague.
- **Who may:** either participant: the owner instead of taking the work on, the source
  when it went to the wrong colleague. Anyone else gets `forbidden`.
- **Where to:** only a colleague in `redirect_targets(customer_id=…, objective_id=…)`,
  computed from the team table: the source hands them a responsibility with the same
  capability (a booking stays a booking), they own it and are active, and they are not
  the current owner. Each target carries `user_id`, `full_name`, `responsibility_key`,
  `description` and `mode`. Any other `to_user_id` is refused with `forbidden`, naming
  the allowed ones, so the dispatch authority rule and `reconcile_policy` accept the
  successor as they would a `request()`.
- **Result:** the objective closes `cancelled` with `cancel_reason` `REDIRECTED` and a
  `REDIRECT_EVENT` event whose payload names `successor_objective_id`. Its queued rows
  are cancelled and its pending wake-ups closed (`skipped_reason: objective_redirected`).
  The successor opens under the target's responsibility, from the same source connection,
  with the context carried over plus `redirected_from`. Only the new owner is woken; the
  source's check carries over, or moves a day ahead if it was already due.
- **Booking:** not checked again, since the hand-off itself is unchanged. An open
  objective already on the target's responsibility raises `already_active`; a replay on
  the same key returns the recorded redirect and its successor.

`AccountCoordinationService.record_observed_booking(...)` records a `calendar_booking`
hand-off the owner already carried out by hand.
- **Who:** the same source and owner that `request()` would pick.
- **Result:** the objective is `fulfilled` straight away, with one `booking_observed`
  event. Nobody is woken and no check is scheduled.
- **The owner's connection:** created, if missing, at the Meeting Booked status of the
  lead's workflow (the source's status only when no workflow can be read). The source's
  own connection keeps its stage. An existing owner connection keeps its status.
- **Verification:** core checks the evidence from the capture job itself: not cancelled,
  organized by the target, a lead address among the attendees who has not declined, and
  ended at most 2 days ago. Anything else raises `CoordinationError` with code
  `not_observed`.
- **An open objective** for the same key and owner is closed the same way: its queued
  actions are cancelled and the owner is woken. A second walk that sees the same meeting
  gets the recorded objective back.
- **Where the proof lives:** `objective.context.observed_booking`, including the
  `lead_email` the invite went to. A receiver may not have that address among its own
  contacts, because `lead_contact` holds each address once per lead.

Hand-off events: `request` writes `account.handoff.initiated`, `redirect`
`account.handoff.redirected` and an objective closing `fulfilled` (`fulfil`, or a booking
observed by hand) `account.handoff.completed` (`HANDOFF_INITIATED` / `HANDOFF_REDIRECTED` /
`HANDOFF_COMPLETED`). One `outreach_events` row per connection involved, at the
coordination event's `created_at`, with `objective_id`, `coordination_event_id`,
`responsibility_key`, `from_user_id`, `to_user_id`, `reason` and the connection's `role`
(`source`, `owner`, `previous_owner`); count one role to count hand-offs. A redirect's
successor writes no `initiated`. Replays write nothing.

`UserConnectionRepository.get_active_booking_calendar(user_id)` returns the calendar an
invite can be created in. It never returns Recall, which is category `calendar` too;
`get_active_by_user_and_category(user, "calendar")` can.

## LinkedIn browser operations

`simpl_core.services.linkedin_proxy.DatabaseLinkedInOperations` (port:
`LinkedInOperationPort`) runs LinkedIn requests through the seller's browser extension.
Each operation is a row in `extension_proxy.operation_config_revision`; the gateway
renders it and the extension fetches it with the seller's session.

- `search_people(context, company_linkedin_id, keyword)` reads the people search page
  filtered to one company (`people_search`). Many accounts get at most three results.
- `search_company_people(context, company_linkedin_id, keyword)` searches a company's
  members by keyword on its People tab (`company_people_search`, what the tab's "search
  employees" box runs): one page of 12, not capped at three (live on three accounts,
  2026-11-06). Pass the keyword as typed: it is percent-encoded into the inputs, and the
  batch's `keyword` is the keyword as given. A response that is not the search (LinkedIn
  ignored the company filter, a body that is not JSON) comes back as an error batch, never
  an exception. The caller reserves the pacing slot, as for `search_people`. Needs
  `migrations/20261106_company_people_search.sql` applied.
- `list_company_people(context, company_linkedin_id, company_vanity_name)` lists a
  company's members. LinkedIn serves some accounts its old Ember app and others the new
  frontend, and two reads answer this:
  - `company_people_api`: the People tab's voyager search, by numeric company id.
    It answered on both apps, so it is the default.
  - `company_people`: the `/company/<vanity name>/people/` page, which lists members
    only on the new frontend.
  The read that last answered for the user is kept in
  `extension_proxy.user_operation_variant` and tried first. A misfit (the page served as
  the old app's shell, the API ignoring its filters or refusing the query) tries the
  other read in the same call and remembers it. A failure that says nothing about the
  account (throttling, a provider error) returns as it is, with no switch. When
  neither fits, none is remembered for 24 h and the call returns `None`. Each read
  reserves its own pacing slot, so callers do not. Pass the vanity name decoded from
  `sales.company.linkedin_url` (`linkedin_company_vanity_name`), or `None` to skip the
  page. The batch's `keyword` is `COMPANY_PEOPLE_LABEL`; neither read gives a location.

All three return a `LinkedInSearchResultBatch`. A page that is not the expected screen, or a
provider error, comes back as a batch with `error` set, and its raw body stays in the
ledger for debugging. Parsers read pages by structure only: headings, degree badges and
counts are in the viewer's language.

`fetch_profile_positions(context, public_identifier)` reads one profile's positions
(`profile_positions`) and returns `ProfilePositions`: one `ObservedPosition` per position,
its employer named by the numeric LinkedIn company id (`None` for a position with no
company page), and `public_identifier` / `profile_urn` / `full_name`, the profile the
response is about. Use the positions and the name only when that is the lead's own profile
(`LeadRepository.fill_full_name_if_null` gives a nameless lead the name). A provider error
or an unreadable page comes back with `error` set and the page kept; a channel failure
raises `LinkedInOperationError`. The caller reserves the pacing slot
(`reserve_operation_slot`). LinkedIn serves the dash profile decorated (`elements[0]`, the
positions nested in `profilePositionGroups`), as the first live fetch showed on 2026-10-16;
the normalized (`included`) and legacy (`positionView`) shapes are read too. The operation
is enabled by revision 2 (`migrations/20261016b_enable_profile_positions.sql`); revision 1
(`20261008b`) shipped it disabled.

`simpl_core.services.lead_positions.positions_to_experience(session, lead_id=…,
positions=…, source=…, today=None)` writes those positions; it never commits.
- **Companies:** found by numeric LinkedIn id: the row holding the id; else
  `CompanyByIdService.get_or_create`: the single id-less row on its LinkedIn company page
  (a real row first; a lone placeholder is promoted: it takes the id and `origin=source`,
  and its pending claims resolve to it), else a new row with `origin=source`. A position
  without an id is skipped. No name is compared, the lead's own company's included: a row
  of the position's name without the id stays apart until entity resolution merges the two
  on a key they share.
- **Experience:** one `sales.lead_experience` row per position the lead does not have yet
  (`LeadRepository.add_experience_if_absent`, which returns the row found or written,
  `ExperienceRow(id, inserted)`).
- **Current employer:** when the positions held on `today` name exactly one company, it
  fills `sales.lead.company_id` if that is NULL, and then the company of each of the
  lead's connections that has none. A lead with another company keeps it.
- **Result:** `PositionsWritten` (`outcome`, `companies`, `skipped`, `experience_added`,
  `experience_ids` (the rows of the positions with an id, written or already there; bigint
  ids), `current_employer_id`, `lead_company_filled`, `connections_filled`).

## ICP target coverage

`simpl_core.sales_domain.icp_target_coverage` puts each company of an ICP's target in
one bucket (taken, rejected, not proposable, still to propose) on an asyncpg connection.
`icp_target_coverages(conn, icp_ids=[…])` counts several ICPs and reads the customer's
connected companies once for all of them (`connected_companies(conn, customer_id=…,
icp_id=None)`); `icp_target_coverage(conn, icp_id=…)` is one ICP and raises
`IcpWithoutOwnerError` for an ICP without an owner. A company is taken through a claimed
connection's lead company or the lead's current roles (at most three), read from
`sales.connection_search` like simpl_flow's opportunity pipeline (ENG-1212): a former
employer does not take it. The biggest customer (95k claimed connections) takes one read
of 2-3 s, whatever its targets' size; callers that count often cache the result per
customer (simpl_api `icp_target_left`). Each
target is read once, whole: its rows also give `target_refreshed_at` (their latest
`updated_at`), so no second walk of `opportunity.icp_company_matches` runs.

## Autopilot yield (Sales Domain)

`simpl_core.sales_domain.autopilot_yield` measures how much of a person's daily company
supply autopilot claims, and returns facts only — it decides nothing. Rates count a
company once, by its latest `execution` row in `sales.autopilot_activity_log`, because
the flow runner reviews a monitored company again on later days.
`autopilot_yield_summary(conn, user_id=…)` rides on the operating snapshot as
`autopilot_yield_7d`; `db_read_autopilot_yield(ctx, …)` is the agent's drill-down, with
monitor / not-in-target reasons, the job headlines each search found, per-keyword empty
rates, companies the person claimed after autopilot monitored them, and ICP changes
pending or applied. The agent records its verdict per ICP in workspace item
`autopilot-yield-<icp_id>` (`props.verdict` is `at_ceiling`, `recoverable` or
`unclear`) and reads it back on later runs.

`icp_search_keywords_update` is a Sales Domain write and a HITL action type: it replaces
`sales.icp.search_keywords`, the titles autopilot searches inside each company, for every
user assigned to that ICP. `customer_brain.sales_domain_pending_action` only accepts it
once simpl_api migration `20260917_sales_domain_pending_icp_search_keywords.sql` has run.
`pending_icp_change(...)` refuses a second open proposal of the same kind on one ICP, so
the users who share it do not queue the same change.

## How an account runs, its calls, its waiting conversations (Sales Domain)

Three readers on an asyncpg connection ride on the operating snapshot
(`db_read_outreach_operating_snapshot`) and decide nothing (ENG-1208):

- `account_operating_model.how_this_account_runs(conn, user_id=…, customer_id=…,
  outreach_prefs=…)` → `how_this_account_runs`: the customer's billing model
  (`sales.customer_subscription`), managed engagement, the seller's `auto_approval`
  setting, who approved, edited and rejected this seller's drafts in 14 days (from
  `user.action.*` events: `via = managed` is SimpL staff in Operations; otherwise the
  actor is the seller, a teammate, or someone outside the customer), and the approval
  queue by message type (`pending_queue.fetch_queue_by_message_type`).
- `call_outcomes.seller_calls(conn, user_id=…)` → `calls`: `call.*` events folded per
  lead connection (agreed, booked, held, no-show, rejected, accepted, billed), listed when
  they moved in 30 days or are dated in the next 14, upcoming first, counts for all.
  `we_wrote_before_the_call` checks a successful send from any seller of the same
  customer to the lead, since calls are handed to the colleague who takes them.
  `meeting_at` is the call's current time. A `call.rescheduled` with no new time, later
  than the last event that gave one, empties it: `moved_from_meeting_at` keeps the slot
  it left and `moved_without_new_time_at` when, and the call is not listed as upcoming.
- `conversations_waiting.conversations_waiting(conn, user_id=…)` → `conversations_waiting`:
  leads whose latest inbound message (14 days) has no later successful send, with the
  newest draft since (its `action_type`: `send_message` is a message, other types are
  requests) and its `send_recheck` / `pre_send_check`, plus `call_since_their_message`,
  the newest `call.*` event on the connection since the lead's message (a lead who booked
  through the calendar never gets a message back).

Each of the three is read through `domain_db_reads._snapshot_section`: a failure returns
`{"error", "section"}` for that section only and is logged
(`sales_domain_snapshot_section_failed`), so the rest of the snapshot still arrives.

`autopilot_health` also carries `extension_socket_state` with its plain meaning
(`extension_socket.extension_socket_meaning`): only `connected` is live, whatever
`extension_last_seen_at` says. Message calibration edits carry `edited_by` (`seller`,
`simpl_staff_in_operations`, `someone_else`, `not_recorded`); what counts as an edit is
unchanged.
## Required company questions

An ICP's required company questions (simpl_api's feature check, `sales_view.icp_required_question`) are filters: the opportunities run proposes only companies whose stored answer (`sales.company_feature`, read from their own website pages, code and LinkedIn posts) is the required one. `simpl_core.services.required_questions.load_required_questions(db, icp_id)` lists them; `check_required_questions(db, icp_id=..., company_ids=...)` gives each company's answer per question (`passes` is `None` when never answered, which is not a pass). Read-only models: `CompanyQuestion`, `CompanyQuestionAnswer`, `IcpRequiredQuestionView` (`CompanyQuestionRepository`); simpl_api owns the writes. simpl_flow's verifier reads them as filters so its judges never re-decide a rule a question answered.

## Sales Domain approval queue

`customer_brain.sales_domain_pending_action` holds what the Sales Domain agent proposes when HITL is on. A proposal is only good for `PENDING_ACTION_MAX_AGE_DAYS` (4) and only while it still applies to live data; `simpl_core.sales_domain.pending_action_freshness.check_pending_action_freshness` decides the second (prompt patches still land and are not already in the prompt, drafts still `PENDING_APPROVAL`, ICP filter/description not already changed or edited since, seller still active).

- `SalesDomainPendingActionService.refresh_pending()` expires old rows and withdraws the ones that no longer apply (`status = 'rejected'`, `rejection_reason` `expired: …` / `stale: …`, `decided_by` NULL). Hosts call it on their scheduled sweep; `has_open_pending` and the daily review snapshot call it themselves, so a stale row never holds a wake.
- `SalesDomainPendingActionExecutor.approve_and_execute` re-checks first. A row that no longer applies is withdrawn and `StalePendingActionError` (a `ValueError`) is raised with an operator-readable message; nothing executes. When only some drafts are still waiting, it executes on those and records the narrowed payload. `claim_for_approval` refuses rows past the age cap whichever path calls it.
- A deferred `icp_description_update` / `icp_filter_update` (`wish_list`) stores `previous_description` / `previous_wish_list`, the value it was written against, so approval never overwrites a newer edit.

### Signal proposals (`icp_signal_create`, 0.8.322)

The Sales Domain agent proposes website facts signals for an ICP (`icp_signal_propose`; `list_website_technologies` spells names and categories; the skill `website-facts-signal-proposals` says when and how). A signal is saved only after a person approves it, whatever the seller's HITL setting (Len, 2026-11-04).

- **Off by default.** The tool works only in a daily review or a manual run (`icp_signal_tools.proposes_in`, the production triggers) of a seller whose row switches it on: `sales.user.outreach_prefs` `{"sales_domain_signal_proposals": true}` (`outreach_prefs.signal_proposals_enabled`, read into `SalesDomainV2Deps.signal_proposals` by `build_operator_deps`). Any other trigger, `icp_validation_review` included, answers `switched_off`, and the prompt mentions signals only in a daily review or manual run with the switch on. Switch a seller on with `UPDATE sales."user" SET outreach_prefs = COALESCE(outreach_prefs, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"sales_domain_signal_proposals": true}'::jsonb WHERE id = '<user id>';` (a production write).
- **Before queuing**, in order: the checks a simpl_api save runs for the kind (`search.website_facts.website_facts_signal_errors`); a signal the ICP already has (enabled or not) is refused; a signal proposal already waiting on the ICP comes back `already_pending` unless this one supersedes it; then the preview (`open_website_facts_preview`, up to 20 s) counts the ICP's companies it matches. A complete count of none is not queued; a count cut short by its time budget is queued as partial (`count_note`, `matches.cut_short`).
- **Limits:** one signal proposal waits per ICP (the ICP is the target, as for every ICP proposal); one is queued per run and three `preview=true` calls are allowed per run (daily review and manual; `no_novelty_daily_review` and `tactical_observation_only` have none).

Payload, as the dashboard reads it:

```python
{"icp_id": "…", "icp_title": "Italian SMEs with old sites", "title": "PHP past end of life",
 "in_words": "sites running PHP past end of life",          # search.website_facts.condition_in_words
 "filter_json": {"mode": "all_match", "conditions": [{"collection": "website_facts", …}]},
 "importance_level": 3, "change_reason": "…",
 "matches": {"companies": 42, "sites": 40, "icp_companies": 3100, "companies_searched": 3100,
             "cut_short": False, "counted_at": "2026-11-09T…+00:00"},
 "sample_matches": [{"company_name": "…", "host": "…"}]}   # at most 5
```

Approving it (`execute_pending_action_command`) writes `sales.icp_signal` through `services.icp_signal_writes.create_proposed_website_facts_signal` (the only public writer: the unchecked insert is private) with simpl_api's create columns (`ICP_SIGNAL_CREATE_COLUMNS`: `icp_id`, `title`, `filter_json`, `importance_level`, `timeout` NULL, `enabled` true), after the same checks on today's data and a duplicate check; a refusal is the proposal's `execution_error` (status `failed`) and no row is written. Hosts:

- `customer_brain.sales_domain_pending_action` accepts the type only after `migrations/20261109_sales_domain_pending_icp_signal_create.sql` (rollback beside it); apply it before a release that queues it.
- **Deploy order:** the migration, then the dashboard with the approvals card (simpl_dashboard 0.1.408), then simpl_api relocked on this release (approving applies the proposal), then simpl_flow relocked (the agent proposes), then switch an account on. Roll back in reverse: switch accounts off, simpl_flow, simpl_api, the dashboard, then the migration's rollback (it refuses while an `icp_signal_create` row exists).
- The agent's host (simpl_flow) and the approving host (simpl_api) need the `search` extra, the website facts check's reads (SELECT on `sales.website`, `sales.website_technology`, `scraping.technology_rule_versions` past RLS), and, for the agent's count, the query service's settings (`ROW_STORE_QUERY_URL`, `ROW_STORE_QUERY_TOKEN`: the count opens its own client, `open_seek`).
- `db_read_user_icps` (the agent's tool) lists each ICP's signals: `signals` (newest 20: `title`, `kind` post / website / website_facts / article / sql, `enabled`, `in_words` for website facts) and `signals_total`. The seed that rides on every model turn carries `signals_total` only (`db_read_user_icps(ctx, signal_lists=False)`).

## Regenerating a draft (asynchronous)

A regenerate is an ordinary `seller_regenerate` row in `sales_outreach.batch_trigger_queue`; the outreach worker claims it like any other trigger (ranked first, with inbound replies) and nothing runs a walk in the request. The contract is `simpl_core.models.outreach.regenerate` (trigger type, `RegenerateScope`, trigger-data keys, `REGENERATE_RESULT_KEY`, `SUPERSEDE_REASON`, `REGENERATE_LATE_AFTER` = 15 min); the commands are `simpl_core.services.outreach.regenerate`, the query `simpl_core.services.outreach.regenerate_status`.

- **Request.** `request_regenerate(db, journey_log_id=, owner_user_id=, requested_by="user:<uuid>", source=RegenerateSource.SELLER, hint=None, scope=None)` locks the draft and queues the trigger in one transaction and returns `RegenerateRequest(request_id, trigger_id, ...)` (answer 202 with it). `scope=None` lets the worker's triage pick the tier; `RegenerateScope.REWRITE` forces a rewrite. It raises `journey_log_not_found`, `invalid_transition` (not `PENDING_APPROVAL`) or `RegenerateInProgressError` (`regenerate_in_progress: ...`; this draft or another draft of the lead is regenerating). `make_deck=True` asks for a deck instead of new words (the Home's "Create presentation"): only on a `send_message` draft (`invalid_transition` otherwise); the trigger carries `make_deck: true`, the marker `make_deck: true`, and the job reports `scope` `deck` (`DECK_SCOPE`). The caller checks the draft has room for one more file.
- **The lock.** While `payload.regenerate_pending` (`RegeneratePending`) is in any state but `failed`, `set_pending_with_version`, `set_cancelled`, `update_payload_field(s)`, `set_active_version`, `update_schedule`, `admin_override_fields` and `append_regenerated_version` raise `RegenerateInProgressError`: map it to 409. A path that writes `journey_log` in raw SQL calls `assert_not_regenerating(payload, journey_log_id=...)` under its row lock.
- **Status.** `get_regenerate_job_status(session, trigger_id=, owner_user_id=, request_id=None)` returns `RegenerateJobStatus` or `None` (not this owner's): `queued`, `running` (both with `late`), `rewritten` (`version_index`), `replaced` (`new_journey_log_id`), `review_request`, `no_result` (`reason`, `draft_changed` when the draft changed meanwhile; `attachments_full` or `deck_not_made` for a deck request), `deck_attached` (a deck request's deck is on the draft), `failed` (`reason`), `cancelled`.
- **Cancel.** `cancel_regenerate(db, journey_log_id=, owner_user_id=, cancelled_by=)` only once the regenerate failed, is late, or its job already ended; earlier it raises `RegenerateNotCancellableError` (409). It drops the marker and closes a trigger still queued.
- **Worker.** The worker ends each request with one settle call naming it: `settle_rewrite` (`RegeneratedMessage` -> new active version), `settle_review_request`, `settle_superseded` (long tier: withdraws the draft before the root walk; the job reports the row the walk marks `REGENERATE_RESULT_KEY`), `settle_no_result`, `settle_failed` (the draft unlocks, the marker stays `failed` for the card), `settle_deck` (a deck request: the stored deck is appended to the draft's files as `payload.attachment` `made_by: deck_make` and linked to the draft, the text untouched; `no_result` `attachments_full` past 5 files or 15 MiB, and `ValueError("deck_not_attachable: ...")` for a file missing or already on a draft). A draft that changed meanwhile gets nothing (`draft_changed`), and an outcome already recorded on the trigger is never overwritten; `RegenerateTrigger.from_trigger_data(...).settled` tells a retried batch to skip it.
- **Sales Domain.** `writer_regenerate_journey_logs_for_user(...)` queues a forced rewrite per draft and returns `queued` / `skipped` / `failed` per row; it no longer needs an outreach processor (the parameter is ignored).

Review-card option ids are `simpl_core.models.review_option.ReviewOptionId`; core's `FunnelCategory` carries the same ids as simpl-outreach's `StatusMappingId`.

## Entity tags

An agent points at a record inside its prose with a markdown link on a private scheme: `[Mario Rossi](simpl:lead/<id>)`. Kinds are `lead`, `company`, `message` (a `sales_outreach.journey_log` id), `call`, `task` and `doc` (a `customer_brain.workspace_item` id), and `icp`. `simpl://kind/id` is accepted too; ids are uuids or bigints.

`simpl_core.services.entity_tags` is the Python half of that contract — the other half is `lib/entity-tags/tokens.ts` in simpl_sales, and the two are pinned to each other by `tests/services/test_entity_tags.py`.

```python
from simpl_core.services.entity_tags import format_entity_tag, parse_entity_tags, strip_entity_tags

format_entity_tag("lead", lead_id, "Mario Rossi")   # -> "[Mario Rossi](simpl:lead/…)"
parse_entity_tags(body)                             # -> [EntityTag(kind, id, label), …]
strip_entity_tags(body)                             # -> tags become their plain labels
```

The id in a tag is never trusted: the frontend resolves every tag against the caller's customer, so an invented or foreign id renders as plain text. Any surface that does not render markdown — an outbound email, an escaped-HTML notification, a plain-text export, anything a prospect reads — must call `strip_entity_tags` first, or the reader sees link syntax wrapped around an internal record id. A malformed tag is deliberately left exactly as written on both sides.

## Funnel categories, status writes and meetings

- **Categories.** `simpl_core.models.funnel_category.FunnelCategory` names the canonical categories of `mappings.lead_status` (`MEETING_BOOKED = 5`, `NEGOTIATION = 9`: the lead attended the call, `CLOSED = 6`, ...). The ids are foreign keys, not an order: Negotiation sits between Meeting Booked and Closed. Sort and pick with `PRECEDENCE` / `precedence()` / `best_category()` and `FUNNEL_RANK`, test membership with `MEETING_BOOKED_OR_LATER` / `CALL_HELD`; never `<`, `<=` or ranges on the id. `CUSTOMER = 10` (kind `customer`, off the funnel, precedence 110: one Customer connection makes the company a Customer) is a company already a client of the customer (imported client list, or a SimpL close that aged): parked like Monitoring (Raff, 2026-10-28: silent until the person writes, a signal arrives or the seller asks), and never a meeting, a call or a win; `compute_opportunities_for_user_ids` counts it as `customers`, `icp_pipeline` as `imported_client`, autopilot's `execute_action` takes it back only on a claim, an inbound booking moves it to Meeting Booked and an inbound rule moves it on as from Monitoring (`stage_rule.moves_forward`). `CRM_STAGE_FALLBACK` maps Negotiation to Meeting Booked, Customer to Closed and Disqualified to Not Interested for a CRM pipeline without the stage. `DISQUALIFIED = 12` (kind `disqualified`, no funnel rank, precedence 15: between Opted out and Not Interested) is a lead met on a call whom the customer then decided not to sell to (ENG-1057): the call stays billed (it is not in `CALL_HELD`, and a move out of 12 is never a held call), it leaves call → customer rates; an inbound rule never moves it (`stage_rule.moves_forward`), the CRM pull moves it only into Closed or Customer, an unmapped push goes to the lost stage and never to the default (`NO_DEFAULT_STAGE`), `icp_pipeline` names it `disqualified` (a seller rejection in `icp_levers.REJECTED_STAGES`), and `TriggerSchedulerService.schedule_followup` arms no follow-up on it. A Disqualified that only SimpL's reading of the call made (`sales.call_disqualification` source `SIMPL_READING_SOURCE`, `decided_by` `SIMPL_READING_ACTOR_ID`, both in `funnel_category`; a seller who confirms the proposal is the decider) is `disqualified_by_simpl_reading` in `icp_pipeline`: worked, never a rejection. `LOST = 13` (kind `lost_after_call`: `lost` is Not Interested's; no funnel rank, precedence 12, between Disqualified and Not Interested; Raff, 2026-11-11) is a lead met on a call, in target, who did not go ahead: the call stays billed (not in `CALL_HELD`) and stays in call → customer rates; it is entered only from Negotiation by simpl_api's Lost path. An inbound rule never moves it (`stage_rule.moves_forward`), an inbound booking moves it to Meeting Booked (`ingestion.meetings`), the CRM pull never moves it in or out (a CRM stage mapped to 13 moves nobody; onboarding maps it to Not Interested), the push sends it to the lost stage (`CRM_STAGE_FALLBACK` 13 → 7, `NO_DEFAULT_STAGE`), it is not active pipeline, autopilot's `execute_action` never moves a Lost or Disqualified person (like Non sales), strategy plans never write to a Lost or Disqualified person (`CompanyReads.refused_leads`; an id `CATEGORY_KIND` does not know is not in progress), `evaluate_dispatch_authority` rule 1l cancels any first message to it (see "Opted out and Not In Target stop every send"), `contact_left` keeps it for the call, `icp_pipeline` names it `lost` (worked, not a rejection), and `schedule_followup` arms only its reopening (`trigger_source` = `trigger_scheduler.LOST_REOPEN` = `lost_reopen`; never recognised by the free-text `reason`).
- **Status writes say who and when.** `UserLeadConnectionRepository.update_status(ulc_id=..., status_id=..., changed_by=..., effective_at=..., reason=..., change_ref=None)` takes the actor, the date the move became true (`None` = now) and what it came from (`change_ref`: `journey_log:<uuid>`, `pipeline_run:<id>`, `review:<uuid>`, `crm_sync:<id>`, `import:<id>`, read by the trigger from `sales.status_change_ref`). A writer in raw SQL calls `simpl_core.services.status_history.set_status_change_context(conn, changed_by=..., effective_at=..., reason=..., change_ref=None)` in the same transaction before its `UPDATE`; a SQLAlchemy writer calls the repository's `set_status_change_context`. `get_or_create_company_dummy_ulc` and `get_or_create_ulc_for_lead` take optional `changed_by` / `reason` / `change_ref`; an author left out is `orchestrator` with `pipeline_run:<id>` inside an agent-framework run and nobody outside one. The CRM pull writes `crm:<provider>` / `crm_sync`, autopilot `autopilot`, the ICP cleanup `icp_pipeline:not_in_target`. `last_lead_message_at(ulc_id, before=...)` is the date a move reacting to the lead becomes true. Moving a lead into Negotiation is for the owner or call evidence only: pay per call charges it.
- **The status time is `status_changed_at`, not `updated_at`.** `UserLeadConnection.status_changed_at` is when the connection's status was last changed or confirmed; the writers that stamped a time with the status set it instead of `updated_at` (`update_status`, `update_ulc_status`, the CRM pull and blocklist, autopilot's moves, the ICP cleanup; those that stamped none, such as the strategy directive claims, are left to the database), and every reader that means "status since" or "worked last" reads it (the CRM export's `since` window and its pick of a company's connection, the flow facts' fallback, the sales datasets' company tie-break, `find_connection_thread`, the Sales Domain windows). `updated_at` is when the row last changed (set by the database): a company move or a claim moves it too, so only a reader that wants any change reads it (`FlowFactsLoader.changed_company_ids`). The database stamps `status_changed_at` on every `status_id` change and `updated_at` on every change (simpl_api 20261117200000); a writer sets `status_changed_at` only to confirm a status without changing it, and `sales.keep_change_times` = on leaves both for a write that must not count. `signal_seen_at` is when the seller pressed "Keep monitoring" on a monitoring signal, written only by simpl_sales. The simpl_api migration that adds both columns must be applied before a service runs this `simpl_core`: every `select(UserLeadConnection)` reads them.
- **ICP definition writes say who and from where.** simpl_api's `sales.icp_definition_change` triggers log every changed part of an ICP (title, description, keywords, size, filters, embeddings, guidance/mission/brief, industries, roles). A writer calls `simpl_core.services.icp_change_context.set_icp_change_context(conn, IcpChange.by_user(id, path) | .by_staff(id, path) | .by_agent(name, path))` inside the transaction that writes (never in autocommit: the settings last one statement there); `maintenance=True` marks rewrites that keep the meaning. The Sales Domain writers in `services.sales_domain_icp_writes` take a required `change`: approved actions are `staff:<decided_by>` from `sales_domain_approval`, the agent's own writes `agent:sales_domain` from `sales_domain`.
- **Meetings and calls in a period** come from `sales_view.company_funnel_facts` (simpl_api migration 20260925e, rules of 20261002_billable_call_rules) through `simpl_core.services.reports.funnel_facts`: `fetch_company_facts(conn, user_ids, since)` (`since=None`: all time) and `period_counts(facts, from_ts, to_ts, users=None, include_external=False)`. A meeting is a company's first booking, a call its first call that took place, a win its first close by a person from a working stage. `PeriodCounts` gives SimpL's as `CohortSplit` (`meetings`, `calls_held`, `won`: cohort = taken in the period, earlier = before), the others as `meetings_other` / `calls_held_other` (`meetings_all` / `calls_all` for every one), `won_all_time`, the cohort rate numerators and the same per user. `compute_opportunities_for_user_ids` returns `meetingBookedInPeriod` / `callsHeldInPeriod` (all), `meetingsAttributableInPeriod` / `callsBillableInPeriod` / `wonInPeriod` (SimpL's), `split` (cohort/earlier and `show_rate` / `win_rate`), `won` = won with SimpL all time and `proposals` = distinct companies, next to where companies stand today (`meetingBooked`, `negotiation`, `closed`, `customers`); Managed's `count_calls_booked_auto` counts first bookings in the engagement window. The admin overview and pay per call read the same function, so the numbers agree everywhere.

## Metrics contract (one definition per number, 2026-10-02)

- **Catalog.** `simpl_core.services.reports.definitions` holds every number of the metrics contract (`METRICS`: key, label, unit, hover `description`, `split`) and its rules: `is_simpl_connection(origin, born_category)` (SimpL scope; `NOT_SIMPL_ORIGINS`, Dummy Imported born in Monitoring, born in Closed / Customer / Not In Target / Opted out), `fact_in_scope` / `fact_is_win` (a funnel fact as a SimpL meeting / win, `include_external` lifts `SCOPE_REASONS`, a client the customer already had is never won), `CohortSplit`, `rate` (None without a denominator), `parse_view` (`all` / `cohort`), `is_internal_customer_name` (SimpL itself), `COMPARISONS` (Report Wrapped's Average / Top hover), `REPORT_TIMEZONE` (Europe/Rome). Every API payload serves its hover from `describe(keys)`, never text written in pages.
- **Per customer, per window.** `simpl_core.services.reports.period_metrics` reads `sales_view.customers_metrics_overview_v2` (simpl_api migration 20261002f): `fetch_period_metrics(conn, from_day=, to_day=, customer_id=None, user_ids=None, tz_name=None, include_external=False)` -> `PeriodMetrics` per customer (companies and people taken apart, distinct companies proposed, first-contacted / first-replied people (`leads_replied_after_invite` apart; the activity `leads_written` / `leads_replying`), engaged companies, meetings, calls and wins as `CohortSplit`, the rates on a cohort with their numerators (the response rate's: every first contact of the window, replied within 30 days, `response_maturing` until then; `today=` sets the day), active users, the stock, `previous`, `daily`); `fetch_customer_period_metrics` for one; `combine` / `totals(metrics, is_internal=)` to add customers up (rates from the sums) with and without SimpL; `metrics_payload(m, view)` and `definitions_payload()` for the API shape. The admin table, customer page, app strip, Report Wrapped and ICP cards all read it.
- **Outreach is people.** `compute_outreach_for_user_ids(..., include_external=False)` and `compute_outreach_sankey(..., include_external=False)` count people only (a company placeholder is never an outreach lead) and SimpL's unless asked (`funnel.fetch_connection_scope`); results carry `split` (contacted / messaged / emailed / replied as cohort + earlier) and `responseRate` is the cohort's. `sankey.compute_company_sankey(conn, user_ids, from_ts, to_ts, include_external=)` is the company Sankey (proposed -> taken -> contacted -> engaged -> meeting -> call -> won), companies only.
- **Report Wrapped** months are Europe/Rome calendar months; `won` / `closed` are the month's wins (facts), `companiesClaimed` / `leadsClaimed` apart (`claimed` = people), the payload carries `contract` + `definitions`, and the Average / Top compare only billed customers that are not SimpL (`fetch_benchmark_customer_ids`).

## Weekly sales review (WBR)

The admin WBR page (simpl_api `admin/wbr`, contract `audits/2026-10-13-sales-wbr/SPEC_B.md`) is built from pure pieces here; the numbers come from simpl_api's `sales_wbr.*` SQL functions.

- **Weeks** (`services.reports.wbr.weeks`): Monday to Sunday in Europe/Rome, named by their Monday. `week_start(aware_dt, tz="Europe/Rome") -> date` (a naive datetime raises), `weeks_between(from_day, to_day)` (every Monday, both ends included), `is_week_start(day)`. The admin Overview's weekly buckets use `week_start` too.
- **Series** (`services.reports.wbr.series`): `moving_average(cells, window, rate=)`, `cumulative_average(cells, rate=)`, `total(cells, rate=)`. A week without data (`value` None, or a rate without numerator/denominator) is skipped, a real zero counts; a rate is averaged as sum of numerators over sum of denominators. The first `window - 1` weeks have no moving average: pass the weeks before and keep the tail.
- **Grid** (`services.reports.wbr.grid`): Pydantic DTOs `WbrCell`, `WbrRow`, `WbrSection`, `WbrGrid` (`model_dump(mode="json")` is the page's JSON), `WbrDimension(user_id, channel, step)`. `build_row(key, cells, window=, dimension=)` takes label, unit and hover from `definitions.METRICS` and computes the series. `validate_row` / `validate_grid` run at construction and refuse: a rate without numerator and denominator, outside 0–1 or not equal to their ratio; a count that is not a whole number ≥ 0; a manual cell without `entry_id` and `entered_by` (or those on a non-manual cell); `restated` without `frozen_value` (or the reverse); `value != computed_value` on a cell that is neither; weeks that are not consecutive Mondays; a row whose cells are not the grid's weeks; a restated cell in a week that is not closed; a key that is not in `METRICS`. `model_copy(update=...)` skips the check: rebuild with the constructor.
- **Stored data** (`models.wbr`, `repositories.wbr`, one repository per table, caller commits):
  - `CustomerActivationRepository.upsert_activation(...) -> (row, created)` derives `activated_at` (later of subscription start and payment method) and never rewrites a stored subscription; `customer_id` may be None with `customer_setup_id` (an onboarding paid, not finalized). `unlinked_stripe_customers()`; `link_customer(stripe_customer_id=, customer_id=) -> int` fills only a missing `customer_id`; `first_activation_per_customer(customer_ids=None)` leaves out rows with no customer.
  - `UserTagRepository.upsert_user_tag(user_id=, key=, value=, set_by=)` (same value: nothing written), `list_user_tags(user_ids)`.
  - `WbrManualEntryRepository.insert_entry(..., idempotency_key=) -> (row, created)`: `replaces_id` must be the cell's current entry (`ManualEntryConflict`), a key reused for another command raises `IdempotencyKeyReused`; `list_entries(customer_id=, from_week=, to_week=, include_replaced=False)`. A cell's dimension is stored with only the keys that are set.
  - `WbrWeekCloseRepository.close_week(...) -> (row, created)`: a week closes once, a second close returns the first; `get_closed_weeks(customer_id=, from_week=, to_week=)`. `grid` is the week's rows dumped as one-cell `WbrRow`s.
  - `WbrCustomerOriginRepository.propose(...)` replaces a proposal, reopens a rejection only for another lead or company, never touches a confirmed origin; `decide(..., status=confirmed|rejected, decided_by=, company_id=None, lead_id=None)` (naming the origin stores it as `manual`; nothing to decide raises `NotFoundError`); `list_proposed(seller_customer_id)`.

## Inbound signals (ingestion)

`simpl_core.services.ingestion` turns what a customer's inbound sources report (SDK, signed
webhooks; transport lives in simpl_ingestion) into leads owned by the right seller, with an
`inbound_signal` trigger outreach walks. Tables: `integration.ingestion_inbox`,
`integration.ingestion_rule` (migrations/20260808_ingestion_inbox_and_rules.sql,
20261014c_ingestion_record_lead.sql, 20261016c_ingestion_retry_backoff.sql), `integration.ingestion_playbook`
(20261014d_ingestion_playbook.sql), one
`(Unknown company)` row (`INBOUND_UNKNOWN_COMPANY_ID`, migrations/20260820_inbound_unknown_company.sql).
- **Accept:** `IngestionService(session).accept(customer_id=…, connection_id=…, signal=IngestedSignal, sender_dates_signal=bool)`
  -> `IngestionAcceptResult(inbox_id, created, status)`; idempotent on `(connection_id, external_id)`;
  the caller commits. The row's `occurred_at` is the time everything the drain writes is
  dated by (the connection's status history, the trigger, the event): the signal's own,
  capped at now, when `sender_dates_signal` (a server key, SimpL's replay); the arrival
  when not (a web page could backdate it). `IngestedSignal` refuses an empty `external_id`, `source` or `name`,
  `properties` over 16 KB of JSON (`MAX_PROPERTIES_BYTES`) and unknown fields, at the top level
  and inside `person`, `company`, `context`, `consent` and `owner_hint` (a misspelled
  `person.linkedin` is refused, not dropped): a sender passes a stable `external_id` per event
  and `person.linkedin_url` when it knows it. `properties.spoken_languages` is stored as a list
  of language names (a list sent as JSON text or comma-separated is read as that list) and
  refused when an item is a single character, which is what `list()` makes of the JSON text.
  `IngestedSignal.test: true` (on the envelope,
  never in `properties`) marks a test: it is accepted and shown like any other and the drain
  writes nothing (`test_signal`).
- **Drain:** `drain_ingestion_inbox(db, *, batch_size=10, max_batches=5, default_owner_by_customer=None, only=None, max_seconds=None, lock_timeout_ms=None) -> int`
  (rows processed), rows in `occurred_at` order. A request drains what it accepted with
  `only` (those rows, never another customer's), `max_seconds` (no batch claimed once spent)
  and `lock_timeout_ms` (a row waiting on another writer of the person fails that attempt and
  stays pending); the sweep drains the rest. One transaction per row, holding the row's lock while it is still this
  drain's claim: a claim reclaimed by another drain (stale after 300 s) is left to that drain,
  so a row's triggers are queued once. A failing row goes back to `pending` with
  `last_error` and `next_attempt_at` (1, 2, 4 ... 60 minutes after each attempt:
  `repositories.ingestion.retry_delay`), and ends `failed` after `max_attempts` (8). An
  `IngestionPermanentError` (a `fixed` rule's owner gone, no active seat, a seat with no Not
  Connected stage, a stored signal or rule stage that no longer validates;
  `IngestionRuleError` is one) fails the row on its first attempt. A row whose claim went stale
  on its last attempt is failed as `claim lost` (`IngestionInboxRepository.fail_lost_claims`).
  `requeue_signals(session, customer_id, *, inbox_ids=None, requested_by=) ->
  InboxRequeue(requeued, superseded)` puts failed rows back (`pending`, attempts 0), and,
  among the rows named in `inbox_ids` only, those `ignored` as `no_matching_rule` too (decided
  again by the rules as they are when drained), except a meeting signal a later one about the
  same `properties.event_id` supersedes; the caller commits. Rows end `completed` (outreach queued, `record_only` or `notify_only`) or `ignored`; `resolution` holds an
  `IngestionResolution` (`reason`: `ignored_by_rule`, `no_matching_rule`, `own_team`,
  `opted_out`, `test_signal`, `unresolved_identity`, `identity_escalated` (dedup could not tell
  the person apart: nothing placed, the claim kept in the ledger for the reconciler),
  `not_in_pipeline`, `not_from_an_inbound_source`, `notify_only`, `record_only`;
  `account_worked_outside_the_lane` only on rows before 2026-10-16; `contacts_blocked`:
  the contact types the owner could not get because another user holds them privately).
- **Rules:** the first enabled rule by priority decides. `create_lead` may add somebody new;
  `record_lead` places the person as `create_lead` does but wakes nobody: the signal is written
  as a processed `inbound_signal` (status `recorded`, read by outreach as handled; no
  `scheduled_at`, queued at `occurred_at`) the next walk reads as history, the `lead_claimed` of
  a connection it opened is settled with the same reason, a seat already holding the person
  keeps them whatever the strategy and no other seat hears of it;
  `attach_signal` reaches only a person an active seat of the customer is already connected
  to (anybody else, even a lead SimpL's shared base knows, is `not_in_pipeline`);
  `notify_only` records who the person is and who would own them, writing nothing; `ignore`.
  `match_conditions` take equality, `{"$exists": bool}` and `{"$any_word": [...]}` (the value
  holds one of the words as a whole word, any case: `{"person.job_title": {"$any_word":
  ["founder", "ceo"]}}`). `action_config.seller_approval_required: true` makes the walks the
  rule wakes write only drafts the seller approves; `action_config.playbook_section` names the
  playbook section its people follow; `action_config.wake_after_working_hours: n` makes the
  owner's trigger on a connection that already existed due `n` of the owner's working hours
  later (`BusinessHoursScheduler.add_working_hours` on their `active_hours`, default 09:00-18:00
  Europe/Rome Mon-Fri; a new connection still wakes at once with its `lead_claimed`).
  `action_config.move_to_stage: <mappings.lead_status id>` (`stage_rule.MOVE_TO_STAGE`) moves
  the connection the signal lands on to that stage, forward only by `PRECEDENCE`, never out of
  Opted out, Non sales, Disqualified or Lost, dated at `occurred_at` with `change_ref` `inbound_signal:<inbox id>`
  (after a booking's own move; not for a `properties.replayed` signal, nor a meeting fact
  `books_meetings` refuses). A rule may name only `MOVE_TO_STAGE_TARGETS` (Pending, Connected,
  Messaged, Meeting Booked, Negotiation, Closed, Customer): `validate_move_to_stage(value)`,
  which simpl_api's rule writer calls.
  `action_config.expects_reply: bool` (`reply_rule.EXPECTS_REPLY`) says whether somebody should
  answer the people the rule takes (the sales Home's "Inbound waiting for an answer"):
  `rule_expects_reply(action, action_config)` reads it, else the action's default (`create_lead`
  yes, the others no; an `ignore` rule never); `validate_expects_reply(action, value)` is the
  rule writer's check.
- **Plan and preview:** `planning.plan_signal(session, *, customer_id, signal,
  default_owner_user_id, hold, screen_tests=True, inbox_id=None) -> SignalPlan |
  IngestionResolution` is the drain's whole decision before it writes (rules, the test screen,
  own team and opt-outs, the person and their holders, a meeting fact's source check, the
  owner); the drain passes `hold=True` (the person's, the company keys' and the lead's locks).
  `preview_signal(session, *, customer_id, signal, default_owner_user_id=None) ->
  SignalPreview(action, rule_id, reason, owner_user_id, owner_chosen_by, takes_over_user_ids,
  lead_id, stage, wakes_agent, wake_after_working_hours, approval_required, expects_reply,
  playbook_section)` reads the same plan with no lock and no test screen (an example address
  reads as somebody new) and writes nothing: Settings > Inbound's "Try an event". `lead_id` is
  set only for a person the customer works; `stage` is `StagePreview(target, current, outcome)`
  with `outcome` `moves`, `kept`, `replayed`, `no_stage` or `another_lane`.
- **Playbook** (`services/ingestion/playbook.py`, store `playbook_store.py`): one per customer,
  `InboundPlaybook(free_text, sections=[PlaybookSection(key, label, applies_to{sources, names,
  channels in linkedin|email}, fit, steps=[{when, action, link}], links)])`, bounded (12 sections,
  20 steps, 10 http(s) links, 8,000 chars). `load_playbook(session, customer_id) -> StoredPlaybook`
  (revision 0 when none), `save_playbook(session, customer_id, playbook, replaces_revision=,
  updated_by=)` (a stale revision raises `repositories.ingestion.PlaybookRevisionConflict`; two
  first writes cannot both win). `playbook_section_for(playbook, rule_section=, source=, name=,
  channel=)` resolves the section: the rule's, else the first whose set lists all hold the
  value, else the one with an empty `applies_to`. The drain stamps its key on the trigger
  (`playbook_section`); outreach reads the playbook as the seller's own instructions.
  A signal no enabled rule matches is `no_matching_rule` and nothing is written, also for a
  customer with no rules at all (decision 7, 2026-10-16: no inbound process, no inbound; until
  then such a customer got every reachable person as a lead). The signal stays in the inbox,
  `ignored`; disabling every rule stops the lane.
- **Asking SimpL to answer** (the Home's "Ask SimpL to reply"):
  `triggers.request_inbound_answer(session, *, ulc_id, inbox_id, requested_by) ->
  InboundAnswerRequest(trigger_id, ulc_id, lead_id, inbox_id)` queues a copy of the signal's
  `inbound_signal` trigger on the connection, due now, with `seller_approval_required` forced
  (a signal a `record_lead` rule only recorded is answered too), and writes
  `inbound.answer.requested` (`INBOUND_ANSWER_REQUESTED_EVENT`; `event_data`: `inbox_id`,
  `trigger_id`, `requested_by_user_id`, `signal_name`, `origin`) in the same transaction, on the
  lead's lock (the drain's). Refused with `InboundAnswerRefused(reason)`: `pending` (an
  `inbound_signal` on the connection no walk took yet), `draft` (a lead-facing journey row
  waiting), `recent` (a request for the person at the same customer in the last 10 minutes,
  `ANSWER_REQUEST_COOLDOWN`), `no_signal`. The caller decides who may ask and commits.
- **Stops before any lookup:** a test is `test_signal`: `test` set, or the person's address
  or the company's domain on `example.com` / `.net` / `.org` or under `.test` / `.invalid`
  (the setup prompt's `ada@example.com` became a real lead in August). The customer's own
  people are `own_team`, by the rule email
  ingestion applies (`simpl_core.services.email.internal_identity`): a user's address or the
  customer's own mail domain (`+tag` ignored), an active seat's Supabase login, or a user's
  LinkedIn profile. An address in `gdpr.email_opt_out` is `opted_out`. Nothing else
  suppresses: a pending draft or a lost stage reaches the agent, which decides.
- **Identity (one person, one lead):** dedup's `LeadResolver` on a claim scoped to the
  receiving customer: by LinkedIn profile, then by email through
  `LeadContactRepository.leads_for_addresses(customer_id=…, addresses=…, preferred_user_id=None)`
  (global rows and rows private to the customer's users; never Dummy or nameless leads),
  then by entity resolution's name rules (`ResolutionService.resolve` under the `inbound`
  policy, with the signal's job title): a full name at the company the signal names, or in
  the customer's pipeline, among the leads the customer may see, and only when what we
  hold corroborates it (`resolution.person_evidence`). A lead found by email or name whose
  stored profile contradicts the signal's is somebody else (`profiles_contradict`). When the
  profile names
  one lead and the address another the customer holds privately or works
  (`LeadContactRepository.leads_customer_knows_by_address(customer_id=…, address=…) ->
  list[KnownAddressHolder]`, worked longest first), the address's lead wins and the
  profile's is reported as `IngestionResolution.twin_lead_id` (no merge), unless the
  customer worked the profile's lead first
  (`UserLeadConnectionRepository.customer_first_connected_at(customer_id=…, lead_id=…)`):
  then the profile's lead keeps the signal and the address's is the twin. When nothing
  matched (a profile no lead holds, the address's lead storing another one), the lead the
  person gave this customer's inbound that address for still takes the signal
  (`LeadContactRepository.leads_given_address(customer_id=…, address=…, source=…) ->
  list[UUID]`, private rows of `source` for the customer's users, first given first;
  `LeadMatch.EMAIL`): a profile link corrected between two signals is no second lead. A
  team's mailbox (`is_role_address`) keeps dedup's answer. A meeting fact
  (`calendar_booking`, `call_recording`) about somebody no seat holds is first looked for
  among the leads the customer's active seats work at the company the signal places them
  at or on their work domain's site
  (`UserLeadConnectionRepository.customer_leads_at_company(customer_id=…, company_id=…,
  site_host=…, limit=…) -> list[PipelineLead]`), by first and last name, accents and case
  aside (`resolution.pipeline_match.find_in_pipeline`, `LeadMatch.PIPELINE_NAME`). Profile URLs go
  through the one parser, `simpl_core.utils.linkedin_identity.linkedin_profile_identifier`
  (any LinkedIn host, the segment after `/in/`; a company page or another host is no
  profile).
- **Owner** (`OwnerStrategy`; `ingestion_rule.owner_strategy` stores `fixed`, or `default` /
  `existing` / `hint` / `round_robin`, which all mean `existing_owner`; a rule writer stores
  `STORED_OWNER_STRATEGY[strategy]`, i.e. `existing` or `fixed`): `existing_owner`
  keeps a person with the holder already connected to them (the most advanced stage
  wins among several), else the seat a switched-off holder hands off to
  (`OwnerChoice.HANDOFF`), else the signal's owner hint, the rule's owner, the host's default
  owner, the oldest active seat. Holders are the connections that work for the customer
  (`UserLeadConnectionRepository.work_connections_to_lead(customer_id=…, lead_id=…)`, through
  `sales.connection_customer`: a SimpL identity lent to the customer holds the people it works
  for it); a switched-off user's connection holds nobody, and its hand-off (the loan's, else
  `sales.user.handoff_to_user_id`) inherits the person when it is an active seat
  (`owner_routing.split_holders`). `fixed` is the rule's owner always (an inactive one fails
  the row). Every other seat holding the person gets an `inbound_signal` carrying
  `took_over_by_user_id`, once per stand-down: a seat whose newest signal already says this
  owner took the person (read in the order its walk reads them, by `occurred_at`) hears
  nothing more from later signals of the person, while a seat the person came back to in
  between, or a takeover by another owner, is told again. The owner's trigger carries
  `took_over_from_user_id` on every signal. A meeting fact goes to the seat its owner hint
  names for somebody new (whatever the rule), stays with the seat holding a held person,
  and takes nobody over (`resolve_owner(..., meeting_fact=True)`).
- **Writes:** somebody new is placed by `LeadPlacementService` (a LinkedIn profile makes a
  real lead of origin `inbound`, an address alone a `placeholder` lead in the claim ledger)
  on the company owner rule 2 places them at (`services/ingestion/company_placement.py`:
  the company typed, by page or site, then by its exact name, then a placeholder for it;
  else the work address's domain, found or founded as `domain:<host>`; else their current
  LinkedIn role; else their own stand-in, never `(Unknown company)`), with a pending claim
  the reconciler keeps trying while on a stand-in. The customer's own connections to the
  person carry that company (`place_customer_connections`); a typed company the lead's does
  not match goes on its inbound connections, never on the shared lead, and only when no
  seat holds the person through another lane
  (`UserLeadConnectionRepository.move_customer_connections(customer_id=…, lead_id=…,
  to_company_id=…, replace_origin=…)` replaces a company only on connections of
  `replace_origin`). Outbound, a reply naming another company the person answers for moves
  that one connection there (`conversation_company.move_connection_to_named_company(session,
  ulc_id=…, company=CompanyIdentity(…))`: their current roles first, then rule 2, never
  their address). A meeting fact applies only to a person one of the customer's inbound
  sources brought (2026-10-16): an `inbound.lead.received` whose `origin` is not a meeting
  fact, on a connection a user of the customer holds to the lead
  (`meetings.came_through_an_inbound_source(session, customer_id=…, lead_id=…) -> bool`).
  Anybody else (an investor who booked on the seller's page, an outbound lead, a person a
  calendar placed before) writes nothing: no lead, contact, connection, booking, trigger or
  event, the row `ignored` with `not_from_an_inbound_source`. A meeting fact about a person
  a source brought but any seat also holds through another lane books nothing
  (`meetings.books_meetings(signal, holder_origins=…)`). simpl_api's recording linker
  sends nobody to the lane: it creates a call's attendee nobody knew itself. Acquisition sources (`request_access`, forms) still open inbound connections anywhere. A parked lead
  moves once a later signal or the reconciler finds its company, and its connections and
  their journey runs follow it
  (`UserLeadConnectionRepository.reassign_company_for_lead(lead_id=…, from_company_id=…,
  to_company_id=…) -> CompanyReassignment(connections, journey_runs)`: only rows still on
  the old company move, so the app's cards, which file drafts under the run's company, show
  them under the new one). A lead on any other company never moves. Drains writing one
  lead take turns (an advisory lock on the lead), whatever keys their signals name the
  person by, and drains that may found one company take turns
  (`CompanyPlacementService.lock_lookup_keys`), so colleagues naming their new company at
  once found it once; the owner's connection in their Not Connected stage (origin
  `inbound`); the email and phone as contacts private to the owner
  (`LeadContactRepository.record_user_contact`: never global, never someone else's row); the
  triggers. A lead found by name alone is never written to (no profile, title or name), and
  gets no address or phone of a type its owner can already reach
  (`LeadContactRepository.find_best_contact`). `ingestion_rule.delay_seconds` is ignored.
- **Trigger:** `inbound_signal` (`INGESTION_TRIGGER_TYPE`, defined in
  `simpl_core.models.ingestion`), due at the transaction's `now()` like the `lead_claimed` a
  new connection queues, so both are claimed in one batch. The queue ranks it first, with
  replies and seller regenerates; unlike replies it waits out the seller's vacation, together
  with its `lead_claimed`.
  `trigger_data` is `InboundSignalTriggerData`: `trigger_source`, `inbox_id`, `occurred_at`,
  `signal{source,name,kind}`, `properties`, `payload` (the facts under the channel catalog's
  names: its `property` fields, or `properties` when the channel has no catalog; a field of
  any other role fills the signal's own fields and never reaches `payload`), `channel_id`,
  `channel_name`, `agent_context` (a rule's `action_config.agent_context` wins over the
  channel's), `page_path`, `anonymous_id`, `browsing_summary` (the page views the visitor
  left up to `occurred_at` on the site the channel names in `ChannelConfig.analytics_site_id`,
  newest 8 paths and the full `page_view_count`; `null` when the channel names no site),
  `consent`, `owner_strategy`
  (`OwnerChoice`: `existing_owner`, `fixed`, `owner_hint`, `rule_owner`, `default_owner`,
  `oldest_active_seat`), `took_over_from_user_id`, `took_over_by_user_id`,
  `seller_approval_required` (the rule's, read by simpl-outreach's `prefs_for_walk`),
  `playbook_section`, and the legacy aliases `source`, `kind`, `name`. `occurred_at` is the
  inbox row's.
- **Event:** every placed signal writes `inbound.lead.received`
  (`INBOUND_LEAD_RECEIVED_EVENT`, in `services.ingestion.base`) on the owner's connection in `outreach_events`, dated at
  the inbox row's `occurred_at`, with `inbox_id`, `connection_id`, `external_id`, `rule_id`,
  `action`, `origin` (the signal's `source`), `signal_name`, `signal_kind`, `company_id`
  (the connection's), and `event_id` / `capture_job_id` when the signal's properties carry
  them. Holders told of a takeover and `notify_only` signals get none.

## Reply Confidence settings and return to review

The Reply Confidence gate decides, for every inbound lead reply, whether SimpL sends its draft or asks the seller. A reply that clears its threshold is written `PENDING` with `scheduled_at` ten minutes ahead, `journey_log.auto_send_at` = `scheduled_at` (the queued state, below) and `payload.reply_confidence` (block `schema_version` 3, written by simpl-outreach) carrying `auto_approve: true`, what the stage decided. SimpL staff control it; the seller's consent is their own `outreach_prefs.auto_approval` (and its ICP overrides). There is no Python wrapper: call the SQL directly (`text()` with bound parameters). The maintainer applies the one migration, agents never do: `migrations/20261012_reply_confidence.sql` (not applied anywhere yet); its header says what it creates and how to apply it. Apply it before any service runs a `simpl_core` whose `JourneyLog` model has `auto_send_at`: every `select(JourneyLog)` reads that column.

**The queued state** is the column `sales_outreach.journey_log.auto_send_at timestamptz NULL` (`JourneyLog.auto_send_at`): the time a reply the pipeline queued to send on its own may leave, set by its writer and NULL on every other row. The BEFORE trigger `trg_journey_log_reply_confidence_auto_send_at` (on insert and on any update of `status`, `payload` or `auto_send_at`) keeps it only on a `PENDING` or `RUNNING` row (a sender's claim keeps it, a release back to `PENDING` too) whose `payload.authority.approved_by` is unset, and clears it on every other row, whoever moved it: return to review, the recall, the send-time checks, the email claim's hold, the dispatch authority rule, a send's completion, a seller's approval. When a queued reply leaves the queue for anything but the outcome of its own send (`COMPLETED`, `FAILED`, `ERROR`, the statuses `sales_outreach.mark_journey_log_completed` writes) and the write did not record the return itself, the trigger records it ("The audit trail" below). The trigger sorts after the other BEFORE triggers on `journey_log`, so it judges the status and the payload they settled on. What the block says (`auto_approve`) never makes a row queued.

Beside it, `journey_log.auto_send_draft_hash text NULL` is the draft a reply was queued with: when `auto_send_at` is set (by the insert, or by an update from NULL) the same trigger stamps it with `_reply_confidence_draft_hash(payload)`, the SHA-256 in hex of the JSON array `[payload.subject, payload.message]` (each `""` when missing); it keeps that value while the row stays queued, whatever a write puts in the column, and clears it with `auto_send_at`. Nothing else writes it, and no reader outside the send-time checks needs it.

**The block is written only by its owners**: the stage that scored the reply (simpl-outreach) and the moves above, which the database makes. The same holds for `payload.authority`, the dispatch authority rule's stamp and the approval it records. `JourneyLogRepository.update_payload_field` and `update_payload_fields`, the field-level editors, refuse a field naming `reply_confidence` or `authority`, or a dotted path under either (`reply_confidence.withdrawn`), before reading the row, and a batch naming one writes none of its fields: `ValueError("payload_field_not_writable: payload.authority, payload.reply_confidence")`, naming the keys the edit named (`assert_payload_fields_writable` and `OWNED_PAYLOAD_KEYS`, exported from `simpl_core.repositories.outreach`). `admin_override_fields` takes named fields only (channel, message type, status, scheduled time, content, subject), none of them either key. `update_payload` merges what it is given; a sender that spreads the payload back leaves both keys out.

**The table** `sales_outreach.reply_confidence_settings`: one row per scope, written by simpl_dashboard as `service_role`; sellers cannot read or write it.

| Column | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | uuid pk | |
| `scope` | text, `global` \| `customer` \| `user` | CHECKs tie the id columns and the value columns to it |
| `customer_id` | uuid, fk `sales.customer` on delete cascade | set on `customer` rows only |
| `user_id` | uuid, fk `sales."user"` on delete cascade | set on `user` rows only |
| `paused` | boolean | `global` row: pause everything |
| `auto_reply_enabled` | boolean | `customer` row: the customer switch |
| `email_enabled`, `linkedin_enabled` | boolean | `customer` and `user` rows |
| `threshold` | numeric(5,2), 0..100 | `customer` and `user` rows |
| `follows_customer` | boolean | `user` row |
| `updated_by` | uuid | the staff member who wrote it: the audit row's `user_id`, and `admin:<uuid>` as the recall's actor |
| `updated_at` | timestamptz | set on every update |

A value column belongs to the scopes the table names for it; on any other scope it must be NULL (`reply_confidence_settings_global_columns`, `..._customer_columns`, `..._user_columns`), so a `customer` row refuses `paused` and `follows_customer`, a `user` row `paused` and `auto_reply_enabled`, and the `global` row everything but `paused`.

At most one `global` row, one per `customer_id`, one per `user_id` (partial unique indexes `reply_confidence_settings_one_global` on `(scope) WHERE scope = 'global'`, `..._one_per_customer` on `(customer_id) WHERE scope = 'customer'`, `..._one_per_user` on `(user_id) WHERE scope = 'user'`; upsert with `ON CONFLICT (customer_id) WHERE scope = 'customer'` and so on). Missing rows are defaults: not paused; customer off, email off, LinkedIn off, threshold 90; the user follows the customer. A user's own flags and threshold stay stored while the user follows.

**The staff lookup** `sales_outreach.reply_confidence_settings_for(p_user_id uuid) RETURNS jsonb` (STABLE), the staff layers only:

```json
{"all_paused": false, "customer_id": "uuid or null", "customer_enabled": false,
 "email_enabled": false, "linkedin_enabled": false, "threshold": 90,
 "threshold_source": "default", "follows_customer": true}
```

- `email_enabled` / `linkedin_enabled` are effective: the customer's flag AND (`follows_customer` OR the user's own flag); a null flag is off. They fold in neither `customer_enabled` nor `all_paused`.
- `threshold` is the customer's when the user follows, else the user's own, falling back to the customer's when the user has none. `threshold_source` is `user`, `customer`, or `default` (90) when the customer has no threshold (no row, or null). Numbers come back without trailing zeros (`87.5`, `90`).
- An unknown user gets every per-customer and per-user default and `customer_id: null`; `all_paused` is the global row's for everyone.

**The resolver** `sales_outreach.reply_confidence_authorization(p_user_id uuid, p_icp_id uuid DEFAULT NULL) RETURNS jsonb` (STABLE) is the one answer to "may a reply to a lead of this user (and ICP) go out on its own?". The pipeline, the send-time check, the recall, simpl_api and the dashboard all ask it; do not re-derive seller consent elsewhere (and not with `parse_auto_approval_rules`, which uses `bool(...)`). It returns everything the staff lookup returns, plus:

```json
{"seller": {"enabled": true, "pause_on": false, "channels": ["linkedin_message", "email"],
            "icp_id": "uuid or null", "icp_restricted": false},
 "channels": {"email": {"allowed": true, "block": null, "block_kind": null},
              "linkedin_message": {"allowed": false, "block": "channel_off", "block_kind": "seller"}}}
```

Every layer must allow; each can only restrict; the first that refuses names the block:

1. the global pause → `all_paused`;
2. the customer switch → `customer_off`;
3. the channel's effective staff flag (`email_enabled` for `email`, `linkedin_enabled` for `linkedin_message`) → `channel_off`, `block_kind` `staff`;
4. the seller's `outreach_prefs.auto_approval`: `enabled` is JSON `true` (else `seller_paused` / `master_off`), `stop_on_lead_response` is JSON `false` (else `seller_paused` / `pause_on`), `channels` is an array holding the key (else `channel_off` / `seller`);
5. the lead's ICP (`sales.user_lead_connection.icp_id`; null applies nothing): when `outreach_prefs.icp_overrides[<icp_id>]` has an `auto_approval` key, its value must be an object (anything else, JSON `null` included, refuses both channels: `seller_paused` / `icp_restriction`), and every key present in it must pass the same test (`enabled`, `stop_on_lead_response` → `seller_paused` / `icp_restriction`; `channels` → `channel_off` / `seller`); an absent key inherits the base. An override entry that is not an object, or without `auto_approval`, restricts nothing. An override never widens what the base refuses.

Only exact JSON booleans count: `"true"`, `1`, `null` or a missing key are no consent. The delivery channel keys are exactly `email` and `linkedin_message` (`journey_log.payload.channel` on reply rows); any other channel (`linkedin_connect`, `call`, `calendar`, a different spelling, null) is never allowed: `channel_off` / `staff`, after layers 1 and 2. `seller.channels` is the base list (`[]` when missing or not an array); `seller.pause_on` is true unless the base pause is stored `false`; `icp_restricted` says whether the ICP layer alone refuses at least one channel.

**The one-channel wrapper** `sales_outreach.reply_confidence_send_block(p_user_id uuid, p_icp_id uuid, p_channel text) RETURNS text` (STABLE): NULL when allowed, else the block code (`all_paused`, `customer_off`, `channel_off`, `seller_paused`), the same layers in the same order.

**A call invite's confirmation** (ENG-1254, `20261120090000`) answers to two layers only: the global pause (`all_paused`) and the seller's "Inviti alle call automatici": `auto_approval.call_invites` when stored (true/false always wins), else `auto_approval.enabled`, on only as JSON `true` (else `seller_paused` / `call_invites_off`; Raff 2026-11-20); a channel outside the two delivery channels is `channel_off` / `staff`. The customer switch, SimpL's channel flags, the seller's master switch, pause, channel list and ICP override do not hold it. The resolver gives it per channel as `call_invite_channels`, in the shape of `channels`, and as one boolean `call_invites_allowed` (simpl_api's ready-invites job reads it). A queued reply is a call invite's confirmation when its block's `versions.send_rule` (comma-separated) names `call_invite`; `sales_outreach.reply_confidence_queued_send_block(p_user_id uuid, p_icp_id uuid, p_payload jsonb) RETURNS text` (STABLE) answers such a reply by those two layers and any other through `reply_confidence_send_block`, and the recall and the send-time check ask it.

**A farewell with a scheduled follow-up** (`20261120150000`, Raff 2026-11-20) answers to two layers only: the global pause and the seller's "Send goodbyes without my approval" (`auto_approval.closing_reply_auto_approve` stored as JSON `true`, else `seller_paused` / `farewell_off`), as the close handler's farewell. The resolver gives it per channel as `farewell_channels`; `reply_confidence_queued_send_block` answers a queued reply whose `versions.send_rule` names `farewell_followup` by those two layers.

**Queued** has one definition, used by every reader here and the predicate of the partial index `ix_journey_log_reply_confidence_queued` on `journey_log (auto_send_at)`, so a reader that writes it so scans the index:

```sql
jl.status = 'PENDING' AND jl.auto_send_at IS NOT NULL
```

The send-time checks judge a row with `auto_send_at` set, `PENDING` or `RUNNING`.

**The decisions index** `ix_journey_log_reply_confidence_created`, a partial index on `journey_log (created_at, id)` for `payload ? 'reply_confidence'`, holds only the rows that carry a block. It serves the monitoring reads of the decisions in a time window: a page newest first, keyset-paged on `(created_at, id)`, or an aggregate over the window. A reader, a view included, writes the predicate exactly so beside its bounds on `created_at`, and the planner then reads those rows alone instead of scanning `journey_log`:

```sql
jl.payload ? 'reply_confidence' AND jl.created_at >= $1 AND jl.created_at < $2
```

The owner of a queued reply is the user of the `user_lead_connection` the row names itself, `journey_log.ulc_id`, as for the email claim. `JourneyLogRepository.is_owned_by` / `get_owned_for_user` / `load_owned_for_user_locked` (and `PendingActionsRepository.get_details`) read that rule widened by one case (`owned_by`): a row on a connection a SimpL identity opened under a loan to the user's customer (`identity_loan_id`, open or ended loan) is each of that customer's people's to approve, edit or reject; the reply-confidence functions below keep the owner alone. Return to review, the counts and the recall all use it, so a reply any of them lists or counts is one its seller can return; a row with no `ulc_id` of its own (one only its `journey_run` ties to a seller) is nobody's to them. The send-time checks judge the row as the dispatch authority rule's owner (`journey_log.ulc_id`, else `journey_run.ulc_id`), the user it would be sent as.

**Return to review** `sales_outreach.reply_confidence_return_to_review(p_user_id uuid, p_reason text, p_journey_log_id uuid DEFAULT NULL, p_channel text DEFAULT NULL, p_actor text DEFAULT NULL) RETURNS uuid[]`:

- Moves a row only when it is queued, belongs to `p_user_id`, and matches `p_journey_log_id` / `p_channel` when given. `p_channel` is `email` or `linkedin` (every `linkedin*` channel); `p_reason` is `seller_returned` or `settings_changed`; `p_actor` is who returns it, `user:<uuid>` (the seller), `admin:<uuid>` (SimpL staff) or `system:<name>`, required despite its default; anything else raises `invalid_parameter_value`.
- In one UPDATE: status `PENDING_APPROVAL`; `auto_send_at` NULL; `scheduled_at` = `least(scheduled_at, now())`, so an approval after the return goes out at once; in `payload.reply_confidence`: `withdrawn: {"reason", "at" (ISO), "by": p_actor}`, `{"code": p_reason, "class": "policy", "text_key": p_reason, "detail": ""}` appended to `reasons`, and `top_reason: {"code": p_reason, "text_key": p_reason, "params": {}}`; what the stage decided (`auto_approve`, `route_kind`, the score) stays as it was, and a payload without a block gets one holding the reason alone. Nothing else in the payload changes, except `payload.authority`, which the dispatch authority trigger refreshes on the way into `PENDING_APPROVAL`. The recall and the send-time check use the same rewrite (one helper, `_reply_confidence_withdraw`), with reason `settings_changed` and `detail` = the block code, or, for a block that does not hold, reason `auto_approval_invalid`, and for a draft that changed since it was queued, reason `draft_changed`, each with `detail` `""`; so does the clearing trigger for every other move ("The audit trail" below).
- Writes one `reply_confidence.returned_to_review` event per row moved (see "The audit trail" below).
- Returns the ids moved, `{}` when none: a row a sender already picked up is `RUNNING`, one the seller approved or one already returned does not match. The authority trigger may still settle a moved row on `CANCELLED` (objective closed, user gone). simpl_api turns an empty array into its 409 `not_returnable`; check the row is the caller's first (404), because the function trusts `p_user_id`.

**The recall** re-evaluates each queued reply of the users a change reaches with its own ICP and channel through `reply_confidence_queued_send_block`, and returns to review (`settings_changed`, `detail` = the block) only the ones no longer allowed. It moves `PENDING` rows only, never a claimed (`RUNNING`) one, and never queues anything again when a restriction is lifted.

- A settings row insert, update or delete reaches every user with something queued (global row), the customer's users (customer row) or the user (user row). It reads the current values only, so it is idempotent and a threshold change alone recalls nothing. The actor (`withdrawn.by`) is `admin:<updated_by>`, or `system:reply_confidence_settings` on a delete or a row without `updated_by`.
- An update of `sales."user".outreach_prefs` that changes `auto_approval` or `icp_overrides` reaches that seller; the actor is `user:<seller id>`. A restriction on ICP A leaves ICP B's replies queued; a channel taken out of the list recalls only that channel.
- The recall never fails or stalls the write that fires it. Each trigger runs it inside `BEGIN ... EXCEPTION WHEN OTHERS`: an error rolls back the recall alone (every move of that recall) and raises a `WARNING` (`recall skipped (<SQLSTATE>: <message>)`), and the settings or prefs write commits. The recall runs with a short `lock_timeout`, so a queued row another transaction holds locked (a sender's claim) ends it with `55P03` instead of making the write wait. Replies a skipped recall left queued are judged against the new values by the send-time check before any send.
- Each move writes one `reply_confidence.recalled` event; a recall rolled back takes its events with it.

**The send-time checks.** `sales_outreach.recheck_journey_log_authority` (the executor's last call before the provider, wrapped by `JourneyLogRepository.recheck_send_authority`; simpl_flow's email sender and the LinkedIn guard call it) first judges a queued reply (`auto_send_at` set), after the cancel step. One whose block does not carry the stage's approval (an object with `auto_approve` JSON `true` and a numeric `schema_version` of 3 or more) can never go out on its own and goes, `PENDING` or `RUNNING`, back to `PENDING_APPROVAL` with the same rewrite (reason `auto_approval_invalid`, `detail` `""`): `{"outcome": "review", "reason": "reply_confidence_auto_approval_invalid", "status": "PENDING_APPROVAL"}`. So does one whose draft is not the one it was queued with (`_reply_confidence_draft_hash(payload)` differs from `auto_send_draft_hash`: the subject or the message was edited since, by the seller, staff or any writer), whatever its delay: reason `draft_changed` (also its `text_key`), `detail` `""`, `{"outcome": "review", "reason": "reply_confidence_draft_changed", "status": "PENDING_APPROVAL"}`; the edited draft stays for the seller to approve. While `auto_send_at` is ahead, a claimed row goes back to `PENDING` with `scheduled_at` no earlier than `auto_send_at`, so the claim takes it again once it may leave, `{"outcome": "release", "reason": "reply_confidence_undo_window_open", "status": "PENDING"}`, and a `PENDING` row is left as it is, `{"outcome": "skip", ...}`. The authority trigger judges either move on the way and may settle the row on `PENDING_APPROVAL` (`"review"`) or `CANCELLED` (`"cancel"`). It then judges a queued reply, `PENDING` or `RUNNING`, after the seat check: if `reply_confidence_send_block(owner, lead ICP, payload.channel)` refuses, the row goes back to `PENDING_APPROVAL` in the same statement with the same rewrite, and the call answers `{"outcome": "review", "reason": "reply_confidence_settings_changed", "detail": <block>, "status": "PENDING_APPROVAL"}` (`"cancel"` / `"CANCELLED"` when the authority trigger cancelled it on the way). Each of these moves to review carries the actor `system:send_time_check` and writes one `reply_confidence.withdrawn_at_send` event; the release and the skip write none. A cancel request and a switched-off seat still answer first. Every other row, a returned reply the seller approved again included, is judged as before; nothing reads `payload.response_automation` for it. The guarantee: a restrictive setting visible to the last recheck prevents the send; a provider call already allowed may finish. The LinkedIn pre-send guard calls the recheck after its own checks and reports these answers as `block_reason` `authority_reply_confidence_undo_window_open`, `authority_reply_confidence_auto_approval_invalid`, `authority_reply_confidence_draft_changed` and `authority_reply_confidence_settings_changed`.

**The audit trail.** Every move of a queued reply out of the queue for anything but its send is recorded as a return, in the same transaction: the rewrite above (`withdrawn`, `reasons`, `top_reason`) and one `sales_outreach.outreach_events` row, `event_type` `reply_confidence.returned_to_review`, `reply_confidence.recalled` or `reply_confidence.withdrawn_at_send` for the moves above, `reply_confidence.left_queue` for any other; `event_status` `success`; `action_id` the journey_log id; `ulc_id`, `user_id` and `lead_id` of the connection the row is sent for (`journey_log.ulc_id`, else its run's; null when the connection is gone); `agent_type` the row's `action_type`; and

```json
{"journey_log_id": "uuid",
 "reason": "seller_returned | settings_changed | auto_approval_invalid | draft_changed | send_hold | dispatch_authority | cancelled | completed_externally | approved | held_for_review",
 "detail": "<see the reason, or empty>", "actor": "user:<uuid> | admin:<uuid> | system:<name>",
 "previous_status": "PENDING | RUNNING", "new_status": "<the status the row settled on>"}
```

The same reason and actor are `payload.reply_confidence.withdrawn.reason` and `.by`, and the reason is the `code` and the `text_key` of the reason appended to `reasons` and of `top_reason`. Any move whose write left `withdrawn` unchanged is recorded by the clearing trigger (`reply_confidence.left_queue`), with the reason of the move:

- `send_hold`: the email claim's hold handed it back (`payload.send_hold`); `detail` its reason (`auto_approval_off`, `approval_lapsed`, `mailbox_reconnected`); actor `system:email_send_hold`.
- `dispatch_authority`: the dispatch authority rule held it for review or cancelled it (`payload.authority.outcome` `review` or `cancel`), at a sender's claim, at the send-time recheck or on a cancel request there; `detail` the rule's reason (`account_handed_off`, `cancel_requested`, ...); actor `system:dispatch_authority`.
- `cancelled`: any other writer cancelled it; `detail` `payload.cancellation.reason`, `""` when none; actor `system:other_writer`.
- `completed_externally`: it was marked `EXECUTED`, done outside the pipeline (`JourneyRepository.mark_action_completed_externally`, e.g. the seller sent it from their own inbox); `detail` `payload.external_completion.reason`; actor `system:other_writer`.
- `approved`: an approver was stamped on it while `PENDING` or `RUNNING`; `detail` `""`; actor `system:other_writer`.
- `held_for_review`: any other move; `detail` the new status; actor `system:other_writer`.

So a reader decides from the row alone: `withdrawn` set → returned (its reason says by what); `auto_send_at` set on a `PENDING` or `RUNNING` row → queued; `auto_approve` true on an ended row without `withdrawn` → sent on its own.

Every insert, update or delete of a `reply_confidence_settings` row writes one `sales.audit_log` row: `operation` `reply_confidence_settings_insert` / `_update` / `_delete`, `table_name` `sales_outreach.reply_confidence_settings`, `record_count` 1, `user_id` the row's `updated_by` (null on a delete), and `details` (text) `{"scope", "customer_id", "user_id", "changed": {<column>: {"from", "to"}}}` naming the value columns that changed (`paused`, `auto_reply_enabled`, `email_enabled`, `linkedin_enabled`, `threshold`, `follows_customer`); an update that changes none of them writes nothing.

**The counts** `sales_outreach.reply_confidence_queued_counts(p_customer_id uuid DEFAULT NULL) RETURNS TABLE (user_id uuid, channel text, queued bigint)` (STABLE): queued replies per seller and `payload.channel` as stored, aggregated in the database, for one customer or (NULL) all.

**What a change would do** `sales_outreach.reply_confidence_what_if(p_customer_id uuid, p_change jsonb DEFAULT '{}') RETURNS jsonb` (STABLE) answers, for one customer, what a settings change would do, without making it. The dashboard's switch-on and recall previews ask it instead of computing the layers themselves.

- `p_change` is `{"customer": {<column>: <value>}, "users": {"<user id>": {<column>: <value>}}}`, both parts optional: what the upsert of the customer's row and of each user's row would write, every other column kept. The customer's columns are `auto_reply_enabled`, `email_enabled`, `linkedin_enabled` and `threshold`, a user's `follows_customer`, `email_enabled`, `linkedin_enabled` and `threshold`; each value is a JSON boolean (a number for `threshold`) or `null`, read through the table's column types (a threshold keeps two decimals). The table's CHECKs, such as the threshold's 0 to 100, stay the write's to enforce. An unknown customer raises `no_data_found`; a malformed change, or a user of another customer, `invalid_parameter_value`.
- The users reached are the ones the recall would judge after those writes: every user of the customer when the change names the customer's row, else the users it names, ordered by id. Users with a banned login are listed like the others; leaving them out is the caller's choice.
- The answer:

```json
{"customer_id": "uuid",
 "customer": {"before": {"all_paused": false, "customer_enabled": false, "threshold": 90, "...": "..."},
              "after": {"...": "..."}},
 "users": [{"user_id": "uuid",
            "before": {"settings": {"...": "..."},
                       "channels": {"email": {"allowed": false, "block": "customer_off", "block_kind": null},
                                    "linkedin_message": {"...": "..."}}},
            "after": {"settings": {"...": "..."}, "channels": {"...": "..."}},
            "seller_allows": {"email": true, "linkedin_message": false},
            "returned": 1}],
 "returned": 1}
```

- `customer` is the staff lookup of a user who follows the customer: the customer's switch, flags and threshold, the default threshold when none is stored. Each user's `settings` and `channels`, `before` and `after`, are what `reply_confidence_settings_for` and `reply_confidence_authorization` (no ICP) answer before the change and once it is made. `seller_allows` is the seller's own consent alone (layer 4) per channel, what a staff switch-on can start. `returned` is how many of the user's queued replies the recall would then return to review, each judged with its own ICP and channel: every queued reply the settings refuse after the change, one refused already included, as the recall moves it; the top-level `returned` is their sum.

**The settings screen in one read** `sales_outreach.reply_confidence_settings_overview(p_customer_id uuid DEFAULT NULL) RETURNS jsonb` (STABLE) answers everything the dashboard's settings screen shows, so it makes one call instead of reading the rows, the logins and each user's answers one by one:

```json
{"default_threshold": 90,
 "global": {"scope": "global", "paused": false, "...": "..."},
 "customer": {"id": "uuid", "name": "text",
              "row": {"scope": "customer", "auto_reply_enabled": true, "threshold": 85.00, "...": "..."},
              "settings": {"all_paused": false, "customer_enabled": true, "threshold": 85,
                           "threshold_source": "customer", "...": "..."}},
 "users": [{"id": "uuid", "full_name": "text", "email": "text", "is_active": true, "disabled": false,
            "row": null,
            "settings": {"...": "the staff layers"},
            "channels": {"email": {"allowed": true, "block": null, "block_kind": null},
                         "linkedin_message": {"...": "..."}},
            "seller": {"enabled": true, "pause_on": false, "channels": ["email"],
                       "allows": {"email": true, "linkedin_message": false}},
            "icp_overrides": [{"icp_id": "uuid", "restricted": true,
                               "channels": {"email": {"...": "..."}, "linkedin_message": {"...": "..."}}}],
            "ignored_icp_consent": [{"icp_id": "uuid", "icp_title": "text", "channels": ["email"]}]}],
 "queued": [{"user_id": "uuid", "channel": "email", "queued": 2}]}
```

- `default_threshold` is the staff lookup's threshold for a user with no settings anywhere (the `default` source), so nobody asks `reply_confidence_settings_for` with an unknown user to learn it.
- `global`, `customer.row` and each user's `row` are the stored `reply_confidence_settings` rows (every column, `to_jsonb`), `null` when there is none. `customer.settings` is the staff lookup of a user who follows the customer: its switch, flags and threshold, the default when none is stored (`reply_confidence_what_if`'s `customer.before`).
- `users` are every user of the customer, ordered by `full_name` (nulls last), then `id`. `disabled` is true while their Supabase login is banned (`auth.users.banned_until` in the future); a user without a login is not disabled. Disabled users are listed like the others; leaving them out of lists and counts is the caller's choice.
- Each user's `settings`, `channels` and `seller` (`enabled`, `pause_on`, `channels`) are what `reply_confidence_authorization(user)` answers, with no ICP; `seller.allows` is, per channel, whether the seller's own consent alone (layer 4) lets a reply go, `reply_confidence_what_if`'s `seller_allows`. `icp_overrides` has one entry per key of `outreach_prefs.icp_overrides` that is a uuid, ordered by it: `restricted` and `channels` are `reply_confidence_authorization(user, icp)`'s `seller.icp_restricted` and `channels`. A key that is no uuid matches no lead's ICP and is left out.
- `ignored_icp_consent` lists, ordered by `icp_id`, each of those ICPs whose override's `auto_approval`, merged key by key over the base `outreach_prefs.auto_approval`, allows a channel that the base alone refuses, both judged by the seller-consent rule the resolver applies to the base (`enabled`, `stop_on_lead_response`, `channels`). `channels` are those channels, `email` before `linkedin_message`; `icp_title` is the ICP's `sales.icp.title`, `null` when there is none. The resolver judges the base first and an ICP override only restricts it, so no reply for such an ICP goes out on its own on those channels; the outreach routing and the email send hold (`JourneyLogRepository.hold_unconfirmed_email_sends_for_user`) read the merged preferences, so that ICP's auto-approved messages still go out without the seller. It does not depend on the staff layers: like `seller.allows`, it says what a staff switch-on would not start. A user without such an override has `[]`.
- `queued` is `reply_confidence_queued_counts(p_customer_id)` as rows.
- With `p_customer_id` NULL the answer is the global part alone: `customer` null, `users` `[]`, and `queued` over every customer. An unknown customer raises `no_data_found`.

**Grants.** The table (`SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE`) and `reply_confidence_settings_for`, `reply_confidence_authorization`, `reply_confidence_send_block`, `reply_confidence_return_to_review`, `reply_confidence_queued_counts`, `reply_confidence_what_if`, `reply_confidence_settings_overview` (`EXECUTE`) are granted to `service_role` only; `PUBLIC`, `anon` and `authenticated` have nothing, and the internal helpers (leading underscore) and trigger functions are granted to nobody. simpl_api and simpl-outreach connect as the owner. The functions are `SECURITY DEFINER` with `search_path = pg_catalog` and run as the role that applied the migration (`postgres`), which `reply_confidence_settings_overview` needs to read `auth.users` past its row-level security.

## Company locations (`services.location`)

`sales.company.location_id` points at a `geolocation.locations` row, keyed by its unique
`original_location` text. `LocationLinker` finds or creates that row without calling
anything outside the database (a stub; simpl_flow's `gcp_location_parser_flow` geocodes it
later), and `LocationInputParser.clean` makes the key from a free text.

- `LocationLinker().resolve_location_id(location=..., session=...)` (and
  `resolve_location_ids`): the row of the bare text. Unchanged.
- `LocationLinker().resolve_location_id_for_country(location=..., country=...,
  session=...)` (0.8.360): the `location_id` for a company whose country is known (a code
  or a name). A bare locality ("Cambridge", "Milan") can name a row geocoded to another
  country, so:
  - the row of the text qualified with the country's name ("Milano, Italy") links unless
    it was geocoded to another country;
  - else the bare text's row links only when it was geocoded to the company's country (a
    bare row not geocoded yet, or with a country no reader knows such as the geocoder's
    "n/a", could be anywhere);
  - else the qualified text's stub is created (a row with the same text inserted meanwhile
    is reused) and its id returned;
  - None when there is nothing to link, or the qualified text already has a row in another
    country.

  With no country, or one no reader knows, the bare text links as `resolve_location_id`
  links it, wherever its row is. It inserts on the caller's session; the caller commits.
- The same rules as pure functions, for callers that read and write the rows themselves
  (simpl_scraping's `company_profile` profile, inside an agent-framework pipeline), from
  `simpl_core.services.location`: `location_key` (the cleaned text), `country_iso3` (ISO-3
  from a code or a name), `iso3_from_alpha2` (ISO-3 from an alpha-2 code only),
  `qualified_location_key(location, iso3)`, `location_keys(location, countries)`
  (qualified keys first, then the bare text), `may_link_place(place_country, iso3,
  qualified=...)` and `place_to_link(location, iso3, places)` (`places`: each key's row
  country as read; returns the key to link or the key to create).
- The qualified text adds pycountry's common name ("South Korea"), else its ISO short
  `name` ("Saint Helena, Ascension and Tristan da Cunha"). The name is kept once only when
  the text already has it as a whole comma-separated part, in any case: "Cambridge, United
  Kingdom" stays, "Cambridge, UK" becomes "Cambridge, UK, United Kingdom".
- Country names and codes are read once, in `simpl_core.utils.countries`
  (`country_alpha2`, `country_names`, `COUNTRY_ALIASES`): pycountry's codes and names, plus
  the names people and the geocoder write that pycountry lacks ("Russia", "The Gambia",
  "Vatican City", "São Tomé and Príncipe"). `utils.lead_timezone` and
  `LocationInputParser.extract_components` (which part of a text is the country) read
  through it; add a name there, once.
- `CompanyWriteService.create`/`update` still link a written `location` by its bare text
  and set `location_id` from it, whatever `location_id` the payload carries.
- `JobOfferWriteService.bulk_create` writes an offer it already holds (by `job_id`) only
  for the fields whose value differs, and not at all when none does (0.8.385). A uuid
  given as text and a timestamp given for a date column compare as the column loads
  them. Its second count is still every offer the batch matched, written or not.

## Common pitfalls

- Do not duplicate autopilot, CRM, email, or report logic in app repos before checking whether `simpl_core` already owns it.
- Do not retry a database failure without classifying it first: retrying a constraint violation or a bad query burns the backoff budget and fails identically.
- Do not add optional provider SDK usage without guarding it behind the relevant extra or import boundary.
- Do not run tests with global Python. Activate `conda activate simpl_core`, then use `poetry run ...`.

## Testing

```bash
conda activate simpl_core
poetry install
poetry run pytest
poetry run ruff check .
```

The DB-backed tests need Docker running (an ephemeral Postgres via testcontainers)
and a `psql` client on `PATH` (the schema is applied with `psql`). On macOS,
`export PATH="/opt/homebrew/opt/libpq/bin:$PATH"` before running the suite.

## Agent memory

Agents opt into memory with a `memory:` policy on the agent definition. An agent with
no `memory:` block makes zero memory calls and needs none of what follows.

**Dependency and migration.** `simpl_core[agent-framework]` now pulls `simpl_memory`.
Apply `migrations/20260719_agent_framework_memory_run_level_ops.sql` before any agent
opts into `anticipate` or `observe`: it widens the `memory_runs.op` CHECK to admit
`anticipate` / `prefetch` / `interject`, and without it those writes are rejected.

**Memory belongs to `llm_agent`.** A `memory:` block on a `deterministic` agent is
rejected at validation — a code producer has no context window to enrich and can
hold no tools, so every layer would be inert.

**Every memory capability comes from policy.** An agent never names a memory tool in
`available_tools` — the loader rejects it. `write.mode: hot_path` offers `memory_save`,
`recall.when: on_demand` offers `memory_recall`, and `observe` injects the watcher's
`reasoning_checkpoint`. The framework stages that effective set on
`ResolverContext.producer_tools`; a consumer building its own producer must bind *that*
rather than `definition.available_tools`, or injected tools never reach the model. The
bundled `LLMAgent` already does.

**Delivery is `project_into`, and only `project_into`.** A recalled block reaches a
producer by being written onto the `ai_context` object your resolver-context factory
supplies, at the leaf the policy names. The ephemeral surface is an audit mirror, not a
delivery channel. So the target must exist and must accept that attribute — a
`@dataclass(slots=True)` context without the declared leaf cannot receive it. Both
failure modes are logged (`memory_projection_no_target`, `memory_projection_failed`) and
neither fails the node, because the recall succeeded and only the handoff broke.

**Projection paths must not nest.** The reflex writes a string at its
`retrieve.project_into`; anticipation writes a namespace at its `anticipate.project_into`
(default `ai_context.memory_brief`). If one is nested under the other, the layer that
writes second overwrites the first. The boot gate reports this.

**A background write may produce several memories.** `write.mode: background` runs the
agent named in `write.agent` after a passing produce and ingests what it returns. Its
verdict evidence may carry a list under `BACKGROUND_WRITE_MEMORIES_KEY` (`"memories"`),
and each item is written separately — one provider call and one `memory_runs` row each,
because a memory is the unit the store dedups, supersedes and recalls. Evidence without
that key is ingested whole, as one memory.

**The framework runs the extractor; the host does not.** The agent named in
`write.agent` is resolved off the walk's own Loader and run through the framework's one
agent executor, so it is pinned to the run's config version and audited like any other
agent. The host owes only an LLM transport (`llm_client` / `llm_client_resolver` on the
coordinator) — there is no `background_write_spec_for` callback any more. A typed-policy
extractor with no host resolver runs on `default_llm_client_resolver()`; a bare-name one
with no `llm_client` gets a logged, isolated failure rather than a silent no-op.

**The extractor's per-memory contract is `ExtractedMemory`** (exported from
`simpl_core.agent_framework`): `text` (required), `kind`, `tags`, `scope_level`,
`confidence`. It **forbids** unexpected fields — a returned item carrying one is
rejected on its own, logged, and counted into the verdict evidence under
`"rejected_memories"`; the other items still write. An item with no `scope_level`
is written at `user`. Ask the model for a field, and add it here in the same change:
the whole point of the typed contract is that the two cannot drift silently.

Keep the extractor's job to *what is worth remembering*. Do not ask it for entities,
relations, or supersession decisions: an agent writes memory for its own use, and
`simpl_memory` resolves concepts from the entry body at index time and arbitrates
supersession in its own dreaming pipeline. Structure hand-built by an extractor
duplicates that work and ages badly.

**Anticipation is per agent.** `anticipate.when: agent_start` fires once for each agent
that declares it, before its first turn — not once per run, and not per rewrite
iteration. The watcher's two-call fallback still needs a `watcher_telemetry_for` hook
supplying `thinking_tokens`; without it only the in-arc path runs.

**Failure posture.** `retrieve` failures propagate and fail the node — a declared
recall is a required input. Everything else (anticipation, the watcher, the ledger)
is best-effort: it logs and the run proceeds. When testing, use
`simpl_core.agent_framework.testing.strict_run`, which turns those swallowed
failures into test failures.

## Sandbox tools for agents (`code_task`, `browse_task`)

**Status: experimental** (Vercel Sandbox on a personal Hobby account, test data only).
An agent's `sandbox` policy offers two tools; the sandboxes, the sub-agent and the
isolation live in `simpl_agent_sandbox` (a dependency of the `agent-framework` extra).
Importing the framework never loads it: which tools a policy offers is
`agent_framework.sandbox_policy`, and the tools come from their own modules
(`sandbox_tools`, `sandbox_runs`, `sandbox_specs`; not re-exported by
`simpl_core.agent_framework`), loaded only by a coordinator given a `sandbox_host` and
by the loader's boot check when an agent declares a `sandbox` policy. A host with no
sandboxes is unaffected by the sandbox release in its lock.

- `code_task(tasks=[...])`: each task is `{task, inputs: [file refs], checks: [...]}`; a
  helper writes and runs code in a box with no network until every check on the files
  it writes to `/workspace/out` passes (`file_exists`, `file_size`, `pdf`, `image`,
  `video`, `csv`, `json_keys`).
- `browse_task(tasks=[...])`: each task is `{task, start_url, goals, allow_downloads}`;
  a helper drives a browser on public sites only, starting at `start_url` (which must
  be public) and never reaching localhost, private, link-local or other non-public
  addresses (D28). Goals: `download`, `page_at` (host and path prefix, at least one of these),
  `answer_contains`. Never put private data in a browse task: a page can trick the
  helper into carrying it to another site.
- Up to 6 tasks per call, `parallel` (default 3) at once; 8 sandboxes per process and 2
  browsers starting on one site at most; each task gets its share of the call's
  remaining time.
- The reply is a compact dict: per task `outcome`, `done`, `checks`, kept files' `ref`s,
  and `untrusted_summary` / `untrusted_answer` cut to 4,000 characters (plus
  `untrusted_final_url` for a browse task). Treat those texts as data. The audit
  (`tool_call_runs`) stores exactly that reply.

```yaml
timeout_seconds: 900           # required with a sandbox block, at least 180
sandbox:
  provider: vercel
  tools: [code_task, browse_task]   # left out: code_task only
  allow_downloads: true        # browse_task downloads into the file store; left out: off
```

The host wires `NodeCoordinator(..., sandbox_host=SandboxToolHost(...))` (from
`agent_framework.sandbox_runs`; without a host no sandbox tool is offered): the sub-agent's
model (`structured_client_sub_agents(deepseek_client(key))`), a `SandboxFileStore`
(`load(ref) -> (name, bytes)`, `save(...) -> ref`) and `on_usage`, which receives one
`SandboxUsageReport` per sandbox for cost recording (without it a tracking gap is
reported). It registers the provider in `simpl_agent_sandbox.SandboxProviderRegistry`.
A host running its own agent loop (simpl_ops) offers the same `code_task` /
`browse_task` callables and wraps its run in `bind_sandbox_tool_context(...)`. Guide:
`docs/agent_framework/13-sandboxes.md`.

## Experiment tools for agents

`simpl_core.agent_framework.experiment_tools` lets an agent read a trainer's code and plan,
run and watch experiments of a `simpl_foundry` recipe. It needs the `agent-framework` and
`foundry` extras, and the package does not re-export it. Importing it registers
`experiment_code`, `experiment_plan`, `experiment_submit`, `experiment_progress`,
`experiment_results`, `experiment_diff`, `experiment_leaderboard`, `experiment_stop` and
`experiment_notes` in `ToolRegistry` (`EXPERIMENT_TOOL_IDS`); list them in an agent's
`available_tools`. Bind what the model must not choose around its run
(`simpl_core.agent_framework.experiment_context`):

```python
with bind_experiment_tool_context(
    ExperimentToolContext(recipes={"example": path}, database=settings)
):
    ...
```

A candidate is `{"overrides": {...}, "patch": "<unified diff from the repository root>",
"base": "<experiment hash>", "hypothesis": "<what it should do and why>"}`, all but
`overrides` optional. A refusal is a result (`{"status": "refused", "reason": ...}`), not
an exception. Recipes, sweeps and what they run are `simpl_foundry`'s: see its docs.

**Where jobs run** (`experiment_placement`). The context's `target` (`batch`, `cloud_run`,
`leonardo`; `local` for tests), `compute` (the options `simpl_foundry launch` takes:
`gpus`, `cpus`, `memory_gb`, `hours`, `nodes`, `queue`) and `files_only` are resolved by
`simpl_foundry.targets.destination`, as `launch` and `sweep run` resolve them: a recipe's
`compute` section with the project's settings, or its own `resources` section. What it
refuses (an unset setting, a request the platform cannot give) is a refusal naming it. One
trial costs what the resolved job asks for: `max_hours` x GPUs on Batch and Cloud Run, the
allocation's hours x GPUs x 4 attempts on Leonardo (a time limit requeues a job up to
3 times). On Leonardo the context needs `leonardo` (`LeonardoAccess`: the settings and the
SSH login), and each sweep is checked there (`check_launch`: the image of the code's lock)
before it is queued. `created_by` names the submitter of every sweep (`campaign:<id>`).

**Where unreviewed code may run.** A patch or a base trains code no person read, which can
use everything its job holds and reaches. `experiment_plan` and `experiment_submit` refuse
such a candidate, before its text reaches git, unless the context has `allow_patches` and
its jobs run:

- on Cloud Run, files-only, as the project's files-only account: no secret, no database
  login, and with the network settings no internet;
- on Leonardo: the job sees only its own run's files and reaches no internet.

Never on Batch (its GPU containers run privileged on their VM), never on the local target,
never in a job that holds secrets. `experiment_results` scores a finished sweep in the
host's process only with `evaluate=True` (off by default), which `allow_patches` rules out:
such a host scores sweeps with simpl_foundry's evaluation job or under its own account.

**The patch gate** (`experiment_patch`) runs before a patched candidate is planned or
submitted: at most `MAX_PATCH_CHARS` (30,000) characters; only files of the recipe's
`code_surface`, never the evaluator's (`EVALUATOR_CODE` and its entry point's module); no
link or submodule; and no Python it adds that starts a process (`subprocess`,
`os.system`, ...), opens a network connection (`socket`, `urllib`, `requests`, ...),
reads the environment (`os.environ`, `os.getenv`, `dotenv`, cloud credential libraries) or
runs code built at run time (`eval`, `exec`, `importlib`, ...), import aliases resolved. It
is hygiene, not a sandbox: a determined patch can hide such a call, so what protects is the
job that runs it, remote, under an account set apart, with no secret and no network.

`experiment_code` reads the trainer's package: without `path` it lists its files (paths
from the repository root, each `changeable` when the code surface allows a patch and
`evaluator` for the evaluator's), with `path` it returns up to 16,000 characters from
`start_line` and `next_start_line` when there is more. `base` reads that experiment's
commit instead of the current code. It reads the files of the experiment's code manifest
through git, never the file system: any other path, a link and a submodule are refused.

`experiment_progress` and `experiment_stop` take a `sweep_id` and act only on the sweeps the
context's own submissions queued (`ExperimentToolContext.sweeps`); any other is refused.
`experiment_progress` is a query: each trial's run status, attempt, last step, heartbeat age
while running, and the last, lowest and highest value of up to 20 training metric keys of
the run's summary (`system/` keys left out). Never a series, never a row, never a held-out
score. `experiment_stop` ends the sweep's unfinished runs through `cancel_sweep`, on any
target (`{"status": "stopped", "runs_ended": n}`); on a sweep that has ended it changes
nothing (`"ended"`). Stopping gives no budget back. Without a working Leonardo login,
submitting to and stopping on Leonardo are refusals that tell the model to ask a person to
log in; reading stays allowed.

`ExperimentToolContext.max_scored` caps how many experiments the context's submissions may
queue to be scored on the held-out split (`scored` holds them); `recent_cap`
(`RecentCap(max_experiments, days)`) caps how many experiments of the recipe were scored on
its objective's split within the last `days`, across all campaigns
(`EvaluationRepository.experiments_scored`), new ones included. Past either a submission is
refused and reading stays allowed. With `notes` (a campaign's `CampaignNotes`), every
submitted candidate needs a `hypothesis` (20 to 2,000 characters), a candidate whose change
the notes show rejected is refused, and each experiment a submission queues is noted.
`experiment_notes` is refused without notes.

### Experiment campaigns

`simpl_core.agent_framework.experiment_campaign` runs one or more agents that improve a
recipe over rounds of sweeps: the ledger agent `experiment_campaign_planner` (config key
`experiment_campaign_root`, authored in that package's `agent_policy.yaml`), whose only
tools are the nine above. The recipe needs `budget` and `objective`, and a `compute` or a
`resources` section the target can run.

```bash
python -m simpl_core experiment_campaign path/to/recipe.yaml --rounds 4 \
    [--agents explore,refine] [--on batch|cloud_run|leonardo] [--gpus N] [--cpus N] \
    [--memory-gb N] [--hours H] [--nodes N] [--queue Q] [--files-only] [--allow-patches] \
    [--scoring here|evaluator_job|elsewhere] [--max-scored N] [--max-scored-recently N] \
    [--recent-days 30] [--max-hours 24] [--poll-minutes 1] [--max-wait-minutes 60] \
    [--parallel 4] [--work-dir DIR] [--config-version V]
```

- **Agents**: `--agents` (`CampaignSettings.agents`) gives each agent's focus, one to four of
  `improve` (the default, alone), `explore` (changes nobody tried) and `refine` (small steps
  around the base); they are named `<focus>_<n>`. Each round calls them in turn, so an agent
  sees what the ones before it submitted that round. They share one budget, the sweeps and
  the notes; an identical candidate never trains twice (its seeds are covered).
- **A turn** is one `execute_agent` call. Its inputs carry the state: the agent, its focus
  and the campaign's agents, the recipe, the round and how many there are, the GPU hours
  left and what one trial reserves (what its resolved job asks for), the candidates that may
  still be scored, `jobs` (the target, whether jobs write files only, and who scores), the
  objective, each search-surface key with what it allows and its
  declared value, whether code patches are allowed and the code surface, the base (the
  experiment to build on, with its mean), a count of the notes by outcome, the notes
  written this round so far, and each sweep with its status and counts of trial states and
  run statuses. The planner answers `reasoning`, `decision` (`submitted`, `waiting`,
  `stop`), the `sweeps` it submitted and `built_on`, the experiment it built on.
- **Notes** (`agent_framework.experiment_notes`, one row per experiment a submission
  queued): the hypothesis, round, agent, base, overrides, patched paths and the hash of the
  change (the lines the patch adds and removes and the settings that differ from the
  base's). Scores are read from `training` when the notes are read, never copied. Each
  experiment is judged at the round of its first note against the best experiment kept
  before that round, starting from the recipe's best when the campaign started: `kept` when
  it beats it by more than the objective's noise band, `rejected` otherwise, `pending`
  until scored; the best kept one is the `base`. A rejected change is refused when proposed
  again, whatever its base.
- **Where trials train**: `--on` (`CampaignSettings.target`), else the recipe's
  `compute.platform` when it is `batch`, `cloud_run` or `leonardo`, else `batch`; the size
  options replace the compute section's fields (`CampaignSettings.compute`). `--files-only`
  runs every job with no database login and no secret.
- **Code changes**: with `--allow-patches` (`CampaignSettings.allow_patches`) candidates may
  carry a patch or a base, and **every** job of the campaign runs files-only, so reviewed and
  unreviewed candidates never share a job that holds secrets. It needs `--on cloud_run`
  (files-only, as the files-only account) or `--on leonardo`, a recipe with a sealed split
  (`Recipe.sealed_split`: an `acceptance_rule` on another held-out split than the
  objective's), and `--scoring` other than `here`; each is refused at start otherwise.
  Without it, patches are refused.
- **Scoring** (`--scoring`, `CampaignSettings.scoring`): `here` scores finished trials in the
  campaign's process when `experiment_results` reads them (refused with patches);
  `evaluator_job` submits simpl_foundry's evaluation job on Cloud Run (`sweep evaluate
  --on cloud_run`) for a sweep once its trials' runs have all ended, once (a failed
  submission is retried at the next poll, 3 times at most); `elsewhere` scores nothing: a
  person or a schedule runs `sweep evaluate`, and the campaign waits.
- **Held-out caps**: `--max-scored` (`CampaignSettings.max_scored`) is how many candidates the
  campaign may have scored on the held-out split; `--max-scored-recently N` with
  `--recent-days D` (`CampaignSettings.recent_cap`) how many experiments of the recipe may
  have been scored on the objective's split in the last D days, by any campaign or person.
  Past either, new submissions are refused with the reason, and results, notes and the
  leaderboard stay readable.
- **Before round one** the campaign refuses (`CampaignRefused`, exit 2) what could never
  run: a setting the resolution needs and lacks, named; one trial over the whole budget; the
  patch conditions above; the evaluation job's settings; and on Leonardo its settings and a
  working SSH login.
- **Between rounds** plain code reads the campaign's sweeps every poll and calls no model.
  For jobs that write files only (files-only, or on Leonardo, where the courier copies them
  to the artifact root) it first applies their new verified log pieces with
  simpl_foundry's following sync, under the host's database settings and log secret, so
  `experiment_progress` shows epochs about a minute behind; a run whose log cannot be applied
  is logged and the campaign goes on. With `evaluator_job` it then submits the evaluation
  jobs that are due. It goes on when a sweep or a run changes status, a trial changes state
  (scored included) or a run logs a later step, after `--max-wait-minutes` otherwise, and at
  once when no sweep is open.
- **It stops** when every agent of a round decides `stop`, after the last round, when the
  budget left cannot pay for one trial, or at `--max-hours`. Ctrl-C stops it at once (exit
  130). It never ends a sweep: those still running are printed and stay running. It prints
  the base its notes name.
- **What an agent cannot do**: act on data, the evaluator or a held-out row, write a score
  or a note itself, run a command, or read a file outside the trainer's package.
- **Audit**: each turn is a `node_runs` row (`node_id` `experiment_campaign_planner`,
  `step` `round_<n>`, `correlation_id` the campaign's id, `route` the decision, `scope`
  the recipe, the agent and its focus), and under it the call's `agent_runs` row and one
  `tool_call_runs` row per tool call; a note names its turn's `node_run_id`. Every sweep, its runs and
  the experiments and snapshots they create name the campaign as `created_by`
  (`campaign:<id>`).
- **Environment**: the platform database (the ledger, the audit and the notes) and the
  `training` schema from the Supabase variables (`DatabaseSettings()`), the model keys of
  the default resolver (`OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY`), and what the target needs,
  named in simpl_foundry's README:
  - `batch`, `cloud_run`: Docker with the registry's credentials, Application Default
    Credentials, `SIMPL_TRAINING_REGISTRY`, `SIMPL_TRAINING_REGION`,
    `SIMPL_TRAINING_SERVICE_ACCOUNT`, and `SIMPL_TRAINING_ARTIFACT_ROOT` for a recipe with no
    artifact root; files-only (and so with patches) also `SIMPL_TRAINING_FILES_ONLY_ACCOUNT`
    and `SIMPL_TRAINING_LOG_SECRET`, and `SIMPL_TRAINING_NETWORK` with
    `SIMPL_TRAINING_SUBNETWORK` for jobs with no internet;
  - `leonardo`: `SIMPL_LEONARDO_HOST`, `SIMPL_LEONARDO_ACCOUNT`, `SIMPL_LEONARDO_DIR`, a valid
    SSH login, the image (`simpl_foundry image build <recipe> --on leonardo`), and
    `SIMPL_LEONARDO_GCS_KEY`, the courier's key there: a campaign is refused at its start
    without it, as nothing would arrive until a person runs `simpl_foundry collect`;
  - `--scoring evaluator_job`: `SIMPL_TRAINING_EVALUATOR_ACCOUNT` with the registry and the
    region, Docker, and a checkout whose evaluator package matches its commit.
  The notes need migration `20261105c_experiment_notes.sql`.
- **What a person still does**: score sweeps when no evaluation job is configured
  (`simpl_foundry sweep evaluate`), log in to Leonardo again when the login lapses (the
  campaign keeps reading meanwhile), and promote (`simpl_foundry sweep promote`, which
  alone reads the sealed split, then a pull request).
- **Publishing the planner** is a new ledger version, as for any document: `simpl_core
  af_config_migrate --yaml src/simpl_core/agent_framework/experiment_campaign/agent_policy.yaml
  --dest migrations/ --config-key experiment_campaign_root --version <n> --parent <n-1>
  --prefix <migration number> --summary "..." --activate` (`--parent 0` for the first), then
  a person applies the seed. Until a version is active, the command fails with `LookupError`.
  The document's entries are at version 3.

## What this library does NOT do

- Does not expose the HTTP API. That is `simpl_api`.
- Does not schedule or deploy Prefect workflows. That is `simpl_flow`.
- Does not own generic ORM primitives. That is `simpl_orm`.
- Does not own tracking/logging helpers. That is `simpl_tracker`.
- Does not run sandboxes or their sub-agents. That is `simpl_agent_sandbox`; the framework only offers its tasks as agent tools.
- Does not train models. That is `simpl_foundry`; the framework only offers its experiments as agent tools, and the row store is one of its recipe sources.
- Does not score or orchestrate the Reply Confidence gate (the confidence, the threshold, the decision). That is simpl-outreach; `simpl_core` owns only its settings table, the SQL functions and triggers around it, and the send-time check.

